## Final Solution (single file)

```js
// custom-errors.js
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, code) {
    super(message);
    this.name = this.constructor.name;
    this.code = code ?? "UNKNOWN";
    if (Error.captureStackTrace) Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
  toJSON() {
    return { name: this.name, message: this.message, code: this.code, stack: this.stack };
  }
}

class ValidationError extends AppError {
  constructor(message, code = "VALIDATION_FAILED") { super(message, code); }
}
class EmailError extends ValidationError {
  constructor(message = "Invalid email address", code = "INVALID_EMAIL") { super(message, code); }
}
class AgeError extends ValidationError {
  constructor(message = "Age must be 0-150", code = "INVALID_AGE") { super(message, code); }
}

function validateUser(input) {
  if (!input || typeof input !== "object") throw new ValidationError("Input must be object", "INVALID_INPUT");
  const { name, email, age } = input;
  if (typeof name !== "string" || name.trim().length < 2)
    throw new ValidationError("Name too short", "INVALID_NAME");
  if (typeof email !== "string" || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email))
    throw new EmailError();
  if (!Number.isInteger(age) || age < 0 || age > 150)
    throw new AgeError();
  return true;
}

function registerUser(input) {
  try {
    validateUser(input);
    return { ok: true };
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof EmailError) return { ok: false, retry: "email" };
    if (err instanceof AgeError)   return { ok: false, retry: "age" };
    if (err instanceof ValidationError) return { ok: false, retry: "general" };
    throw err;
  }
}

module.exports = { AppError, ValidationError, EmailError, AgeError, validateUser, registerUser };

// demo
if (require.main === module) {
  console.log(registerUser({ name: "Al", email: "bad", age: 25 }));   // retry email
  console.log(registerUser({ name: "Alice", email: "a@b.com", age: 30 })); // ok
}
```

### Key takeaways
- Always `super(message)` first (so `this` is initialized).
- Set `name = this.constructor.name` so logs/stacks are meaningful.
- Use `Error.captureStackTrace` for clean stacks in V8.
- Prefer `instanceof` over string checks; design the hierarchy so broad → specific checks go **specific first**.
- Add `toJSON()` if errors cross process boundaries (logs, HTTP).

## Custom Exceptions in JavaScript — Interview-Style Answer

### 1. What / Why

JavaScript has built-in error types (`Error`, `TypeError`, `RangeError`, …).  
**Custom exceptions** are user-defined error classes that extend `Error` (or another built-in) so you can:

- Represent **domain-specific failures** (`ValidationError`, `NotFoundError`, `PaymentDeclinedError`).
- Attach **extra metadata** (HTTP status, error code, offending field, retryable flag).
- Use **`instanceof`** for precise `catch` handling instead of parsing `message` strings.
- Preserve a **stack trace** and (modern JS) an **error cause chain**.

### 2. Core Design Rules

| Rule | Reason |
|------|--------|
| `class X extends Error` | Keeps `instanceof Error` true and inherits `stack`, `message`. |
| Call `super(message)` first | Initializes the base `Error` (message/stack). |
| Set `this.name = 'X'` | Makes `toString()` and logs show the real type. |
| Use `Error.captureStackTrace(this, ctor)` when available | Removes the constructor frame from the stack (V8/Node). |
| Don’t lose the prototype chain | Class syntax handles this; with manual functions use `Object.setPrototypeOf`. |
| Support `cause` (ES2022) | Enables `new Err(msg, { cause: original })` chaining. |

### 3. Implementation

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Base application error: every domain error extends this.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
class AppError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message  Human-readable description.
   * @param {object} [options]
   * @param {string} [options.code]     Machine-readable code, e.g. "USER_NOT_FOUND".
   * @param {number} [options.status]   HTTP-ish status (optional).
   * @param {unknown} [options.cause]    Original error for chaining.
   * @param {Record<string, unknown>} [options.meta]  Extra context.
   */
  constructor(message, options = {}) {
    // Pass message + cause to the base Error (ES2022 supports cause).
    super(message, { cause: options.cause });

    // Restore the correct name (defaults to "Error").
    this.name = new.target.name; // e.g. "ValidationError" for subclasses

    // Domain metadata.
    this.code = options.code ?? 'APP_ERROR';
    this.status = options.status ?? 500;
    this.meta = options.meta ?? {};

    // V8/Node: drop this constructor from the stack trace.
    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }

  // Convenience: serialize for APIs/logs without leaking internals.
  toJSON() {
    return {
      name: this.name,
      message: this.message,
      code: this.code,
      status: this.status,
      meta: this.meta,
      cause: this.cause ? String(this.cause) : undefined,
    };
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Concrete domain errors.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
class ValidationError extends AppError {
  constructor(message, field, options = {}) {
    super(message, {
      code: 'VALIDATION_ERROR',
      status: 400,
      meta: { field, ...options.meta },
      cause: options.cause,
    });
  }
}

class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource, id, options = {}) {
    super(`${resource} not found${id != null ? `: ${id}` : ''}`, {
      code: 'NOT_FOUND',
      status: 404,
      meta: { resource, id, ...options.meta },
      cause: options.cause,
    });
  }
}

class PaymentDeclinedError extends AppError {
  constructor(reason, options = {}) {
    super(`Payment declined: ${reason}`, {
      code: 'PAYMENT_DECLINED',
      status: 402,
      meta: { reason, ...options.meta },
      cause: options.cause,
    });
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo usage.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
function chargeCard(amount, cardToken) {
  if (typeof amount !== 'number' || amount <= 0) {
    throw new ValidationError('Amount must be a positive number', 'amount');
  }
  // Simulate a downstream failure we want to wrap.
  try {
    // pretend: gateway.charge(...)
    throw new Error('gateway timeout');
  } catch (err) {
    throw new PaymentDeclinedError('gateway unavailable', { cause: err });
  }
}

function getUser(id) {
  const user = null; // pretend DB lookup
  if (!user) throw new NotFoundError('User', id);
  return user;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Caller: precise, type-driven handling.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
try {
  getUser(42);
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof NotFoundError) {
    console.log('404-ish:', e.code, e.meta); // NOT_FOUND { resource: 'User', id: 42 }
  } else if (e instanceof ValidationError) {
    console.log('bad input:', e.meta.field);
  } else if (e instanceof AppError) {
    console.log('app error:', e.status);
  } else {
    console.log('unexpected:', e);
  }
}

try {
  chargeCard(10, 'tok_123');
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.name);        // PaymentDeclinedError
  console.log(e.toJSON());    // serialized form
  console.log(e.cause);       // original gateway timeout Error
  console.log(e.stack);       // stack WITHOUT the constructor frame
}
```

### 4. Interview Talking Points

- **Why not just `throw new Error('...')`?** Strings are fragile; `instanceof` + metadata is robust and testable.
- **Prototype chain:** ES6 classes fix the old `function MyError()` + `Object.setPrototypeOf` boilerplate.
- **Stack traces:** `captureStackTrace` is V8-specific; in other engines the stack still works, just slightly noisier.
- **Cause chaining:** `{ cause }` (ES2022) is the standard way to preserve the original failure; older code used `this.originalError = err`.
- **Serialization:** `toJSON()` keeps logs/APIs clean and avoids circular structures.
- **Don’t over-engineer:** If a simple `Error` with a code string suffices, prefer simplicity; reserve custom classes for real domain boundaries.

### 5. Common Pitfall (worth mentioning)

```js
// ❌ Loses stack / wrong name in older patterns
function BadError(msg) {
  Error.call(this, msg);
  this.name = 'BadError';
}
// ✅ Modern fix
class GoodError extends Error {}
```

That’s the core: **extend `Error`, set `name`, add metadata, capture stack, support `cause`, and handle by `instanceof`.**

Below are the main edge cases when defining and throwing custom exceptions in JavaScript, with practical patterns for each.

## 1. Prototype / `instanceof` correctness

If you extend `Error` incorrectly (especially in transpiled ES5 output), `instanceof` can break.

```js
class HttpError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'HttpError';
    this.statusCode = statusCode;

    // Only needed in some transpiled/older environments:
    if (Object.setPrototypeOf) {
      Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
    }
  }
}

const e = new HttpError('not found', 404);
console.log(e instanceof HttpError); // true
console.log(e instanceof Error);     // true
```

Key points:
- Call `super(message)` first.
- Set `this.name` explicitly; otherwise it may stay `"Error"`.
- In TS/old transpilation, restore the prototype with `Object.setPrototypeOf`.

---

## 2. Stack traces that include the constructor itself

By default, the constructor frame may appear in the stack. Use `Error.captureStackTrace` in V8 environments.

```js
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message, field) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
    this.field = field;

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }
}
```

Edge case: `Error.captureStackTrace` is V8-only. Always guard it.

---

## 3. `name` collisions and minification

Relying only on `err.name` or `err.constructor.name` is fragile after bundling/minification.

```js
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, options = {}) {
    super(message, options);
    // A stable brand is safer than "name" alone
    this.isAppError = true;
    this.code = options.code ?? 'APP_ERROR';

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }
}

function isAppError(err) {
  return Boolean(err && typeof err === 'object' && err.isAppError === true);
}
```

Cross-realm/cross-bundle edge case: `instanceof` can fail even when the class is “the same” conceptually. A brand property (`isAppError`) or unique code is more robust.

---

## 4. Error cause chaining (ES2022)

Always support `cause`, and preserve chains when wrapping.

```js
class NetworkError extends Error {
  constructor(message, options = {}) {
    super(message, options); // preserves options.cause
    this.name = 'NetworkError';
    this.url = options.url;

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }
}

async function fetchUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return res.json();
  } catch (cause) {
    throw new NetworkError('Failed to fetch user', {
      cause,
      url: `/users/${id}`,
    });
  }
}
```

Traversal helper:

```js
function getErrorChain(err) {
  const chain = [];
  let current = err;
  while (current instanceof Error) {
    chain.push(current);
    current = current.cause;
  }
  return chain;
}
```

---

## 5. Serialization and logging lose non-enumerable properties

Default Error properties (`message`, `stack`, `name`) are often non-enumerable, so `JSON.stringify(err)` may produce `{}`.

```js
class DomainError extends Error {
  constructor(message, options = {}) {
    super(message, options);
    this.name = 'DomainError';
    this.code = options.code ?? 'DOMAIN_ERROR';
    this.details = options.details ?? null;

    // Make metadata explicit for serializers if desired
    this.toJSON = () => ({
      name: this.name,
      message: this.message,
      code: this.code,
      details: this.details,
      stack: this.stack,
      cause: serializeCause(this.cause),
    });

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }
}

function serializeCause(cause) {
  if (cause instanceof Error) {
    return cause.toJSON?.() ?? {
      name: cause.name,
      message: cause.message,
      stack: cause.stack,
    };
  }
  return cause;
}
```

Node-specific inspection edge case:

```js
const inspect = Symbol.for('nodejs.util.inspect.custom');

class DebuggableError extends Error {
  constructor(message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'DebuggableError';

    this[inspect] = function (depth, options, inspectFn) {
      return `${this.name}: ${this.message}\n${this.stack}`;
    };
  }
}
```

---

## 6. Cross-realm, cross-frame, and duplicate module instances

`instanceof` is identity-based. In iframes, VM contexts, or duplicated bundler copies, it can fail.

```js
function handleUnknownError(err) {
  // Prefer duck typing / branding over strict instanceof across boundaries
  if (err && err.isAppError === true) {
    console.error('Known app error:', err.code, err.message);
    return;
  }

  // Fall back to name/code checks
  if (err && (err.name === 'HttpError' || err.code?.startsWith?.('HTTP_'))) {
    console.error('HTTP-ish error:', err);
    return;
  }

  throw err; // unknown: do not swallow
}
```

---

## 7. Async and promise rejection edge cases

Custom errors behave normally with `throw`, but callers may only catch `Error` subclasses inconsistently. Ensure rethrowing unknown errors.

```js
async function safeOperation() {
  try {
    throw new DomainError('invalid input', { code: 'INVALID_INPUT' });
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof DomainError) {
      return { ok: false, code: err.code };
    }

    // Do not hide programming errors or unrelated failures
    throw err;
  }
}
```

Unhandled rejection handling:

```js
// Browser
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', event => {
  console.error('Unhandled rejection:', event.reason);
});

// Node
process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason) => {
  console.error('Unhandled rejection:', reason);
});
```

Edge case: `event.reason` or `reason` may not be an `Error`. Normalize at boundaries:

```js
function toError(value) {
  if (value instanceof Error) return value;
  try {
    return new Error(typeof value === 'string' ? value : JSON.stringify(value));
  } catch {
    return new Error(String(value));
  }
}
```

---

## 8. Compatibility with older transpilation targets

If targeting ES5, subclassing built-ins can lose the custom prototype unless fixed manually.

```js
function LegacyError(message) {
  const err = Error.call(this, message);
  if (Object.setPrototypeOf) {
    Object.setPrototypeOf(err, LegacyError.prototype);
  } else {
    err.__proto__ = LegacyError.prototype;
  }
  err.name = 'LegacyError';
  return err;
}

LegacyError.prototype = Object.create(Error.prototype, {
  constructor: { value: LegacyError, writable: true, configurable: true },
});
```

For modern code, prefer real `class` syntax and only keep the `Object.setPrototypeOf` guard.

---

## 9. `toString`, printing, and stack limits

Default `toString` gives `Name: message`. Override when custom formatting matters, but keep stack traces readable.

```js
class QuotaError extends Error {
  constructor(message, limit, used) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'QuotaError';
    this.limit = limit;
    this.used = used;

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }

  toString() {
    return `${this.name}: ${this.message} (used ${this.used}/${this.limit})`;
  }
}
```

Edge case: deep recursion or huge traces. You can cap stack size:

```js
Error.stackTraceLimit = 20; // Node/V8
```

---

## 10. Never throw non-Error values

Some code throws strings or plain objects. Standardize at API boundaries.

```js
function assertPositive(n) {
  if (typeof n !== 'number' || n <= 0) {
    throw new ValidationError('value must be a positive number', 'value');
  }
}
```

And in catch blocks:

```js
try {
  risky();
} catch (thrown) {
  const err = thrown instanceof Error ? thrown : new Error(String(thrown));
  // now err is always an Error instance
}
```

---

## Recommended base class

A solid reusable base that handles most of the above:

```js
class BaseError extends Error {
  constructor(message, options = {}) {
    super(message, options);

    // Ensure subclass name is used
    this.name = new.target.name;

    // Stable branding for cross-realm/bundler safety
    this.isBaseError = true;

    // Optional structured fields
    if (options.code !== undefined) this.code = options.code;
    if (options.details !== undefined) this.details = options.details;

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }

    // Serialization helper
    this.toJSON = () => ({
      name: this.name,
      message: this.message,
      code: this.code,
      details: this.details,
      stack: this.stack,
      cause:
        this.cause instanceof Error
          ? (typeof this.cause.toJSON === 'function'
              ? this.cause.toJSON()
              : {
                  name: this.cause.name,
                  message: this.cause.message,
                  stack: this.cause.stack,
                })
          : this.cause,
    });
  }
}

class NotFoundError extends BaseError {
  constructor(resource, id, options = {}) {
    super(`${resource} not found`, {
      ...options,
      code: options.code ?? 'NOT_FOUND',
      details: { resource, id, ...(options.details ?? {}) },
    });
  }
}
```

### Summary checklist

- Call `super(message, options)` and set `this.name`.
- Capture stack traces safely with a V8 guard.
- Preserve and serialize `cause`.
- Add stable branding for cross-realm/bundle safety.
- Implement `toJSON` because Error properties are often non-enumerable.
- Guard async/promise boundaries and normalize thrown values to `Error`.
- Re-throw unknown errors instead of swallowing them.
- Handle transpilation/cross-frame `instanceof` pitfalls.

Below is a complete, runnable Node.js example that implements database-style indexes in pure JavaScript: a hash index (equality) and an ordered (B-tree-like) index (prefix equality + range), plus a tiny query planner that picks the best index, maintains indexes on writes, enforces unique constraints, and supports `EXPLAIN`.

Save as `indexes.js` and run with `node indexes.js`.

```js
// indexes.js  --  run with: node indexes.js
'use strict';

// ----------------------------- helpers -----------------------------
const MIN_SENTINEL = { __min: true };
const MAX_SENTINEL = { __max: true };

function get(obj, path) {
  return String(path).split('.').reduce((o, k) => (o == null ? undefined : o[k]), obj);
}

function deepEqual(a, b) {
  return JSON.stringify(a) === JSON.stringify(b);
}

function serialize(v) {
  return JSON.stringify(v, (key, val) => {
    if (val instanceof Date) return { __date: val.toISOString() };
    if (val === undefined) return { __undef: true };
    return val;
  });
}

function cmpVal(a, b) {
  if (a && a.__min) return (b && b.__min) ? 0 : -1;
  if (a && a.__max) return (b && b.__max) ? 0 : 1;
  if (b && b.__min) return 1;
  if (b && b.__max) return -1;
  if (a == null && b == null) return 0;
  if (a == null) return -1;
  if (b == null) return 1;
  if (typeof a === 'number' && typeof b === 'number') return a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0;
  if (a instanceof Date && b instanceof Date) return a.getTime() - b.getTime();
  const sa = String(a), sb = String(b);
  return sa < sb ? -1 : sa > sb ? 1 : 0;
}

function cmpArr(a, b) {
  const n = Math.max(a.length, b.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const va = i < a.length ? a[i] : MIN_SENTINEL;
    const vb = i < b.length ? b[i] : MIN_SENTINEL;
    const c = cmpVal(va, vb);
    if (c !== 0) return c;
  }
  return 0;
}

let _idCounter = 0;
function genId() { return 'doc_' + (++_idCounter); }

// ----------------------------- filter normalization -----------------------------
function normalizeFilter(filter) {
  const map = new Map();
  if (!filter) return map;

  const conditions = [];
  if (Array.isArray(filter.$and)) conditions.push(...filter.$and);
  else {
    for (const k of Object.keys(filter)) {
      if (k === '$or' || k === '$and') continue;
      conditions.push({ [k]: filter[k] });
    }
  }

  for (const c of conditions) {
    for (const key of Object.keys(c)) {
      const val = c[key];
      const cond = map.get(key) || { eq: undefined, ne: undefined, inList: undefined, range: null };
      if (val && typeof val === 'object' && !Array.isArray(val) && !(val instanceof Date)) {
        if (val.$eq !== undefined) cond.eq = val.$eq;
        if (val.$ne !== undefined) cond.ne = val.$ne;
        if (Array.isArray(val.$in)) cond.inList = val.$in;
        if (val.$gt !== undefined || val.$gte !== undefined || val.$lt !== undefined || val.$lte !== undefined) {
          const r = cond.range || { hasLo: false, lo: null, loInc: false, hasHi: false, hi: null, hiInc: false };
          if (val.$gt !== undefined) { r.hasLo = true; r.lo = val.$gt; r.loInc = false; }
          if (val.$gte !== undefined) { r.hasLo = true; r.lo = val.$gte; r.loInc = true; }
          if (val.$lt !== undefined) { r.hasHi = true; r.hi = val.$lt; r.hiInc = false; }
          if (val.$lte !== undefined) { r.hasHi = true; r.hi = val.$lte; r.hiInc = true; }
          cond.range = r;
        }
      } else {
        cond.eq = val;
      }
      map.set(key, cond);
    }
  }
  return map;
}

function matches(doc, filter) {
  const conds = normalizeFilter(filter);
  for (const [field, c] of conds) {
    const v = get(doc, field);
    if (c.eq !== undefined && !deepEqual(v, c.eq)) return false;
    if (c.ne !== undefined && deepEqual(v, c.ne)) return false;
    if (c.inList && !c.inList.some(x => deepEqual(x, v))) return false;
    if (c.range) {
      const r = c.range;
      if (r.hasLo) {
        const cmp = cmpVal(v, r.lo);
        if (r.loInc ? cmp < 0 : cmp <= 0) return false;
      }
      if (r.hasHi) {
        const cmp = cmpVal(v, r.hi);
        if (r.hiInc ? cmp > 0 : cmp >= 0) return false;
      }
    }
  }
  // $or support
  if (Array.isArray(filter && filter.$or)) {
    if (!filter.$or.some(sub => matches(doc, sub))) return false;
  }
  return true;
}

// ----------------------------- Index base -----------------------------
class Index {
  constructor({ name, fields, unique, type }) {
    this.name = name;
    this.fields = [...fields];
    this.unique = !!unique;
    this.type = type; // 'hash' | 'ordered'
  }
  keyOf(doc) { return this.fields.map(f => get(doc, f)); }
}

// ----------------------------- Hash index -----------------------------
class HashIndex extends Index {
  constructor(opts) { super(opts); this.map = new Map(); } // serialized key -> Set<docId>
  insert(doc) {
    const k = serialize(this.keyOf(doc));
    if (!this.map.has(k)) this.map.set(k, new Set());
    const s = this.map.get(k);
    if (this.unique && s.size > 0 && !s.has(doc._id)) {
      throw new Error(`Unique constraint violation on index ${this.name}`);
    }
    s.add(doc._id);
  }
  remove(doc) {
    const k = serialize(this.keyOf(doc));
    const s = this.map.get(k);
    if (!s) return;
    s.delete(doc._id);
    if (s.size === 0) this.map.delete(k);
  }
  findEqual(vals) {
    const k = serialize(vals);
    const s = this.map.get(k);
    return s ? [...s] : [];
  }
  canServe(filter) {
    const conds = normalizeFilter(filter);
    let p = 0;
    while (p < this.fields.length) {
      const c = conds.get(this.fields[p]);
      if (c && c.eq !== undefined && !c.range) p++;
      else break;
    }
    if (p === this.fields.length) return p + 0.5 + (this.unique ? 0.5 : 0);
    return 0;
  }
  search(filter) {
    const conds = normalizeFilter(filter);
    const vals = this.fields.map(f => {
      const c = conds.get(f);
      return c ? c.eq : undefined;
    });
    return new Set(this.findEqual(vals));
  }
}

// ----------------------------- Ordered index -----------------------------
class OrderedIndex extends Index {
  constructor(opts) { super(opts); this.entries = []; } // sorted by key array
  insert(doc) {
    const key = this.keyOf(doc);
    // upper-bound position
    let lo = 0, hi = this.entries.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (cmpArr(this.entries[mid].key, key) <= 0) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid;
    }
    if (lo > 0 && cmpArr(this.entries[lo - 1].key, key) === 0) {
      const entry = this.entries[lo - 1];
      if (this.unique && entry.ids.size > 0 && !entry.ids.has(doc._id)) {
        throw new Error(`Unique constraint violation on index ${this.name}`);
      }
      entry.ids.add(doc._id);
    } else {
      this.entries.splice(lo, 0, { key, ids: new Set([doc._id]) });
    }
  }
  remove(doc) {
    const key = this.keyOf(doc);
    let lo = 0, hi = this.entries.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (cmpArr(this.entries[mid].key, key) < 0) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid;
    }
    if (lo < this.entries.length && cmpArr(this.entries[lo].key, key) === 0) {
      this.entries[lo].ids.delete(doc._id);
      if (this.entries[lo].ids.size === 0) this.entries.splice(lo, 1);
    }
  }
  canServe(filter) {
    const conds = normalizeFilter(filter);
    let p = 0;
    while (p < this.fields.length) {
      const c = conds.get(this.fields[p]);
      if (c && c.eq !== undefined && !c.range) p++;
      else break;
    }
    let hasRange = false;
    if (p < this.fields.length) {
      const c = conds.get(this.fields[p]);
      if (c && c.range && c.eq === undefined) hasRange = true;
    }
    if (p === 0 && !hasRange) return 0;
    let score = p + (hasRange ? 1 : 0);
    if (this.unique) score += 0.5;
    return score;
  }
  search(filter) {
    const conds = normalizeFilter(filter);
    const fields = this.fields;
    let p = 0;
    while (p < fields.length) {
      const c = conds.get(fields[p]);
      if (c && c.eq !== undefined && !c.range) p++;
      else break;
    }
    let hasRange = false, range = null;
    if (p < fields.length) {
      const c = conds.get(fields[p]);
      if (c && c.range && c.eq === undefined) { hasRange = true; range = c.range; }
    }
    if (this.type === 'hash') {
      // shouldn't happen for ordered, but guard
      if (p !== fields.length) return null;
      const vals = fields.map(f => conds.get(f).eq);
      return new Set(this.findEqual ? this.findEqual(vals) : []);
    }
    if (p === 0 && !hasRange) return null;

    const low = new Array(fields.length);
    const high = new Array(fields.length);

    for (let i = 0; i < p; i++) {
      const v = conds.get(fields[i]).eq;
      low[i] = v; high[i] = v;
    }
    if (hasRange) {
      low[p] = range.hasLo ? range.lo : MIN_SENTINEL;
      high[p] = range.hasHi ? range.hi : MAX_SENTINEL;
    }
    const startFill = hasRange ? p + 1 : p;
    for (let i = startFill; i < fields.length; i++) {
      low[i] = MIN_SENTINEL;
      high[i] = MAX_SENTINEL;
    }
    // if no range and p<fields.length, fill p.. with sentinels
    if (!hasRange) {
      for (let i = p; i < fields.length; i++) {
        low[i] = MIN_SENTINEL;
        high[i] = MAX_SENTINEL;
      }
    }

    const entries = this.entries;
    // lower bound
    let loIdx = 0, hiIdx = entries.length;
    while (loIdx < hiIdx) {
      const mid = (loIdx + hiIdx) >> 1;
      if (cmpArr(entries[mid].key, low) < 0) loIdx = mid + 1;
      else hiIdx = mid;
    }
    // upper bound
    let l = 0, r = entries.length - 1, ans = -1;
    while (l <= r) {
      const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
      if (cmpArr(entries[mid].key, high) <= 0) { ans = mid; l = mid + 1; }
      else r = mid - 1;
    }
    const res = new Set();
    for (let i = loIdx; i <= ans && i < entries.length; i++) {
      for (const id of entries[i].ids) res.add(id);
    }
    return res;
  }
}

// ----------------------------- Collection -----------------------------
class Collection {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    this.docs = new Map(); // id -> doc
    this.indexes = new Map();
    this.createIndex(['_id'], { name: '_id', unique: true, type: 'hash' });
  }
  createIndex(fields, opts = {}) {
    const name = opts.name || fields.join('_');
    if (this.indexes.has(name)) throw new Error(`Index ${name} already exists`);
    const type = opts.type || 'btree';
    const Cls = type === 'hash' ? HashIndex : OrderedIndex;
    const idx = new Cls({ name, fields: [...fields], unique: !!opts.unique, type: type === 'hash' ? 'hash' : 'ordered' });
    for (const doc of this.docs.values()) idx.insert(doc);
    this.indexes.set(name, idx);
    return name;
  }
  dropIndex(name) { this.indexes.delete(name); }

  insert(doc) {
    const d = { ...doc };
    if (d._id == null) d._id = genId();
    if (this.docs.has(d._id)) throw new Error(`Duplicate _id ${d._id}`);
    const inserted = [];
    try {
      for (const idx of this.indexes.values()) { idx.insert(d); inserted.push(idx); }
    } catch (e) {
      for (const idx of inserted) idx.remove(d);
      throw e;
    }
    this.docs.set(d._id, d);
    return d;
  }
  insertMany(arr) { return arr.map(d => this.insert(d)); }

  update(id, patch) {
    const oldDoc = this.docs.get(id);
    if (!oldDoc) return null;
    const newDoc = { ...oldDoc };
    for (const k of Object.keys(patch)) newDoc[k] = patch[k];
    newDoc._id = oldDoc._id;

    for (const idx of this.indexes.values()) idx.remove(oldDoc);
    const inserted = [];
    try {
      for (const idx of this.indexes.values()) { idx.insert(newDoc); inserted.push(idx); }
    } catch (e) {
      for (const idx of inserted) idx.remove(newDoc);
      for (const idx of this.indexes.values()) idx.insert(oldDoc);
      throw e;
    }
    this.docs.set(id, newDoc);
    return newDoc;
  }

  delete(id) {
    const doc = this.docs.get(id);
    if (!doc) return false;
    for (const idx of this.indexes.values()) idx.remove(doc);
    this.docs.delete(id);
    return true;
  }

  _chooseIndex(filter) {
    let best = null, bestScore = 0;
    for (const idx of this.indexes.values()) {
      const s = idx.canServe(filter);
      if (s > bestScore) { bestScore = s; best = idx; }
    }
    return { best, bestScore };
  }

  find(filter = {}) {
    const { best, bestScore } = this._chooseIndex(filter);
    let ids;
    if (best && bestScore > 0) {
      const r = best.search(filter);
      ids = r === null ? [...this.docs.keys()] : [...r];
    } else {
      ids = [...this.docs.keys()];
    }
    const res = [];
    for (const id of ids) {
      const doc = this.docs.get(id);
      if (doc && matches(doc, filter)) res.push(doc);
    }
    return res;
  }

  explain(filter = {}) {
    const { best, bestScore } = this._chooseIndex(filter);
    let ids;
    if (best && bestScore > 0) {
      const r = best.search(filter);
      ids = r === null ? [...this.docs.keys()] : [...r];
    } else {
      ids = [...this.docs.keys()];
    }
    let postFiltered = 0;
    for (const id of ids) {
      const doc = this.docs.get(id);
      if (doc && matches(doc, filter)) postFiltered++;
    }
    return {
      collection: this.name,
      filter,
      index: best ? best.name : 'COLLSCAN',
      indexType: best ? best.type : null,
      score: bestScore,
      candidatesFromIndex: ids.length,
      finalResults: postFiltered,
      totalDocs: this.docs.size,
    };
  }

  count(filter = {}) { return this.find(filter).length; }
}

// ----------------------------- Database -----------------------------
class Database {
  constructor() { this.collections = new Map(); }
  collection(name) {
    if (!this.collections.has(name)) this.collections.set(name, new Collection(name));
    return this.collections.get(name);
  }
}

// ----------------------------- Demo -----------------------------
function main() {
  const db = new Database();
  const users = db.collection('users');

  const cities = ['NYC', 'LA', 'SF', 'CHI', 'BOS', 'SEA'];
  const N = 5000;
  console.log(`Inserting ${N} documents...`);
  const t0 = Date.now();
  const docs = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    docs.push({
      name: `user_${i}`,
      email: `user${i}@example.com`,
      age: 18 + (i % 48),
      city: cities[i % cities.length],
      score: Math.round(Math.random() * 1000),
    });
  }
  users.insertMany(docs);
  console.log(`Inserted in ${Date.now() - t0} ms\n`);

  // indexes
  users.createIndex(['age'], { type: 'btree' });
  users.createIndex(['city', 'age'], { type: 'btree' });
  users.createIndex(['email'], { unique: true, type: 'hash' });
  console.log('Indexes:', [...users.indexes.keys()].join(', '), '\n');

  // 1. equality on age
  let t = Date.now();
  let res = users.find({ age: 30 });
  console.log(`find({age:30}) -> ${res.length} docs, ${Date.now() - t} ms`);
  console.log('  explain:', JSON.stringify(users.explain({ age: 30 })));

  // 2. range on age
  t = Date.now();
  res = users.find({ age: { $gte: 25, $lte: 35 } });
  console.log(`\nfind({age:{$gte:25,$lte:35}}) -> ${res.length} docs, ${Date.now() - t} ms`);
  console.log('  explain:', JSON.stringify(users.explain({ age: { $gte: 25, $lte: 35 } })));

  // 3. composite: city equality + age range
  t = Date.now();
  res = users.find({ city: 'NYC', age: { $gte: 30 } });
  console.log(`\nfind({city:'NYC', age:{$gte:30}}) -> ${res.length} docs, ${Date.now() - t} ms`);
  console.log('  explain:', JSON.stringify(users.explain({ city: 'NYC', age: { $gte: 30 } })));

  // 4. hash index on _id / email
  const sampleId = users.find({ city: 'LA' })[0]._id;
  t = Date.now();
  res = users.find({ _id: sampleId });
  console.log(`\nfind({_id:'${sampleId}'}) -> ${res.length} docs, ${Date.now() - t} ms`);
  console.log('  explain:', JSON.stringify(users.explain({ _id: sampleId })));

  t = Date.now();
  res = users.find({ email: 'user42@example.com' });
  console.log(`\nfind({email:'user42@example.com'}) -> ${res.length} docs, ${Date.now() - t} ms`);
  console.log('  explain:', JSON.stringify(users.explain({ email: 'user42@example.com' })));

  // 5. unique constraint enforcement
  console.log('\n--- unique constraint test ---');
  try {
    users.insert({ name: 'dup', email: 'user42@example.com', age: 20, city: 'NYC', score: 1 });
    console.log('ERROR: duplicate insert did not throw');
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('Threw as expected:', e.message);
  }

  // 6. index maintenance on update + delete
  console.log('\n--- maintenance test ---');
  const target = users.find({ email: 'user7@example.com' })[0];
  console.log('before update: city =', target.city, 'age =', target.age);
  users.update(target._id, { city: 'MARS', age: 99 });
  const after = users.find({ _id: target._id })[0];
  console.log('after update:  city =', after.city, 'age =', after.age);
  console.log('old (city NYC-ish) query no longer returns it:',
    users.find({ city: target.city === 'MARS' ? 'NYC' : target.city, age: 99 }).length === 0 ? 'OK' : 'FAIL');
  console.log('new composite query returns it:',
    users.find({ city: 'MARS', age: 99 }).length === 1 ? 'OK' : 'FAIL');

  users.delete(target._id);
  console.log('after delete, find by _id returns:', users.find({ _id: target._id }).length, '(expect 0)');
  console.log('after delete, email index returns:', users.find({ email: 'user7@example.com' }).length, '(expect 0)');

  // 7. full collection scan (no usable index)
  t = Date.now();
  res = users.find({ score: { $gt: 990 } });
  console.log(`\nfind({score:{$gt:990}}) -> ${res.length} docs (collscan), ${Date.now() - t} ms`);
  console.log('  explain:', JSON.stringify(users.explain({ score: { $gt: 990 } })));
}

main();
```

### What this implements

- **Two index kinds**
  - `HashIndex`: `Map<serializedKey, Set<docId>>` for O(1) equality lookups (including composite keys and unique constraints).
  - `OrderedIndex`: a sorted array of `{key, ids}` entries with binary search — a simplified ordered (B+ tree-like) index supporting **prefix equality** and **range** scans (`$gt/$gte/$lt/$lte`). Production engines use a balanced B+ tree; the algorithm (sorted keys + binary search bounds) is the same idea.
- **Composite indexes & prefix matching**: an index on `[city, age]` serves queries on `city` alone or `city + age range`, but not `age` alone (the planner falls back to the `age` index or a scan).
- **Query planner**: `Collection._chooseIndex` scores each index by number of equality-prefix fields matched plus a range bonus (and a small unique bonus), then `find` retrieves candidate ids from the winning index and **post-filters** with `matches` (so exclusive bounds, `$ne`, `$in`, `$or` are still correct even when the index returns a slightly wider candidate set).
- **Maintenance**: `insert`/`update`/`delete` keep every index in sync; `insert`/`update` roll back index changes if a unique constraint throws.
- **`explain()`**: shows chosen index, type, score, candidate count, final count, and total docs — useful for verifying index usage.
- **Sentinel bounds** (`MIN_SENTINEL`/`MAX_SENTINEL`) let trailing (unconstrained) composite fields match any value during range scans without type-specific “next value” logic.

Run it to see index selection, timing, unique-constraint enforcement, and maintenance all working end to end.

## Idea

A **database index** is an auxiliary data structure (usually a **B+ tree**, sometimes a hash table) that maps column values to the physical location of rows.  
Instead of scanning every row (**full table scan**), the engine traverses the index to jump straight to matching rows.

Trade-offs:
- **Faster reads**: `SELECT … WHERE`, `JOIN`, `ORDER BY`, range queries.
- **Slower writes**: inserts/updates/deletes must maintain every index.
- **Extra storage**: indexes can be large.
- **Selectivity matters**: indexes help most on high-cardinality columns (emails, IDs), not on low-cardinality ones (booleans, status enums with few values).

Common index types:
- **Single-column**
- **Composite / multi-column** (order matters: leftmost prefix rule)
- **Unique** (also enforces constraints)
- **Partial** (only rows matching a condition)
- **Covering** (index contains all columns needed, no table lookup)
- **Full-text / specialized** (text search, geospatial)

