## Key interview talking points
1. **Prove correctness**: show greedy choice property + optimal substructure (e.g., exchange argument for activity selection).
2. **Complexity**: usually O(n log n) due to sort + O(n) scan.
3. **Pitfalls**: don’t apply greedy to 0/1 knapsack or arbitrary coin systems — counterexamples exist.
4. **State**: greedy is stateless decisions; if you need to “undo,” it’s not greedy.

If you’d like, I can also implement **Huffman coding** or **Interval Partitioning** (min rooms) in the same style.

Greedy algorithms in JavaScript are usually “sort, then scan.” Most failures come from that sort step, from floating-point data, or from assuming a greedy choice is globally optimal. Below are the common edge cases, with idiomatic fixes.

## 1. Empty, null, or malformed input

Never assume the input exists or has elements.

```js
function selectActivities(activities) {
  if (!Array.isArray(activities)) {
    throw new TypeError("activities must be an array");
  }
  if (activities.length === 0) return [];

  // validate each item before sorting
  const valid = activities.filter(a =>
    a &&
    Number.isFinite(a.start) &&
    Number.isFinite(a.end) &&
    a.start <= a.end
  );

  if (valid.length === 0) return [];
  // ...
}
```

Filter invalid records before sorting. Otherwise `NaN` comparators can make `sort()` behave unpredictably.

## 2. The default array sort is lexicographic

For numbers, `[10, 2, 1].sort()` is wrong.

```js
const nums = [10, 2, 1, 20];

// wrong
console.log([...nums].sort()); // [1, 10, 2, 20]

// right
console.log([...nums].sort((a, b) => a - b)); // [1, 2, 10, 20]
```

For strings, prefer `localeCompare` when human ordering matters:

```js
names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, "en", { numeric: true }));
```

## 3. Mutating the caller’s array

`sort()` mutates. Clone first.

```js
function greedySchedule(jobs) {
  // bad: jobs.sort(...);
  const sorted = [...jobs].sort((a, b) => a.deadline - b.deadline);
  return sorted;
}
```

For nested objects, a shallow clone is usually enough for sorting, but if you modify objects, deep-clone or create new result objects.

## 4. Comparator must return a number, never a boolean

This is a classic JavaScript bug.

```js
const items = [{ w: 2 }, { w: 10 }, { w: 5 }];

// bad: boolean comparator
items.sort((a, b) => a.w < b.w); // unreliable

// good
items.sort((a, b) => a.w - b.w);
```

Also avoid `a - b` when values can be `undefined`, `null`, `Infinity`, or very large, because it can produce `NaN` or `Infinity`. Filter first or return explicit comparison values.

## 5. Ties and deterministic tie-breaking

When two greedy candidates have the same key, the secondary key matters.

```js
const activities = [
  { start: 1, end: 3 },
  { start: 2, end: 3 },
  { start: 3, end: 5 },
];

const sorted = [...activities].sort((a, b) => {
  const endDiff = a.end - b.end;
  if (endDiff !== 0) return endDiff;
  // tie-break by start ascending
  return a.start - b.start;
});
```

Without the tie-breaker, equal-end activities may remain in input order, making results nondeterministic across environments before ES2019 and harder to test.

## 6. Floating-point comparisons

Direct equality on floats is fragile. Use an epsilon or, better, scale to integers.

### Epsilon comparator

```js
function cmpFloat(a, b, eps = 1e-9) {
  const diff = a - b;
  if (Math.abs(diff) < eps) return 0;
  return diff < 0 ? -1 : 1;
}
```

Use it carefully: epsilon comparisons can violate strict transitivity in extreme cases, so prefer integer scaling for money and weights.

### Money should be integer cents

```js
function dollarsToCents(dollars) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(dollars)) throw new TypeError("invalid amount");
  return Math.round(dollars * 100);
}

const price = dollarsToCents(19.99); // 1999
```

## 7. Ratios and cross-multiplication

Fractional knapsack often sorts by value/weight. Dividing can lose precision and divide by zero.

```js
function fractionalKnapsack(items, capacity) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(capacity) || capacity < 0) {
    throw new RangeError("capacity must be a non-negative finite number");
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(items) || items.length === 0 || capacity === 0) {
    return { value: 0, chosen: [] };
  }

  const normalized = [];
  let bonusValue = 0;

  for (const item of items) {
    if (!Number.isFinite(item.weight) || !Number.isFinite(item.value) ||
        item.weight < 0 || item.value < 0) {
      throw new RangeError("item weight/value must be non-negative finite numbers");
    }

    if (item.weight === 0) {
      if (item.value > 0) {
        throw new Error("positive value with zero weight makes optimum unbounded");
      }
      continue; // zero-value, zero-weight item is irrelevant
    }

    normalized.push(item);
  }

  // Sort by value/weight descending using cross multiplication.
  // Avoids division and most precision issues for moderate-sized inputs.
  const sorted = [...normalized].sort((a, b) => {
    const left = a.value * b.weight;
    const right = b.value * a.weight;
    if (left !== right) return right - left; // descending
    return a.weight - b.weight; // deterministic tie-break
  });

  let remaining = capacity;
  let totalValue = 0;
  const chosen = [];

  for (const item of sorted) {
    if (remaining <= 0) break;
    const take = Math.min(item.weight, remaining);
    totalValue += item.value * (take / item.weight);
    chosen.push({ ...item, taken: take });
    remaining -= take;
  }

  return { value: totalValue, chosen };
}
```

For very large integer products, cross-multiplication can overflow JavaScript’s safe integer range; use `BigInt` comparison instead.

## 8. Zero capacity, zero-weight items, and empty results

Greedy code often divides by capacity or weight. Handle these explicitly:

```js
if (capacity === 0) return { value: 0, chosen: [] };
if (item.weight === 0) { /* handle separately, do not divide */ }
```

Also, if no combination is possible, return a clear sentinel:

```js
function makeChangeGreedy(coins, amount) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(amount) || amount < 0) {
    throw new RangeError("amount must be a non-negative integer");
  }

  const sorted = [...new Set(coins)]
    .filter(c => Number.isInteger(c) && c > 0)
    .sort((a, b) => b - a);

  const used = [];
  let remaining = amount;

  for (const coin of sorted) {
    while (remaining >= coin) {
      remaining -= coin;
      used.push(coin);
    }
  }

  return remaining === 0 ? used : null; // null means exact change impossible
}
```

## 9. Negative values

Many greedy proofs assume non-negative weights, values, or durations. Validate them.

```js
if (item.weight < 0 || item.value < 0) {
  throw new RangeError("weights and values must be non-negative");
}
```

If negative edges are genuinely part of the problem, the greedy strategy may no longer be valid; you likely need dynamic programming.

## 10. Strict vs non-strict boundaries

In interval scheduling, decide whether touching intervals are compatible.

```js
const compatible = allowTouching
  ? next.start >= last.end
  : next.start > last.end;
```

This changes which sets are feasible. Document the convention and test boundary cases such as `[1, 2]` and `[2, 3]`.

## 11. Greedy is not always globally optimal

A common JavaScript example is coin change with non-canonical denominations.

```js
// Greedy gives 4 + 1 + 1 = 3 coins for amount 6
console.log(makeChangeGreedy([4, 3, 1], 6)); // [4, 1, 1]

// Optimal is 3 + 3 = 2 coins
```

If the coin system is not canonical, greedy can fail. Either restrict inputs to canonical systems or use DP:

```js
function minCoinsDP(coins, amount) {
  const INF = amount + 1;
  const dp = Array(amount + 1).fill(INF);
  dp[0] = 0;

  for (let x = 1; x <= amount; x++) {
    for (const coin of coins) {
      if (coin <= x) {
        dp[x] = Math.min(dp[x], dp[x - coin] + 1);
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[amount] > amount ? null : dp[amount];
}
```

## 12. Large numbers and safe integers

JavaScript numbers are IEEE-754 doubles. Sums can exceed `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`.

```js
console.log(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER + 1 === Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER + 2); // true

// Use BigInt for exact large totals
const total = [10n, 20n, 30n].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0n);
console.log(total); // 60n

// Sort BigInts explicitly
const bigs = [5n, 2n, 9n];
bigs.sort((a, b) => (a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0));
```

BigInt cannot be mixed with Number in arithmetic, so convert deliberately and validate ranges.

## 13. Sorting objects by computed keys

Compute the key once if it is expensive, rather than repeatedly inside the comparator.

```js
const enriched = items.map(item => ({
  item,
  key: expensiveKey(item),
}));

enriched.sort((a, b) => a.key - b.key);
const sortedItems = enriched.map(x => x.item);
```

This avoids O(n log n) repeated calculations and makes tie-breaking easier.

## 14. Performance and data structures

Greedy algorithms are often O(n log n) because of one sort. Avoid repeated linear scans for the minimum.

```js
// Avoid this O(n^2) pattern
while (remaining.length) {
  const min = Math.min(...remaining); // O(n) each iteration
  // ...
}

// Prefer sorting once
remaining.sort((a, b) => a - b);
```

If you need repeated insertion and extraction, implement or import a binary heap/priority queue; JavaScript has no built-in heap.

## 15. Zero-duration and equal intervals

Activities with `start === end` can be mishandled by strict comparisons.

```js
const zeroDuration = { start: 5, end: 5 };

// If allowTouching is true, it can be scheduled with another [5, ...]
// If using strict >, it may be rejected incorrectly depending on convention.
```

Decide and document whether zero-length tasks are allowed and how they interact with boundaries.

## 16. Input should be treated as immutable domain data

If the caller expects the original order to remain intact, clone and validate:

```js
function solve(input) {
  const data = Array.isArray(input) ? input.map(x => ({ ...x })) : [];
  // validate, sort, and solve using data only
  return result;
}
```

## Summary checklist

Before shipping a greedy solution in JavaScript, verify:

- Input exists and is the expected type.
- Invalid or `NaN` records are filtered before sorting.
- Arrays are cloned before `sort()`.
- Comparators return numbers, not booleans.
- Ties have a deterministic secondary comparator.
- Floats are either compared with an epsilon or scaled to integers.
- Money uses integer cents, not decimal dollars.
- Zero weights, zero capacity, empty inputs, and no-solution cases are handled.
- Negative values are either rejected or the problem is reformulated.
- Boundary strictness is explicit and tested.
- Greedy optimality is actually proven for the chosen input domain.
- Large sums use `BigInt` or are checked against safe integer limits.
- The original input is not mutated.

Here is a complete, runnable JavaScript implementation of a hash table using **separate chaining**. It supports primitive and object keys, resizes automatically based on load factor, and provides common `Map`-like operations.

```js
// hash-table.js
class HashTable {
  constructor(initialCapacity = 16, loadFactorThreshold = 0.75) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(initialCapacity) || initialCapacity <= 0) {
      throw new RangeError("initialCapacity must be a positive integer");
    }
    if (typeof loadFactorThreshold !== "number" || loadFactorThreshold <= 0 || loadFactorThreshold > 1) {
      throw new RangeError("loadFactorThreshold must be between 0 and 1");
    }

    this._capacity = initialCapacity;
    this._loadFactorThreshold = loadFactorThreshold;
    this._buckets = Array.from({ length: this._capacity }, () => []);
    this._size = 0;

    // Gives stable identities to object/function keys without preventing GC.
    this._objectIds = new WeakMap();
    this._nextObjectId = 0;
  }

  get size() {
    return this._size;
  }

  get capacity() {
    return this._capacity;
  }

  // -----------------------------
  // Core operations
  // -----------------------------

  set(key, value) {
    // Resize before inserting if the next insertion would exceed the load factor.
    if ((this._size + 1) / this._capacity > this._loadFactorThreshold) {
      this._resize(this._capacity * 2);
    }

    const index = this._hash(key);
    const bucket = this._buckets[index];
    const existingIndex = this._findInBucket(bucket, key);

    if (existingIndex >= 0) {
      bucket[existingIndex][1] = value;
      return this;
    }

    bucket.push([key, value]);
    this._size++;
    return this;
  }

  get(key) {
    const bucket = this._buckets[this._hash(key)];
    const entry = this._findEntry(bucket, key);
    return entry ? entry[1] : undefined;
  }

  has(key) {
    const bucket = this._buckets[this._hash(key)];
    return this._findEntry(bucket, key) !== null;
  }

  delete(key) {
    const index = this._hash(key);
    const bucket = this._buckets[index];
    const entryIndex = this._findIndexInBucket(bucket, key);

    if (entryIndex < 0) return false;

    bucket.splice(entryIndex, 1);
    this._size--;
    return true;
  }

  clear() {
    this._buckets = Array.from({ length: this._capacity }, () => []);
    this._size = 0;
  }

  // -----------------------------
  // Iteration
  // -----------------------------

  *entries() {
    for (const bucket of this._buckets) {
      for (const entry of bucket) {
        yield entry;
      }
    }
  }

  *keys() {
    for (const [key] of this.entries()) yield key;
  }

  *values() {
    for (const [, value] of this.entries()) yield value;
  }

  forEach(callback, thisArg) {
    for (const [key, value] of this.entries()) {
      callback.call(thisArg, value, key, this);
    }
  }

  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    return this.entries();
  }

  // -----------------------------
  // Internal helpers
  // -----------------------------

  _hash(key) {
    const text = this._toHashText(key);

    // FNV-1a 32-bit hash
    let hash = 0x811c9dc5;
    for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
      hash ^= text.charCodeAt(i);
      hash = Math.imul(hash, 0x01000193);
    }
    return (hash >>> 0) % this._capacity;
  }

  _toHashText(key) {
    if (key === null) return "null:null";
    if (key === undefined) return "undefined:undefined";

    const type = typeof key;

    // Object/function keys are hashed by a stable internal id.
    if (type === "object" || type === "function") {
      if (!this._objectIds.has(key)) {
        this._objectIds.set(key, this._nextObjectId++);
      }
      return `object:${this._objectIds.get(key)}`;
    }

    // Symbols are not safely coercible with String() in all contexts,
    // but toString() gives a useful, mostly-unique representation.
    if (type === "symbol") {
      return `symbol:${key.toString()}`;
    }

    // Tag primitives by type so, for example, 1 and "1" do not collide
    // in a way that makes equality checks ambiguous.
    return `${type}:${String(key)}`;
  }

  _findEntry(bucket, key) {
    for (const entry of bucket) {
      if (Object.is(entry[0], key)) return entry;
    }
    return null;
  }

  _findIndexInBucket(bucket, key) {
    return bucket.findIndex(([existingKey]) => Object.is(existingKey, key));
  }

  _findInBucket(bucket, key) {
    return this._findIndexInBucket(bucket, key);
  }

  _resize(newCapacity) {
    const oldEntries = [...this.entries()];

    this._capacity = newCapacity;
    this._buckets = Array.from({ length: newCapacity }, () => []);
    this._size = 0;

    for (const [key, value] of oldEntries) {
      this.set(key, value);
    }
  }
}

// -----------------------------
// Runnable demo / smoke test
// -----------------------------

const table = new HashTable();

table.set("name", "Ada Lovelace");
table.set("language", "JavaScript");
table.set(42, "the answer");
table.set(true, "boolean key");

// Object keys are compared by identity.
const userA = { id: 1 };
const userB = { id: 1 };
table.set(userA, "first user");
table.set(userB, "second user");

console.log("size:", table.size);
console.log("get('name'):", table.get("name"));
console.log("get(42):", table.get(42));
console.log("has(true):", table.has(true));
console.log("get(userA):", table.get(userA));
console.log("get(userB):", table.get(userB));
console.log("get({id:1}):", table.get({ id: 1 })); // undefined: different object identity

console.log("\nAll entries:");
for (const [key, value] of table) {
  console.log(JSON.stringify(key), "=>", value);
}

// Update existing key
table.set("name", "Grace Hopper");
console.log("\nAfter update, get('name'):", table.get("name"));

// Delete
console.log("delete('language'):", table.delete("language"));
console.log("has('language'):", table.has("language"));
console.log("size after delete:", table.size);

// NaN handling: all NaN keys are equal under Object.is-ish semantics
table.set(NaN, "not a number");
console.log("get(NaN):", table.get(NaN));

// Resize behavior can be observed by inserting many keys.
const big = new HashTable(4, 0.75);
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) big.set(`key-${i}`, i);
console.log("\nbig size:", big.size, "capacity:", big.capacity);
console.log("big get('key-99'):", big.get("key-99"));

// Clear
big.clear();
console.log("big size after clear:", big.size);
```

