## Exercise

Build a small **notification system** in JavaScript:

1. Create an **abstract base class** `Notifier` that:
   - cannot be instantiated directly,
   - has a concrete method `format(message)` that timestamps and validates a message,
   - declares an abstract method `send(message)`.

2. Create two concrete subclasses:
   - `EmailNotifier`
   - `SMSNotifier`

3. Create a `NotificationService` that accepts any object satisfying the **Notifier contract** (duck typing). It should fail clearly if the object does not implement `send` and `format`.

4. Add a small runtime “interface check” so callers get a helpful error instead of a cryptic `undefined is not a function`.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Simulate an abstract class in JavaScript

JavaScript has no `abstract` keyword at runtime, so we simulate it:

- Throw in the constructor when `new.target` is the base class itself.
- Throw in `send()` so subclasses must override it.

```js
class Notifier {
  constructor() {
    if (new.target === Notifier) {
      throw new Error("Notifier is abstract and cannot be instantiated directly.");
    }
  }

  format(message) {
    if (typeof message !== "string" || message.trim() === "") {
      throw new TypeError("message must be a non-empty string");
    }
    return `[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${message.trim()}`;
  }

  send(message) {
    throw new Error("send(message) must be implemented by a subclass.");
  }
}
```

### Step 2: Implement concrete notifiers

Each subclass calls `super()` and implements `send()`.

```js
class EmailNotifier extends Notifier {
  constructor(address) {
    super();
    if (typeof address !== "string" || !address.includes("@")) {
      throw new TypeError("address must be a valid email string");
    }
    this.address = address;
  }

  send(message) {
    const payload = this.format(message);
    // In a real app, call an email API here.
    console.log(`EMAIL -> ${this.address}: ${payload}`);
    return { channel: "email", to: this.address, payload };
  }
}

class SMSNotifier extends Notifier {
  constructor(phoneNumber) {
    super();
    if (typeof phoneNumber !== "string" || !/^\+?\d{7,15}$/.test(phoneNumber)) {
      throw new TypeError("phoneNumber must be a valid phone number");
    }
    this.phoneNumber = phoneNumber;
  }

  send(message) {
    const payload = this.format(message);
    // In a real app, call an SMS gateway here.
    console.log(`SMS -> ${this.phoneNumber}: ${payload}`);
    return { channel: "sms", to: this.phoneNumber, payload };
  }
}
```

### Step 3: Define a runtime “interface” check

Because JavaScript is duck-typed, an interface is really a contract: “has the required methods.”

```js
function isNotifier(value) {
  return (
    value !== null &&
    typeof value === "object" &&
    typeof value.send === "function" &&
    typeof value.format === "function"
  );
}
```

This is the JavaScript equivalent of saying: “this object implements the `Notifier` interface.”

### Step 4: Use the contract in a service

```js
class NotificationService {
  constructor(notifier) {
    if (!isNotifier(notifier)) {
      throw new TypeError(
        "NotificationService requires an object implementing send(message) and format(message)."
      );
    }
    this.notifier = notifier;
  }

  notify(message) {
    return this.notifier.send(message);
  }
}
```

### Step 5: Test the system

```js
const email = new EmailNotifier("alice@example.com");
const sms = new SMSNotifier("+15551234567");

const emailService = new NotificationService(email);
const smsService = new NotificationService(sms);

console.log(emailService.notify("Your order has shipped."));
console.log(smsService.notify("Your code is 4829."));

// Abstract class cannot be instantiated:
try {
  new Notifier();
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Expected error:", err.message);
}

// Bad object fails interface check:
try {
  new NotificationService({ send() {} }); // missing format()
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Expected error:", err.message);
}
```

---

