### Why this matters

- **Abstract class**: `Notifier` defines shared behavior and forces subclasses to implement `send()`.
- **Interface-like contract**: `isNotifier()` checks the required shape instead of relying on a class name.
- **Duck typing**: `NotificationService` works with any object that has `send` and `format`, not just instances of `Notifier`.
- **TypeScript note**: In real projects, you would usually write `interface Notifier { send(message: string): ...; format(message: string): string; }` and `abstract class BaseNotifier`, letting the compiler enforce the contract at build time.

## Interfaces and Abstract Classes in JavaScript

JavaScript doesn’t have first-class `interface` or `abstract` keywords (TypeScript does). In plain JS we simulate them with runtime patterns:

- **Abstract class**: a base class that cannot be instantiated directly and declares methods subclasses must override. We enforce this by checking `new.target` in the constructor and throwing in abstract methods.
- **Interface**: a contract (a set of required method names). Since JS is dynamically typed, we enforce it at runtime by checking that an object actually has those methods. This is essentially “duck typing with a guard.”

### Key ideas
- **Duck typing**: JS cares about behavior, not declared types. If it walks like a duck…
- **Runtime vs compile-time**: Real interfaces (TS) are erased at compile time and cost nothing at runtime. Our JS version adds runtime checks—useful for libraries/public APIs, but heavier.
- **Abstract methods**: throw if called on the base; subclasses override them.
- **Composition over inheritance**: interfaces encourage composing capabilities (`Flyable`, `Swimmable`) rather than deep class hierarchies.

---

## Implementation

```js
// ============================================================
// 1) ABSTRACT CLASS
// ============================================================
// Base class that cannot be instantiated directly.
// Subclasses must override every method that throws "abstract".
class AbstractClass {
  constructor() {
    // `new.target` is the constructor actually invoked by `new`.
    // If someone does `new AbstractClass()`, block it.
    if (new.target === AbstractClass) {
      throw new TypeError("AbstractClass cannot be instantiated directly");
    }
  }

  // Abstract method: concrete subclasses MUST override this.
  doWork() {
    throw new Error("Abstract method 'doWork' must be implemented by subclass");
  }

  // Concrete (shared) method available to all subclasses.
  identify() {
    return `Instance of ${this.constructor.name}`;
  }
}

// ============================================================
// 2) INTERFACE (runtime contract)
// ============================================================
// An interface is just a frozen descriptor: a name + required method names.
function createInterface(name, methods) {
  if (typeof name !== "string" || !name.trim()) {
    throw new TypeError("Interface name must be a non-empty string");
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(methods) || methods.length === 0) {
    throw new TypeError("Interface must declare at least one method");
  }
  // Freeze to keep the contract immutable.
  return Object.freeze({ name, methods: [...methods] });
}

// Runtime guard: verify that `obj` satisfies every given interface.
function implementsInterfaces(obj, ...interfaces) {
  for (const iface of interfaces) {
    for (const method of iface.methods) {
      if (typeof obj[method] !== "function") {
        throw new TypeError(
          `Object does not implement interface '${iface.name}': missing method '${method}'`
        );
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}

// ============================================================
// 3) EXAMPLE USAGE
// ============================================================

// Capability interfaces (composition-friendly)
const Flyable = createInterface("Flyable", ["fly", "land"]);
const Swimmable = createInterface("Swimmable", ["swim"]);

// Abstract base for all animals
class Animal extends AbstractClass {
  constructor(name) {
    super(); // sets up abstract-class protection
    this.name = name;
  }

  breathe() {
    return `${this.name} breathes`;
  }
}

// Bird: Animal + Flyable
class Bird extends Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    super(name);
    // Enforce interface contract at construction time.
    implementsInterfaces(this, Flyable);
  }

  // Implements abstract method
  doWork() {
    return `${this.name} is foraging`;
  }

  // Implements Flyable
  fly() {
    return `${this.name} flies`;
  }
  land() {
    return `${this.name} lands`;
  }
}

// Fish: Animal + Swimmable
class Fish extends Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    super(name);
    implementsInterfaces(this, Swimmable);
  }

  doWork() {
    return `${this.name} is migrating`;
  }

  swim() {
    return `${this.name} swims`;
  }
}

// ============================================================
// 4) DEMO / SELF-TESTS
// ============================================================
const eagle = new Bird("Eagle");
console.log(eagle.identify());   // Instance of Bird
console.log(eagle.breathe());    // Eagle breathes
console.log(eagle.doWork());     // Eagle is foraging
console.log(eagle.fly());        // Eagle flies
console.log(eagle.land());       // Eagle lands

const salmon = new Fish("Salmon");
console.log(salmon.doWork());    // Salmon is migrating
console.log(salmon.swim());      // Salmon swims

// Abstract class cannot be instantiated directly
try {
  new AbstractClass();
} catch (e) {
  console.log("OK (abstract):", e.message);
}

// Interface violation is caught early
class BadBird extends Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    super(name);
    implementsInterfaces(this, Flyable); // will throw: missing fly/land
  }
  doWork() {
    return "x";
  }
}
try {
  new BadBird("Broken");
} catch (e) {
  console.log("OK (interface):", e.message);
}
```

### Interview takeaways
- In JS, **interfaces are contracts, not types**—enforce them with runtime checks or TypeScript.
- **Abstract classes** give you shared implementation + forced overrides; **interfaces** give you capability contracts without inheritance.
- Prefer **composition of interfaces** (`Flyable`, `Swimmable`) over deep inheritance trees.
- If you need zero runtime cost, move these checks to **TypeScript** and keep JS as the emitted output.

JavaScript does not have native `interface` or `abstract` keywords. In practice:

- **Abstract classes** are emulated with runtime guards.
- **Interfaces** are usually TypeScript compile-time constructs or runtime “duck typing” checks.

Below are common edge cases and how to handle them.

---

## 1. Preventing direct instantiation of an abstract base

Use `new.target`.

```js
class AbstractRepository {
  constructor() {
    if (new.target === AbstractRepository) {
      throw new TypeError("AbstractRepository cannot be instantiated directly");
    }
  }

  findById(id) {
    throw new Error("findById() must be implemented by a subclass");
  }
}

class UserRepository extends AbstractRepository {
  findById(id) {
    return { id, type: "user" };
  }
}

new UserRepository();        // OK
// new AbstractRepository(); // throws
```

**Edge case:** `Object.create(AbstractRepository.prototype)` bypasses the constructor, so the guard never runs. Mitigate by validating state lazily inside methods.

---

## 2. Detecting unimplemented abstract methods

JS will not fail until the method is called. Validate in the base constructor.

```js
class AbstractView {
  constructor() {
    if (new.target === AbstractView) throw new TypeError("abstract");

    const required = ["render", "mount"];
    for (const name of required) {
      const impl = this[name];
      const baseImpl = AbstractView.prototype[name];

      if (typeof impl !== "function" || impl === baseImpl) {
        throw new TypeError(`${new.target.name} must implement ${name}()`);
      }
    }
  }

  render() { throw new Error("abstract render"); }
  mount() { throw new Error("abstract mount"); }
}

class PageView extends AbstractView {
  render() { return "<page />"; }
  mount() { /* ... */ }
}
```

**Edge cases:**
- A subclass may inherit the base throwing method without overriding it; the identity check catches this.
- Do not validate subclass-specific fields here because field initialization happens after `super()`.

---

## 3. Static “abstract” methods

Static methods are inherited, but there is no enforcement.

```js
class Factory {
  static create() {
    throw new Error("create() must be implemented by subclass");
  }
}

class UserFactory extends Factory {
  static create(data) {
    return { ...data, kind: "user" };
  }
}

// Enforcement check:
function ensureStaticImplemented(Subclass, Base, methodName) {
  if (Subclass[methodName] === Base[methodName]) {
    throw new TypeError(`${Subclass.name} must implement static ${methodName}()`);
  }
}

ensureStaticImplemented(UserFactory, Factory, "create");
```

---

## 4. Private fields (`#`) are not inheritable

Subclasses cannot access a base class’s `#private` fields. If an abstract method depends on private state, subclasses cannot reuse it directly.

```js
class AbstractCounter {
  #count = 0;

  increment() {
    this.#count += 1;
    return this.#count;
  }
}

class BrokenCounter extends AbstractCounter {
  // SyntaxError: Private field '#count' must be declared in an enclosing class
  reset() {
    // this.#count = 0;
  }
}
```

**Handling:** expose protected state through non-private methods or symbols.

```js
const COUNT = Symbol("count");

class SafeCounter {
  constructor() {
    this[COUNT] = 0;
  }
  increment() {
    return ++this[COUNT];
  }
  _getCount() {
    return this[COUNT];
  }
}
```

---

## 5. `this` binding for interface-style methods

Methods passed as callbacks lose their receiver.

```js
class Renderer {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  render() {
    return `rendered by ${this.name}`;
  }
}

const r = new Renderer("main");
const fn = r.render;
// fn(); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined

// Fix 1: bind in constructor
class BoundRenderer {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    this.render = this.render.bind(this);
  }
  render() {
    return `rendered by ${this.name}`;
  }
}

// Fix 2: arrow field
class ArrowRenderer {
  name = "arrow";
  render = () => `rendered by ${this.name}`;
}
```

---

## 6. Mixins and multiple interface-like behavior

JS has single inheritance. Mixins allow multiple behaviors but introduce collision and `instanceof` issues.

```js
const Serializable = (Base) => class extends Base {
  serialize() {
    return JSON.stringify(this);
  }
};

const Validatable = (Base) => class extends Base {
  validate() {
    return true;
  }
};

class User extends Serializable(Validatable(Object)) {
  constructor(name) {
    super();
    this.name = name;
  }
}

const u = new User("Ada");

console.log(u instanceof Serializable); // false in some mixin shapes
console.log(typeof u.serialize);       // "function"
```

**Edge cases:**
- Name collisions: later mixin overwrites earlier one.
- `instanceof` for mixin functions is not automatic.
- Methods added via `Object.assign(proto, methods)` are enumerable by default.

Fix enumerability:

```js
function applyMixin(targetProto, methods) {
  for (const [name, fn] of Object.entries(methods)) {
    Object.defineProperty(targetProto, name, {
      value: fn,
      writable: true,
      configurable: true,
      enumerable: false
    });
  }
}
```

---

## 7. Duck typing and structural interface checks

At runtime, “implements interface” usually means “has the right shape.” Validate defensively.

```js
function renderView(view) {
  if (view == null || typeof view.render !== "function") {
    throw new TypeError("Expected an object with render()");
  }
  // Optional methods:
  if (typeof view.mount === "function") view.mount();
  return view.render();
}

renderView({ render: () => "ok" });   // OK
// renderView(null);                  // throws
// renderView({});                    // throws
```

**Edge cases:**
- Primitive values such as strings have methods but are not objects.
- `undefined`/`null` must be checked first.
- Extra properties are allowed; missing required ones are not.

---

## 8. Nominal typing with brands

Structural typing can accept accidentally compatible objects. Use a unique symbol brand.

```js
const Brand = Symbol("Renderable");

class RenderableImpl {
  constructor() {
    this[Brand] = true;
  }
  render() {
    return "ok";
  }
}

function isRenderable(value) {
  return value != null && value[Brand] === true;
}

console.log(isRenderable(new RenderableImpl())); // true
console.log(isRenderable({ render() {} }));      // false
```

For cross-realm checks, use the global symbol registry:

```js
const GLOBAL_BRAND = Symbol.for("app.Renderable");

function brand(value) {
  value[GLOBAL_BRAND] = true;
  return value;
}
```

---

## 9. Cross-realm `instanceof` failures

Objects from different iframes/VMs have different prototypes.

```js
// In another realm, Array is a different constructor.
function safeIsArray(value) {
  return Array.isArray(value); // realm-safe
}

// For custom classes, prefer branding over instanceof.
```

---

## 10. Subclassing built-ins

Built-ins have special internal slots. Abstract guards still work, but species and constructor behavior matter.

```js
class AbstractList extends Array {
  constructor(...args) {
    if (new.target === AbstractList) {
      throw new TypeError("AbstractList is abstract");
    }
    super(...args);
  }

  abstractTransform() {
    throw new Error("must implement");
  }
}

class NumberList extends AbstractList {
  abstractTransform() {
    return this.map((x) => x * 2);
  }
}

const list = new NumberList(1, 2, 3);
console.log(list.abstractTransform()); // [2, 4, 6]
```

**Edge cases:**
- Methods like `map` use `this.constructor[Symbol.species]`.
- Some built-ins require `new.target` handling for correct subclass construction.

---

## 11. Abstract getters and setters

Accessors can be abstract too.

```js
class AbstractModel {
  get id() {
    throw new Error("id getter must be implemented");
  }
  set id(value) {
    throw new Error("id setter must be implemented");
  }
}

class UserModel extends AbstractModel {
  #id;
  get id() {
    return this.#id;
  }
  set id(value) {
    if (typeof value !== "string") throw new TypeError("id must be string");
    this.#id = value;
  }
}

const u = new UserModel();
u.id = "42";
console.log(u.id);
```

**Edge cases:**
- A read-only interface property should only define a getter.
- Overriding only a getter while inheriting the base setter creates inconsistent behavior.

---

## 12. TypeScript interface edge cases

If using TypeScript, interfaces are compile-time only.

```ts
interface User {
  id: string;
  name?: string;          // optional
  readonly createdAt: Date;
  [key: string]: unknown; // index signature
}

interface Admin extends User {
  role: "admin" | "superadmin";
}

// Function interface
interface Formatter {
  (value: number): string;
  locale: string;
}

// Declaration merging
interface Box { width: number; }
interface Box { height: number; }
// Box now has width and height
```

**Edge cases:**
- Excess property checks apply to object literals assigned directly, not to variables.
- `implements` checks assignability, not identical structure.
- Readonly class properties may still be assignable in constructors depending on TS settings.
- Optional properties make runtime validation necessary if data comes from JSON.

---

## 13. Runtime contract validation for interface-shaped data

Because interfaces disappear at runtime, validate external data explicitly.

```js
function parseUser(input) {
  if (!input || typeof input !== "object") throw new TypeError("User must be object");
  if (typeof input.id !== "string") throw new TypeError("User.id must be string");
  if (input.name != null && typeof input.name !== "string") {
    throw new TypeError("User.name must be string or null");
  }
  return Object.freeze({
    id: input.id,
    name: input.name ?? null
  });
}
```

---

## 14. `for...in` and enumeration pitfalls

Class methods are non-enumerable, but mixin-assigned methods may be enumerable.

```js
class A {
  method() {}
}

const a = new A();
for (const key in a) console.log("enumerable:", key); // nothing

const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(a);
Object.assign(proto, {
  extra() {}
});

for (const key in a) console.log("enumerable:", key); // "extra"
```

Fix with `Object.defineProperty`, as shown above.

---

## 15. Bypassing abstract guards with `Object.create`

```js
class Base {
  constructor() {
    if (new.target === Base) throw new TypeError("abstract");
    this.value = 0;
  }
  getValue() {
    return this.value;
  }
}

const ghost = Object.create(Base.prototype);
// ghost.getValue(); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
```

**Handling:** initialize defaults inside methods or validate:

```js
getValue() {
  if (typeof this.value !== "number") this.value = 0;
  return this.value;
}
```

---

## Summary checklist

When emulating interfaces/abstract classes in JavaScript:

- Guard `new.target` in constructors.
- Validate required methods exist and are overridden.
- Check static methods separately.
- Avoid relying on `#private` across inheritance boundaries.
- Bind `this` for callback usage.
- Protect against `Object.create` bypass.
- Use brands for nominal identity across realms.
- Validate runtime data separately from TypeScript interfaces.
- Control property enumerability in mixins.
- Test getter/setter and built-in subclass edge cases.

# Iterators in JavaScript — A Complete Guide

JavaScript iterators are objects that implement the **iterator protocol**: a `next()` method returning `{ value, done }`. Anything with a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method is **iterable** and can be consumed by `for...of`, spread, destructuring, etc.

Below is one self-contained, runnable Node.js script that demonstrates every major facet. Save it as `iterators.js` and run with `node iterators.js`.

```js
// iterators.js
'use strict';

// ============================================================
// 1. THE LOW-LEVEL ITERATOR PROTOCOL
// ============================================================
// An iterator is just an object with a next() method.
function makeRangeIterator(start, end, step = 1) {
  let current = start;
  return {
    next() {
      if (current <= end) {
        const value = current;
        current += step;
        return { value, done: false };
      }
      return { value: undefined, done: true };
    },
    // Optional but recommended: lets consumers stop early (break/return).
    return() {
      console.log('  [range] cleanup: stopped early');
      return { value: undefined, done: true };
    },
    // Optional: thrown errors propagate back into the iterator.
    throw(err) {
      console.log('  [range] received throw:', err.message);
      return { value: undefined, done: true };
    },
  };
}

console.log('--- 1. Manual consumption ---');
const it = makeRangeIterator(1, 5);
let r;
while (!(r = it.next()).done) {
  console.log('  value =', r.value);
}
console.log('  final =', r);

// Early termination triggers return()
console.log('--- 1b. Early stop ---');
for (const v of makeRangeIterator(1, 10)) {
  console.log('  v =', v);
  if (v === 3) break; // calls return()
}

// ============================================================
// 2. MAKING AN OBJECT ITERABLE (Symbol.iterator)
// ============================================================
class Countdown {
  constructor(from) { this.from = from; }
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let i = this.from;
    return {
      next() {
        if (i >= 0) return { value: i--, done: false };
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      },
    };
  }
}

console.log('--- 2. Iterable class ---');
const cd = new Countdown(3);
console.log('  spread:', [...cd]);          // [3,2,1,0]
console.log('  destructure:', [...cd]);    // re-iterable because Symbol.iterator is a factory

// ============================================================
// 3. GENERATOR FUNCTIONS (the idiomatic way)
// ============================================================
// `function*` automatically implements the protocol + cleanup.
function* fibonacci(limit) {
  let [a, b] = [0, 1];
  while (a <= limit) {
    yield a;
    [a, b] = [b, a + b];
  }
}

console.log('--- 3. Generator ---');
console.log('  fib <= 20:', [...fibonacci(20)]);

// Two-way communication via next(arg)
function* echo() {
  const x = yield 'send me something';
  yield `you sent: ${x}`;
}
const g = echo();
console.log('  g.next()     ->', g.next());        // {value:'send me something', done:false}
console.log('  g.next(42)   ->', g.next(42));     // {value:'you sent: 42', done:false}

// ============================================================
// 4. INFINITE / LAZY SEQUENCES
// ============================================================
function* naturals() {
  let n = 0;
  while (true) yield n++;
}
function* take(n, iter) {
  for (const x of iter) {
    if (n-- <= 0) return;
    yield x;
  }
}
console.log('--- 4. Lazy infinite ---');
console.log('  first 6 naturals:', [...take(6, naturals())]);

// ============================================================
// 5. ASYNC ITERATORS (Symbol.asyncIterator)
// ============================================================
async function* asyncPages(baseUrl, pages) {
  for (let p = 1; p <= pages; p++) {
    // simulate network latency
    await new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, 10));
    yield { page: p, url: `${baseUrl}?p=${p}` };
  }
}

console.log('--- 5. Async iterator ---');
(async () => {
  for await (const item of asyncPages('https://api.example.com/items', 3)) {
    console.log('  ', item);
  }

  // Manual async consumption
  const ai = asyncPages('https://api.example.com/items', 2)[Symbol.asyncIterator]();
  console.log('  manual:', await ai.next());
})();

// ============================================================
// 6. ITERATOR HELPERS (ES2025) — available in modern runtimes
// ============================================================
// Iterator.from turns any iterable/iterator into an Iterator object
// with .map, .filter, .take, .drop, .flatMap, .reduce, .toArray, etc.
console.log('--- 6. Iterator helpers (if supported) ---');
try {
  const doubled = Iterator.from([1, 2, 3, 4])
    .map(x => x * 2)
    .filter(x => x > 4)
    .toArray();
  console.log('  doubled>4:', doubled);
} catch (e) {
  console.log('  Iterator helpers not available in this runtime:', e.message);
}

// ============================================================
// 7. CUSTOM ASYNC ITERABLE CLASS (real-world pattern)
// ============================================================
class PagedFetcher {
  constructor(fetchFn, pageSize = 2) {
    this.fetchFn = fetchFn;
    this.pageSize = pageSize;
  }
  async *[Symbol.asyncIterator]() {
    let offset = 0;
    while (true) {
      const batch = await this.fetchFn(offset, this.pageSize);
      if (!batch || batch.length === 0) return;
      for (const row of batch) yield row;
      offset += batch.length;
      if (batch.length < this.pageSize) return; // last page
    }
  }
}

// Fake fetcher
const fetcher = new PagedFetcher(async (offset, size) => {
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 5));
  const all = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'];
  return all.slice(offset, offset + size);
});

console.log('--- 7. Paged async iterable ---');
(async () => {
  const collected = [];
  for await (const row of fetcher) collected.push(row);
  console.log('  collected:', collected);
})();

// ============================================================
// 8. INTEROP: converting between forms
// ============================================================
console.log('--- 8. Interop ---');
const set = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
console.log('  Set iterator:', [...set.values()]);
console.log('  Map entries:', [...new Map([['a', 1]]).entries()]);
console.log('  String chars:', [...'abc']);

// Array.from accepts any iterable
console.log('  Array.from(range):', Array.from(makeRangeIterator(10, 13)));

// ============================================================
// 9. ERROR HANDLING & CLEANUP CONTRACT
// ============================================================
function* guarded() {
  try {
    yield 1;
    yield 2;
  } finally {
    console.log('  [guarded] finally runs on close');
  }
}
console.log('--- 9. Cleanup on early exit ---');
for (const v of guarded()) {
  console.log('  v =', v);
  if (v === 1) break; // triggers finally via return()
}
```

### Key concepts recap

| Concept | Protocol method | Consumed by |
|---|---|---|
| Iterator | `next()` → `{value, done}` | manual `while` loops |
| Iterable | `[Symbol.iterator]()` | `for...of`, spread `[...]`, destructuring, `Array.from` |
| Async iterable | `[Symbol.asyncIterator]()` | `for await...of` |
| Generator | `function*` / `async function*` | all of the above, plus two-way `next(arg)` |

### Design tips

- **Prefer generators** (`function*`) for custom iteration — they handle `return()`/`throw()` and cleanup (`finally`) automatically.
- **Make objects iterable** by implementing `[Symbol.iterator]` as a *factory* (returns a fresh iterator each call) so the same object can be iterated multiple times.
- **Lazy evaluation** is the main win: don't materialize huge arrays; `yield` one item at a time.
- **Async iterators** are ideal for paginated APIs, streams, and DB cursors.
- **Always implement `return()`** in hand-written iterators if resources (file handles, DB cursors) need releasing on early `break`.
- **Iterator helpers** (`Iterator.from(...).map/filter/take/...`) are now widely available (Node 22+, modern browsers) and give you lazy pipelines without arrays.

### Run it

```bash
node iterators.js
```

Expected output is a sequence of labeled sections showing manual consumption, spreading, generators, lazy infinite streams, async paging, and cleanup-on-break behavior.

# JavaScript Iterators

## 1. The core idea

An **iterator** is an object that lets you traverse a sequence one item at a time.  
Its only required method is `next()`, which returns an object shaped like:

```js
{ value: <current item>, done: <boolean> }
```

- `value`: the next item, or `undefined` when finished  
- `done`: `false` while items remain, `true` when exhausted

An **iterable** is any object that has a special method named `[Symbol.iterator]`.  
Calling it returns a fresh iterator.

```js
const arr = [10, 20, 30];

const iterator = arr[Symbol.iterator]();
console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 10, done: false }
console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 20, done: false }
console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 30, done: false }
console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }
```

Key distinction:

- **Iterable**: can produce an iterator (`[Symbol.iterator]()`).
- **Iterator**: does the actual stepping (`next()`).

Many built-ins are iterable: `Array`, `String`, `Map`, `Set`, `TypedArray`, `arguments`, `NodeList`, etc.

---

## 2. Standard consumption syntax

You usually do not call `next()` manually. JavaScript consumes iterables with:

### `for...of`

```js
for (const n of [1, 2, 3]) {
  console.log(n);
}
```

### Spread operator

```js
const a = [1, 2];
const b = [...a, 3, 4]; // [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

### Destructuring

```js
const [first, ...rest] = "abc"; // first='a', rest=['b','c']
```

### `Array.from`

```js
Array.from(new Set([1, 1, 2])); // [1, 2]
```

### Other consumers

Functions that accept iterables: `Promise.all`, `Promise.race`, `new Map(iterable)`, `new Set(iterable)`, etc.

```js
await Promise.all([
  fetch('/a'),
  fetch('/b')
]);
```

---

## 3. Custom iterables

To make an object iterable, implement `[Symbol.iterator]()` and return an iterator.

```js
const range = {
  from: 1,
  to: 3,

  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let current = this.from;
    const last = this.to;

    return {
      next() {
        if (current <= last) {
          return { value: current++, done: false };
        }
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      }
    };
  }
};

for (const n of range) console.log(n); // 1 2 3
console.log([...range]);                // [1, 2, 3]
```

Each call to `[Symbol.iterator]()` should return a **new** iterator so the same object can be iterated multiple times.

---

## 4. Iterator protocol details

A well-behaved iterator may also implement:

### `return(value)`

Called when iteration stops early (e.g. `break`, `return`, exception).  
Useful for cleanup.

```js
const resource = {
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let i = 0;
    return {
      next() {
        if (i < 3) return { value: i++, done: false };
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      },
      return() {
        console.log('cleaning up');
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      }
    };
  }
};

for (const x of resource) {
  if (x === 1) break; // triggers return()
}
```

### `throw(error)`

Allows injecting an error into a generator/iterator. Mostly relevant to generators.

---

## 5. Generators: the easiest way to create iterators

A generator function (`function*`) automatically returns an iterator.

```js
function* range(from, to) {
  for (let i = from; i <= to; i++) {
    yield i;
  }
}

for (const n of range(1, 3)) console.log(n);
console.log([...range(5, 7)]); // [5, 6, 7]
```

Generators also support:

- Lazy evaluation
- Two-way communication via `next(arg)`
- `return()` and `throw()` handling

```js
function* gen() {
  const x = yield 'first';
  yield x + 10;
}

const g = gen();
console.log(g.next());      // { value: 'first', done: false }
console.log(g.next(5));     // { value: 15, done: false }
```

---

## 6. Typical use cases

### a. Lazy sequences (memory efficient)

```js
function* fibonacci() {
  let a = 0, b = 1;
  while (true) {
    yield a;
    [a, b] = [b, a + b];
  }
}

const fib = fibonacci();
console.log(fib.next().value); // 0
console.log(fib.next().value); // 1
console.log(fib.next().value); // 1
```

Infinite sequences are only possible because values are produced lazily.

### b. Custom data structures

```js
class LinkedList {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;
  }

  *[Symbol.iterator]() {
    let node = this.head;
    while (node) {
      yield node.value;
      node = node.next;
    }
  }
}
```

### c. Pagination / streaming API results

```js
async function* fetchPages(url) {
  let page = 1;
  while (true) {
    const res = await fetch(`${url}?page=${page}`);
    const data = await res.json();
    if (!data.length) break;
    yield* data;
    page++;
  }
}

for await (const item of fetchPages('/api/items')) {
  console.log(item);
}
```

Note: `for await...of` consumes **async iterables** (objects with `[Symbol.asyncIterator]()`).

### d. Transforming streams

```js
function* map(iterable, fn) {
  for (const x of iterable) yield fn(x);
}

console.log([...map([1, 2, 3], x => x * 2)]); // [2, 4, 6]
```

### e. Tree traversal

```js
function* dfs(node) {
  if (!node) return;
  yield node.value;
  yield* dfs(node.left);
  yield* dfs(node.right);
}
```

---

## 7. Important pitfalls

### Iterators are stateful and single-use

```js
const it = [1, 2, 3][Symbol.iterator]();
console.log([...it]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...it]); // []  (exhausted)
```

But the **iterable** is reusable:

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
console.log([...arr]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...arr]); // [1, 2, 3]
```

### `done` becomes permanently true

Once exhausted, `next()` keeps returning `{ value: undefined, done: true }`.

### Spread/destructuring consume the whole iterable

```js
const it = [1, 2, 3][Symbol.iterator]();
const [a] = it; // consumes first
console.log([...it]); // [2, 3]
```

### Not all array-like objects are iterable

`NodeList`, `arguments`, typed arrays are iterable; plain objects are not.

```js
for (const k of Object.keys({a: 1})) console.log(k);
```

---

## 8. Quick mental model

- **Iterable**: “something you can loop over with `for...of`.”
- **Iterator**: “the cursor produced by calling `[Symbol.iterator]()`.”
- **Generator**: “syntax sugar for creating iterators lazily.”

If an object supports `[Symbol.iterator]`, it works with `for...of`, spread, destructuring, `Array.from`, and most collection APIs.

Below are common JavaScript iterator pitfalls, with runnable examples and safer alternatives.

## 1. Iterators are stateful and single-use

An iterable can be used many times, but an iterator returned from it is consumed once.

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
const it = arr[Symbol.iterator]();

console.log([...it]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...it]); // []  <- exhausted
```

Fix: create a fresh iterator each time, or keep using the original iterable.

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
console.log([...arr]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...arr]); // [1, 2, 3]
```

## 2. Destructuring partially consumes an iterator

Destructuring does not “peek” at values; it calls `next()`.

```js
function* gen() { yield 1; yield 2; yield 3; }

const it = gen();
const [first] = it;

console.log(first);      // 1
console.log([...it]);    // [2, 3]
```

A rest pattern consumes the rest:

```js
const it2 = gen();
const [a, ...rest] = it2;

console.log(a);     // 1
console.log(rest);  // [2, 3]
console.log([...it2]); // []
```

## 3. Spreading or converting an iterator consumes it

`...`, `Array.from`, `new Set`, `new Map`, and destructuring all drain the iterator.

```js
function* gen() { yield 1; yield 2; }

const it = gen();
const arr = [...it];

console.log(arr);      // [1, 2]
console.log([...it]);  // []
```

If you need the values twice, materialize once:

```js
const values = [...gen()];
console.log(values);
console.log(values);
```

## 4. Modifying a collection while iterating

Mutation during `for...of` can skip values, visit values twice, or even create an infinite loop.

```js
const arr = [1, 2];

for (const x of arr) {
  console.log(x);
  if (arr.length < 6) arr.push(x + 10);
}

// 1
// 2
// 11
// 12
```

Safer: iterate over a snapshot or build a new collection.

```js
const arr = [1, 2];
for (const x of [...arr]) {
  console.log(x);
}
```

## 5. Sparse arrays yield `undefined` for holes

Array iterators treat holes as `undefined`, unlike `forEach`, which skips holes.

```js
const arr = [, "a"];

console.log([...arr]); // [undefined, "a"]

arr.forEach((x) => console.log(x)); // only "a"
```

Be explicit if holes matter:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  console.log(i, arr[i]); // distinguishes missing from undefined
}
```

## 6. Strings iterate by code points, not UTF-16 code units

Indexing uses UTF-16; iteration uses Unicode code points.

```js
const s = "a😀b";

console.log(s.length);       // 4
console.log([...s].length);  // 3
console.log(s[1]);           // "\ud83d" (surrogate half)

for (const ch of s) console.log(ch); // a, 😀, b
```

Use `[...str]` or `Array.from(str)` when you need characters/code points.

## 7. `for...in` is not `for...of`

`for...in` enumerates keys, including inherited enumerable properties; `for...of` uses `Symbol.iterator`.

```js
const arr = ["a", "b"];

for (const x in arr) console.log(x); // "0", "1"
for (const x of arr) console.log(x); // "a", "b"
```

## 8. Plain objects are not iterable

```js
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };

// for (const x of obj) {} // TypeError: obj is not iterable

for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(obj)) {
  console.log(key, value);
}
```

Array-like objects may also not be iterable:

```js
const arrayLike = { 0: "a", 1: "b", length: 2 };

// [...arrayLike] // TypeError
console.log(Array.from(arrayLike)); // ["a", "b"]
```

## 9. Map entries vs keys vs values

A `Map` iterator yields `[key, value]` pairs. Iterating the Map directly gives entries, not values.

```js
const map = new Map([["a", 1], ["b", 2]]);

for (const x of map) console.log(x);        // ["a", 1], ["b", 2]
for (const key of map.keys()) console.log(key);   // "a", "b"
for (const val of map.values()) console.log(val); // 1, 2
```

## 10. Assuming async iterables work with sync iteration

Async iterators are not sync iterables. You cannot spread them directly.

```js
async function* gen() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
}

const it = gen();

// console.log([...it]); // TypeError
for await (const x of it) console.log(x); // 1, 2
```

## 11. Infinite iterators and memory blowups

Spreading an infinite generator never finishes.

```js
function* naturals() {
  let n = 0;
  while (true) yield n++;
}

const it = naturals();
const first5 = [];

for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  first5.push(it.next().value);
}

console.log(first5); // [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
// console.log([...naturals()]); // never finishes
```

## 12. Custom iterators must follow the protocol exactly

`next()` must return an object `{ value, done }`. Returning a primitive breaks `for...of`.

```js
const bad = {
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let i = 0;
    return {
      next() {
        return i++ < 3 ? i : undefined; // wrong
      }
    };
  }
};

// for (const x of bad) console.log(x); // TypeError

const good = {
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let i = 0;
    return {
      next() {
        return i < 3 ? { value: i++, done: false } : { value: undefined, done: true };
      }
    };
  }
};

console.log([...good]); // [0, 1, 2]
```

## 13. Cleanup on early exit requires `return()`

When a loop exits early via `break`, `return`, or an exception, JavaScript calls `iterator.return()`. Custom iterators should implement it to release resources.

```js
const resource = {
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let i = 0;
    let closed = false;
    return {
      next() {
        if (closed || i >= 5) return { done: true };
        return { value: i++, done: false };
      },
      return() {
        closed = true;
        console.log("cleaned up");
        return { done: true };
      }
    };
  }
};

for (const x of resource) {
  console.log(x);
  if (x === 1) break;
}
// 0
// 1
// cleaned up
```

Generators handle this automatically with `try/finally`:

```js
function* gen() {
  try {
    yield 1;
    yield 2;
  } finally {
    console.log("cleanup");
  }
}

const it = gen();
it.next();      // {value: 1, done: false}
it.return();    // cleanup
```

## 14. `next(argument)` only matters for generators

For normal array iterators, the argument to `next()` is ignored. In generators, it becomes the result of the `yield` expression.

```js
const arrIt = [1, 2][Symbol.iterator]();
console.log(arrIt.next("ignored")); // {value: 1, done: false}

function* g() {
  const sent = yield "a";
  console.log("received:", sent);
  yield sent;
}

const git = g();
console.log(git.next().value);     // "a"
console.log(git.next(42).value);    // 42
// logs: received: 42
```

## 15. `yield*` does not automatically yield the delegated `return` value

`yield*` forwards yielded values, but the delegated generator’s `return` value becomes the value of the `yield*` expression, not an automatically yielded item.

```js
function* inner() {
  yield 1;
  return "inner result";
}

function* outer() {
  const result = yield* inner();
  console.log("got:", result); // got: inner result
  yield 2;
}

console.log([...outer()]); // [1, 2]
```

If consumers need that result, yield it explicitly:

```js
function* outer2() {
  const result = yield* inner();
  yield result;
}
```

## 16. A generator `return` value is not yielded to `for...of`

When `done` is `true`, `for...of` ignores `value`. The final `return` value is only available via manual `next()`.

```js
function* g() {
  yield 1;
  return "final";
}

const it = g();
console.log(it.next()); // {value: 1, done: false}
console.log(it.next()); // {value: "final", done: true}
console.log(it.next()); // {value: undefined, done: true}

for (const x of g()) console.log(x); // only 1
```

## 17. `throw()` injects an exception at the current `yield`

```js
function* g() {
  try {
    yield 1;
  } catch (e) {
    yield "caught: " + e.message;
  }
}

const it = g();
it.next();
console.log(it.throw(new Error("boom")).value); // caught: boom
```

## 18. Confusing sync and async iteration tools

`for...of`, spread, and `Array.from` require sync iterables. Async iterables need `for await...of` or manual `next()`.

```js
async function* asyncGen() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
}

const it = asyncGen();
console.log(typeof it[Symbol.iterator]);      // undefined
console.log(typeof it[Symbol.asyncIterator]);  // function

for await (const x of it) console.log(x);
```

## Summary of safe habits

- Treat iterators as consumable cursors, not reusable collections.
- Materialize with `[...iterable]` before multiple passes.
- Avoid mutating a collection while iterating over it.
- Use `for...of` for values, `for...in` only when keys are intended.
- Implement `{ value, done }` and `return()` correctly in custom iterators.
- Remember Unicode, sparse arrays, and async/sync distinctions.

## Short practical exercise

Build a lazy, iterable `Range(start, end, step)` in JavaScript. It should:

1. Support `for...of`, spread (`[...]`), and destructuring.
2. Work with both positive and negative steps.
3. Stop lazily instead of building an array.
4. Support early exit with `break`, and ensure the iterator cleans up.
5. Use it to compute the sum of even numbers from `1` to `100`.

---

## Key concepts

An **iterable** is any object with a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method.  
That method returns an **iterator**, an object with a `next()` method returning:

```js
{ value: <any>, done: <boolean> }
```

Optionally, an iterator can implement:

- `return()` — called when iteration stops early, such as with `break`.
- `throw(error)` — called when an error is thrown into the iterator.

A generator function is the easiest way to create an iterator, but implementing the protocol manually helps you understand what generators do internally.

---

## Step 1: Validate inputs

A range with `step === 0` would never terminate, so reject it.

```js
if (step === 0) {
  throw new RangeError("Range step cannot be zero");
}
```

---

## Step 2: Implement the manual iterator

We use closure state: `current`, `finished`, and the range bounds.

```js
class Range {
  constructor(start, end, step = 1) {
    if (!Number.isFinite(start) || !Number.isFinite(end) || !Number.isFinite(step)) {
      throw new TypeError("Range values must be finite numbers");
    }
    if (step === 0) {
      throw new RangeError("Range step cannot be zero");
    }

    this.start = start;
    this.end = end;
    this.step = step;
  }

  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let current = this.start;
    const end = this.end;
    const step = this.step;
    const ascending = step > 0;
    let finished = false;

    const inBounds = (value) =>
      ascending ? value <= end : value >= end;

    return {
      next() {
        if (finished) {
          return { value: undefined, done: true };
        }

        if (!inBounds(current)) {
          finished = true;
          return { value: undefined, done: true };
        }

        const value = current;
        current += step;
        return { value, done: false };
      },

      return() {
        // Called on break, return, or throw during iteration.
        finished = true;
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      },

      throw(error) {
        finished = true;
        throw error;
      }
    };
  }
}
```

Why `finished` matters: if someone calls `return()` early and then calls `next()` again, the iterator should remain exhausted.

---

## Step 3: Use it with normal iteration tools

```js
const range = new Range(1, 5);

// for...of
for (const n of range) {
  console.log(n); // 1 2 3 4 5
}

// spread
console.log([...new Range(10, 6, -1)]); // [10, 9, 8, 7, 6]

// destructuring
const [first, second] = new Range(100, 200, 10);
console.log(first, second); // 100 110
```

---

## Step 4: Demonstrate lazy early exit and cleanup

```js
const numbers = new Range(1, 1000000);

for (const n of numbers) {
  if (n > 5) break; // triggers iterator.return()
  console.log(n);
}

// The iterator is now exhausted; no million-element array was created.
console.log([...numbers]); // []
```

---

## Step 5: Compose iterators with a generator

This keeps memory low because values are produced one at a time.

```js
function* evenValues(iterable) {
  for (const value of iterable) {
    if (value % 2 === 0) {
      yield value;
    }
  }
}

const total = [...evenValues(new Range(1, 100))]
  .reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);

console.log(total); // 2550
```

You can also consume manually:

```js
const iterator = new Range(1, 4)[Symbol.iterator]();

console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 1, done: false }
console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 2, done: false }
iterator.return();            // simulate early stop
console.log(iterator.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }
```

---

## Final complete solution

```js
class Range {
  constructor(start, end, step = 1) {
    if (!Number.isFinite(start) || !Number.isFinite(end) || !Number.isFinite(step)) {
      throw new TypeError("Range values must be finite numbers");
    }
    if (step === 0) {
      throw new RangeError("Range step cannot be zero");
    }

    this.start = start;
    this.end = end;
    this.step = step;
  }

  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let current = this.start;
    const end = this.end;
    const step = this.step;
    const ascending = step > 0;
    let finished = false;

    const inBounds = (value) =>
      ascending ? value <= end : value >= end;

    return {
      next() {
        if (finished) {
          return { value: undefined, done: true };
        }

        if (!inBounds(current)) {
          finished = true;
          return { value: undefined, done: true };
        }

        const value = current;
        current += step;
        return { value, done: false };
      },

      return() {
        finished = true;
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      },

      throw(error) {
        finished = true;
        throw error;
      }
    };
  }
}

function* evenValues(iterable) {
  for (const value of iterable) {
    if (value % 2 === 0) {
      yield value;
    }
  }
}

// Exercise solution: sum even numbers from 1 to 100.
const sumEvenNumbers = [...evenValues(new Range(1, 100))]
  .reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);

console.log(sumEvenNumbers); // 2550

// Additional checks
console.assert([...new Range(1, 5)].join(",") === "1,2,3,4,5");
console.assert([...new Range(5, 1, -1)].join(",") === "5,4,3,2,1");
console.assert([...new Range(1, 1000000)].length === 0); // only after exhausting? not true after construction alone
```

Note: the last assertion is only meaningful after iteration; since `Range` is lazy, `[...new Range(1, 1000000)]` would create a huge array, so do not run it in real code. For testing large ranges, iterate with `break` or count lazily instead.

## Iterators in JavaScript

An **iterator** is an object that implements the *iterator protocol*: it has a `next()` method that returns `{ value, done }`.

- `value`: the next produced item.
- `done`: `true` when iteration is finished.

An **iterable** is an object that implements the *iterable protocol*: it has a method keyed by `Symbol.iterator` that returns an iterator.

Consumers like `for...of`, spread (`...`), `Array.from`, and destructuring call `[Symbol.iterator]()` to get an iterator, then repeatedly call `next()` until `done` is `true`.

Key ideas:
- Iterators are **stateful** and usually **single-use**.
- They enable **lazy evaluation**, including infinite sequences.
- Generators (`function*`) are a convenient way to create iterators, but the protocol itself is just `next()`.

---

## Core implementation

Below is a clean, dependency-free implementation of the core iterator/iterable protocol using a range example. It supports ascending and descending ranges and is lazy.

```js
/**
 * Creates an iterator over a numeric range.
 *
 * Iterator protocol:
 *   next() -> { value, done }
 *
 * @param {number} start - Inclusive start.
 * @param {number} stop  - Exclusive stop.
 * @param {number} step  - Increment (may be negative).
 * @returns {{ next: () => { value: number | undefined, done: boolean } }}
 */
function createRangeIterator(start, stop, step = 1) {
  // Internal mutable state lives in the closure.
  let current = start;

  // Determine whether we are still inside the range.
  const isDone = () =>
    step > 0 ? current >= stop : current <= stop;

  return {
    next() {
      if (isDone()) {
        // By convention, `value` is undefined when done.
        return { value: undefined, done: true };
      }

      const value = current;
      current += step;
      return { value, done: false };
    },
  };
}

/**
 * Wraps the iterator in an iterable so it can be used with
 * for...of, spread, Array.from, destructuring, etc.
 */
function range(start, stop, step = 1) {
  return {
    // The iterable protocol requires this exact symbol key.
    [Symbol.iterator]() {
      return createRangeIterator(start, stop, step);
    },
  };
}

// ---- Demo ----
const r = range(1, 6); // 1,2,3,4,5

console.log([...r]); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

// Iterators are single-use: create a fresh one each time.
for (const n of range(10, 0, -2)) {
  console.log(n); // 10, 8, 6, 4, 2
}

// Manual consumption
const it = range(0, 3)[Symbol.iterator]();
console.log(it.next()); // { value: 0, done: false }
console.log(it.next()); // { value: 1, done: false }
console.log(it.next()); // { value: 2, done: false }
console.log(it.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }
```

---

## A class-based iterable (more idiomatic for reusable types)

```js
class Range {
  constructor(start, stop, step = 1) {
    if (step === 0) throw new Error("step cannot be 0");
    this.start = start;
    this.stop = stop;
    this.step = step;
  }

  // Implementing the iterable protocol makes instances work with for...of.
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let current = this.start;
    const { stop, step } = this;

    const inRange = () =>
      step > 0 ? current < stop : current > stop;

    return {
      next() {
        if (!inRange()) {
          return { value: undefined, done: true };
        }
        const value = current;
        current += step;
        return { value, done: false };
      },
    };
  }
}

const r2 = new Range(2, 11, 3);
console.log(Array.from(r2)); // [2, 5, 8]
```

---

## Optional: lazy iterator helpers (core “pipeline” idea)

Iterators shine when transformed lazily. Here are minimal `map` and `take` helpers that return new iterators without building intermediate arrays:

```js
function mapIter(iterator, fn) {
  return {
    next() {
      const { value, done } = iterator.next();
      if (done) return { value: undefined, done: true };
      return { value: fn(value), done: false };
    },
  };
}

function takeIter(iterator, n) {
  let remaining = n;
  return {
    next() {
      if (remaining <= 0) return { value: undefined, done: true };
      remaining--;
      return iterator.next();
    },
  };
}

// Infinite source + lazy transform + lazy limit
function* naturals() {
  let n = 0;
  while (true) yield n++;
}

const firstSquares = takeIter(mapIter(naturals(), (x) => x * x), 5);
console.log(Array.from({ [Symbol.iterator]: () => firstSquares })); // [0,1,4,9,16]
```

### Interview takeaways
- Always distinguish **iterator** (`next`) from **iterable** (`Symbol.iterator`).
- `for...of` calls `[Symbol.iterator]()` once per loop.
- Returning `{ done: true }` ends iteration; `value` is ignored by consumers.
- Because iterators are stateful, re-running a loop requires creating a new iterator.

# Edge Cases When Using Iterators in JavaScript

Below are the most important edge cases, grouped by theme, with runnable examples.

---

## 1. Iterators Are Single-Use

An iterator is a *cursor*, not a collection. Once exhausted, it stays exhausted.

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
const it = arr[Symbol.iterator]();

console.log([...it]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...it]); // []  ← exhausted
```

**Fix:** re-create the iterator, or wrap the collection in a generator function.

```js
function* makeIt() { yield* arr; }
console.log([...makeIt()]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...makeIt()]); // [1, 2, 3]
```

---

## 2. `done: true` Can Carry a Value

The final `next()` call may return a meaningful `value` (e.g., generator return values).

```js
function* gen() { yield 1; return 42; }
const it = gen();
console.log(it.next()); // { value: 1, done: false }
console.log(it.next()); // { value: 42, done: true }
```

**Edge case:** spread/destructuring **ignore** the final value.

```js
console.log([...gen()]); // [1]  (42 is lost)
const [a, b] = gen();
console.log(a, b);      // 1 undefined
```

---

## 3. Mutation During Iteration

Mutating a collection while iterating over it yields unspecified / surprising results.

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];
for (const x of arr) {
  if (x === 2) arr.splice(1, 1); // remove 2
  console.log(x);
}
// 1, 2, 4  (3 skipped!)
```

For `Set`/`Map`, the spec defines behavior (entries added during iteration are visited; deleted ones skipped), but relying on it is fragile.

```js
const s = new Set([1, 2]);
for (const x of s) {
  s.add(3); // 3 will be visited
  console.log(x);
}
// 1, 2, 3
```

**Fix:** iterate over a snapshot.

```js
for (const x of [...arr]) { /* safe */ }
```

---

## 4. Early Termination & Cleanup (`return` / `finally`)

`for...of`, spread, and destructuring call `iterator.return()` when exiting early. Generators run their `finally` blocks.

```js
function* gen() {
  try { yield 1; yield 2; }
  finally { console.log('cleanup'); }
}
const it = gen();
console.log(it.next().value); // 1
it.return();                  // triggers finally
// cleanup
```

**Edge case:** if you manually drive `next()` and never call `return()`, cleanup never runs.

```js
const it = gen();
it.next(); // no cleanup until GC/finalization
```

---

## 5. `throw()` Propagation

`iterator.throw(err)` throws inside the generator at the current `yield`.

```js
function* gen() {
  try { yield 1; }
  catch (e) { console.log('caught', e.message); yield 2; }
}
const it = gen();
it.next();
console.log(it.throw(new Error('boom')).value); // caught boom → 2
```

If the generator doesn't catch it, the error propagates out of `throw()`.

---

## 6. Non-Iterable Values

`for...of`, spread, and destructuring require the iterable protocol. Passing `null`/`undefined` throws `TypeError`.

```js
try { for (const x of null) {} }
catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name); } // TypeError

try { [...undefined]; }
catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name); } // TypeError
```

**Also:** objects with a broken `Symbol.iterator` (returns non-object) throw.

```js
const bad = { [Symbol.iterator]() { return 42; } };
try { [...bad]; }
catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name); } // TypeError
```

---

## 7. Iterator Helpers Are Lazy and Consume the Source (ES2025)

New iterator helper methods (`.map`, `.filter`, `.take`, `.drop`, `.flatMap`, `.reduce`, `.toArray`, `.forEach`, `.some`, `.every`, `.find`) operate lazily but **consume** the underlying iterator.

```js
const it = [1,2,3,4][Symbol.iterator]();
const mapped = it.map(x => x * 2);
console.log([...mapped]); // [2,4,6,8]
console.log([...it]);     // []  ← source consumed
```

**Edge case:** helpers return *new* iterators; the original is still advanced.

Also `.take(n)` stops early and calls `return()` on the source (triggering cleanup).

---

## 8. Infinite Iterators & `take`

Without a bound, spread/destructuring will hang.

```js
function* naturals() { let i = 0; while (true) yield i++; }
// [...naturals()]  ← infinite loop / OOM
```

Use `.take()` (ES2025) or manual limits.

```js
const first5 = naturals().take(5);
console.log([...first5]); // [0,1,2,3,4]
```

---

## 9. `next()` After `done: true`

Calling `next()` on an exhausted iterator must keep returning `{ value: undefined, done: true }` (or the final value). Implementations that violate this break `for...of`.

```js
function* g() { yield 1; }
const it = g();
it.next(); it.next(); // {value: undefined, done: true}
console.log(it.next()); // {value: undefined, done: true}  (stable)
```

Custom iterators must honor this contract.

---

## 10. `this` Binding in Iterator Methods

If you destructure `next` off an iterator, `this` is lost.

```js
const it = [1,2,3][Symbol.iterator]();
const { next } = it;
try { next(); } // TypeError: next called on non-iterator? (engine-dependent)
catch (e) { console.log(e.message); }
```

**Fix:** bind or call as method.

```js
const n = it.next.bind(it);
console.log(n()); // { value: 1, done: false }
```

---

## 11. String Iteration: Code Points vs UTF-16

`for...of` on strings iterates **code points** (not UTF-16 code units). Indexing with `[]` uses code units.

```js
const s = 'a😀b';
console.log(s.length);        // 4 (UTF-16 units)
console.log([...s].length);   // 3 (code points)
console.log(s[1]);            // '\ud83d' (half surrogate)
```

**Edge case:** regex with `u` flag vs iteration.

---

## 12. Async Iterators & `for await...of`

Async iterators use `Symbol.asyncIterator` and return promises from `next()`.

```js
async function* agen() { yield 1; await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,10)); yield 2; }
for await (const x of agen()) console.log(x); // 1, 2
```

**Edge cases:**
- Sync iterables are *not* automatically async-iterable in all contexts; `for await...of` will call `Symbol.asyncIterator` then fall back to `Symbol.iterator` (wrapping values in resolved promises) — but only if the sync iterable exists.
- Mixing: `Array.fromAsync` for async iterables.

```js
const arr = await Array.fromAsync(agen());
console.log(arr); // [1, 2]
```

---

## 13. `yield*` Delegation & Nested `return`

`yield*` delegates to inner iterables and propagates `return()`/`throw()`.

```js
function* outer() {
  try { yield* [1, 2, 3]; }
  finally { console.log('outer cleanup'); }
}
const it = outer();
console.log(it.next().value); // 1
it.return(); // triggers finally of outer AND inner cleanup
```

**Edge case:** if the inner iterable lacks `return()`, delegation still works for values but cleanup isn't propagated.

---

## 14. Custom Iterator Protocol Compliance

A valid iterator must be an object whose `next` returns `{ value, done }`. `done` must be a boolean-ish truthy/falsy; engines check truthiness.

```js
const bad = {
  next() { return { value: 1 }; } // missing done → treated as not done forever!
};
// [...bad]  ← infinite loop!
```

**Fix:** always set `done`.

```js
let i = 0;
const good = {
  next() { return i < 3 ? { value: i++, done: false } : { value: undefined, done: true }; }
};
console.log([...good]); // [0,1,2]
```

Also make it iterable by adding `Symbol.iterator` returning `this`.

---

## 15. `Array.from` / Spread on Array-Like Non-Iterables

Array-likes without `Symbol.iterator` are *not* iterable.

```js
const arrayLike = { 0: 'a', 1: 'b', length: 2 };
try { [...arrayLike]; }
catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name); } // TypeError
```

Use `Array.from` or `Array.prototype.slice.call`.

```js
console.log(Array.from(arrayLike)); // ['a','b']
```

---

## 16. Generators and `this`

Generator functions create their own `this` (or inherit from prototype). Arrow functions can't be generators.

```js
const obj = {
  *gen() { yield this.x; },
  x: 42
};
console.log([...obj.gen()]); // [42]

// Arrow generator is a SyntaxError:
// const g = *() => {};  // SyntaxError
```

---

## 17. `return()` Must Return an Iterator Result

Custom `return()` must return `{ value, done: true }`. If it returns something else, engines may misbehave.

```js
const it = {
  i: 0,
  next() { return this.i < 2 ? { value: this.i++, done: false } : { value: undefined, done: true }; },
  return() { console.log('return called'); return { value: 'bye', done: true }; }
};
console.log([...it].pop()); // 'bye' is NOT included (done:true), prints 'bye'? 
// Actually spread stops; value ignored.
```

---

## 18. `for...of` on `arguments` / NodeList in Old Environments

In very old environments (pre-ES6), `arguments` and `NodeList` weren't iterable. Modern engines fix this, but polyfills must add `Symbol.iterator`.

```js
// Polyfill pattern:
if (!NodeList.prototype[Symbol.iterator]) {
  NodeList.prototype[Symbol.iterator] = Array.prototype[Symbol.iterator];
}
```

---

## 19. Destructuring Rest Consumes the Iterator

```js
const it = [1,2,3,4][Symbol.iterator]();
const [a, ...rest] = it; // rest is an ARRAY, consumes rest of iterator
console.log(a, rest);     // 1 [2,3,4]
console.log([...it]);     // [] 
```

**Edge case:** you can't get the remaining iterator back after rest destructuring.

---

## 20. `Iterator.from` (ES2025) Wrapping

`Iterator.from` accepts iterables *or* iterator-like objects (with `next`). It normalizes them.

```js
const like = { next() { return { value: 1, done: false }; } }; // infinite-ish
const it = Iterator.from(like).take(3);
console.log([...it]); // [1,1,1]
```

**Edge case:** if the wrapped object's `next` isn't bound, `Iterator.from` calls it as a method on the object (safe). But passing a bare function breaks.

---

## 21. Concurrent Iteration & Generators

Generators are **not** re-entrant: calling `next()` while a previous `next()` is suspended (e.g., inside an async operation) throws.

```js
function* g() { yield 1; }
const it = g();
it.next();
try { it.next(); } // fine sequentially
catch {}
// But re-entrancy across awaits:
async function* ag() { yield await Promise.resolve(1); }
const ait = ag();
ait.next().then(() => ait.next()); // still serialized by microtasks, ok
```

True re-entrancy (calling `next()` synchronously inside `next()`) throws `TypeError: Generator is already running`.

```js
function* g() {
  const it = g();
  it.next(); // re-entrant call
  yield 1;
}
try { [...g()]; }
catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name); } // TypeError
```

---

## 22. `for...of` and `break` / `return` in Functions

A `break` inside `for...of` calls `iterator.return()` if it exists. If `return()` is missing, no cleanup.

```js
function* g() { try { yield 1; yield 2; } finally { console.log('ran'); } }
for (const x of g()) { if (x === 1) break; }
// ran  (because for...of calls return())
```

But manual `while` loop does NOT.

```js
const it = g();
let r = it.next();
while (!r.done) {
  if (r.value === 1) break;
  r = it.next();
}
// NO 'ran' printed — leak!
```

---

## Summary Checklist

| # | Edge case | Mitigation |
|---|-----------|------------|
| 1 | Single-use iterators | Re-create or snapshot |
| 2 | Final `value` dropped by spread | Read via `next()` manually |
| 3 | Mutation during iteration | Iterate `[...col]` |
| 4 | Missing cleanup on manual loops | Call `return()` or use `for...of` |
| 5 | Uncaught `throw()` | Wrap in try/catch |
| 6 | Non-iterable input | Validate / use `Array.from` |
| 7 | Helpers consume source | Don't reuse source after |
| 8 | Infinite iterators | `.take(n)` |
| 9 | `next()` after done | Ensure `{done:true}` stable |
| 10 | `this` loss | `.bind()` |
| 11 | String code points vs units | Use `[...str]` |
| 12 | Async mixing | `Array.fromAsync`, `for await` |
| 13 | `yield*` cleanup propagation | Ensure inner has `return()` |
| 14 | Missing `done` field | Always set boolean |
| 15 | Array-likes | `Array.from` |
| 16 | Arrow generators | Use function* |
| 17 | `return()` contract | Return iterator result |
| 18 | Legacy non-iterables | Polyfill `Symbol.iterator` |
| 19 | Rest destructuring | Consume remainder into array |
| 20 | `Iterator.from` wrapping | Pass object, not bare fn |
| 21 | Re-entrancy | Never re-enter synchronously |
| 22 | Manual loops skip cleanup | Call `return()` explicitly |

