## Quick checklist

- Controllers coordinate; models decide; views render.
- Pass data down, events up.
- Never mutate or expose internal model references accidentally.
- Clean up listeners.
- Guard against duplicate/stale async work.
- Keep DOM/framework code at the edges.
- Render incrementally when possible.
- Make loading/error states explicit.

If you want, I can tailor these pitfalls to a specific stack (React, Vue, Express, Backbone, etc.).

## Practical Exercise

**Exercise:** Build a tiny “Todo” app in vanilla JavaScript using the **Model–View–Controller (MVC)** pattern.

Requirements:
- The user can **add** a task, **mark it complete/incomplete**, and **delete** it.
- The **Model** owns the data and notifies observers when it changes.
- The **View** only renders the DOM and forwards user actions.
- The **Controller** connects the Model and View and contains the application logic.

Keep it small enough to understand, but complete enough to run in a browser.

---

## Step-by-Step Solution

### 1. Understand the roles

- **Model**: Source of truth. It stores tasks and exposes methods like `addTask`, `toggleTask`, `deleteTask`. It does not know about HTML.
- **View**: Knows about the DOM. It renders tasks and binds DOM events, but delegates decisions to callbacks.
- **Controller**: Creates Model and View, subscribes to Model changes, and connects View events to Model methods.

This separation makes the code testable and easier to change.

### 2. HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>MVC Todo</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 480px; margin: 2rem auto; }
    .done { text-decoration: line-through; color: #888; }
    li { display: flex; gap: .5rem; align-items: center; margin: .25rem 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Todo MVC</h1>
  <form id="add-form">
    <input id="task-input" placeholder="New task" required />
    <button>Add</button>
  </form>
  <ul id="task-list"></ul>

  <script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 3. Model implementation

The Model stores tasks and uses a simple observer pattern. Whenever data changes, it calls subscribed listeners.

```js
// app.js
class TodoModel {
  constructor() {
    this.tasks = [];
    this.listeners = [];
  }

  subscribe(listener) {
    this.listeners.push(listener);
  }

  notify() {
    this.listeners.forEach((listener) => listener(this.tasks));
  }

  addTask(text) {
    const task = {
      id: crypto.randomUUID(),
      text,
      done: false,
    };
    this.tasks.push(task);
    this.notify();
    return task;
  }

  toggleTask(id) {
    const task = this.tasks.find((t) => t.id === id);
    if (task) {
      task.done = !task.done;
      this.notify();
    }
  }

  deleteTask(id) {
    this.tasks = this.tasks.filter((t) => t.id !== id);
    this.notify();
  }
}
```

### 4. View implementation

The View renders the current task list and binds DOM events. It does not mutate model state directly.

```js
class TodoView {
  constructor(root) {
    this.root = root;
    this.form = root.querySelector("#add-form");
    this.input = root.querySelector("#task-input");
    this.list = root.querySelector("#task-list");

    // Event binding helpers accept handlers from the controller.
    this.bindAdd = (handler) => {
      this.form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        const text = this.input.value.trim();
        if (text) {
          handler(text);
          this.input.value = "";
          this.input.focus();
        }
      });
    };

    this.bindToggle = (handler) => {
      this.list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
        const toggleBtn = e.target.closest("[data-action='toggle']");
        if (toggleBtn) {
          handler(toggleBtn.dataset.id);
        }
      });
    };

    this.bindDelete = (handler) => {
      this.list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
        const deleteBtn = e.target.closest("[data-action='delete']");
        if (deleteBtn) {
          handler(deleteBtn.dataset.id);
        }
      });
    };
  }

  render(tasks) {
    this.list.innerHTML = tasks
      .map(
        (task) => `
        <li>
          <span class="${task.done ? "done" : ""}">${escapeHtml(task.text)}</span>
          <button data-action="toggle" data-id="${task.id}">
            ${task.done ? "Undo" : "Done"}
          </button>
          <button data-action="delete" data-id="${task.id}">Delete</button>
        </li>
      `
      )
      .join("");
  }
}

function escapeHtml(value) {
  const div = document.createElement("div");
  div.textContent = value;
  return div.innerHTML;
}
```

### 5. Controller implementation

The Controller wires everything together. It listens to Model changes and tells the View to render. It also connects View events to Model methods.

```js
class TodoController {
  constructor(model, view) {
    this.model = model;
    this.view = view;

    // Render whenever model changes.
    this.model.subscribe((tasks) => this.view.render(tasks));

    // Connect view events to model actions.
    this.view.bindAdd((text) => this.model.addTask(text));
    this.view.bindToggle((id) => this.model.toggleTask(id));
    this.view.bindDelete((id) => this.model.deleteTask(id));
  }
}
```

### 6. Bootstrap

```js
const model = new TodoModel();
const view = new TodoView(document);
const controller = new TodoController(model, view);

// Initial render
view.render(model.tasks);
```

