## Optional variation: use `fork` instead of `spawn`

For JavaScript-to-JavaScript subprocesses, `child_process.fork` is often cleaner because it automatically sets up an IPC channel.

```js
const { fork } = require('child_process');

const child = fork('./worker.js', [String(n)]);

child.on('message', result => {
  console.log('result from child:', result);
});

child.send({ task: 'compute' });
```

In that design, the worker would use `process.send(result)` instead of writing to `stdout`. This is useful when you need structured message passing rather than plain text output.

