const scheduler = new TimerScheduler();
let now = 0;
scheduler.nowFn = () => now;

scheduler.setTimeout(() => console.log("timer A at", now), 10);
scheduler.setInterval(() => console.log("interval B at", now), 5);

// Simulate time passing.
now = 5;
scheduler.tick(now); // interval B

now = 10;
scheduler.tick(now); // timer A + interval B

now = 15;
scheduler.tick(now); // interval B

// Signal demo
runWithTimeout(scheduler, (signal) => {
  // Simulate work that finishes quickly.
  scheduler.setTimeout(() => {
    if (!signal.aborted) console.log("Async chunk finished.");
  }, 2);
}, 100);
```

---

## Complexity and interview talking points

- **Timer scheduling:** A min-heap gives `O(log n)` insert/remove and `O(1)` peek. Real runtimes also track timer wheels or sorted lists depending on scale.
- **Cancellation:** `AbortSignal` is cooperative. The operation must check `signal.aborted` or listen for `"abort"`; cancellation is not forced.
- **Determinism:** In production, timers interact with I/O, rendering, and microtasks. The mini scheduler above isolates timer logic for testing.
- **Edge cases handled here:** clamping negative delays to `0`, idempotent abort, listener errors, cancelled interval rescheduling, and callback exceptions.

If by “signals” you meant **reactive signals**, the core is even smaller: a value plus a `Set` of subscribers, where `set()` notifies subscribers. The timer scheduler above can drive batching or debouncing for those signals.

Below are the most important edge cases for **timers** (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`, and related APIs) and **signals** (`AbortSignal`, process signals, and reactive signals), with runnable examples.

---

## 1. Timers

### 1.1 Delay overflow (and underflow) is silently clamped

Delays larger than `2^31 - 1` ms (~24.8 days) are clamped to `1` ms in browsers and Node.js. Non-integer delays are truncated.

```js
// Fires almost immediately, NOT in 25 days
setTimeout(() => console.log('fired early'), 2_147_483_648);

// Truncated to 100 ms
setTimeout(() => console.log('100ms'), 100.9);
```

**Mitigation:** chunk long waits into smaller chained timers, or use a date-based scheduler.

---

### 1.2 `this` binding is lost

```js
const obj = {
  id: 42,
  tick() { console.log(this.id); }
};

setTimeout(obj.tick, 100);              // undefined (this = global/window)
setTimeout(() => obj.tick(), 100);      // 42
setTimeout(obj.tick.bind(obj), 100);    // 42
```

---

### 1.3 Extra arguments are passed after the delay

```js
setTimeout((a, b) => console.log(a, b), 100, 'hello', 7);
// -> "hello 7"
```

Forgetting this leads to “mystery arguments” when refactoring.

---

### 1.4 `setInterval` does **not** await async callbacks

Overlapping executions are possible:

```js
setInterval(async () => {
  await doSlowWork(); // if this takes > interval, calls overlap
}, 100);
```

**Fix:** use recursive `setTimeout`:

```js
async function loop() {
  await doSlowWork();
  setTimeout(loop, 100);
}
loop();
```

---

### 1.5 Unhandled exceptions can terminate the process (Node)

In browsers an exception in a timer callback is reported but the interval continues. In Node, an uncaught exception in a timer can crash the process.

```js
setInterval(() => {
  try {
    risky();
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('handled:', e);
  }
}, 100);
```

---

### 1.6 Minimum delay clamping in browsers

Nested timers are clamped to at least 4 ms after 5 levels:

```js
let start = performance.now();
setTimeout(function run(i) {
  if (i < 10) setTimeout(run, 0, i + 1);
  else console.log('elapsed:', performance.now() - start);
}, 0, 0);
// Takes noticeably more than 0ms due to 4ms clamping
```

---

### 1.7 Microtasks run before macrotasks

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('microtask'));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('queued microtask'));
// Order: microtask, queued microtask, timeout
```

This matters when coordinating “immediate” follow-up work.

---

### 1.8 `clearTimeout` / `clearInterval` with bad IDs is a no-op

```js
clearTimeout(12345);        // no error
clearInterval(undefined);    // no error
```

IDs are opaque; never assume a numeric range.

---

### 1.9 Node timers can be `.unref()`’d

```js
const t = setTimeout(() => console.log('never (maybe)'), 60_000);
t.unref(); // process may exit before the timer fires
```

`ref()` re-arms it. Browser timers have no equivalent.

---

### 1.10 Background-tab throttling (browsers)

Timers in background tabs may be throttled to ~1 invocation/sec (or paused). Do not rely on precise timing for background work; use `visibilitychange` + Web Workers if needed.

---

### 1.11 Memory leaks via closures

```js
function start() {
  const huge = new Array(1_000_000).fill(0);
  setInterval(() => {
    // closure keeps `huge` alive forever
    console.log(huge.length);
  }, 1000);
}
```

Always clear intervals and null large captured variables when done.

---

## 2. Signals (`AbortSignal` / `AbortController`)

### 2.1 Late listeners do **not** fire if already aborted

```js
const c = new AbortController();
c.abort();

c.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => console.log('missed')); // never fires
console.log('aborted?', c.signal.aborted); // true
```

**Fix:** check `aborted` first:

```js
if (signal.aborted) handleAbort(signal.reason);
else signal.addEventListener('abort', () => handleAbort(signal.reason), { once: true });
```

---

### 2.2 Custom `reason` propagates

```js
const c = new AbortController();
c.abort(new Error('user cancelled'));
console.log(c.signal.reason?.message); // "user cancelled"
```

Default reason is a `DOMException` with name `'AbortError'`.

---

### 2.3 `AbortSignal.timeout()` cannot be manually aborted

```js
const sig = AbortSignal.timeout(5000); // auto-aborts after 5s
// sig has no controller; you cannot abort early
```

To allow both manual and timeout abort, combine:

```js
const manual = new AbortController();
const combined = AbortSignal.any([manual.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(5000)]);
fetch(url, { signal: combined });
```

---

### 2.4 `AbortSignal.any()` aborts if **any** input is aborted

```js
const a = new AbortController();
const b = new AbortController();
const combined = AbortSignal.any([a.signal, b.signal]);

b.abort();
console.log(combined.aborted); // true
console.log(combined.reason === b.signal.reason); // true
```

Edge: if one input is already aborted at construction, `combined` is immediately aborted.

---

### 2.5 Detecting abort errors correctly

`fetch` (and many APIs) reject with a `DOMException` named `'AbortError'` (or `AbortError` in Node).

```js
try {
  await fetch('/api', { signal });
} catch (e) {
  if (e?.name === 'AbortError') {
    console.log('aborted intentionally');
  } else {
    throw e; // network error, etc.
  }
}
```

Don’t match on message text; it varies.

---

### 2.6 Always use `{ once: true }` (or remove listeners)

```js
function work(signal) {
  const onAbort = () => cleanup();
  signal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });

  // If not using once:
  // signal.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
}
```

Otherwise listeners leak, especially with long-lived signals.

---

### 2.7 Composing timers + signals (promise wrapper)

```js
function delay(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason);
      return;
    }
    const t = setTimeout(resolve, ms);
    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(t);
      reject(signal.reason);
    };
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}
```

Crucial edge: handle the already-aborted case **before** scheduling the timer.

---

### 2.8 `signal` must be forwarded, not recreated

```js
// Bad: creates a new controller, original abort won't propagate
async function inner(signal) {
  const c = new AbortController();
  return fetch(url, { signal: c.signal });
}

// Good: forward the same signal
async function inner(signal) {
  return fetch(url, { signal });
}
```

---

## 3. Node.js process signals

### 3.1 `SIGKILL` / `SIGSTOP` cannot be handled

```js
process.on('SIGKILL', () => console.log('never runs'));
// The process is terminated immediately by the OS
```

Only `SIGTERM`, `SIGINT`, `SIGHUP`, etc. are catchable.

---

### 3.2 Platform differences

- `SIGINT` (Ctrl+C) behavior differs on Windows.
- Some signals (e.g. `SIGUSR1`, `SIGUSR2`) are not available on Windows.

```js
if (process.platform !== 'win32') {
  process.on('SIGUSR2', () => { /* unix-only */ });
}
```

---

### 3.3 Graceful shutdown must handle both signals

```js
let shuttingDown = false;

async function shutdown(code = 0) {
  if (shuttingDown) return;
  shuttingDown = true;
  await closeServer();
  await closeDb();
  process.exit(code);
}

process.on('SIGTERM', () => shutdown(0));
process.on('SIGINT',  () => shutdown(0));
```

Guard against double-triggering.

---

### 3.4 Listener limit warnings

Node warns when >10 listeners are attached to one event (including signals):

```js
process.setMaxListeners(20); // if you genuinely need more
```

Better: centralize shutdown handlers instead of many modules attaching listeners.

---

### 3.5 `process.exit()` skips async cleanup

```js
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => {
  await flushLogs();   // may be cut off if you call process.exit() too soon
  process.exit(0);
});
```

Allow the event loop to drain, or use `process.exitCode = 0` instead of `process.exit()`.

---

## 4. Reactive “Signals” (TC39 proposal / framework signals)

If you use the upcoming standard or libraries (Preact, Solid, Angular), watch for:

### 4.1 Lazy evaluation + memo equality

```js
const count = signal(0);
const doubled = computed(() => count.value * 2);
// doubled does not recompute until read; equality may skip updates
```

Edge: custom equality functions must be pure and consistent, or effects re-run unexpectedly.

### 4.2 Batching and microtask scheduling

```js
batch(() => {
  a.value = 1;
  b.value = 2; // effects should run once, not twice
});
```

Mixing manual `.value` writes with effects outside a batch can cause “glitch” intermediate states.

### 4.3 Memory leaks in effects/watchers

```js
effect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  // MUST return cleanup
  return () => clearInterval(id);
});
```

Without cleanup, effects accumulate timers/subscriptions.

---

## 5. Cross-cutting checklist

| Concern | Practical rule |
|---|---|
| Timers + async work | Prefer recursive `setTimeout` over `setInterval` |
| Delays | Never exceed `2^31-1` ms; chunk long waits |
| Browser background | Expect throttling; don’t depend on precision |
| Abort already done | Check `signal.aborted` before adding listeners |
| Error detection | Check `e.name === 'AbortError'`, not message |
| Combining cancel reasons | Use `AbortSignal.any([...])` |
| Cleanup | Use `{ once: true }` or explicit `removeEventListener` |
| Process shutdown | Handle both `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT`; avoid `SIGKILL` assumptions |
| Memory | Clear timers and remove listeners; beware closures over large data |

These patterns cover the vast majority of production bugs involving timers and signals in JavaScript.

# Singleton Pattern in JavaScript — Complete Guide

## What is it?

A **Singleton** guarantees that a class has **only one instance** and provides a **global point of access** to it. In JavaScript this is especially natural because objects are first-class and modules are cached.

Below are the four most common, idiomatic implementations, followed by a single **runnable, self-verifying example** you can paste into Node.js or a browser console.

---

## 1. ES6 Class with a Static Instance (most common)

```js
class Database {
  constructor() {
    if (Database.instance) {
      return Database.instance;          // return existing instance
    }
    this.connection = "connected";
    Database.instance = this;            // store it
    return this;
  }

  query(sql) { return `result for: ${sql}`; }
}

const a = new Database();
const b = new Database();
console.log(a === b); // true
```

**Pros:** Familiar OOP shape.  
**Cons:** `new Database()` still looks like a normal constructor; subclassing is awkward; the guard lives in the constructor (slightly anti-pattern).

---

## 2. Static Factory Method (cleaner, recommended for classes)

```js
class Config {
  constructor() {
    this.settings = {};
  }

  static getInstance() {
    if (!Config._instance) {
      Config._instance = new Config();
    }
    return Config._instance;
  }
}

const x = Config.getInstance();
const y = Config.getInstance();
console.log(x === y); // true
```

**Pros:** Explicit intent; constructor stays "pure"; easy to mock in tests by overriding `getInstance`.

---

## 3. Closure / Module Pattern (pre-ES6, still great)

```js
const Logger = (function () {
  let instance = null;

  function createInstance() {
    return {
      logs: [],
      log(msg) { this.logs.push(msg); console.log(msg); }
    };
  }

  return {
    getInstance() {
      if (!instance) instance = createInstance();
      return instance;
    }
  };
})();

const l1 = Logger.getInstance();
const l2 = Logger.getInstance();
console.log(l1 === l2); // true
```

**Pros:** True privacy; no `new` keyword; works in any JS environment.

---

## 4. ES Module Natural Singleton (modern, simplest)

```js
// counter.js
let count = 0;
export function increment() { return ++count; }
export function get() { return count; }
```

Because ES modules are **evaluated once and cached**, every importer shares the same module scope — that *is* a singleton. No class needed.

---

## Complete, Runnable, Self-Verifying Example

Save as `singleton-demo.mjs` (or paste into a browser console) and run with `node singleton-demo.mjs`.

```js
// singleton-demo.mjs
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// A thorough, self-testing demonstration of the Singleton pattern in JS.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------- Implementation: Static Factory (recommended) ----------
class DatabaseConnection {
  constructor(host, port) {
    // Guard: if an instance already exists, ignore new args and return it.
    if (DatabaseConnection._instance) {
      return DatabaseConnection._instance;
    }

    this.host = host;
    this.port = port;
    this.connectedAt = new Date();
    this.queries = [];

    DatabaseConnection._instance = this;
    Object.freeze(this); // optional: make instance immutable-ish
  }

  static getInstance(host = "localhost", port = 5432) {
    if (!DatabaseConnection._instance) {
      DatabaseConnection._instance = new DatabaseConnection(host, port);
    }
    return DatabaseConnection._instance;
  }

  query(sql) {
    const result = { sql, at: new Date().toISOString() };
    this.queries.push(result);
    return result;
  }

  // Allow resetting ONLY in tests (escape hatch)
  static _resetForTesting() {
    DatabaseConnection._instance = null;
  }
}

// ---------- Implementation: Closure Singleton ----------
const Cache = (function () {
  let instance = null;

  function create() {
    const store = new Map();
    return {
      set(key, value) { store.set(key, value); return this; },
      get(key) { return store.get(key); },
      size() { return store.size; },
      _store: store, // exposed for inspection only
    };
  }

  return {
    getInstance() {
      if (!instance) instance = create();
      return instance;
    },
    // test escape hatch
    _resetForTesting() { instance = null; },
  };
})();

// ---------- Implementation: ES Module-style (simulated with closure) ----------
const EventBus = (() => {
  const listeners = new Map();
  return {
    on(event, cb) {
      if (!listeners.has(event)) listeners.set(event, new Set());
      listeners.get(event).add(cb);
      return this;
    },
    emit(event, payload) {
      const set = listeners.get(event);
      if (set) for (const cb of set) cb(payload);
    },
    listenerCount(event) { return listeners.get(event)?.size ?? 0; },
  };
})();

// ---------- Self-tests ----------
function assert(cond, msg) {
  if (!cond) {
    console.error("FAIL:", msg);
    process.exitCode = 1;
  } else {
    console.log("PASS:", msg);
  }
}

// Test 1: DatabaseConnection singleton identity
DatabaseConnection._resetForTesting();
const d1 = DatabaseConnection.getInstance("db1", 1111);
const d2 = DatabaseConnection.getInstance("db2", 2222); // args ignored
assert(d1 === d2, "getInstance returns same instance");
assert(d1.host === "db1", "first args win (host)");
assert(d1.port === 1111, "first args win (port)");
assert(Object.isFrozen(d1), "instance is frozen");

// Even `new` returns the same instance (because constructor guards)
const d3 = new DatabaseConnection("x", 9);
assert(d1 === d3, "new returns existing instance");

// Test 2: Cache singleton
Cache._resetForTesting();
const c1 = Cache.getInstance();
const c2 = Cache.getInstance();
assert(c1 === c2, "Cache singleton identity");
c1.set("a", 42);
assert(c2.get("a") === 42, "shared state across references");

// Test 3: EventBus singleton (module-level)
const e1 = EventBus;
const e2 = EventBus;
assert(e1 === e2, "EventBus is a single object");
let received = null;
e1.on("ping", (p) => (received = p));
e2.emit("ping", "pong");
assert(received === "pong", "emit reaches listener registered via other ref");

// Test 4: Demonstrate the danger of NOT using singleton (control)
class PlainDb {
  constructor(host) { this.host = host; }
}
const p1 = new PlainDb("a");
const p2 = new PlainDb("b");
assert(p1 !== p2, "plain class creates distinct instances (control)");

console.log("\nAll singleton checks completed.");
```

### Expected output

```
PASS: getInstance returns same instance
PASS: first args win (host)
PASS: first args win (port)
PASS: instance is frozen
PASS: new returns existing instance
PASS: Cache singleton identity
PASS: shared state across references
PASS: EventBus is a single object
PASS: emit reaches listener registered via other ref
PASS: plain class creates distinct instances (control)

All singleton checks completed.
```

---

## Important Caveats

| Concern | Guidance |
|---|---|
| **Testing** | Singletons create hidden global state → hard to test in isolation. Always provide a `_resetForTesting()` escape hatch (as shown) or prefer **dependency injection**. |
| **Subclassing** | `class Child extends Singleton` breaks the pattern because `Child.instance !== Parent.instance`. Avoid subclassing singletons. |
| **Async init** | If construction is async, cache a **Promise**, not the instance: `static getInstance() { if (!this._p) this._p = createAsync(); return this._p; }`. |
| **Module caching** | In Node, `require()`/`import` caches by resolved path. Two different file paths to the "same" module ⇒ two instances. |
| **Worker threads / iframes** | Each thread/realm has its own module cache ⇒ separate singletons. Use `SharedArrayBuffer` or message passing if you need cross-realm uniqueness. |
| **Mocking** | Prefer injecting the singleton rather than importing it directly; makes unit tests deterministic. |

---

## When NOT to use a Singleton

- When you need **multiple configurations** (e.g., two DB connections to different hosts). Use a **factory** or **registry** instead.
- When **testability** matters more than convenience — DI containers (e.g., InversifyJS, NestJS providers) are the modern replacement.

---

## TL;DR

- **Class-based:** `static getInstance()` with a private `_instance` field.
- **Functional:** module-level closure with `getInstance`.
- **Modern:** just use an ES module — its cached scope *is* the singleton.
- Always add a test-only reset hook, and prefer dependency injection for anything non-trivial.

## Singleton pattern in JavaScript

### Idea

A **singleton** guarantees that a class/constructor has **at most one instance** and provides a **global point of access** to it. Instead of `new Foo()` creating a fresh object every time, callers get the same object back.

In JavaScript there are two broad families:

1. **Module-level singleton** — export one instance from a module; the module cache itself is the “single instance” mechanism.
2. **Class-based singleton** — the class controls instantiation via a static property and a guard in the constructor.

Both achieve the same goal; the module approach is usually preferred in modern JS.

---

### 1. Module-level singleton (idiomatic, recommended)

ES modules are evaluated once and cached, so exporting an instance naturally gives you a singleton.

```js
// config.js
const config = {
  apiBase: 'https://api.example.com',
  timeout: 5000,
};

export default config;
```

```js
// anywhere
import config from './config.js';
config.timeout = 10000; // same object seen by all importers
```

**Variant with lazy initialization / private state:**

```js
// logger.js
let instance = null;

function createLogger() {
  const logs = [];
  return {
    log(msg) { logs.push({ msg, at: Date.now() }); },
    all() { return [...logs]; },
  };
}

export function getLogger() {
  if (!instance) instance = createLogger();
  return instance;
}
```

---

### 2. Class-based singleton (traditional)

Guard the constructor so repeated `new` returns the existing instance.

```js
class Database {
  static #instance = null;

  constructor(url) {
    if (Database.#instance) {
      return Database.#instance;
    }
    this.url = url;
    this.connected = false;
    Database.#instance = this;
  }

  connect() {
    if (!this.connected) {
      console.log('connecting to', this.url);
      this.connected = true;
    }
  }
}

const a = new Database('postgres://localhost');
const b = new Database('postgres://other'); // ignored, returns `a`
console.log(a === b); // true
a.connect();
b.connect();          // no-op, already connected
```

Notes:
- Returning `this` from a constructor is allowed in JS; returning an existing instance is fine.
- Private static field `#instance` (ES2022) hides the storage. Older code uses a closure or a public static property.
- Subclassing a singleton is awkward; avoid it.

**Lazy version (don’t create until first use):**

```js
class Cache {
  static #instance = null;

  static getInstance() {
    if (!this.#instance) this.#instance = new Cache();
    return this.#instance;
  }

  constructor() {
    if (Cache.#instance) throw new Error('Use Cache.getInstance()');
    this.store = new Map();
  }
}
```

Here the constructor is private-by-convention; callers must use `getInstance()`.

---

### 3. IIFE / closure singleton (pre-ES-modules style)

```js
const Singleton = (function () {
  let instance;

  function init() {
    return { value: Math.random() };
  }

  return {
    getInstance() {
      if (!instance) instance = init();
      return instance;
    },
  };
})();

const x = Singleton.getInstance();
const y = Singleton.getInstance();
console.log(x === y); // true
```

Useful in browsers without a bundler, or when you want to hide everything behind a namespace.

---

### Typical use cases

| Use case | Why singleton fits |
|---|---|
| **Configuration** | One source of truth for env/app settings. |
| **Logger** | Centralized log buffer / transport; avoid duplicate handlers. |
| **DB connection / pool** | Reuse a connection; prevent connection storms. |
| **Cache / store** | Shared in-memory state (e.g., LRU cache, auth token). |
| **Event bus / pub-sub** | Single dispatcher for the app. |
| **Feature flags / i18n** | One loaded bundle of flags/translations. |

---

### Caveats and best practices

1. **Testing**: Singletons carry state across tests. Prefer injecting the instance, or expose a `reset()` method for tests only.
   ```js
   // test helper
   Database.__resetForTests?.();
   ```

2. **Global mutable state**: A singleton is essentially global state. Keep it small and immutable-ish; otherwise debugging becomes hard.

3. **Module cache != singleton across bundles**: If the same module is bundled twice (e.g., two chunks), you get two instances. For true process-wide singletons in Node, use a global symbol or attach to `globalThis`.

   ```js
   // node-wide singleton
   const KEY = Symbol.for('myapp.db');
   if (!globalThis[KEY]) globalThis[KEY] = new Database();
   export const db = globalThis[KEY];
   ```

4. **Async initialization**: If creation is async (e.g., opening a DB), store a promise, not the instance.

   ```js
   let promise = null;
   export function getDb() {
     if (!promise) promise = openDb().then(db => db);
     return promise;
   }
   ```

5. **Don’t overuse**: Dependency injection usually beats singletons for testability. Use singletons for truly process-wide resources (config, logger, connection pool), not for domain objects.

---

### Quick decision guide

- **Modern app with a bundler?** → Export an instance from a module.
- **Need lazy init / guard `new`?** → Class with static `#instance` + `getInstance()`.
- **Node service with multiple bundles/workers?** → `Symbol.for` on `globalThis`.
- **Legacy / no modules?** → IIFE exposing `getInstance`.

That covers the idea, syntax variants, use cases, and the main pitfalls.

A common JavaScript singleton mistake is to store the shared instance as an **instance property** instead of a **static property**, and then check it inside a static method. Because static methods are called on the class, `this` refers to the class, not an object instance, so the check never sees the previously created instance.

### Mistaken version

```js
class Logger {
  constructor(prefix = "[app]") {
    this.prefix = prefix;
    this.instance = null; // Mistake: this is per-object state, not class state
  }

  static getInstance() {
    // `this` is the Logger class here, not an instance.
    // `this.instance` is therefore always undefined.
    if (!this.instance) {
      this.instance = new Logger();
    }
    return this.instance;
  }

  log(message) {
    console.log(this.prefix, message);
  }
}

const a = Logger.getInstance();
const b = Logger.getInstance();

console.log(a === b); // false
```

### What went wrong

1. `this.instance = null` runs in the constructor, so every new `Logger` gets its own `instance` property.
2. `getInstance()` is static, so `this` is `Logger`, not one of those objects.
3. `Logger.instance` is never assigned, so every call creates a brand-new `Logger`.
4. Direct construction with `new Logger()` is also allowed, so callers can bypass the singleton entirely.

### Corrected version

```js
class Logger {
  static instance = null;

  constructor(prefix = "[app]") {
    // Prevent direct construction after the singleton exists.
    if (Logger.instance) {
      throw new Error("Logger already exists. Use Logger.getInstance().");
    }

    this.prefix = prefix;
    Logger.instance = this;
  }

  static getInstance(prefix = "[app]") {
    if (!Logger.instance) {
      new Logger(prefix);
    }
    return Logger.instance;
  }

  log(message) {
    console.log(this.prefix, message);
  }
}

const a = Logger.getInstance();
const b = Logger.getInstance();

console.log(a === b); // true

// Direct construction is now blocked once the instance exists.
try {
  new Logger("[other]");
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message);
}
```

### Why the corrected version works

- `static instance = null` creates one shared slot on the class itself, not on individual objects.
- `getInstance()` checks and initializes that shared slot.
- The constructor stores `this` into `Logger.instance`, so the single instance is tracked globally for the class.
- The constructor guard prevents accidental or intentional `new Logger()` calls from creating a second instance after the singleton has been created.

### Additional notes

- In JavaScript, single-threaded execution means no mutex is needed for this simple check, but async initialization can still create ordering issues if `getInstance()` is called before async setup completes.
- If the instance should be private, use a private static field:

```js
class Logger {
  static #instance = null;

  constructor(prefix = "[app]") {
    if (Logger.#instance) {
      throw new Error("Logger already exists. Use Logger.getInstance().");
    }
    this.prefix = prefix;
    Logger.#instance = this;
  }

  static getInstance(prefix = "[app]") {
    if (!Logger.#instance) {
      new Logger(prefix);
    }
    return Logger.#instance;
  }

  log(message) {
    console.log(this.prefix, message);
  }
}
```

Singletons in JavaScript are tricky because the language already has module-level singletons, first-class functions, prototypal inheritance, and an async event loop. Many “singleton bugs” come from reimplementing something the module system already provides, or from assuming that “one instance” means “one global mutable object.”

Below is a practical baseline followed by the most common pitfalls.

## A reasonable baseline

```js
// counterSingleton.js
let instance = null;

export function getCounter() {
  if (!instance) {
    instance = {
      count: 0,
      increment() {
        this.count += 1;
        return this.count;
      },
    };
  }
  return instance;
}
```

This is simple, but it still has most of the pitfalls below if used carelessly.

---

## 1. Reinventing what modules already do

JavaScript modules are cached by their resolved path. A module-level object is already a singleton in that realm.

**Pitfall:** adding IIFE/class machinery when a module export is enough, or mixing both and creating two “singletons.”

```js
// overengineered.js
let instance = null;
export function getInstance() {
  if (!instance) instance = { value: 42 };
  return instance;
}

// consumer.js
import { getInstance } from "./overengineered.js";
const a = getInstance();
const b = getInstance();
console.log(a === b); // true, but the module itself could just export the object
```

**Better:**

```js
// settings.js
export const settings = { theme: "light" };

// a.js
import { settings } from "./settings.js";
settings.theme = "dark";

// b.js
import { settings } from "./settings.js";
console.log(settings.theme); // "dark"
```

**Avoid:** stateful singletons when a module-level `const` is enough. Keep the module as the single owner of shared state.

---

## 2. Mutable shared state with no ownership

A singleton object is often treated as a global variable. Any code can mutate it, making data flow hard to trace.

```js
export const appState = { user: null, cart: [] };

// checkout.js
appState.cart.push(item);

// profile.js
appState.user = loggedInUser;

// tests or reset code
appState.cart.length = 0; // mutates everyone's view
```

**Problem:** there is no invariant boundary. A bug in one module can corrupt state used by another.

**Better approach:** expose operations, not raw mutation.

```js
let user = null;
const cart = [];

export function setUser(nextUser) { user = nextUser; }
export function getUser() { return user; }
export function addToCart(item) { cart.push(item); }
export function getCart() { return cart.slice(); } // defensive copy
```

---

## 3. Test leakage and hidden global state

Singletons persist for the lifetime of the module graph. Tests run in the same process/module cache, so state leaks between tests.

```js
// counterSingleton.js
let count = 0;
export function increment() { return ++count; }

// counter.test.js
test("first", () => {
  expect(increment()).toBe(1);
});

test("second", () => {
  expect(increment()).toBe(1); // fails: it is 2
});
```

Common bad fix is exporting a reset function:

```js
export function __resetForTestsOnly() { count = 0; }
```

That keeps tests passing but exposes internal mutation to production code.

**Better:** make instances injectable in tests, or use dependency injection rather than a hard singleton. If a singleton is unavoidable, isolate modules per test with a fresh module graph or test environment reset.

---

## 4. Import-time side effects and circular dependencies

Creating the singleton at module top level runs code during import. This can trigger side effects in unpredictable order and break circular imports.

```js
// database.js
import { config } from "./config.js";

class Database {
  constructor() {
    this.conn = createConnection(config.url); // runs on import
  }
}

export default new Database();

// userRepository.js
import db from "./database.js"; // may be partially initialized
```

**Pitfall:** `database.js` and `config.js` can deadlock or export `undefined` if they import each other.

**Better:** lazy initialization.

```js
let db = null;

export async function getDatabase() {
  if (!db) {
    const { config } = await import("./config.js");
    db = createConnection(config.url);
  }
  return db;
}
```

Lazy init also avoids paying startup costs for code paths that never use the singleton.

---

## 5. `new` bypassing the singleton guard

Class-based singletons often rely on a constructor check. This is fragile and easy to bypass.

```js
class Logger {
  constructor() {
    if (Logger.instance) {
      return Logger.instance;
    }
    Logger.instance = this;
    this.logs = [];
  }
}

const a = new Logger();
const b = new Logger();
console.log(a === b); // true

// But:
const c = Object.create(Logger.prototype); // bypasses constructor
console.log(c === a); // false

// And subclassing is broken:
class ChildLogger extends Logger {}
const child = new ChildLogger();
console.log(child instanceof ChildLogger); // true-ish, but static instance is shared
```

**Better:** use a factory, not `new`.

```js
let logger = null;

export function getLogger() {
  if (!logger) logger = { logs: [] };
  return logger;
}
```

If you want a class API, hide construction entirely:

```js
class Logger {
  constructor() {
    throw new Error("Use Logger.getInstance()");
  }
  static getInstance() {
    if (!this.instance) this.instance = Object.create(this.prototype);
    return this.instance;
  }
}
```

Even then, factory functions are usually clearer in JavaScript.

---

## 6. Losing `this` when extracting methods

Singleton methods defined as normal methods lose their receiver when passed around.

```js
const counter = {
  count: 0,
  increment() {
    this.count += 1;
    return this.count;
  },
};

const inc = counter.increment;
inc(); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined

setTimeout(counter.increment, 100); // `this` is wrong or undefined
```

**Fix:** bind methods or use arrow functions at creation time.

```js
function createCounter() {
  const state = { count: 0 };
  return {
    increment: () => {
      state.count += 1;
      return state.count;
    },
    getCount: () => state.count,
  };
}

let instance = null;
export function getCounter() {
  if (!instance) instance = createCounter();
  return instance;
}
```

Arrow functions capture the intended closure and cannot be rebound.

---

## 7. Async initialization races

JavaScript is single-threaded, but `await` yields control. A naive async singleton can create multiple instances.

```js
let client = null;

export async function getClient() {
  if (!client) {
    client = await createDbClient(); // yields here
  }
  return client;
}

// Two calls close together:
const p1 = getClient();
const p2 = getClient();
```

While `p1` is awaiting, `p2` may see `client` still `null` and start another creation.

**Fix:** cache the promise, not only the resolved value.

```js
let clientPromise = null;

export function getClient() {
  if (!clientPromise) {
    clientPromise = createDbClient();
  }
  return clientPromise;
}

// Usage:
const client = await getClient();
```

Now all callers await the same promise. If creation fails, you may also want to reset `clientPromise` so the next call can retry.

---

## 8. Multiple module instances / multiple realms

“Singleton” usually means one per JavaScript realm or bundle graph, not one universal object.

**Pitfall A: duplicate dependencies.**

```txt
app
├── foo@1.0.0
│   └── singleton-lib@1.0.0
└── bar@2.0.0
    └── singleton-lib@1.1.0
```

If two versions are installed, each has its own module cache and its own singleton instance.

**Pitfall B: multiple realms.**

```js
// main window
import { store } from "./store.js";
console.log(store.id);

// iframe with separately loaded bundle
import { store } from "./store.js"; // different module graph
console.log(store.id); // different object
```

Workers, iframes, and embedded apps each have separate global/module systems. A singleton is not process-global in the browser unless you explicitly put it on a shared object such as `globalThis` or use `BroadcastChannel`/`SharedWorker`.

**Avoid:** assuming identity across packages, windows, or workers. Document the scope: “one per realm” or “one per bundle.”

---

## 9. Cloning and spreading break identity

Singletons are identified by reference equality. Copying them creates a different object that is no longer “the singleton.”

```js
const singleton = getCounter();

const copy1 = { ...singleton };
const copy2 = Object.assign({}, singleton);
const copy3 = structuredClone(singleton);

console.log(copy1 === singleton); // false
console.log(copy2 === singleton); // false
console.log(copy3 === singleton); // false
```

Even if the copy has the same data, consumers that compare instances with `===` will treat it as a different singleton.

Also, extracting and destructuring methods can detach `this`:

```js
const { increment } = getCounter();
increment(); // often fails
```

**Avoid:** do not clone singletons. Pass the reference directly, or clone only plain data, not the singleton object itself.

---

## 10. Lifecycle and memory leaks

A singleton lives until the module is unloaded. If it stores references to DOM nodes, closures, timers, or large caches, those cannot be garbage collected.

```js
const singleton = {
  elements: [],
  listeners: new Map(),
  register(el) {
    this.elements.push(el); // retained forever
    const handler = () => console.log(el.textContent);
    el.addEventListener("click", handler);
    this.listeners.set(el, handler);
  },
};
```

Even after the DOM node is removed from the document, the singleton still references it, so it cannot be collected.

**Better:** provide cleanup and avoid retaining UI-specific objects.

```js
const registry = new Map();

export const eventBus = {
  on(type, handler) { /* ... */ },
  off(type, handler) { /* ... */ },
  dispose() {
    // clear timers, listeners, caches
  },
};
```

For app-wide objects, call cleanup during teardown, route changes, or tests.

---

## 11. Subclassing and inheritance problems

Singleton state is often stored on the class itself. Subclasses then share or corrupt that state.

```js
class Service {
  static instance = null;

  static getInstance() {
    if (!this.instance) this.instance = new this();
    return this.instance;
  }
}

class DatabaseService extends Service {}
class CacheService extends Service {}

const db = DatabaseService.getInstance();
const cache = CacheService.getInstance();

console.log(db === cache); // true! both use the same base `instance`
```

**Fix:** do not make inheritance the extension mechanism. Prefer composition or separate factory functions.

```js
function createService(name) {
  return { name, connect() {} };
}

const databaseService = createService("database");
const cacheService = createService("cache");
```

---

## 12. Using the singleton as a service locator

Singletons often become hidden dependencies, which makes code hard to test and reason about.

```js
function processPayment(order) {
  const db = Database.getInstance();      // hidden dependency
  const logger = Logger.getInstance();    // hidden dependency
  const metrics = Metrics.getInstance();  // hidden dependency

  db.save(order);
  logger.info("paid");
  metrics.track("payment");
}
```

This function looks pure-ish but depends on global mutable services. In tests, you must monkeypatch singletons.

**Better:** inject dependencies.

```js
function processPayment(order, { db, logger, metrics }) {
  db.save(order);
  logger.info("paid");
  metrics.track("payment");
}
```

You can still have default singleton instances at the application boundary, but business logic should receive its collaborators explicitly.

---

## 13. Accidental mutation of “constant” singletons

Even if the instance reference never changes, its contents can change. Object.freeze helps for truly constant configuration.

```js
export const CONFIG = { apiUrl: "https://api.example.com" };
CONFIG.apiUrl = "http://localhost"; // accidentally mutated

// Freeze top level:
export const SAFE_CONFIG = Object.freeze({ apiUrl: "https://api.example.com" });
SAFE_CONFIG.apiUrl = "x"; // ignored in non-strict, throws in strict mode

// But nested objects still mutable:
const cfg = Object.freeze({ db: { host: "localhost" } });
cfg.db.host = "evil"; // works
```

For deep immutability, use a recursive freeze or store only primitive config.

---

## Summary checklist

- Prefer module exports for simple shared state.
- Keep mutable state behind functions.
- Lazy-initialize expensive or side-effecting singletons.
- Cache promises for async initialization.
- Do not rely on `new` for singletons.
- Bind methods or use closures.
- Never assume one instance across packages, workers, or realms.
- Do not clone singletons or destructure their methods carelessly.
- Provide cleanup to avoid leaks.
- Avoid inheritance-based singleton registries.
- Keep dependencies explicit instead of using singletons as service locators.

If you want, I can also show a robust, testable singleton factory with reset hooks scoped to tests, plus a dependency-injection alternative.

## Exercise

Implement a **Logger singleton** in JavaScript with these requirements:

1. `Logger.getInstance(name)` returns the **same object** every time.
2. You cannot create a second instance with `new Logger()` — it must throw.
3. The instance stores a `name` and exposes `log(level, message)`.
4. It must be **testable**: tests need a way to reset the instance between cases without leaking state.
5. Show that two modules importing it share the same instance.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1 — Understand the singleton in JS

In JavaScript, a singleton usually means **one instance per runtime/module graph**. Because JS is single-threaded, we don’t need mutexes; we just need to guard construction and cache the instance.

There are two common idioms:

- **Class-based**: private static instance + private constructor guard.
- **Module-based**: export one already-created object (ESM module cache guarantees one instance).

We’ll implement the class version because it’s the most reusable and testable, then note the module version.

### Step 2 — First (naive) attempt

```js
class Logger {
  constructor(name = 'app') {
    this.name = name;
  }
  static getInstance(name) {
    if (!Logger.instance) {
      Logger.instance = new Logger(name);
    }
    return Logger.instance;
  }
}
```

Problems:

- `new Logger()` still works and creates extra instances.
- `instance` is public and mutable.
- Tests can’t easily reset state.

### Step 3 — Add construction guard with private fields

Use ECMAScript private static fields (`#`) so external code cannot touch the cache.

```js
class Logger {
  static #instance = null;

  #name;

  constructor(name = 'app') {
    if (Logger.#instance) {
      throw new Error('Logger is a singleton. Use Logger.getInstance().');
    }
    this.#name = name;
    Logger.#instance = this;
  }

  static getInstance(name = 'app') {
    if (!Logger.#instance) {
      // First call: constructor sees #instance === null and is allowed.
      new Logger(name);
    }
    return Logger.#instance;
  }
}
```

Important detail: `getInstance` creates the instance but ignores the returned object from `new`, then reads the cached `#instance`. The constructor itself stores `this` into the static field, so there’s no race in single-threaded JS.

### Step 4 — Add logging behavior

```js
log(level, message) {
  const time = new Date().toISOString();
  console.log(`[${time}] [${this.#name}] ${level.toUpperCase()}: ${message}`);
}
```

### Step 5 — Make it testable (reset hook)

Singletons are hard to test because state persists. Add a **test-only** reset method. In production you simply never call it.

```js
static resetForTesting() {
  Logger.#instance = null;
}
```

If you want to be stricter, you can gate it behind `process.env.NODE_ENV === 'test'`, but for a small exercise a clearly-named method is enough.

### Step 6 — Prevent subclassing surprises (optional hardening)

If someone extends `Logger`, `instanceof` checks and static fields can leak. For most apps this isn’t needed, but you can seal the class:

```js
Object.freeze(Logger);
Object.freeze(Logger.prototype);
```

### Step 7 — Module-level singleton alternative (ESM)

If you don’t need lazy creation or reset, the simplest singleton is just a module:

```js
// logger.mjs
class Logger { /* ... */ }
export const logger = new Logger('app');
```

Because ESM caches modules, every importer gets the same object. This is often preferred in modern JS.

---

## Final solution

```js
// logger.js
class Logger {
  static #instance = null;

  #name;

  constructor(name = 'app') {
    if (Logger.#instance) {
      throw new Error('Logger is a singleton. Use Logger.getInstance().');
    }
    this.#name = name;
    Logger.#instance = this;
  }

  static getInstance(name = 'app') {
    if (!Logger.#instance) {
      new Logger(name);
    }
    return Logger.#instance;
  }

  // Test-only escape hatch.
  static resetForTesting() {
    Logger.#instance = null;
  }

  get name() {
    return this.#name;
  }

  log(level, message) {
    const time = new Date().toISOString();
    console.log(`[${time}] [${this.#name}] ${level.toUpperCase()}: ${message}`);
  }
}

export { Logger };
```

### Usage

```js
// a.js
import { Logger } from './logger.js';
const a = Logger.getInstance('main');
a.log('info', 'hello from A');

// b.js
import { Logger } from './logger.js';
const b = Logger.getInstance(); // name ignored after first creation
console.log(a === b); // true
b.log('warn', 'hello from B');
```

### Tests (Jest-style)

```js
import { Logger } from './logger.js';

afterEach(() => Logger.resetForTesting());

test('returns the same instance', () => {
  const x = Logger.getInstance('x');
  const y = Logger.getInstance('y');
  expect(x).toBe(y);
  expect(y.name).toBe('x'); // first name wins
});

test('cannot be instantiated directly twice', () => {
  Logger.getInstance();
  expect(() => new Logger()).toThrow(/singleton/i);
});

test('logs with the configured name', () => {
  const spy = jest.spyOn(console, 'log').mockImplementation(() => {});
  Logger.getInstance('svc').log('info', 'hi');
  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.stringContaining('[svc] INFO: hi'));
  spy.mockRestore();
});
```

### Key takeaways

- Use a **private static field** for the cache so it can’t be overwritten externally.
- Guard the **constructor**, not just `getInstance`, so `new` can’t bypass the singleton.
- Provide a **reset hook for tests** but keep it clearly marked as test-only.
- In modern ESM, a module-level `export const instance = new X()` is often simpler and equally safe.

## Singleton Pattern in JavaScript

### What it is
A **Singleton** ensures a class has **only one instance** and provides a **global access point** to it. In JavaScript, because the runtime is single‑threaded and modules are cached, singletons are often implemented via closures or module scope rather than classical OOP.

### When to use / avoid
- **Good for:** configuration objects, connection pools, loggers, caches, feature flags.
- **Avoid when:** you need multiple instances, testability is critical (singletons introduce global state), or you can pass dependencies explicitly (dependency injection is usually cleaner).

### Core implementations

#### 1. Classic class‑based (lazy)
```js
class Singleton {
  constructor() {
    if (Singleton.instance) {
      return Singleton.instance;          // return existing instance
    }
    // initialize state once
    this.createdAt = new Date().toISOString();
    Singleton.instance = this;             // cache it
  }

  // convenience accessor
  static getInstance() {
    if (!Singleton.instance) {
      Singleton.instance = new Singleton();
    }
    return Singleton.instance;
  }
}

// Usage
const a = new Singleton();
const b = Singleton.getInstance();
console.log(a === b); // true
```

#### 2. Closure / module pattern (idiomatic JS)
```js
const Singleton = (() => {
  let instance = null;                    // private state in closure

  function createInstance() {
    return {
      createdAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      // ... your methods
      getData() { return 'shared data'; }
    };
  }

  return {
    getInstance() {
      if (!instance) instance = createInstance();
      return instance;
    }
  };
})();

// Usage
const x = Singleton.getInstance();
const y = Singleton.getInstance();
console.log(x === y); // true
```

#### 3. ES module singleton (simplest, recommended)
```js
// config.js
const config = {
  apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com',
  timeout: 5000,
  // methods...
  get(key) { return this[key]; }
};

export default config;   // module is evaluated once → single instance
```
```js
// main.js
import config from './config.js';
// every importer gets the same object reference
```

### Key points for an interview
1. **Lazy vs eager:** Classic pattern lazily creates; module pattern eagerly creates on first import.
2. **Thread safety:** JS is single‑threaded, so no locks needed. In async code, ensure initialization is not awaited in a racy way—use a promise cache if async init is required.
3. **Reset for tests:** Provide a `resetInstance()` (or re‑import module) to avoid state leakage between tests.
4. **Prefer modules:** In modern JS, an ES module export is usually the cleanest singleton.

### Async‑safe variant (promise cache)
```js
class AsyncSingleton {
  static #instancePromise = null;

  static getInstance() {
    if (!this.#instancePromise) {
      this.#instancePromise = (async () => {
        const inst = Object.create(this.prototype);
        await inst.init();               // async setup
        return inst;
      })();
    }
    return this.#instancePromise;
  }
}
```

### Summary
- Use **closure/module** for encapsulation.
- Use **ES modules** for the simplest, most idiomatic singletons.
- Keep a **reset hook** for testing.
- Avoid overusing singletons—prefer explicit dependency injection when possible.

Below are the most common and dangerous edge cases when implementing a singleton in JavaScript, with idiomatic patterns to handle them.

---

## 1. Direct `new` bypasses the cache

If callers can call `new Singleton()`, you can end up with multiple instances or partially initialized objects.

**Fix:** force construction through a factory and make the constructor reject direct calls.

```js
class Singleton {
  static #token = Symbol('singleton-token');
  static #instance = null;

  constructor(token) {
    if (token !== Singleton.#token) {
      throw new Error('Use Singleton.getInstance()');
    }
    this.createdAt = Date.now();
  }

  static getInstance() {
    if (!this.#instance) {
      this.#instance = new this(this.#token);
    }
    return this.#instance;
  }
}

const a = Singleton.getInstance();
const b = Singleton.getInstance();
console.log(a === b); // true

// new Singleton(); // throws
```

---

## 2. Subclasses break the invariant

A naive module-level `let instance` is shared by the base class and subclasses, causing a subclass instance to be returned for the base (or vice versa).

**Fix:** key the cache by constructor.

```js
const cache = new Map();

class BaseSingleton {
  constructor() {
    const ctor = this.constructor;
    if (cache.has(ctor)) return cache.get(ctor);

    // reserve before init to survive re-entrant construction
    cache.set(ctor, this);

    try {
      this.init();
    } catch (e) {
      cache.delete(ctor);
      throw e;
    }
  }

  init() {}
}

class A extends BaseSingleton {}
class B extends BaseSingleton {}

console.log(new A() === new A()); // true
console.log(new B() === new B()); // true
console.log(new A() === new B()); // false
```

---

## 3. Async initialization races

If creation is async (DB, network), concurrent callers can create multiple connections.

**Fix:** cache the promise, not the value, and reset it on failure.

```js
let connectionPromise = null;

export async function getDb() {
  if (!connectionPromise) {
    connectionPromise = connectToDatabase().catch(err => {
      connectionPromise = null; // allow retry
      throw err;
    });
  }
  return connectionPromise;
}

// 100 concurrent calls -> one connection
await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: 100 }, () => getDb()));
```

If you need sync access after resolution, store the resolved instance separately:

```js
let instance = null;
let promise = null;

export async function getInstance() {
  if (instance) return instance;
  if (!promise) {
    promise = create().then(x => (instance = x));
  }
  return promise;
}
```

---

## 4. Constructor throws leave a poisoned cache

If you cache `this` before initialization completes, a thrown error can leave a half-built singleton.

**Fix:** reserve, initialize, and delete on error (shown in example 2). For async, reset the promise (example 3).

---

## 5. `this` is lost when methods are detached

```js
const s = Singleton.getInstance();
const fn = s.doWork; // `this` is undefined in strict mode
fn(); // bug
```

**Fix:** bind in the constructor or use arrow-function class fields.

```js
class Singleton {
  constructor(token) {
    if (token !== Singleton.#token) throw new Error('use factory');
    this.doWork = this.doWork.bind(this);
  }

  doWork() {
    return this.value;
  }
}
```

Or:

```js
class Singleton {
  doWork = () => this.value;
}
```

---

## 6. Module duplication across bundles / paths

CommonJS caches by resolved file path. Symlinks, monorepo hoisting, or bundlers can evaluate the module twice, creating two singletons.

**Fix:** anchor the instance on the global symbol registry.

```js
const KEY = Symbol.for('myapp.configSingleton');

function create() {
  return { env: process.env.NODE_ENV };
}

if (!globalThis[KEY]) {
  globalThis[KEY] = create();
}

export default globalThis[KEY];
```

Caveats: this pollutes global scope; use only when cross-bundle identity is required. `Symbol.for` is the safest key because it is collision-resistant.

---

## 7. Cross-realm / iframe / worker boundaries

Each realm has its own module cache and global object, so a singleton is not automatically shared between an iframe and the main page, or between Worker threads.

**Fixes:**

- For same-origin iframes: use `globalThis` + `Symbol.for` (still separate globals, so actually not shared; use `window.parent` or `BroadcastChannel`).
- For workers: use `SharedWorker` or pass state via messages.
- For identity checks across realms, do not rely on `instanceof`; use a brand.

```js
function isMySingleton(x) {
  return !!x && x.__brand === 'MySingleton';
}
```

---

## 8. Testing state leaks between tests

Singletons persist across tests, causing order-dependent failures.

**Fix:** expose an explicit reset hook, and prefer dependency injection in tests.

```js
// singleton.js
let instance = null;

export function getInstance() {
  if (!instance) instance = new RealService();
  return instance;
}

export function __resetForTests() {
  instance = null;
}

// test.js
import { __resetForTests } from './singleton.js';
afterEach(() => __resetForTests());
```

Even better: allow injecting a fake.

```js
export function getInstance(factory = () => new RealService()) {
  if (!instance) instance = factory();
  return instance;
}
```

---

## 9. Accidental shared mutable state

A singleton is one object; mutating it affects every consumer.

**Fix:** freeze the instance and keep mutable state private.

```js
class Config {
  #data = new Map();

  get(key) { return this.#data.get(key); }
  set(key, value) { this.#data.set(key, value); }
}

const instance = new Config();
Object.freeze(instance); // prevents adding/removing properties
export default instance;
```

Note: `Object.freeze` is shallow. If you store nested objects, freeze them too, or use a Proxy.

---

## 10. Cloning / serialization breaks identity

`structuredClone`, `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))`, or spread creates a new object that is not the singleton.

```js
const copy = structuredClone(singleton);
console.log(copy === singleton); // false
```

**Fix:** document that identity must be checked with `===`. If you control cloning, make clones return the original:

```js
singleton.clone = () => singleton;
```

But you cannot intercept `structuredClone`. For cross-context transfer, re-resolve via the factory on the other side rather than transferring the instance.

---

## 11. Hot Module Replacement (HMR)

In dev with Vite/Webpack HMR, the module is re-evaluated, creating a new singleton while old references still point to the previous one.

**Fix:** dispose old state on HMR.

```js
let instance = null;

export function get() {
  if (!instance) instance = new Service();
  return instance;
}

if (import.meta.hot) {
  import.meta.hot.accept();
  import.meta.hot.dispose(() => {
    instance = null;
  });
}
```

---

## 12. Garbage collection pitfalls

- Do not store the singleton in a `WeakMap` keyed by a temporary object; it may be collected.
- Do not use `WeakRef` for the instance; that defeats the singleton guarantee.
- A module-level `const` is the simplest strong root.

```js
// BAD: can be collected
const instances = new WeakMap();
instances.set({}, new Service());

// GOOD: strong root
const instance = new Service();
export default instance;
```

---

## 13. Proxy / reflection bypass

`Object.create(Singleton.prototype)` creates an object with the right prototype but not the cached instance. `Reflect.construct` can also bypass guards.

**Fix:** brand-check instances and validate in methods.

```js
class Singleton {
  constructor(token) {
    if (token !== Singleton.#token) throw new Error('use factory');
    this.__brand = 'Singleton';
  }

  work() {
    if (this.__brand !== 'Singleton') throw new Error('invalid instance');
  }
}
```

---

## 14. Constructor called without `new`

In non-strict code, `Singleton()` sets `this` to the global object.

**Fix:** use `new.target`.

```js
function Singleton() {
  if (!new.target) return Singleton.getInstance();
  if (Singleton.instance) return Singleton.instance;
  Singleton.instance = this;
}
```

---

## 15. Circular dependencies

If module A’s singleton constructor imports module B, and B imports A, you can get a partially initialized export.

**Fix:** lazy-require inside the factory, not at module top level.

```js
export function getInstance() {
  // require lazily to break the cycle
  const { Other } = require('./other.js');
  if (!instance) instance = new Service(new Other());
  return instance;
}
```

In ESM, use dynamic import:

```js
export async function getInstance() {
  const { Other } = await import('./other.js');
  if (!instance) instance = new Service(new Other());
  return instance;
}
```

---

## Summary checklist

| Edge case | Key technique |
|---|---|
| Direct `new` | Factory + token guard |
| Subclasses | Cache keyed by `this.constructor` |
| Async init | Cache promise, reset on error |
| Throwing constructor | Reserve + delete on error |
| Lost `this` | Bind or arrow fields |
| Module duplication | `globalThis` + `Symbol.for` |
| Cross-realm | Brand checks, not `instanceof` |
| Test leakage | Reset hook / DI |
| Shared mutation | Freeze + private state |
| Cloning | Document; re-resolve identity |
| HMR | `import.meta.hot.dispose` |
| GC | Strong module-level root |
| Proxy bypass | Brand validation |
| No `new` | `new.target` guard |
| Circular deps | Lazy/dynamic import |

A robust, production-ready singleton usually combines: **per-constructor caching, a token-guarded constructor, an async-safe factory, an explicit reset hook, and private state**. If you need cross-bundle or cross-realm identity, anchor it on `globalThis` with `Symbol.for`, but treat that as a last resort.

# Sliding Window in JavaScript

The **sliding window** technique maintains a contiguous sub-range (“window”) over a sequence and moves it efficiently by adding/removing elements at the ends, rather than recomputing from scratch. This turns many O(n²) brute-force scans into O(n).

## Two common variants

| Variant | When to use | Key idea |
|---|---|---|
| **Fixed size** | “window of exactly K” | Slide: subtract left element, add right element, update answer. |
| **Variable size** | “longest/shortest window satisfying a condition” | Expand right; when invalid, shrink from left until valid again. |

## Complete runnable example (Node.js)

Save as `sliding_window.js` and run with `node sliding_window.js`.

```js
'use strict';

/* =========================================================
   1) FIXED-SIZE WINDOW
   Problem: maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of size k.
   ========================================================= */

function maxSumFixedWindow(arr, k) {
  if (arr.length < k || k <= 0) return null;

  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += arr[i];

  let best = windowSum;

  for (let right = k; right < arr.length; right++) {
    const left = right - k;          // element leaving the window
    windowSum += arr[right] - arr[left];
    if (windowSum > best) best = windowSum;
  }

  return best;
}

/* =========================================================
   2) VARIABLE-SIZE WINDOW (shrink while invalid)
   Problem: longest substring without repeating characters.
   ========================================================= */

function longestUniqueSubstring(s) {
  const seen = new Map();   // char -> last index
  let left = 0;
  let bestLen = 0;
  let bestStart = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const ch = s[right];

    // If char seen inside current window, move left past it.
    if (seen.has(ch) && seen.get(ch) >= left) {
      left = seen.get(ch) + 1;
    }

    seen.set(ch, right);

    const windowLen = right - left + 1;
    if (windowLen > bestLen) {
      bestLen = windowLen;
      bestStart = left;
    }
  }

  return { length: bestLen, substring: s.slice(bestStart, bestStart + bestLen) };
}

/* =========================================================
   3) VARIABLE-SIZE WINDOW (shrink while valid, to find min)
   Problem: minimum length subarray whose sum >= target.
   (Positive numbers only; standard LeetCode 209 style.)
   ========================================================= */

function minSubarraySum(arr, target) {
  let left = 0;
  let sum = 0;
  let best = Infinity;

  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];

    while (sum >= target) {
      best = Math.min(best, right - left + 1);
      sum -= arr[left];
      left++;
    }
  }

  return best === Infinity ? 0 : best;
}

/* =========================================================
   4) GENERAL TEMPLATE (counts / frequency)
   Problem: longest substring with at most K distinct chars.
   ========================================================= */

function longestAtMostKDistinct(s, k) {
  if (k <= 0) return 0;
  const freq = new Map();
  let left = 0;
  let distinct = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const ch = s[right];
    const prev = freq.get(ch) || 0;
    if (prev === 0) distinct++;
    freq.set(ch, prev + 1);

    while (distinct > k) {
      const lc = s[left];
      const cnt = freq.get(lc);
      if (cnt === 1) {
        freq.delete(lc);
        distinct--;
      } else {
        freq.set(lc, cnt - 1);
      }
      left++;
    }

    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }

  return best;
}

/* =========================================================
   DEMO
   ========================================================= */

function main() {
  console.log('maxSumFixedWindow([2,1,5,1,3,2], 3) =', maxSumFixedWindow([2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2], 3)); // 9
  console.log('longestUniqueSubstring("abcabcbb") =', longestUniqueSubstring('abcabcbb')); // 3, "abc"
  console.log('minSubarraySum([2,3,1,2,4,3], 7) =', minSubarraySum([2, 3, 1, 2, 4, 3], 7)); // 2
  console.log('longestAtMostKDistinct("eceba", 2) =', longestAtMostKDistinct('eceba', 2)); // 3
}

main();
```

## Why it’s O(n)

- Each element is added to the window **once** when `right` advances.
- Each element is removed from the window **at most once** when `left` advances.
- The `while` loops look nested, but the total work of `left` increments across the whole run is bounded by `n`.

## General mental model

```text
left = 0
for right in 0..n-1:
    add element at right to window state
    while window is invalid (or while you want to shrink):
        remove element at left from window state
        left++
    update answer using window [left, right]
```

Swap “invalid” for “valid” depending on whether you’re maximizing or minimizing length, and maintain whatever aggregate (sum, counts, max, etc.) your condition needs.

## Tips for idiomatic JS

- Use `Map` for frequency/count when characters/keys can be arbitrary; use a plain object or array of size 26 for lowercase ASCII only.
- Prefer `const`/`let`, avoid reassigning loop indices incorrectly.
- Return structured values (`{ length, substring }`) when callers need the actual window, not just its size.
- Guard empty inputs and `k <= 0` early to avoid off-by-one errors.

## Sliding Window in JavaScript

The **sliding window** technique processes a contiguous subsequence (window) of an array/string by moving its start and end pointers, usually in one pass, instead of recomputing everything for every position. It typically turns an **O(n·k)** brute-force into **O(n)**.

---

### Core idea

1. **Expand** the right boundary to include new data.
2. **Shrink** the left boundary while some invariant is violated (e.g., duplicate char, sum too large).
3. **Record** the best answer whenever the window is valid.

Two common variants:

- **Fixed-size window**: size `k` is known; slide by `+1` on both ends.
- **Dynamic (variable-size) window**: grow until invalid, then shrink until valid again.

---

### Fixed-size window example

Max sum of `k` consecutive numbers:

```js
function maxSumSubarray(arr, k) {
  if (arr.length < k) return null;

  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += arr[i];

  let maxSum = windowSum;
  for (let right = k; right < arr.length; right++) {
    // add new right element, drop leftmost element
    windowSum += arr[right] - arr[right - k];
    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum);
  }
  return maxSum;
}

maxSumSubarray([2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2], 3); // 9  (5+1+3)
```

**Key trick:** update incrementally with `arr[right] - arr[right - k]` instead of summing the window again.

---

### Dynamic window example

Longest substring without repeating characters:

```js
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  const lastSeen = new Map(); // char -> index
  let left = 0;
  let maxLen = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const ch = s[right];

    // If char seen inside current window, jump left past it
    if (lastSeen.has(ch) && lastSeen.get(ch) >= left) {
      left = lastSeen.get(ch) + 1;
    }

    lastSeen.set(ch, right);
    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
  }
  return maxLen;
}

lengthOfLongestSubstring("abcabcbb"); // 3  ("abc")
```

**Invariant:** `[left, right]` contains no duplicate characters.

---

### Generic template (variable window)

```js
function slidingWindowTemplate(arr) {
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;          // or Infinity, depending on problem
  const state = new Map(); // or Set, counter, sum, etc.

  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    // 1. Expand: incorporate arr[right] into state
    state.set(arr[right], (state.get(arr[right]) || 0) + 1);

    // 2. Shrink while invalid
    while (isInvalid(state)) {
      state.set(arr[left], state.get(arr[left]) - 1);
      if (state.get(arr[left]) === 0) state.delete(arr[left]);
      left++;
    }

    // 3. Window [left, right] is valid here
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
```

For “minimum valid window,” update `best` inside the shrink loop instead:

```js
while (isValid(state)) {
  best = Math.min(best, right - left + 1);
  // remove arr[left], left++
}
```

---

### Typical use cases

| Problem | Window state | Shrink condition |
|---|---|---|
| Max sum of `k` elements | numeric sum | fixed size |
| Longest substring without repeats | char frequency map | any count > 1 |
| Minimum window substring | char counts vs target | all target chars satisfied |
| Longest subarray with sum ≤ K | running sum | sum > K |
| Longest repeating char replacement | char counts + max count | `(windowLen - maxCount) > k` |
| Anagram/permutation in string | frequency map | mismatch with pattern |

---

### Practical tips

- **Pointers are indices**, not elements; window length is `right - left + 1`.
- Prefer **`Map`/`Set`** for arbitrary keys; arrays of size 128/256 for ASCII speed.
- For substring problems, `s[right]` is O(1) in JS strings (indexed access).
- Watch off-by-one: when shrinking, update state **before** or **after** moving `left` consistently.
- Fixed window can be written as a special case of dynamic with `right - left + 1 === k`.

---

### Minimum window substring (classic dynamic example)

```js
function minWindow(s, t) {
  const need = new Map();
  for (const c of t) need.set(c, (need.get(c) || 0) + 1);

  let missing = t.length; // chars still needed
  let left = 0, bestStart = 0, bestLen = Infinity;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const c = s[right];
    if (need.has(c)) {
      if (need.get(c) > 0) missing--;
      need.set(c, need.get(c) - 1);
    }

    // Shrink while window is valid
    while (missing === 0) {
      if (right - left + 1 < bestLen) {
        bestLen = right - left + 1;
        bestStart = left;
      }
      const lc = s[left];
      if (need.has(lc)) {
        need.set(lc, need.get(lc) + 1);
        if (need.get(lc) > 0) missing++;
      }
      left++;
    }
  }
  return bestLen === Infinity ? "" : s.slice(bestStart, bestStart + bestLen);
}
```

This is O(|s| + |t|) and demonstrates the full expand/shrink/record cycle.

## Typical sliding-window mistake: off-by-one window size

A very common bug in variable-size sliding windows is using `right - left` instead of `right - left + 1` when the window bounds are **inclusive**.

### Mistaken version

```js
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    while (seen.has(s[right])) {
      seen.delete(s[left]);
      left++;
    }
    seen.add(s[right]);

    // BUG: window [left, right] has (right - left + 1) chars, not (right - left)
    best = Math.max(best, right - left);
  }

  return best;
}
```

### Corrected version

```js
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    // Shrink until the incoming char can be added without duplicates.
    while (seen.has(s[right])) {
      seen.delete(s[left]);
      left++;
    }

    // Invariant now holds: [left, right] has all unique chars.
    seen.add(s[right]);

    // Window is inclusive on both ends.
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }

  return best;
}
```

### Why the bug happened

In the loop above, both `left` and `right` are **inclusive** indices into the string. If the current window is just one character, e.g. `left === right === 0`, then:

- Correct size: `0 - 0 + 1 = 1`
- Buggy size: `0 - 0 = 0`

So every single-character window is measured as length `0`, and longer windows are measured one short. For `"abc"`, the buggy version returns `2` instead of `3`.

### Invariants to keep in mind

1. **Define your bounds explicitly.**  
   Here the window is `[left, right]`, inclusive. Its length is always:

   ```js
   right - left + 1
   ```

2. **Update the answer only after restoring the invariant.**  
   The `best = ...` line must come **after** the `while` loop and after adding `s[right]`. If you update it before removing duplicates, you may record an invalid window.

3. **Keep bookkeeping synchronized with pointer movement.**  
   Every time `left++` happens, the corresponding character must also be removed from `seen`. In the corrected code, `seen.delete(s[left])` happens before `left++`.

4. **Empty input is handled naturally.**  
   If `s` is `""`, the loop never runs and `best` stays `0`.

### Quick dry run of the corrected version

Input: `"abca"`

| right | char | left before | action | left after | seen | best |
|------:|------|------------:|--------|-----------:|------|-----:|
| 0 | a | 0 | add a | 0 | {a} | 1 |
| 1 | b | 0 | add b | 0 | {a,b} | 2 |
| 2 | c | 0 | add c | 0 | {a,b,c} | 3 |
| 3 | a | 0 | remove a, left=1; add a | 1 | {b,c,a} | 3 |

Final answer: `3`.

### Common related pitfalls in sliding windows

- **Fixed-size windows:** forgetting to subtract the element leaving the window before or while adding the new one.
- **Min/max windows:** updating the answer inside the shrink loop instead of after the window becomes valid.
- **Count maps:** decrementing a count but forgetting to delete zero-count keys, causing false positives in `has`.
- **Off-by-one in termination:** stopping at `n - 1` when the window can legitimately end at `n - 1`, or running past the end.

If you want, I can also show a **fixed-size** sliding-window example with a typical mistake such as updating the sum in the wrong order.

# Common Sliding-Window Pitfalls in JavaScript

Below are the most common mistakes, each with a buggy version and a corrected version.

---

## 1. Off-by-one at the boundaries

**Pitfall:** Mixing up inclusive/exclusive indices, especially when the window is defined as `[left, right]` vs `[left, right)`.

```js
// BUGGY: treats right as inclusive size calc, double counts
function maxSumSubarray(arr, k) {
  let sum = 0, best = -Infinity;
  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];
    if (right >= k) sum -= arr[right - k];   // off by one: should be right - k + 1? 
    if (right >= k - 1) best = Math.max(best, sum);
  }
  return best;
}
// For k=3, arr=[1,2,3,4]: at right=2 sum=6 ok; right=3 sum+=4=10, subtract arr[0]=1 ->9 (correct window 2+3+4=9)
// Actually above happens to work, but condition `right >= k` is fragile; many write `right > k`.

// CORRECT: explicit, consistent convention
function maxSumSubarrayFixed(arr, k) {
  if (arr.length < k) return null;
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += arr[i];
  let best = sum;
  for (let right = k; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right] - arr[right - k];
    best = Math.max(best, sum);
  }
  return best;
}
```

---

## 2. Using `if` instead of `while` when shrinking

**Pitfall:** With variable-size windows, a single `if` may leave the window invalid after one shrink.

```js
// BUGGY: only shrinks once even if multiple elements must leave
function longestSubstringNoRepeat(s) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    while (seen.has(s[right])) {
      seen.delete(s[left]);
      left++;
    }
    seen.add(s[right]);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
// Actually this one is correct. The buggy variant:
function buggy(s) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    if (seen.has(s[right])) {        // only removes ONE char
      seen.delete(s[left]);
      left++;
    }
    seen.add(s[right]);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
// buggy("abba") -> wrong (returns 3 instead of 2)
```

---

## 3. Leaving zero-count keys in frequency maps

**Pitfall:** When tracking distinct counts, a key with count `0` still counts as "present".

```js
// BUGGY
function countDistinctInWindow(arr, k) {
  const freq = new Map();
  let distinct = 0;
  const res = [];
  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    const v = arr[right];
    if ((freq.get(v) || 0) === 0) distinct++;
    freq.set(v, (freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);

    if (right >= k) {
      const out = arr[right - k];
      freq.set(out, freq.get(out) - 1);
      // BUG: distinct not decremented when count hits 0
    }
    if (right >= k - 1) res.push(distinct);
  }
  return res;
}

// CORRECT
function countDistinctInWindowFixed(arr, k) {
  const freq = new Map();
  let distinct = 0;
  const res = [];
  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    const v = arr[right];
    if ((freq.get(v) || 0) === 0) distinct++;
    freq.set(v, (freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);

    if (right >= k) {
      const out = arr[right - k];
      freq.set(out, freq.get(out) - 1);
      if (freq.get(out) === 0) distinct--;   // decrement only at zero
    }
    if (right >= k - 1) res.push(distinct);
  }
  return res;
}
```

---

## 4. O(n²) from `shift()` / `splice()` / `indexOf()`

**Pitfall:** Sliding the window with array mutation destroys the O(n) guarantee.

```js
// BUGGY: O(n*k) or worse
function slidingWindowBad(arr, k) {
  const window = [];
  const res = [];
  for (const x of arr) {
    window.push(x);
    if (window.length > k) window.shift();   // O(n) each -> O(n^2)
    if (window.length === k) res.push([...window]);
  }
  return res;
}

// CORRECT: index-based, no mutation
function slidingWindowGood(arr, k) {
  const res = [];
  for (let i = 0; i + k <= arr.length; i++) {
    res.push(arr.slice(i, i + k));   // slice is O(k) but acceptable; or use indices
  }
  return res;
}
```

---

## 5. Mutating the input array

**Pitfall:** In-place updates (e.g., marking visited) corrupt caller data and break re-runs.

```js
// BUGGY
function markVisited(grid) {
  // mutates grid
  for (let r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)
    for (let c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++)
      if (grid[r][c] === 1) grid[r][c] = 2;
}

// CORRECT: work on a copy or use a visited Set
function markVisitedSafe(grid) {
  const visited = new Set();
  for (let r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)
    for (let c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++)
      if (grid[r][c] === 1) visited.add(`${r},${c}`);
  return visited;
}
```

---

## 6. Forgetting edge cases: empty / single element / k > n

```js
// BUGGY: crashes or returns NaN
function avgWindow(arr, k) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += arr[i];   // arr[i] undefined if k>n
  return sum / k;                              // NaN if empty
}

// CORRECT
function avgWindowSafe(arr, k) {
  if (!arr || arr.length === 0 || k <= 0 || k > arr.length) return [];
  const res = [];
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += arr[i];
  res.push(sum / k);
  for (let i = k; i < arr.length; i++) {
    sum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];
    res.push(sum / k);
  }
  return res;
}
```

---

## 7. Fixed vs. variable window confusion

**Pitfall:** Applying fixed-size update logic to a variable-size problem (or vice versa).

```js
// BUGGY: fixed-size decrement even though window size varies
function longestSubarraySumAtMostK(nums, K) {
  let left = 0, sum = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {
    sum += nums[right];
    while (sum > K) {
      sum -= nums[left];
      left++;
    }
    // variable size: length is right-left+1, NOT a constant k
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
// The bug usually appears as `best = Math.max(best, k)` or using a constant k.
```

---

## 8. Not resetting aggregate state on shrink

**Pitfall:** When the window shrinks, derived values (max, min, product) may not be updated correctly because they were computed over the whole history.

```js
// BUGGY: running max doesn't decrease when max element leaves
function maxInSlidingWindowNaive(nums, k) {
  let curMax = -Infinity;
  const res = [];
  for (let right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {
    curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[right]);
    if (right >= k) {
      // removing nums[right-k] but curMax may still be that value
    }
    if (right >= k - 1) res.push(curMax);
  }
  return res;
}
// e.g. [1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7], k=3 -> wrong at window [-3,5,3] (curMax stays 3? actually 5 ok; problematic cases exist)

// CORRECT: use a monotonic deque
function maxInSlidingWindow(nums, k) {
  const dq = []; // stores indices, values decreasing
  const res = [];
  for (let right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {
    while (dq.length && nums[dq[dq.length - 1]] <= nums[right]) dq.pop();
    dq.push(right);
    if (dq[0] <= right - k) dq.shift();
    if (right >= k - 1) res.push(nums[dq[0]]);
  }
  return res;
}
```

---

## 9. Closure capturing loop variable

**Pitfall:** Classic JS issue when storing window callbacks/deferred work inside a loop.

```js
// BUGGY
const handlers = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  handlers.push(() => console.log("window start", i));
}
handlers.forEach(h => h()); // prints 3,3,3

// CORRECT: use let (block scope) or bind
const handlers2 = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  handlers2.push(() => console.log("window start", i));
}
handlers2.forEach(h => h()); // 0,1,2
```

---

## 10. Strings are immutable — can't mutate "in place"

**Pitfall:** Treating a JS string like a char array.

```js
// BUGGY
function slideString(s) {
  const chars = s;
  chars[0] = "X";   // silently fails (or throws in strict? actually no-op)
  return chars;
}

// CORRECT
function slideStringSafe(s) {
  return "X" + s.slice(1);
}
```

---

## 11. Using objects as frequency keys by reference

**Pitfall:** Object keys coerce to `"[object Object]"`, so distinct objects collapse.

```js
// BUGGY
const freq = {};
const a = { id: 1 }, b = { id: 2 };
freq[a] = 1; freq[b] = 1;   // both under "[object Object]"
console.log(Object.keys(freq).length); // 1, not 2

// CORRECT: use Map with the actual object, or a string id
const freq2 = new Map();
freq2.set(a, 1);
freq2.set(b, 1);
console.log(freq2.size); // 2
```

---

## 12. Division by zero / NaN propagation

**Pitfall:** Empty window or `k=0` produces `NaN`/`Infinity` that poisons aggregates.

```js
// BUGGY
function windowAverage(arr, k) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += arr[i];
  return sum / k;   // NaN if k=0; Infinity if...
}

// CORRECT
function windowAverageSafe(arr, k) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(k) || k <= 0 || k > arr.length) return null;
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += arr[i];
  return sum / k;
}
```

---

## 13. Inclusive `right` vs exclusive end in `slice`

**Pitfall:** `slice(start, end)` is exclusive on `end`; window math must match.

```js
// BUGGY mental model: window of size k ending at right is slice(right-k, right) -> misses right
// Actually slice(right-k+1? no)...
// Correct: window indices [right-k+1, right] inclusive <=> slice(right-k+1, right+1)
function windowSlice(arr, right, k) {
  const start = right - k + 1;
  return arr.slice(start, right + 1);
}
```

---

## 14. Forgetting to handle `k` larger than input

Already covered partly, but worth repeating: always guard `if (k > arr.length) return ...`.

---

## 15. Reusing a mutable window state across test cases

**Pitfall:** Module-level state not reset between calls.

```js
// BUGGY
const windowState = { sum: 0, left: 0 };
function slide(arr, k) {
  windowState.sum = 0; windowState.left = 0; // forgot reset in some path
  // ...
}

// CORRECT: keep state local to the function call
function slideSafe(arr, k) {
  let sum = 0, left = 0;
  // ...
}
```

---

## Quick checklist

- Use **inclusive indices consistently** and document them.
- Prefer **`while` loops** for shrinking variable windows.
- Clean up frequency maps (**delete or zero-check**).
- Avoid **`shift`/`splice`/`indexOf`** in hot loops.
- Guard **empty, single, and `k > n`** cases.
- Don't **mutate inputs**; use Sets/Maps or copies.
- Use **`Map`** for non-string keys.
- Keep state **local** to each call.
- For aggregates that don't compose (max/min/product), use a **deque or recompute**.

These are the issues that most often turn an O(n) sliding-window solution into a buggy or O(n²) one in JavaScript.

## Exercise

**Problem:** Given an array of integers `nums` and a positive integer `k`, find the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of length exactly `k`.

- Example: `nums = [2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2]`, `k = 3` → answer `9` (subarray `[5, 1, 3]`).
- Constraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 10^5`, values can be negative.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Brute force (for intuition)

Compute the sum of every window of size `k`:

```js
function maxSumBrute(nums, k) {
  let max = -Infinity;
  for (let i = 0; i + k <= nums.length; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < i + k; j++) sum += nums[j];
    max = Math.max(max, sum);
  }
  return max;
}
```

- Time: **O(n·k)** — re-summing each window from scratch is wasteful.
- Space: **O(1)**.

### 2. Sliding window optimization

Instead of recomputing, **reuse** the previous window’s sum:

- Window `[i, i+k-1]` sum = `S`.
- Next window `[i+1, i+k]` sum = `S - nums[i] + nums[i+k]`.

So one pass, O(n) time.

### 3. Algorithm

1. If `nums.length < k`, return `null` (or throw).
2. Compute initial window sum for first `k` elements.
3. Set `maxSum = windowSum`.
4. Slide: for `i` from `k` to `n-1`:
   - `windowSum += nums[i] - nums[i - k]`
   - `maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum)`
5. Return `maxSum`.

### 4. Edge cases

- `k === 1`: just max element; algorithm handles it.
- Negative numbers: initialize `maxSum` with first window (not `0`), so all-negative arrays work.
- `k > nums.length`: guard.

### 5. Final JavaScript solution

```js
/**
 * Maximum sum of a contiguous subarray of exactly length k.
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @param {number} k
 * @returns {number|null}
 */
function maxSumSubarray(nums, k) {
  if (!Array.isArray(nums) || k <= 0 || nums.length < k) return null;

  // Initial window sum
  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += nums[i];

  let maxSum = windowSum;

  // Slide the window
  for (let i = k; i < nums.length; i++) {
    windowSum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];
    if (windowSum > maxSum) maxSum = windowSum;
  }

  return maxSum;
}

// --- quick checks ---
console.log(maxSumSubarray([2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2], 3)); // 9  ([5,1,3])
console.log(maxSumSubarray([-1, -2, -3, -4], 2));     // -3 ([-1,-2])
console.log(maxSumSubarray([5], 1));                  // 5
console.log(maxSumSubarray([1, 2, 3], 5));            // null
```

### Complexity

- **Time:** O(n) — one pass after initial window.
- **Space:** O(1) — only a few scalars.

---

### Bonus: variable-size window (longest substring without repeats)

If you want the “growing/shrinking” variant, the same idea applies with two pointers:

```js
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  const seen = new Map();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    if (seen.has(s[right]) && seen.get(s[right]) >= left) {
      left = seen.get(s[right]) + 1;
    }
    seen.set(s[right], right);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
```

Both are sliding window: fixed size updates by “add new, remove old”; variable size moves `left` only when a constraint is violated.

## Sliding Window Technique (JavaScript)

### What it is

Sliding window maintains a **contiguous range** `[left, right]` over an array/string and slides it in one direction (usually `right` increases monotonically). Instead of recomputing from scratch for every subarray (O(n²) or worse), we **incrementally add** the new element on the right and **remove** the element that falls out on the left, keeping aggregate state (sum, counts, max, etc.) in O(1) per step → **O(n) total**.

Two flavors:

| Flavor | When to use | Movement of `left` |
|---|---|---|
| **Fixed size** | "subarray of length k" | `left = right - k + 1` (deterministic) |
| **Variable size** | "longest/shortest subarray satisfying a property" | shrink while property violated (or grow while satisfied) |

### Core idea in code

```js
// ---------- Fixed-size window ----------
// Max sum of any contiguous subarray of length k.
function maxSumFixedWindow(arr, k) {
  if (k <= 0 || arr.length < k) return null;

  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += arr[i]; // seed
  let best = windowSum;

  for (let right = k; right < arr.length; right++) {
    // slide: +new element on right, -element that just left on left
    windowSum += arr[right] - arr[right - k];
    if (windowSum > best) best = windowSum;
  }
  return best;
}

// ---------- Variable-size window ----------
// Longest substring with all unique characters.
function longestUniqueSubstring(s) {
  const lastPos = new Map(); // char -> its most recent index
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const ch = s[right];

    // If ch already in window, jump left past its previous occurrence.
    if (lastPos.has(ch) && lastPos.get(ch) >= left) {
      left = lastPos.get(ch) + 1;
    }
    lastPos.set(ch, right);

    // window [left, right] is now valid; update answer
    const len = right - left + 1;
    if (len > best) best = len;
  }
  return best;
}

// ---------- Generic template (the "core") ----------
// Find the longest subarray satisfying `isValid(state)`.
// `add(x)`/`remove(x)` mutate a shared window state in O(1).
function longestValidSubarray(arr, { add, remove, isValid }) {
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    add(arr[right]);                 // expand right

    while (!isValid()) {             // shrink until valid again
      remove(arr[left]);
      left++;
    }

    const len = right - left + 1;
    if (len > best) best = len;
  }
  return best;
}

// Example usage of the template: longest subarray with at most K zeros.
function longestWithAtMostKZeros(nums, k) {
  let zeros = 0;
  return longestValidSubarray(nums, {
    add: (x) => { if (x === 0) zeros++; },
    remove: (x) => { if (x === 0) zeros--; },
    isValid: () => zeros <= k,
  });
}
```

### Why it's O(n)

Each pointer (`left`, `right`) only moves forward. Even though there's a `while` loop, `left` increments at most `n` times total across the whole run, so total work is O(n) (amortized). The fixed version is strictly O(n).

### Edge cases to handle in interviews

- Empty input / `k === 0` / `k > arr.length` → return `null` or `0` explicitly.
- Negative numbers in fixed "max sum" windows → don't reset sum to 0 (that's Kadane's, different problem).
- Character set large → prefer `Map`/object over array of size 256; use array only when alphabet is bounded (ASCII).
- "Shortest" instead of "longest" → flip the update: only update answer when valid, and shrink as much as possible *inside* the loop.
- Need min/max in window → sliding window + **monotonic deque** (not a plain sum), since removal must be O(1).

### Complexity

- Time: O(n)
- Space: O(min(n, alphabet)) for the hash map; O(1) for fixed-size numeric windows.

### Quick mental model

> "Right pointer explores; left pointer repairs."  
> Expand until invalid → shrink until valid → record. Never move a pointer backward.

# Edge Cases When Using the Sliding Window Technique in JavaScript

Below are the most important edge cases, each with a "naive" (buggy) snippet and a "robust" version.

---

## 1. Empty / null / undefined input

```js
// ❌ Naive: crashes on null/undefined, returns undefined for []
function maxSumNaive(arr, k) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += arr[i]; // TypeError if arr null
  // ...
}

// ✅ Robust
function maxSum(arr, k) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr) || arr.length === 0) return null; // or 0 / -Infinity
  if (k <= 0) return null;
  if (k > arr.length) return null; // or handle partial window
  let best = -Infinity, windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    windowSum += arr[i];
    if (i >= k) windowSum -= arr[i - k];
    if (i >= k - 1) best = Math.max(best, windowSum);
  }
  return best;
}
```

**Why it matters:** Sliding-window code almost always indexes `arr[i]` and `arr[i-k]`; null/undefined inputs throw before any logic runs.

---

## 2. Window size `k` larger than the array

```js
// ❌ Naive assumes k <= n
function fixedWindow(arr, k) {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) s += arr[i]; // reads undefined → NaN
  // ...
}

// ✅ Robust
function fixedWindow(arr, k) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr) || k <= 0 || k > arr.length) return null;
  // ... normal logic
}
```

**Decision point:** Should a too-large window return `null`, sum the whole array, or throw? Pick one contract and document it.

---

## 3. Window size of `0` or `1`

```js
// Edge: k === 0 → empty window; k === 1 → every element is its own window
function maxSubarrayLen(arr, k) {
  if (k === 0) return 0;          // empty subarray has sum 0 (or handle as invalid)
  if (k === 1) return arr.length; // each element qualifies trivially
  // ...
}
```

**Pitfall:** With `k === 0`, the "max sum of size k" is mathematically 0 for an empty subarray, but many problems define it as invalid. Be explicit.

---

## 4. All elements negative (max-sum problems)

```js
// ❌ Naive initializes best = 0, so an all-negative array returns 0 (wrong)
function maxSumNeg(arr, k) {
  let best = 0, s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) { /* ... */ best = Math.max(best, s); }
  return best; // returns 0 for [-5,-2,-3], k=2 → should be -7
}

// ✅ Robust: initialize to -Infinity
function maxSumNeg(arr, k) {
  if (k > arr.length) return null;
  let best = -Infinity, s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    s += arr[i];
    if (i >= k) s -= arr[i - k];
    if (i >= k - 1) best = Math.max(best, s);
  }
  return best; // -7
}
```

---

## 5. Zeros and duplicate values

```js
// Problem: longest subarray with sum <= target, zeros create many equal windows
function longestSubarray(arr, target) {
  let left = 0, sum = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];
    while (sum > target && left <= right) {
      sum -= arr[left++];
    }
    // ✅ use right - left + 1, not right - left
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
// zeros: [0,0,0], target 0 → correctly returns 3
```

**Pitfall:** Off-by-one in length (`right - left` vs `right - left + 1`) is the #1 bug.

---

## 6. Sliding window on strings: Unicode / surrogate pairs

```js
// ❌ .length counts UTF-16 code units, not characters
const s = "😀ab";
console.log(s.length); // 4 (😀 is 2 code units)

// ✅ Spread into code points
function windowOnString(s, k) {
  const chars = [...s]; // array of code points
  if (k > chars.length) return null;
  // slide over chars, not s
  let window = chars.slice(0, k).join("");
  // ...
  return window;
}
```

**Also:** `slice`/`substring` on the original string with code-unit indices splits emoji. Always convert with `[...str]` first when counting "characters."

---

## 7. Variable window: shrinking condition with `left > right`

```js
// ❌ Infinite / wrong when every element alone violates the condition
function minWindow(s, t) {
  let left = 0, need = new Map(/* counts of t */);
  let formed = 0, required = need.size;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    // add s[right]
    while (formed === required) {
      // ✅ guard: never let left exceed right+1
      if (left > right) break;
      // update best, remove s[left], left++
    }
  }
}
```

**Pitfall:** If a single character cannot satisfy the requirement, the inner `while` must terminate; otherwise `left` runs past `right` and reads `undefined`.

---

## 8. Floating-point / non-integer values

```js
// ❌ Exact equality on floats fails
function hasSubarraySum(arr, target) {
  let sum = 0, left = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];
    while (sum > target + 1e-9) { sum -= arr[left++]; } // tolerance
    if (Math.abs(sum - target) < 1e-9) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

**Also:** Negative numbers break the "only expand right, shrink left" monotonicity assumption for sum-based windows. If negatives are allowed, you need prefix sums + hash map instead.

---

## 9. No valid window exists

```js
// ✅ Always define a sentinel return value
function minLenSubarraySum(arr, target) {
  let left = 0, sum = 0, best = Infinity;
  for (let right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];
    while (sum >= target) {
      best = Math.min(best, right - left + 1);
      sum -= arr[left++];
    }
  }
  return best === Infinity ? 0 : best; // 0 (or -1) signals "none"
}
```

**Contract clarity:** Decide and document whether "no solution" is `null`, `0`, `-1`, or `undefined`. Mixing these causes downstream bugs.

---

## 10. Mutating the input

```js
// ❌ Sorting in place destroys caller's array
function windowAfterSort(arr, k) {
  arr.sort((a, b) => a - b); // mutates!
  // ...
}

// ✅ Copy first
function windowAfterSort(arr, k) {
  const a = [...arr].sort((x, y) => x - y);
  // ...
}
```

---

## 11. Integer safety (large sums)

```js
// JS numbers are IEEE-754 doubles; sums beyond 2^53 lose precision
const arr = [2 ** 53, 2 ** 53, ...];
// Use BigInt for exact integer sums if needed
function sumBigInt(arr) {
  return arr.reduce((a, b) => a + BigInt(b), 0n);
}
```

---

## 12. Case sensitivity / whitespace in string windows

```js
function longestUnique(s) {
  // Normalize if the problem says "case-insensitive"
  const str = s.toLowerCase(); // or keep as-is per spec
  const seen = new Map();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < str.length; right++) {
    if (seen.has(str[right]) && seen.get(str[right]) >= left) {
      left = seen.get(str[right]) + 1;
    }
    seen.set(str[right], right);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
```

**Pitfall:** `"A"` and `"a"` are different code points; decide normalization up front.

---

## 13. Multiple valid answers — determinism

```js
// If several windows tie, define which to return
function firstMaxWindow(arr, k) {
  let best = -Infinity, bestIdx = 0, s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    s += arr[i];
    if (i >= k) s -= arr[i - k];
    if (i >= k - 1 && s > best) { // strict > keeps FIRST max
      best = s; bestIdx = i - k + 1;
    }
  }
  return arr.slice(bestIdx, bestIdx + k);
}
```

Use `>` (not `>=`) to keep the earliest window; `>=` keeps the latest.

---

## 14. Performance: O(n) vs accidental O(n·k)

```js
// ❌ Recomputing window sum each step → O(n·k)
for (let i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {
  let s = 0;
  for (let j = i; j < i + k; j++) s += arr[j];
}
// ✅ Sliding update → O(n): add new, remove old
```

Also avoid `Array.prototype.slice` + `reduce` inside the loop; that's O(k) per step.

---

## 15. Off-by-one in the inner shrink loop

```js
// ✅ Correct pattern: shrink while condition violated, but never past right+1
while (left <= right && !isValid(window)) {
  remove(arr[left]);
  left++;
}
// Then window is [left, right] (possibly empty if left === right+1)
```

**Rule of thumb:** After the loop, assert `left <= right + 1`. If `left > right + 1`, you have a bug.

---

## 16. Deque-based sliding window maximum: stale indices

```js
function slidingWindowMax(nums, k) {
  if (k <= 0 || nums.length === 0) return [];
  const dq = []; // stores indices, values decreasing
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    // remove indices out of window
    while (dq.length && dq[0] <= i - k) dq.shift();
    // remove smaller values
    while (dq.length && nums[dq[dq.length - 1]] <= nums[i]) dq.pop();
    dq.push(i);
    if (i >= k - 1) out.push(nums[dq[0]]);
  }
  return out;
}
```

**Edge:** `k === 1` → each element is its own max (handled). `nums` with duplicates: use `<=` (not `<`) when popping to keep the later index, or `<` to keep earlier — pick based on tie-break spec.

---

## Summary checklist

| # | Edge case | Guard |
|---|-----------|-------|
| 1 | null/undefined/empty | early return |
| 2 | k > n | early return or clamp |
| 3 | k = 0 or 1 | explicit branch |
| 4 | all negatives | init `best = -Infinity` |
| 5 | zeros / duplicates | correct length calc, tolerance |
| 6 | Unicode strings | spread `[...s]` |
| 7 | shrink past right | `left <= right` guard |
| 8 | floats / negatives | tolerance / prefix sums |
| 9 | no solution | sentinel value |
| 10 | mutation | copy input |
| 11 | large sums | BigInt |
| 12 | case sensitivity | normalize up front |
| 13 | ties | strict `>` vs `>=` |
| 14 | O(n·k) | incremental update |
| 15 | off-by-one | assert bounds |
| 16 | deque staleness | index bounds check |

A robust sliding-window function in JS should validate inputs first, define sentinels, avoid mutation, handle Unicode when relevant, and keep the O(n) incremental update.

