## Choosing the right algorithm

| Algorithm | Avg time | Worst time | Space | Stable | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Bubble | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | ✅ | Teaching / tiny n |
| Selection | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | ❌ | Few writes (flash memory) |
| Insertion | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | ✅ | Small or nearly-sorted data |
| Merge | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | ✅ | Linked lists, stable needs |
| Quick | O(n log n) | O(n²) (rare w/ median-of-three) | O(log n) | ❌ | General in-memory arrays |
| Heap | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(1) | ❌ | Memory-constrained, worst-case guarantees |
| Tim (V8/Python) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | ✅ | Real-world mixed data |

## Notes on correctness & edge cases handled

- **Custom comparator**: every sort accepts `(a, b) => number`; negative means `a < b`.
- **Immutability**: all return a new array; originals are untouched (via `slice()`).
- **Stability**: merge/insertion/bubble/tim use `<=` when merging to preserve input order on ties.
- **Quick sort** uses median-of-three pivot + switches to insertion sort below 16 elements — this is what makes it fast in practice and avoids pathological O(n²) on sorted/reverse-sorted input.
- **Early exit**: bubble sort stops as soon as a pass makes no swaps.
- **Duplicates, empty, single, reverse-sorted** all tested.

In production JavaScript, you almost always want the built-in `Array.prototype.sort()` (which is TimSort in V8 and SpiderMonkey), but implementing these yourself is essential for interviews, constrained environments, or understanding the trade-offs.

## Sorting in JavaScript

### 1. The core idea

JavaScript arrays are sorted **in place** by `Array.prototype.sort()`.  
The method takes an optional **comparator function** `(a, b) => number`:

- return `< 0` → `a` comes before `b`
- return `0` → keep relative order (stable sort in modern engines)
- return `> 0` → `a` comes after `b`

If you omit the comparator, elements are converted to strings and sorted by UTF-16 code units. That is why `[10, 2, 1].sort()` gives `[1, 10, 2]`.

Modern engines (V8, SpiderMonkey, JavaScriptCore) use **TimSort** (or similar hybrid sorts):  
- Time: `O(n log n)` average/worst, `O(n)` best for nearly sorted data  
- Space: `O(n)` worst case  
- **Stable** since ES2019

---

### 2. Syntax

```js
arr.sort();                 // default string sort
arr.sort(compareFn);        // custom order

// compareFn examples
(a, b) => a - b;           // ascending numbers
(a, b) => b - a;           // descending numbers
(a, b) => a.localeCompare(b); // locale-aware strings
```

`sort()` **mutates** the original array and also returns it. To avoid mutation:

```js
const sorted = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);
```

---

### 3. Typical use cases & examples

#### A. Numbers

```js
const nums = [10, 2, 33, 1, 7];

nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);   // [1, 2, 7, 10, 33]
nums.sort((a, b) => b - a);   // [33, 10, 7, 2, 1]
```

#### B. Strings (case-insensitive, locale-aware)

```js
const names = ["Émile", "alice", "Bob", "zoe"];

names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, "en", { sensitivity: "base" }));
// ['alice', 'Bob', 'Émile', 'zoe']  (accent/case normalized)
```

#### C. Objects by a property

```js
const users = [
  { name: "Ada", age: 36 },
  { name: "Grace", age: 85 },
  { name: "Linus", age: 54 },
];

users.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);
```

#### D. Multi-level sort (age, then name)

```js
users.sort((a, b) => {
  if (a.age !== b.age) return a.age - b.age;
  return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
});
```

#### E. Descending with stable tie-breaking

Because modern `sort` is stable, equal keys keep their original order:

```js
const tasks = [
  { priority: 1, title: "A" },
  { priority: 2, title: "B" },
  { priority: 1, title: "C" },
];

tasks.sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);
// priority 1 items remain A then C
```

---

### 4. Common pitfalls

| Pitfall | Wrong | Right |
|---|---|---|
| Numeric default sort | `[10,2,1].sort()` → `[1,10,2]` | `[10,2,1].sort((a,b)=>a-b)` |
| Boolean comparator | `arr.sort((a,b) => a > b)` | return numbers, not booleans |
| Mutating source | `const x = arr.sort(...)` | `const x = [...arr].sort(...)` |
| Huge integers | `a - b` can lose precision near `2^53` | `a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0` |
| `undefined` handling | `undefined` is sorted to the end in V8 | filter first or handle explicitly |

---

### 5. How sorting algorithms work (manual implementations)

Built-in sort is usually best, but understanding the algorithms helps.

#### Bubble Sort — `O(n²)`, stable, in-place

```js
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  const a = [...arr];
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
    let swapped = false;
    for (let j = 0; j < a.length - 1 - i; j++) {
      if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
        [a[j], a[j + 1]] = [a[j + 1], a[j]];
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
    if (!swapped) break; // already sorted
  }
  return a;
}
```

#### Insertion Sort — `O(n²)`, great for small/nearly sorted data

```js
function insertionSort(arr) {
  const a = [...arr];
  for (let i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    const key = a[i];
    let j = i - 1;
    while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) {
      a[j + 1] = a[j];
      j--;
    }
    a[j + 1] = key;
  }
  return a;
}
```

#### Merge Sort — `O(n log n)`, stable, needs extra space

```js
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return [...arr];
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid));
  const right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid));
  return merge(left, right);
}

function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) result.push(left[i++]);
    else result.push(right[j++]);
  }
  return result.concat(left.slice(i), right.slice(j));
}
```

#### Quick Sort — `O(n log n)` average, `O(n²)` worst, in-place variant possible

```js
function quickSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return [...arr];
  const pivot = arr[Math.floor(arr.length / 2)];
  const left = arr.filter(x => x < pivot);
  const mid = arr.filter(x => x === pivot);
  const right = arr.filter(x => x > pivot);
  return [...quickSort(left), ...mid, ...quickSort(right)];
}
```

> Note: the functional quick sort above is clear but allocates many arrays. Production quick sorts partition in place.

---

### 6. When to use which

| Situation | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| General app code | `arr.sort(compareFn)` — engine-optimized |
| Need original preserved | `[...arr].sort(...)` |
| Strings for users | `localeCompare` |
| Very large datasets | Built-in sort; consider Web Workers for UI responsiveness |
| Teaching / interviews | Implement merge sort or quick sort manually |
| Stable order matters | Rely on ES2019+ stable `sort` |

### 7. Performance tip

For hot paths, avoid creating comparator closures repeatedly and avoid heavy work inside the comparator:

```js
// Precompute sort key once
const ranked = items
  .map(item => ({ item, key: expensiveKey(item) }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.key - b.key)
  .map(x => x.item);
```

This keeps the comparator cheap and the sort fast.

Below are the most common pitfalls when sorting in JavaScript, grouped from “everyday `.sort()` mistakes” to “hand-rolled algorithm mistakes,” with code for each.

---

### 1. Default `.sort()` sorts lexicographically (as strings)

```js
const nums = [10, 2, 30, 4];
console.log(nums.sort()); // [10, 2, 30, 4]  ❌
console.log(nums.sort((a, b) => a - b)); // [2, 4, 10, 30] ✅
```

Fix: always pass a comparator for non-string data.

---

### 2. Comparator returning a boolean instead of a number

```js
const arr = [3, 1, 2];
// ❌ returns true/false; coercion makes it 1/0-ish, breaks ordering
arr.sort((a, b) => a > b);
// ✅ return negative/zero/positive
arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);
```

Rule: comparator must return a **number** (`<0`, `0`, `>0`).

---

### 3. Mutating the original array unexpectedly

`.sort()` sorts **in place**.

```js
const original = [3, 1, 2];
const sorted = original.sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(original); // [1, 2, 3]  ← changed!
```

Fix: copy first.

```js
const sorted = [...original].sort((a, b) => a - b);
// or
const sorted = original.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);
// or non-mutating (newer)
const sorted = original.toSorted((a, b) => a - b); // ES2023
```

---

### 4. Inconsistent / non-transitive comparator

If your comparator violates transitivity, engines may produce **different** results across browsers/Node versions.

```js
// ❌ Bad: compares by length but ties broken inconsistently
const bad = (a, b) => {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return a.length - b.length;
  return Math.random() - 0.5; // non-deterministic!
};

// ✅ Deterministic tie-break
const good = (a, b) =>
  a.length - b.length || a.localeCompare(b);
```

Always ensure: if `cmp(a,b) < 0` and `cmp(b,c) < 0`, then `cmp(a,c) < 0`.

---

### 5. Relying on sort stability for equal elements (older engines)

ES2019 mandates stable sort, but older engines (and some transpiled targets) don’t guarantee it. If order of equal keys matters, add an explicit tie-breaker.

```js
const items = [
  { id: 1, priority: 2 },
  { id: 2, priority: 2 },
];
// ❌ May not preserve insertion order on old engines
items.sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);

// ✅ Stable by construction
items.sort((a, b) =>
  a.priority - b.priority || a.id - b.id
);
```

---

### 6. NaN / undefined / null in comparators

`NaN` comparisons propagate and corrupt ordering; `undefined` sorts to the end in V8 but behavior varies.

```js
const arr = [3, undefined, 1, NaN, 2];
// ❌ a - b => NaN for NaN, weird ordering
arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);

// ✅ Normalize first, or filter
const clean = arr.filter(Number.isFinite);
clean.sort((a, b) => a - b);
```

For objects, handle missing keys explicitly:

```js
const byName = (a, b) =>
  (a.name ?? "").localeCompare(b.name ?? "");
```

---

### 7. Floating-point comparison issues

Subtraction `a - b` can lose precision or overflow for large numbers.

```js
const big = [Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER - 1];
// ❌ Subtraction may overflow / be 0
big.sort((a, b) => a - b);

// ✅ Use relational operators
big.sort((a, b) => (a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0));
```

---

### 8. `localeCompare` misuse (performance + surprising ordering)

```js
const words = ["ä", "z", "a"];
// ❌ localeCompare defaults can be slow in tight loops / hot paths
words.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));

// ✅ Cache collator for performance
const collator = new Intl.Collator("de");
words.sort((a, b) => collator.compare(a, b));
```

Also note: `localeCompare` ordering depends on locale — `"10"` vs `"2"` may surprise.

---

### 9. Sorting array-likes (NodeList, arguments) directly

`.sort()` exists only on `Array.prototype`.

```js
const nodes = document.querySelectorAll("li");
// ❌ nodes.sort is undefined
// ✅ Convert first
[...nodes].sort((a, b) => a.textContent.localeCompare(b.textContent));
Array.prototype.sort.call(nodes, cmp); // mutates NodeList (if supported)
```

---

### 10. Sparse arrays and holes

`.sort()` compresses holes to the end in most engines; relying on that is non-portable.

```js
const sparse = [3, , 1];
console.log(sparse.sort((a, b) => a - b)); // [1, 3, <empty>] (engine-dependent)
```

Fix: make dense first if holes matter.

```js
const dense = sparse.map((x) => (x === undefined ? 0 : x));
```

---

### 11. Custom sort algorithm: off-by-one / boundary bugs

A hand-written quicksort/mergesort often fails on edge sizes.

```js
// ❌ Typical buggy partition
function quicksort(arr, lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return arr; // okay
  const pivot = arr[hi];
  let i = lo;
  for (let j = lo; j < hi; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
      i++;
    }
  }
  [arr[i], arr[hi]] = [arr[hi], arr[i]];
  quicksort(arr, lo, i - 1);
  quicksort(arr, i + 1, hi);
  return arr;
}
// Edge: duplicates with `<` are fine; using `<=` can cause worst-case but not incorrect.
// But forgetting hi bound or using arr.length inside recursion breaks.
```

Always test: `[]`, `[1]`, all equal, reverse sorted, already sorted.

---

### 12. Quicksort recursion depth / stack overflow

Worst-case O(n²) recursion can blow the call stack on large or adversarial input.

```js
// ❌ Naive quicksort on sorted input → deep recursion
function qs(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;
  const p = arr[0];
  return [...qs(arr.filter(x => x < p)),
          ...arr.filter(x => x === p),
          ...qs(arr.filter(x => x > p))];
}
// ✅ Use iterative or random pivot + tail recursion, or just use .sort()
```

In production, prefer built-in `.sort()` (usually Timsort/IntroSort hybrid) or a tested library.

---

### 13. Sorting by a derived key repeatedly (performance)

Recomputing an expensive key in the comparator is O(n log n × cost).

```js
const rows = largeDataset;
// ❌ expensiveKey called ~n log n times
rows.sort((a, b) => expensiveKey(a) - expensiveKey(b));

// ✅ Schwartzian transform (decorate-sort-undecorate)
const decorated = rows.map((r) => ({ key: expensiveKey(r), r }));
decorated.sort((a, b) => a.key - b.key);
const result = decorated.map((d) => d.r);
```

---

### 14. Ascending vs descending sign errors

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
// ❌ Intended descending but wrote ascending
arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);
// ✅
arr.sort((a, b) => b - a);
```

Double-check direction; tests with small arrays help.

---

### 15. Assuming `.sort()` returns a new array

It returns the **same** (mutated) reference.

```js
const a = [3, 1, 2];
const b = a.sort((x, y) => x - y);
console.log(a === b); // true
```

If you need immutability, copy (see #3).

---

### 16. Timezone / date sorting pitfalls

Comparing `Date` objects directly coerces to primitives (timestamps) — fine — but comparing formatted strings is not.

```js
const dates = ["2024-02-10", "2024-1-5"];
// ❌ String sort: "2024-1..." < "2024-2..." only by char; okay here but fragile
// ✅ Parse to numbers
dates.sort((a, b) => new Date(a) - new Date(b));
```

---

### Quick checklist

- Always pass a numeric comparator for numbers.
- Copy before sorting if immutability matters (or use `toSorted`).
- Make comparators deterministic and transitive.
- Guard against `NaN`, `undefined`, `null`, holes.
- Use relational operators for very large numbers.
- Cache expensive keys (Schwartzian transform).
- Test edge cases: empty, single, duplicates, sorted, reverse, huge.

If you want, I can expand any single pitfall into a deeper benchmark or a production-ready helper.

## Exercise

You have an array of product objects. Sort them so that:

1. `price` is **ascending**
2. if prices are equal, `rating` is **descending** (missing ratings go last)
3. if both are equal, `name` is **ascending**

Some products may be missing `rating`. Write a comparator and sort the array. Then, as a bonus, implement a **stable merge sort** manually.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Sample data with edge cases

```js
const products = [
  { name: "Keyboard", price: 50, rating: 4.5 },
  { name: "Mouse",    price: 25, rating: 4.0 },
  { name: "Monitor",  price: 50, rating: 4.8 },
  { name: "Cable",    price: 10 },            // missing rating
  { name: "Webcam",   price: 25, rating: 4.2 },
  { name: "Hub",      price: 10, rating: 3.9 },
];
```

### 2. Write a robust comparator

A comparator must return a negative number, zero, or a positive number.  
Avoid relying on subtraction for floats or missing values.

```js
function compareProducts(a, b) {
  // 1. price ascending
  if (a.price < b.price) return -1;
  if (a.price > b.price) return 1;

  // 2. rating descending; missing ratings treated as -Infinity (go last)
  const ra = Number.isFinite(a.rating) ? a.rating : -Infinity;
  const rb = Number.isFinite(b.rating) ? b.rating : -Infinity;
  if (ra > rb) return -1; // higher rating first
  if (ra < rb) return 1;

  // 3. name ascending
  return String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name));
}
```

### 3. Sort with the built-in (stable since ES2019)

```js
const sorted = [...products].sort(compareProducts);
console.log(sorted);
```

Expected order:

- price 10: `Hub` (rating 3.9), then `Cable` (missing rating)
- price 25: `Webcam` (4.2), then `Mouse` (4.0)
- price 50: `Monitor` (4.8), then `Keyboard` (4.5)

### 4. Why this works

- Returning `-1/0/1` is safer than `a - b` for floats and missing fields.
- `localeCompare` gives correct string ordering.
- Modern `Array.prototype.sort` is stable, so equal elements keep original order.

### 5. Bonus: stable merge sort (guarantees stability everywhere)

```js
function merge(left, right, compare) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    // <= keeps left element first when equal -> stable
    if (compare(left[i], right[j]) <= 0) {
      result.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      result.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i), right.slice(j));
}

function mergeSort(arr, compare) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr.slice();

  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid), compare);
  const right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid), compare);

  return merge(left, right, compare);
}

// Usage:
const stableSorted = mergeSort(products, compareProducts);
```

### Complexity

- Built-in sort: **O(n log n)** time, **O(n)** or better space depending on engine.
- Merge sort: **O(n log n)** time, **O(n)** extra space, and **stable**.

### Final complete snippet

```js
const products = [
  { name: "Keyboard", price: 50, rating: 4.5 },
  { name: "Mouse",    price: 25, rating: 4.0 },
  { name: "Monitor",  price: 50, rating: 4.8 },
  { name: "Cable",    price: 10 },
  { name: "Webcam",   price: 25, rating: 4.2 },
  { name: "Hub",      price: 10, rating: 3.9 },
];

function compareProducts(a, b) {
  if (a.price < b.price) return -1;
  if (a.price > b.price) return 1;

  const ra = Number.isFinite(a.rating) ? a.rating : -Infinity;
  const rb = Number.isFinite(b.rating) ? b.rating : -Infinity;
  if (ra > rb) return -1;
  if (ra < rb) return 1;

  return String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name));
}

// Built-in
console.log([...products].sort(compareProducts));

// Stable merge sort
function merge(left, right, compare) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (compare(left[i], right[j]) <= 0) result.push(left[i++]);
    else result.push(right[j++]);
  }
  return result.concat(left.slice(i), right.slice(j));
}

function mergeSort(arr, compare) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr.slice();
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  return merge(
    mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid), compare),
    mergeSort(arr.slice(mid), compare),
    compare
