## 5. Design notes & trade-offs

| Concern | Stack (array) | Queue (circular) | Deque |
|---|---|---|---|
| Push/pop one end | O(1) amortized | O(1) amortized both ends | O(1) amortized both ends |
| Memory | tight (engine array) | up to 4× live size (shrink floor) | same |
| Cache locality | excellent | excellent (contiguous) | excellent |
| Worst-case single op | O(n) on resize | O(n) on resize | O(n) on resize |
| Amortized over n ops | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |

**When to use what:**

- **Stack**: recursion simulation, DFS, undo/redo, expression evaluation, monotonic stack problems.
- **Queue**: BFS, task scheduling, rate limiting, producer/consumer buffers.
- **Deque**: sliding window max/min, 0-1 BFS, palindrome checks.
- For a **priority queue**, use a binary heap (min/max) — different structure, O(log n) insert/extract.
- If you only need a queue and don't care about perf, `const q = []; q.push(x); q.shift()` is fine for small n (< ~10⁴). For large n, use the circular buffer above.

**Thread safety:** JavaScript is single-threaded per agent (event loop), so no locking is needed. In `SharedArrayBuffer`/worker scenarios you'd need atomics.

**Private fields (`#`):** require Node ≥ 12 / modern browsers. If you must target older runtimes, replace `#x` with a `WeakMap`-based or convention-prefixed (`_x`) implementation.

