# Stacks and Queues in JavaScript

## 1. The Idea

### Stack — LIFO (Last In, First Out)
The last element added is the first one removed. Think of a stack of plates: you put plates on top and take them off the top.

```
push →  [1, 2, 3]  ← pop returns 3
```

### Queue — FIFO (First In, First Out)
The first element added is the first one removed. Think of a line at a checkout: first person in line is first served.

```
enqueue →  [1, 2, 3]  ← dequeue returns 1
```

---

## 2. Syntax

JavaScript arrays are the natural backing store for both. The difference is *which* methods you use.

### Stack (array as stack)

```js
const stack = [];

stack.push(1);        // add to top
stack.push(2);
stack.push(3);

console.log(stack);   // [1, 2, 3]

const top = stack.pop(); // remove from top → 3
console.log(stack);      // [1, 2]

console.log(stack[stack.length - 1]); // peek top → 2 (without removing)
```

All three operations (`push`, `pop`, peek via index) are **O(1)**.

### Queue (array as queue) — simple but O(n) dequeue

```js
const queue = [];

queue.push(1);        // enqueue (add to back)
queue.push(2);
queue.push(3);

const front = queue.shift(); // dequeue (remove from front) → 1
console.log(queue);          // [2, 3]
```

⚠️ `shift()` is **O(n)** because it re-indexes every element. For large queues, use one of the better implementations below.

### Efficient Queue — head-pointer approach (O(1) amortized)

```js
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
    this.head = 0; // points at the front
  }
  enqueue(x) { this.items.push(x); }
  dequeue() {
    if (this.head >= this.items.length) return undefined;
    const v = this.items[this.head++];
    // occasionally compact to free memory
    if (this.head > 1000 && this.head * 2 > this.items.length) {
      this.items = this.items.slice(this.head);
      this.head = 0;
    }
    return v;
  }
  peek() { return this.items[this.head]; }
  get size() { return this.items.length - this.head; }
  isEmpty() { return this.size === 0; }
}
```

### Efficient Queue — linked list (true O(1))

```js
class Node { constructor(v) { this.v = v; this.next = null; } }

class LinkedListQueue {
  constructor() { this.head = this.tail = null; this._size = 0; }
  enqueue(v) {
    const n = new Node(v);
    if (!this.tail) this.head = this.tail = n;
    else { this.tail.next = n; this.tail = n; }
    this._size++;
  }
  dequeue() {
    if (!this.head) return undefined;
    const v = this.head.v;
    this.head = this.head.next;
    if (!this.head) this.tail = null;
    this._size--;
    return v;
  }
  peek() { return this.head?.v; }
  get size() { return this._size; }
  isEmpty() { return this._size === 0; }
}
```

### Stack with a class wrapper (optional, for clarity)

```js
class Stack {
  constructor() { this.items = []; }
  push(x) { this.items.push(x); }
  pop() { return this.items.pop(); }
  peek() { return this.items[this.items.length - 1]; }
  isEmpty() { return this.items.length === 0; }
  get size() { return this.items.length; }
}
```

---

## 3. Typical Use Cases

### Stack use cases

| Use case | Why a stack fits |
|---|---|
| **Undo / Redo** | Each action pushed; undo pops the last action. |
| **Call stack / recursion** | JS engine itself uses a stack for function calls. |
| **Balanced parentheses / syntax validation** | Push openers, pop on closers; mismatch → invalid. |
| **Depth-First Search (DFS)** | Explore a branch fully before backtracking. |
| **Expression evaluation** | Postfix (Reverse Polish) notation; operator precedence parsing. |
| **Browser back button** | History stack of visited pages. |

Example — balanced brackets:

```js
function isBalanced(s) {
  const stack = [];
  const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  for (const c of s) {
    if ('([{'.includes(c)) stack.push(c);
    else if (')]}'.includes(c)) {
      if (stack.pop() !== pairs[c]) return false;
    }
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}
isBalanced('{[()]}'); // true
isBalanced('{[(])}'); // false
```

Example — DFS (iterative):

```js
function dfs(graph, start) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const stack = [start];
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;
    visited.add(node);
    for (const nbr of graph[node]) stack.push(nbr);
  }
  return visited;
}
```

### Queue use cases

| Use case | Why a queue fits |
|---|---|
| **Task / job scheduling** | Process jobs in arrival order. |
| **Breadth-First Search (BFS)** | Visit nodes level by level. |
| **Message / event buffering** | Handle messages in the order received. |
| **Print spooling** | First document submitted prints first. |
| **Rate limiting / throttling windows** | Sliding window of recent requests. |
| **Streaming data pipelines** | Producer/consumer decoupling. |

Example — BFS:

```js
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const q = new LinkedListQueue();
  q.enqueue(start);
  while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    const node = q.dequeue();
    for (const nbr of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(nbr)) {
        visited.add(nbr);
        q.enqueue(nbr);
      }
    }
  }
  return visited;
}
```

Example — simple task runner:

```js
const tasks = new LinkedListQueue();
tasks.enqueue(() => console.log('A'));
tasks.enqueue(() => console.log('B'));
tasks.enqueue(() => console.log('C'));

while (!tasks.isEmpty()) tasks.dequeue()();
// A, B, C
```

---

## 4. Quick Comparison

| | Stack | Queue |
|---|---|---|
| Order | LIFO | FIFO |
| Add | `push` (top/back) | `enqueue` (back) |
| Remove | `pop` (top) | `dequeue` (front) |
| Inspect | `peek` (top) | `peek` (front) |
| JS array impl | `push`/`pop` → O(1) | `push`/`shift` → O(n) dequeue |
| Better impl | array is fine | linked list or head-pointer |

---

## 5. Practical Tips

- **Prefer arrays for stacks.** `push`/`pop` are O(1) and highly optimized in V8.
- **Don't use `shift()` for large queues.** It's O(n). Use a head-pointer array or a linked list (as above), or a well-tested library like `mnemonist` (`Deque`).
- **Deque (double-ended queue)** generalizes both: push/pop at both ends in O(1). Useful when you need both stack and queue behavior.
- **For async work**, remember the JS event loop already has queues (macrotask/microtask). Your own queue is for *application-level* ordering, not replacing the event loop.
- **Immutability style**: In functional code you might use `const [head, ...rest] = queue` to dequeue, but that's O(n) — fine for small data, avoid in hot paths.

