### Minimal runnable demo

```js
// Stack
const st = [];
st.push(10); st.push(20); st.push(30);
console.log(st.pop()); // 30

// Queue (efficient)
const q = new LinkedListQueue();
q.enqueue('a'); q.enqueue('b'); q.enqueue('c');
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 'a'
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 'b'
```

That covers the idea, syntax, and where each shines.

A common mistake is using an array like a queue but removing elements from the wrong end, accidentally turning it into a stack.

### Mistaken version

```js
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
  }

  enqueue(value) {
    this.items.push(value); // add to back
  }

  dequeue() {
    return this.items.pop(); // WRONG: removes from back (newest)
  }
}

const q = new Queue();
q.enqueue(1);
q.enqueue(2);
q.enqueue(3);

console.log(q.dequeue()); // 3, but a queue should return 1
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 2
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 1
```

### Corrected version

```js
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
  }

  enqueue(value) {
    this.items.push(value); // add to back
  }

  dequeue() {
    return this.items.shift(); // remove from front (oldest)
  }
}

const q = new Queue();
q.enqueue(1);
q.enqueue(2);
q.enqueue(3);

console.log(q.dequeue()); // 1
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 2
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 3
```

### What was wrong

- A **queue** is FIFO: first in, first out.  
- A **stack** is LIFO: last in, first out.  
- In the mistaken version, `push()` adds to the end and `pop()` removes from the end, which gives LIFO behavior. That is a stack, not a queue.
- The corrected version uses `push()` to add to the back and `shift()` to remove from the front, giving FIFO behavior.

### Performance note

`shift()` is simple and correct for small queues, but for large or performance-sensitive code it is `O(n)` because every remaining element must move down one index. A more scalable implementation keeps a head index:

```js
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
    this.head = 0;
  }

  enqueue(value) {
    this.items.push(value);
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.head >= this.items.length) return undefined;
    const value = this.items[this.head];
    this.head++;
    return value;
  }

  size() {
    return this.items.length - this.head;
  }
}
```

This avoids repeatedly shifting the whole array while preserving FIFO order.

Below are common JavaScript pitfalls when using arrays as stacks/queues, plus safer patterns.

## 1. Using `shift()` / `unshift()` for large queues

`Array.prototype.shift()` removes the first element and reindexes everything. Doing it in a loop is `O(n^2)`.

```js
const queue = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) queue.push(i);

while (queue.length) {
  queue.shift(); // O(n) each iteration -> O(n^2) total
}
```

Better: keep a `head` index, and occasionally trim the array.

```js
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
    this.head = 0;
  }

  enqueue(value) {
    this.items.push(value);
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.head >= this.items.length) return { ok: false };

    const value = this.items[this.head++];
    this.trim();
    return { ok: true, value };
  }

  trim() {
    if (this.head > 1000 && this.head * 2 > this.items.length) {
      this.items = this.items.slice(this.head);
      this.head = 0;
    }
  }
}
```

## 2. Ambiguous empty dequeue

`shift()` returns `undefined` both when the queue is empty and when you explicitly enqueued `undefined`.

```js
const q = [];
q.push(undefined);

q.shift(); // undefined
q.shift(); // also undefined, but queue was already empty
```

Better: return a status object or check length first.

```js
function dequeue(queue) {
  if (queue.length === 0) return { ok: false };
  return { ok: true, value: queue.shift() };
}
```

## 3. Confusing stack semantics with queue semantics

A stack is LIFO: `push` + `pop`.  
A queue is FIFO: `enqueue` with `push`, `dequeue` with `shift` or head index.

```js
const data = [1, 2, 3];

// Queue intent, but stack behavior:
data.push(4);
data.pop(); // removes 4, not 1

// Correct queue dequeue:
const first = data.shift(); // removes 1
```

## 4. Deep recursion overflowing the call stack

Recursive functions use the JavaScript call stack. Deep input can throw `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`.

```js
function factorial(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return 1;
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

factorial(200_000); // RangeError
```

Better: convert to an iterative algorithm, often with an explicit stack.

```js
function dfsIterative(root) {
  const stack = [root];
  const visited = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (!node) continue;

    visited.push(node.value);
    stack.push(node.right);
    stack.push(node.left);
  }

  return visited;
}
```

## 5. Mutating a queue/stack while iterating it

Removing items during `for...of` can skip elements because the iterator advances by index.

```js
const queue = [1, 2, 3, 4];

for (const value of queue) {
  if (value % 2 === 0) {
    const index = queue.indexOf(value);
    queue.splice(index, 1);
  }
}

console.log(queue); // Not reliably [1, 3, 4]
```

Better: build a new collection or iterate over a copy.

```js
const queue = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const filtered = queue.filter(value => value % 2 !== 0);
```

## 6. Retaining memory in head-pointer queues

With a `head` index, old slots still reference objects until trimmed.

```js
const big = { data: new Array(1_000_000).fill(0) };
const queue = [big];
let head = 0;

const item = queue[head++]; // queue[0] still references big
// big is not garbage collected until trimmed or overwritten
```

Better: trim periodically, as in the `Queue` example above, or reassign slots to `null`.

## 7. Shared mutable references

Stacks and queues store references, not copies. Mutating an enqueued object affects the stored item.

```js
const stack = [];
const user = { name: "Ada" };

stack.push(user);
user.name = "Grace";

console.log(stack[0].name); // "Grace"
```

Better: clone when independence matters.

```js
stack.push({ ...user });
```

Note that `Set`/`Map` also compare objects by reference, so two different objects with the same contents are not duplicates.

## 8. `delete` on arrays

`delete` removes the element but leaves a hole and does not update `length`.

```js
const stack = [1, 2, 3];

delete stack[stack.length - 1];

console.log(stack.length); // 3
console.log(stack);        // [1, 2, <1 empty item>]
```

Better: use `pop()`, `splice()`, or reassignment, not `delete`.

```js
stack.pop();
```

## 9. Unbounded queues and missing backpressure

An async producer can enqueue faster than the consumer drains, causing memory growth.

```js
const queue = [];

setInterval(() => {
  queue.push({ id: Math.random() }); // producer
}, 0);

setInterval(() => {
  if (queue.length) process(queue.shift()); // slower consumer
}, 10);
```

Better: bound the queue and apply backpressure or dropping.

```js
function enqueueBounded(queue, value, maxSize) {
  if (queue.length >= maxSize) {
    throw new Error("Queue is full");
  }
  queue.push(value);
}
```

## 10. Fire-and-forget async consumers

`forEach` with an `async` callback does not await completion and can process unpredictably.

```js
const queue = urls;

queue.forEach(async (url) => {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  results.push(response);
});

console.log(results.length); // likely 0
```

Better: use a sequential `for...of` loop, or a controlled concurrency pool.

```js
const results = [];
for (const url of queue) {
  results.push(await fetch(url));
}
```

## 11. Misunderstanding the event loop queues

JavaScript has multiple queues. Microtasks such as promise reactions run before macrotasks such as `setTimeout`, even with delay `0`.

```js
console.log("1. sync");

setTimeout(() => console.log("4. macrotask"), 0);

Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("3. microtask"));

console.log("2. sync");

// Output:
// 1. sync
// 2. sync
// 3. microtask
// 4. macrotask
```

If you enqueue UI or I/O work assuming `setTimeout(0)` runs immediately after synchronous code, it may run later than expected.

## 12. Creating huge sparse arrays accidentally

Assigning to a very large index creates an array with a huge `length`, which can waste memory or break assumptions.

```js
const queue = [];
queue[1_000_000] = "x";

console.log(queue.length); // 1000001
```

Better: use a `Map` for sparse integer keys, or validate input ranges.

```js
const sparse = new Map();
sparse.set(1_000_000, "x");
```

## Practical checklist

- Use `push`/`pop` for stacks.
- Use `push` + head index for queues, not repeated `shift()`.
- Distinguish empty queue from `undefined` payload.
- Trim old references in head-pointer queues.
- Avoid deep recursion; use iterative algorithms or explicit stacks.
- Do not mutate collections while iterating them.
- Clone objects when stored state must be independent.
- Bound async queues to avoid memory leaks.
- Await async queue consumers explicitly.
- Avoid `delete` on arrays and huge sparse indexes.

## Exercise

**Task:** Implement a **queue** (FIFO) in JavaScript using **only two stacks** (arrays where you may use `push`, `pop`, and `length`, but not `shift`/`unshift`/indexing for removal). Support:

- `enqueue(x)` – add to back
- `dequeue()` – remove and return front
- `peek()` – return front without removing
- `isEmpty()`

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Idea

A stack is LIFO. If we push elements into one stack (`inStack`), they come out in reverse order.  
To get FIFO order, move all elements from `inStack` to a second stack (`outStack`). Popping from `outStack` then yields the original insertion order.

### 2. Key invariant

- `inStack` holds newly enqueued elements (newest on top).
- `outStack` holds elements ready to dequeue (oldest on top).
- We only transfer when `outStack` is empty, so each element moves at most twice → amortized O(1).

### 3. Operations

- **enqueue(x):** `inStack.push(x)`
- **dequeue():** if `outStack` empty, transfer all from `inStack`; then `outStack.pop()`
- **peek():** same transfer, then look at top of `outStack`
- **isEmpty():** both stacks empty

### 4. Edge cases

- Dequeue/peek on empty queue → return `undefined`
- Interleaved enqueue/dequeue → transfer only when needed
- Multiple dequeues without enqueue → no re-transfer

---

## Final solution

```js
class QueueUsingStacks {
  constructor() {
    this.inStack = [];
    this.outStack = [];
  }

  enqueue(x) {
    this.inStack.push(x);
  }

  // Move elements only when outStack is empty
  _ensureOutStack() {
    if (this.outStack.length === 0) {
      while (this.inStack.length > 0) {
        this.outStack.push(this.inStack.pop());
      }
    }
  }

  dequeue() {
    this._ensureOutStack();
    return this.outStack.pop(); // undefined if empty
  }

  peek() {
    this._ensureOutStack();
    if (this.outStack.length === 0) return undefined;
    // top of stack is last element
    return this.outStack[this.outStack.length - 1];
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.inStack.length === 0 && this.outStack.length === 0;
  }

  size() {
    return this.inStack.length + this.outStack.length;
  }
}

// ---- Demo / tests ----
const q = new QueueUsingStacks();

q.enqueue(1);
q.enqueue(2);
q.enqueue(3);

console.log(q.dequeue()); // 1
console.log(q.peek());    // 2

q.enqueue(4);
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 2
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 3
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 4
console.log(q.dequeue()); // undefined (empty)
console.log(q.isEmpty()); // true
```

---

## Complexity

| Operation | Time | Notes |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `enqueue` | O(1) | single push |
| `dequeue` | Amortized O(1) | transfer cost spread over many ops |
| `peek` | Amortized O(1) | same transfer logic |
| `isEmpty` | O(1) | length checks |

Space: O(n) for n stored elements.

---

## Why this works (intuition)

Stacks reverse order. By using **two reversals** (`inStack` then `outStack`), the overall order becomes FIFO. The lazy transfer (`_ensureOutStack`) avoids moving elements on every operation, keeping performance efficient.

## Stacks and Queues in JavaScript

### Conceptual overview

**Stack (LIFO — Last In, First Out)**
- Think of a stack of plates: you always add/remove from the top.
- Core operations: `push`, `pop`, `peek`, `isEmpty`, `size`.
- Typical uses: undo/redo, call stack, DFS, expression parsing, backtracking.

**Queue (FIFO — First In, First Out)**
- Think of a line at a ticket counter: first person in is first served.
- Core operations: `enqueue`, `dequeue`, `peek` (front), `isEmpty`, `size`.
- Typical uses: BFS, task scheduling, buffering, rate limiting, message queues.

### Implementation notes for JavaScript

- **Stack with `Array`** is usually fine: `push`/`pop` are amortized O(1).
- **Queue with `Array` + `shift()` is O(n)** because it reindexes every element. For a real queue, use a **linked list** or a **head-pointer/circular buffer** so `enqueue` and `dequeue` are O(1).
- Below I implement both with a singly linked list for guaranteed O(1) operations, plus a small array-based stack for comparison.

---

### Clean, commented implementation

```js
// ---------- Node for linked-list based structures ----------
class Node {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
  }
}

// ---------- Stack (LIFO) ----------
class Stack {
  constructor() {
    this.top = null;   // top of the stack
    this._size = 0;
  }

  /** Add an item to the top. O(1) */
  push(value) {
    const node = new Node(value);
    node.next = this.top;
    this.top = node;
    this._size++;
    return this; // allow chaining
  }

  /** Remove and return the top item. O(1) */
  pop() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      throw new Error("Stack underflow: cannot pop from empty stack");
    }
    const value = this.top.value;
    this.top = this.top.next;
    this._size--;
    return value;
  }

  /** Look at the top item without removing it. O(1) */
  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      throw new Error("Stack is empty: nothing to peek");
    }
    return this.top.value;
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this._size === 0;
  }

  size() {
    return this._size;
  }

  /** Convert to array (top -> bottom). Useful for debugging. O(n) */
  toArray() {
    const out = [];
    let cur = this.top;
    while (cur) {
      out.push(cur.value);
      cur = cur.next;
    }
    return out;
  }
}

// ---------- Queue (FIFO) ----------
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;  // front (dequeue side)
    this.tail = null;  // back (enqueue side)
    this._size = 0;
  }

  /** Add an item to the back. O(1) */
  enqueue(value) {
    const node = new Node(value);
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      this.head = this.tail = node;
    } else {
      this.tail.next = node;
      this.tail = node;
    }
    this._size++;
    return this;
  }

  /** Remove and return the front item. O(1) */
  dequeue() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      throw new Error("Queue underflow: cannot dequeue from empty queue");
    }
    const value = this.head.value;
    this.head = this.head.next;
    if (this.head === null) {
      this.tail = null; // queue became empty
    }
    this._size--;
    return value;
  }

  /** Look at the front item without removing it. O(1) */
  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      throw new Error("Queue is empty: nothing to peek");
    }
    return this.head.value;
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this._size === 0;
  }

  size() {
    return this._size;
  }

  /** Convert to array (front -> back). Useful for debugging. O(n) */
  toArray() {
    const out = [];
    let cur = this.head;
    while (cur) {
      out.push(cur.value);
      cur = cur.next;
    }
    return out;
  }
}

// ---------- Optional: Array-backed stack (simple, idiomatic JS) ----------
class ArrayStack {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
  }
  push(v) { this.items.push(v); }
  pop() { return this.items.pop(); } // undefined if empty
  peek() { return this.items[this.items.length - 1]; }
  isEmpty() { return this.items.length === 0; }
  size() { return this.items.length; }
}

// ---------- Demo / sanity checks ----------
function demo() {
  console.log("=== Stack (linked list) ===");
  const s = new Stack();
  s.push(1).push(2).push(3);
  console.log("peek:", s.peek());        // 3
  console.log("pop:", s.pop());          // 3
  console.log("toArray:", s.toArray());  // [2, 1]
  console.log("size:", s.size());        // 2

  console.log("\n=== Queue (linked list) ===");
  const q = new Queue();
  q.enqueue("a").enqueue("b").enqueue("c");
  console.log("peek:", q.peek());        // "a"
  console.log("dequeue:", q.dequeue());  // "a"
  console.log("toArray:", q.toArray());  // ["b", "c"]
  console.log("size:", q.size());        // 2

  console.log("\n=== Empty handling ===");
  const emptyQ = new Queue();
  console.log("isEmpty:", emptyQ.isEmpty()); // true
  try { emptyQ.dequeue(); } catch (e) { console.log("caught:", e.message); }
}

demo();
```

### Key interview takeaways

1. **Know the semantics**: Stack = LIFO, Queue = FIFO.
2. **Complexity matters in JS**: `Array.prototype.shift()` is O(n), so don’t use it for queues in performance-sensitive code.
3. **Linked lists give true O(1)** for all core operations, at the cost of extra memory per node.
4. **Edge cases to mention**: empty pop/dequeue, single-element transitions (tail must become `null` when queue empties), and whether you throw or return `undefined` on underflow (choose one and be consistent).
5. **Real-world variants**: deque (double-ended queue), priority queue (heap), circular buffer (fixed-capacity queue), monotonic stack.

# Edge Cases When Using Stacks and Queues in JavaScript

In JS, stacks and queues are almost always built on `Array` (or a linked list). The language's dynamic typing, single-threaded event loop, and reference semantics create several pitfalls. Below are the most important edge cases, grouped logically, each with a runnable example.

---

## 1. Empty collection: `pop`, `shift`, `peek`

**Problem:** `Array.prototype.pop()` / `shift()` return `undefined` on an empty array, which is indistinguishable from a stored `undefined` value.

```js
const stack = [];
console.log(stack.pop()); // undefined — empty or stored undefined?

// Safer: explicit emptiness check + sentinel
class SafeStack {
  #items = [];
  push(v) { this.#items.push(v); }
  pop() {
    if (this.#items.length === 0) throw new Error('Stack underflow');
    return this.#items.pop();
  }
  peek() {
    if (this.#items.length === 0) throw new Error('Empty stack');
    return this.#items[this.#items.length - 1];
  }
  isEmpty() { return this.#items.length === 0; }
}

const s = new SafeStack();
try { s.pop(); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); } // "Stack underflow"
```

**Queue equivalent:** same issue with `shift()`; prefer a length check or a `null` sentinel.

---

## 2. Single-element collections

**Problem:** Off-by-one in boundary logic; `head === tail` confusion in ring buffers.

```js
// Ring buffer queue with capacity 1
class RingQueue {
  constructor(cap) { this.cap = cap; this.buf = new Array(cap); this.head = 0; this.tail = 0; this.size = 0; }
  enqueue(v) {
    if (this.size === this.cap) throw new Error('Overflow');
    this.buf[this.tail] = v;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.cap;
    this.size++;
  }
  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) throw new Error('Underflow');
    const v = this.buf[this.head];
    this.buf[this.head] = undefined; // help GC
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.cap;
    this.size--;
    return v;
  }
}

const q = new RingQueue(1);
q.enqueue(42);
console.log(q.dequeue()); // 42
try { q.dequeue(); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); } // Underflow
```

---

## 3. Bounded stacks / queues (overflow)

**Problem:** Unbounded growth → memory pressure; silent failure in producer-consumer.

```js
class BoundedStack {
  constructor(max) { this.max = max; this.items = []; }
  push(v) {
    if (this.items.length >= this.max) {
      // Policy choice: throw, drop oldest, or overwrite
      throw new RangeError('Stack overflow (bounded)');
    }
    this.items.push(v);
  }
}

const bs = new BoundedStack(2);
bs.push(1); bs.push(2);
try { bs.push(3); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); }
```

---

## 4. Performance trap: `Array.prototype.shift()` is O(n)

**Problem:** Using an array as a queue with `shift()` is O(n) per dequeue → O(n²) total.

```js
// BAD: O(n²)
const q = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 1e5; i++) q.push(i);
while (q.length) q.shift(); // slow

// GOOD: head pointer, O(1) dequeue
class FastQueue {
  constructor() { this.items = []; this.head = 0; }
  enqueue(v) { this.items.push(v); }
  dequeue() {
    if (this.head >= this.items.length) return undefined; // empty
    const v = this.items[this.head++];
    // occasional compaction to free memory
    if (this.head > 1024 && this.head * 2 > this.items.length) {
      this.items = this.items.slice(this.head);
      this.head = 0;
    }
    return v;
  }
  get size() { return this.items.length - this.head; }
}

const fq = new FastQueue();
fq.enqueue(1); fq.enqueue(2);
console.log(fq.dequeue(), fq.dequeue(), fq.size); // 1 2 0
```

---

## 5. Mutation during iteration

**Problem:** Modifying a stack/queue while iterating (e.g., draining in a `for...of`) causes skipped/duplicated elements.

```js
const stack = [1, 2, 3];
// Dangerous: mutating while iterating by index
for (let i = 0; i < stack.length; i++) {
  console.log(stack.pop()); // pops 3, then length changes → skips
}
// stack now: [1]  (2 was skipped)

// Safe: snapshot first
const snapshot = [...stack];
for (const v of snapshot) console.log('snap', v);

// Or drain explicitly
while (stack.length) console.log('drain', stack.pop());
```

---

## 6. Reference semantics / aliasing

**Problem:** Storing objects means multiple entries share the same reference; mutating one mutates all.

```js
const obj = { n: 1 };
const stack = [];
stack.push(obj);
stack.push(obj);
stack[0].n = 99;
console.log(stack[1].n); // 99 — aliasing surprise

// Defensive copy
class ValueStack {
  push(v) { this.items.push(structuredClone(v)); }
  pop() { return this.items.pop(); }
}
```

Also beware: `structuredClone` throws on functions/symbols/DOM nodes — handle or fall back to `JSON` (lossy) or manual clone.

---

## 7. Async / event-loop ordering

**Problem:** Queues used for scheduling (microtasks/macrotasks) have ordering subtleties; a "queue" built with `setTimeout` is not FIFO with respect to microtasks.

```js
const q = [];
q.push(() => console.log('macro A'));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('micro 1'));
setTimeout(() => console.log('macro B'), 0);
// micro 1 runs before macro B even though pushed differently

// If you need strict FIFO async processing, use a serial async queue:
class AsyncQueue {
  #chain = Promise.resolve();
  enqueue(task) {
    this.#chain = this.#chain.then(task).catch(e => console.error(e));
    return this.#chain;
  }
}
const aq = new AsyncQueue();
aq.enqueue(() => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => { console.log('job1'); r(); }, 10)));
aq.enqueue(() => console.log('job2')); // always after job1
```

---

## 8. Type validation and `null`/`undefined`

**Problem:** JS coerces; `null`/`undefined` may be legitimate payloads or sentinel "empty" markers.

```js
function makeStack() {
  const items = [];
  return {
    push: v => items.push(v),
    pop: () => {
      if (items.length === 0) return Symbol('EMPTY'); // sentinel not a real value
      return items.pop();
    },
  };
}

// Validate input types when domain requires it
class TypedQueue {
  constructor(type) { this.type = type; this.items = []; }
  enqueue(v) {
    if (typeof v !== this.type) throw new TypeError(`Expected ${this.type}`);
    this.items.push(v);
  }
}
```

---

## 9. Sparse arrays

**Problem:** `new Array(n)` creates holes; `length` lies about populated slots; iteration skips holes.

```js
const a = new Array(3);
a.push(10); // length becomes 4: [empty, empty, empty, 10]
console.log(a.length); // 4
for (const v of a) console.log(v); // skips holes → only 10
// Use Array.from or fill
const safe = Array.from({ length: 3 }, () => null);
```

Ring buffers (see §2) must avoid holes by always assigning slots.

---

## 10. Memory retention / leaks

**Problem:** Popped elements may still be referenced by the underlying array (in some engines) or by closures; large payloads persist.

```js
class LeakFreeStack {
  constructor() { this.items = []; }
  push(v) { this.items.push(v); }
  pop() {
    const v = this.items.pop();
    // In engines that don't truncate, explicitly clear:
    this.items.length = this.items.length; // noop-ish; better:
    return v;
  }
}

// For queues with head pointer (§4), periodically slice/compact
// and null out consumed slots:
dequeue() {
  const v = this.items[this.head];
  this.items[this.head] = null; // drop reference
  this.head++;
  return v;
}
```

---

## 11. Equality & search

**Problem:** `indexOf`/`includes` use `===`; objects never equal by value.

```js
const stack = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 1 }];
console.log(stack.includes({ id: 1 })); // false

// Search by predicate
const found = stack.find(x => x.id === 1);
// Or maintain a Map<id, node> for O(1) membership in queues
```

---

## 12. Serialization (JSON)

**Problem:** `undefined`, functions, symbols, `BigInt`, circular refs, and `Date`/`Map`/`Set` don't survive `JSON.stringify`.

```js
const q = [undefined, () => {}, 1n, new Date()];
console.log(JSON.stringify(q)); // "[null,null,null,\"2024-...\"]" (lossy)

// Custom replacer/reviver or store plain data only
const safe = q.map(v => (typeof v === 'bigint' ? { __bigint: v.toString() } : v));
```

---

## 13. Recursion vs. explicit stack (stack overflow)

**Problem:** Deep recursion hits `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`; an explicit stack does not.

```js
function dfsRecursive(graph, node, seen = new Set()) {
  if (seen.has(node)) return;
  seen.add(node);
  for (const n of graph[node]) dfsRecursive(graph, n, seen); // may overflow
}

function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const seen = new Set();
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (seen.has(node)) continue;
    seen.add(node);
    for (const n of graph[node]) stack.push(n);
  }
}
```

---

## 14. Deque (double-ended queue) boundary cases

**Problem:** Mixing `push`/`pop` (stack discipline) with `shift`/`unshift` (queue discipline) accidentally; `unshift` is O(n).

```js
// Accidental O(n²) by using unshift as enqueue
const deque = [];
deque.unshift(1); // O(n) — bad for large

// Use a doubly-linked list or a head/tail pointer array for O(1) both ends
class Deque {
  constructor() { this.items = []; this.head = 0; this.tail = 0; /* ... */ }
  // pushBack, popBack, pushFront, popFront all O(1) with circular buffer
}
```

---

## 15. Cross-realm / worker message passing

**Problem:** Stacks/queues of non-cloneable values (functions, DOM nodes) cannot be posted to workers; `SharedArrayBuffer` needs atomics.

```js
// Main thread
const worker = new Worker('w.js');
const queue = [1, 2, 3];
worker.postMessage(queue); // structured clone — OK for plain data

// For shared mutable queue, use SharedArrayBuffer + Atomics (not a JS array)
const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(1024);
const view = new Int32Array(sab);
Atomics.store(view, 0, 42); // thread-safe enqueue slot
```

---

## 16. Concurrent logical "access" via async generators

**Problem:** A queue consumed by `for await` can deadlock if producer waits for consumer and vice versa.

```js
async function* toAsyncQueue(iterable) {
  const buf = [];
  let resolve;
  const wait = () => new Promise(r => (resolve = r));
  // (simplified) — real impl needs backpressure handling
  for (const v of iterable) {
    buf.push(v);
    if (resolve) { resolve(); resolve = null; }
    await wait(); // backpressure: wait for consumer
  }
}
```

---

## Quick checklist

| Concern | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Empty pop/peek | Explicit `length` check or sentinel |
| Bounded capacity | Throw or policy on overflow |
| Queue dequeue perf | Head pointer + periodic compaction |
| Mutation while iterating | Snapshot `[...arr]` or drain loop |
| Aliasing | `structuredClone` / deep copy |
| Async ordering | Serial async queue (promise chain) |
| `null`/`undefined` payload | Distinct sentinel (Symbol) |
| Sparse arrays | `Array.from` / `fill` |
| Memory leaks | Null out consumed slots |
| Object equality | Predicate search / Map index |
| JSON | Plain-data only or custom replacer |
| Deep recursion | Explicit stack |
| Deque both ends | Circular buffer, not `unshift` |
| Workers | Structured clone or `SharedArrayBuffer` |

---

## Minimal "production-ready" implementations

```js
// Stack
class Stack {
  #a = [];
  push(v) { this.#a.push(v); }
  pop() { if (!this.#a.length) throw new Error('Underflow'); return this.#a.pop(); }
  peek() { if (!this.#a.length) throw new Error('Empty'); return this.#a.at(-1); }
  get size() { return this.#a.length; }
  [Symbol.iterator]() { return this.#a[Symbol.iterator](); } // top-to-bottom? adjust
}

// Queue (O(1) amortized)
class Queue {
  #a = []; #h = 0;
  enqueue(v) { this.#a.push(v); }
  dequeue() {
    if (this.#h >= this.#a.length) { this.#a = []; this.#h = 0; return undefined; }
    const v = this.#a[this.#h];
    this.#a[this.#h++] = null; // help GC
    if (this.#h > 1024 && this.#h * 2 > this.#a.length) {
      this.#a = this.#a.slice(this.#h); this.#h = 0;
    }
    return v;
  }
  get size() { return this.#a.length - this.#h; }
  isEmpty() { return this.size === 0; }
}
```

These patterns cover the vast majority of real-world stack/queue edge cases in JavaScript.

# Static and Instance Members in JavaScript

Below is a complete, runnable example that demonstrates:

- **Instance members** — data and behavior tied to each object (`this`).
- **Static members** — data and behavior tied to the class/constructor itself.
- **Inheritance** of both.
- **Private fields** (instance `#balance`, static `#totalAccounts`).
- **Factories / static methods** creating instances.
- The equivalent **constructor-function (pre-ES6)** pattern.

Save as `members.js` and run with `node members.js` (Node 16+). It also runs in any modern browser console.

```js
// ============================================================================
// 1. Modern ES6 class with static + instance members
// ============================================================================

class BankAccount {
  // ---- static (class-level) state ----
  static #totalAccounts = 0;          // private static field
  static interestRate = 0.03;         // public static field
  static MIN_BALANCE = 0;             // public static constant

  // static initialization block (runs once when class is defined)
  static {
    console.log('[BankAccount] class loaded, current rate:', this.interestRate);
  }

  // ---- instance (per-object) state ----
  #balance;                          // private instance field
  accountNumber;                     // public instance field

  constructor(owner, initial = 0) {
    if (initial < BankAccount.MIN_BALANCE) {
      throw new RangeError('Initial balance below minimum');
    }
    this.owner = owner;
    this.#balance = initial;
    this.accountNumber = ++BankAccount.#totalAccounts; // touches static private
  }

  // ---- instance methods ----
  get balance() {                    // instance getter
    return this.#balance;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) throw new RangeError('Deposit must be positive');
    this.#balance += amount;
    return this;                     // fluent API
  }

  withdraw(amount) {
    if (amount > this.#balance) throw new Error('Insufficient funds');
    this.#balance -= amount;
    return this;
  }

  monthlyInterest() {
    // instance method using a STATIC value via this.constructor
    return this.#balance * this.constructor.interestRate;
  }

  toString() {
    return `Account #${this.accountNumber} (${this.owner}): $${this.#balance.toFixed(2)}`;
  }

  // ---- static methods ----
  static get totalAccounts() {       // static getter
    return BankAccount.#totalAccounts; // or this.#totalAccounts inside static context
  }

  static createFromJSON(json) {      // static factory
    const data = JSON.parse(json);
    const acct = new this(data.owner, data.balance); // `this` = the class
    return acct;
  }

  static applyGlobalRateChange(newRate) {
    BankAccount.interestRate = newRate;
    console.log('[static] new global rate:', BankAccount.interestRate);
  }

  static compare(a, b) {             // static utility
    return a.balance - b.balance;
  }
}

// ============================================================================
// 2. Inheritance of static + instance members
// ============================================================================

class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
  static interestRate = 0.05;        // overrides static field

  #withdrawalsThisMonth = 0;

  withdraw(amount) {
    if (this.#withdrawalsThisMonth >= 6) {
      throw new Error('Monthly withdrawal limit reached');
    }
    this.#withdrawalsThisMonth++;
    return super.withdraw(amount);     // reuse instance logic
  }

  resetMonthlyCounter() {
    this.#withdrawalsThisMonth = 0;
  }
}

// ============================================================================
// 3. Pre-ES6 (constructor function) equivalent — same concepts, older syntax
// ============================================================================

function LegacyAccount(owner, initial = 0) {
  // instance members live on `this`
  this.owner = owner;
  this._balance = initial;            // "private by convention"
  LegacyAccount._count = (LegacyAccount._count || 0) + 1;
  this.number = LegacyAccount._count;
}

// instance methods on prototype
LegacyAccount.prototype.deposit = function (amount) {
  this._balance += amount;
  return this;
};
LegacyAccount.prototype.getBalance = function () {
  return this._balance;
};

// static members attached directly to constructor
LegacyAccount.interestRate = 0.03;
LegacyAccount.create = function (owner, initial) {
  return new LegacyAccount(owner, initial);
};

// ============================================================================
// 4. Demo / smoke test
// ============================================================================

function main() {
  console.log('\n--- Instance vs static basics ---');
  const a = new BankAccount('Alice', 100);
  const b = new BankAccount('Bob', 50);
  console.log(a.toString());
  console.log(b.toString());
  console.log('static totalAccounts (via class):', BankAccount.totalAccounts);
  console.log('static totalAccounts (via subclass):', SavingsAccount.totalAccounts);
  console.log('instance has totalAccounts?', Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(a, 'totalAccounts')); // false

  console.log('\n--- Static method invocation ---');
  const fromJson = BankAccount.createFromJSON('{"owner":"Carol","balance":200}');
  console.log(fromJson.toString());
  // Note: static methods are NOT on instances:
  console.log('a.createFromJSON is', typeof a.createFromJSON); // undefined

  console.log('\n--- Instance method chaining + static field usage ---');
  a.deposit(25).withdraw(10);
  console.log('Alice balance:', a.balance);
  console.log('Monthly interest (base rate):', a.monthlyInterest().toFixed(2));

  console.log('\n--- Static field change affects all instances ---');
  BankAccount.applyGlobalRateChange(0.04);
  console.log('Alice monthly interest now:', a.monthlyInterest().toFixed(2));
  console.log('Bob monthly interest now:', b.monthlyInterest().toFixed(2));

  console.log('\n--- Subclass overrides static field, inherits static methods ---');
  const s = new SavingsAccount('Dana', 300);
  console.log(s.toString());
  console.log('Savings monthly interest (subclass rate 0.05):', s.monthlyInterest().toFixed(2));
  s.withdraw(50);
  console.log('After withdrawal:', s.balance);
  console.log('SavingsAccount inherits createFromJSON?', typeof SavingsAccount.createFromJSON); // function

  console.log('\n--- Static utility on instances passed as args ---');
  const richer = [a, b, s].sort(BankAccount.compare).pop();
  console.log('Richest:', richer.toString());

  console.log('\n--- Accessing class from instance ---');
  // Inside/using instance you reach static via constructor
  console.log('a.constructor === BankAccount?', a.constructor === BankAccount);
  console.log('static via instance.constructor:', a.constructor.interestRate);

  console.log('\n--- Legacy constructor-function version ---');
  const L = LegacyAccount.create('Eve', 75);
  L.deposit(25);
  console.log('Legacy balance:', L.getBalance(), 'number:', L.number, 'rate:', LegacyAccount.interestRate);
}

main();
```

## Key takeaways

| Concept | Where it lives | How to access |
|---|---|---|
| Instance field/method | `this` / prototype | `instance.foo` |
| Static field/method | constructor object | `ClassName.foo` or `instance.constructor.foo` |
| Private instance | `#field` | only inside the class, per instance |
| Private static | `static #field` | only inside the class, shared |
| Inherited static | subclass constructor | `Sub.foo` (lookup walks prototype chain of constructors) |
| Inherited instance | subclass prototype | `instance.method()` |

### Important gotchas

1. **Static methods are not available on instances** — `a.createFromJSON` is `undefined`. Reach them through `BankAccount` or `a.constructor`.
2. **`this` inside a static method** refers to the class (or subclass when called polymorphically): `SavingsAccount.createFromJSON(...)` creates a `SavingsAccount` because `new this(...)`.
3. **Shadowing**: `SavingsAccount.interestRate = 0.05` does not mutate `BankAccount.interestRate`; `monthlyInterest()` uses `this.constructor.interestRate`, so polymorphic dispatch picks the right rate.
4. **Private static fields are truly private** — even subclasses cannot read `#totalAccounts`.
5. Mixing static and instance state is fine, but avoid storing mutable shared state on instances when it should be class-level (and vice versa).

If you want, I can extend this with **static factory + dependency injection**, **mixins**, or a **TypeScript** version with explicit `public/private/static` modifiers.

## Core idea

In JavaScript, **instance members** belong to each object created from a class/constructor, while **static members** belong to the class/constructor itself.

- **Instance member** → stored on the instance (or its prototype chain). Each object gets its own data and shared methods.
- **Static member** → stored on the constructor function. There is exactly one copy, shared by all instances and accessible without creating one.

Think of a `User` class:
- `name`, `email` → instance data (different per person)
- `User.createGuest()`, `User.MAX_AGE` → static (class-level behavior/constants)

---

## Syntax (modern `class`)

```js
class User {
  // ---- Instance fields (per object) ----
  name;
  #password; // private instance field

  constructor(name, password) {
    this.name = name;
    this.#password = password;
  }

  // ---- Instance method (on prototype, shared) ----
  greet() {
    return `Hi, I'm ${this.name}`;
  }

  // ---- Static field (class-level data) ----
  static count = 0;
  static MAX_AGE = 130;
  static #secret = 'shh'; // private static field

  // ---- Static method (class-level behavior) ----
  static createGuest() {
    return new User('Guest', '');
  }

  static validateAge(age) {
    return age >= 0 && age <= User.MAX_AGE;
  }
}

// Usage
const u = new User('Ada', 'x');
console.log(u.greet());          // Hi, I'm Ada
console.log(User.count);         // 0
console.log(User.MAX_AGE);       // 130
const g = User.createGuest();    // no `new` needed here
```

### Same thing without `class` (constructor + prototype)

```js
function User(name) {
  this.name = name;              // instance field
  User.count++;                  // touch static
}
User.count = 0;                  // static field
User.MAX_AGE = 130;              // static field

User.prototype.greet = function () { // instance method
  return `Hi, I'm ${this.name}`;
};
User.createGuest = function () {    // static method
  return new User('Guest');
};
```

`class` is mostly syntax sugar over this model, but it adds private fields, static initialization blocks, and clearer intent.

---

## `this` inside static vs instance

```js
class Demo {
  static staticMethod() {
    console.log(this === Demo); // true when called as Demo.staticMethod()
  }
  instanceMethod() {
    console.log(this instanceof Demo); // true when called on an instance
  }
}

Demo.staticMethod();
new Demo().instanceMethod();
```

Caveat: if you destructure or pass a method as a callback, `this` can be lost.

```js
const d = new Demo();
const fn = d.instanceMethod;
fn(); // `this` is undefined (in strict mode) — common bug

// Fix: bind, or use an arrow function class field
class Better {
  instanceMethod = () => {
    console.log(this); // lexical `this`, safe to pass around
  };
}
```

---

## Inheritance

Static members are inherited by subclasses (unlike some languages).

```js
class Animal {
  static kingdom = 'Animalia';
  static describe() { return `Kingdom: ${this.kingdom}`; }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  static kingdom = 'Canidae';
}

console.log(Dog.describe()); // "Kingdom: Canidae" (polymorphic `this`)
console.log(Dog.kingdom);    // "Canidae"
```

Note: `this` inside a static method refers to the class it was called on, enabling polymorphism across the hierarchy.

---

## Static initialization blocks (ES2022)

Run complex setup once when the class is defined.

```js
class Config {
  static settings;

  static {
    // runs once, at class definition time
    const raw = globalThis.__CONFIG__ ?? '{}';
    Config.settings = JSON.parse(raw);
  }
}
```

Useful for computed static data, registering plugins, or validating invariants.

---

## Typical use cases

### 1. Utility / helper functions (no state needed)
```js
class MathUtil {
  static clamp(x, min, max) {
    return Math.min(Math.max(x, min), max);
  }
}
MathUtil.clamp(5, 0, 3); // 3
```

### 2. Factory methods
```js
class Point {
  constructor(x, y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
  static fromPolar(r, theta) {
    return new Point(r * Math.cos(theta), r * Math.sin(theta));
  }
}
const p = Point.fromPolar(1, Math.PI / 4);
```

### 3. Constants and enums-like values
```js
class HttpStatus {
  static OK = 200;
  static NOT_FOUND = 404;
  static SERVER_ERROR = 500;
}
if (res.status === HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND) { ... }
```

### 4. Counters / metadata / caching (shared state)
```js
class Connection {
  static #nextId = 0;
  static active = new Set();

  constructor(url) {
    this.id = ++Connection.#nextId;
    Connection.active.add(this);
  }
  close() { Connection.active.delete(this); }
}
```

### 5. Singleton-ish access / global registry
```js
class Logger {
  static instance = new Logger();
  log(msg) { console.log(msg); }
}
Logger.instance.log('ready');
```

### 6. Parsing / validation helpers tied to a type
```js
class Money {
  constructor(cents) { this.cents = cents; }
  static fromDollars(d) { return new Money(Math.round(d * 100)); }
  static isValid(v) { return Number.isInteger(v) && v >= 0; }
}
```

---

## Instance members: typical patterns

### Per-object state + behavior
```js
class Cart {
  items = [];
  add(item) { this.items.push(item); }
  total() { return this.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0); }
}
```

### Methods that operate on instance state
```js
class Timer {
  start = 0;
  begin() { this.start = Date.now(); }
  elapsed() { return Date.now() - this.start; }
}
```

### Getters/setters for encapsulated state
```js
class BankAccount {
  #balance = 0;
  get balance() { return this.#balance; }
  deposit(n) { if (n > 0) this.#balance += n; }
}
```

---

## Key differences at a glance

| Aspect | Static | Instance |
|---|---|---|
| Stored on | Constructor function | Each instance (or prototype for methods) |
| Access | `ClassName.member` | `instance.member` |
| `this` in method | The class | The instance |
| Shared across instances | Yes (one copy) | Methods shared via prototype; data usually unique |
| Use for | Utilities, factories, constants, registries | Per-object state and behavior |

---

## Pitfalls & best practices

1. **Don’t overuse static for stateful data.** Static mutable state creates hidden globals and makes testing harder. Prefer instance state or dependency injection.
2. **Private static fields** (`static #x`) are great for internal counters/registries; keep them truly private.
3. **Static methods are not polymorphic by default on instances.** You cannot call `instance.staticMethod()` unless inherited via prototype chain (statics live on constructor, not prototype). Usually call via class.
4. **Arrow-function class fields** capture `this`; use them when passing methods as callbacks, but note they create per-instance functions (memory cost) vs prototype methods.
5. **Initialization order:** instance fields are initialized before constructor body; static fields/init blocks run at class definition. Don’t rely on cross-class static init ordering unless carefully designed.
6. **Subclassing built-ins:** static inheritance works, but some built-ins have non-writable/static quirks; test thoroughly.

---

## Minimal combined example

```js
class Counter {
  static #all = new Set();
  static totalCreated = 0;

  #value = 0;

  constructor(initial = 0) {
    this.#value = initial;
    Counter.totalCreated++;
    Counter.#all.add(this);
  }

  inc() { this.#value++; }
  get value() { return this.#value; }

  static get activeCount() { return Counter.#all.size; }
  static resetAll() { for (const c of Counter.#all) c.#value = 0; }
}

const a = new Counter(10);
const b = new Counter();
a.inc();
console.log(a.value, b.value);      // 11 0
console.log(Counter.totalCreated);  // 2
console.log(Counter.activeCount);   // 2
Counter.resetAll();
console.log(a.value, b.value);      // 0 0
```

This shows instance data (`#value`) plus shared static state/behavior (`totalCreated`, `#all`, `activeCount`, `resetAll`) cooperating.

```js
// Mistaken version
class Product {
  static taxRate = 0.2;

  constructor(name, price) {
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
  }

  // Mistake: this is declared as a static method, but it uses `this.price`,
  // which is an instance property. In a static method, `this` refers to
  // the class itself, not to any Product instance.
  static priceWithTax() {
    return this.price * (1 + this.taxRate);
  }
}

const book = new Product("Book", 10);
console.log(Product.priceWithTax()); // NaN: this.price is undefined
```

### Corrected version

```js
class Product {
  static taxRate = 0.2;

  constructor(name, price) {
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
  }

  // Correct: this is an instance method, so `this` refers to the Product
  // instance. The static tax rate is accessed through the class name.
  priceWithTax() {
    return this.price * (1 + Product.taxRate);
  }
}

const book = new Product("Book", 10);
console.log(book.priceWithTax()); // 12
```

### What was wrong and why

- **Static members belong to the class/constructor**, not to instances.  
  `Product.taxRate` exists on `Product` itself. An instance such as `book` does not own its own `taxRate` by default.

- **Instance members belong to individual objects.**  
  `book.price` exists on the `book` instance, not on the `Product` class.

- In the mistaken code, `priceWithTax()` is `static`, so when calling `Product.priceWithTax()`, JavaScript sets `this` to `Product`. Therefore:
  - `this.taxRate` happens to work because `taxRate` is static.
  - `this.price` is `undefined` because `Product` has no `price` property.

- The corrected code makes `priceWithTax()` an **instance method**, so `this` refers to the actual `Product` instance, where `this.price` exists. The static rate is still reached safely through `Product.taxRate`.

### Alternative corrected static version

If you really want the method to remain static, pass the instance as an argument instead of relying on `this`:

```js
class Product {
  static taxRate = 0.2;

  constructor(name, price) {
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
  }

  static priceWithTax(product) {
    return product.price * (1 + this.taxRate);
  }
}

const book = new Product("Book", 10);
console.log(Product.priceWithTax(book)); // 12
```

In that version, `this` is still the class inside the static method, so `this.taxRate` works, while the instance data is explicitly provided through the `product` parameter.

Below are common pitfalls when mixing **static** and **instance** members in JavaScript classes, with runnable examples and fixes.

---

### 1. Losing `this` when passing an instance method as a callback

Instance methods are not bound to the instance by default.

```js
class Counter {
  count = 0;
  increment() {
    this.count++;
  }
}

const c = new Counter();
setTimeout(c.increment, 100); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'count')
```

**Fix:** use an arrow-function class field (bound instance method) or `.bind()`.

```js
class Counter {
  count = 0;
  increment = () => { this.count++; };
}
// or in constructor: this.increment = this.increment.bind(this);
```

---

### 2. Assuming static members exist on instances (and vice versa)

Static members live on the **constructor**, not on the prototype, so instances do not inherit them through the normal prototype chain.

```js
class User {
  static role = "admin";
  name = "Ada";
}

const u = new User();
console.log(u.role);              // undefined
console.log(u.constructor.role);  // "admin"
console.log(User.name);           // "User"  (function name!), not "Ada"
```

**Rule of thumb:** instance data is reached via `this`; static data via `this.constructor` (inside instance code) or the class name.

---

### 3. Shared mutable static state across all instances

A static array/object is shared by every instance. Mutating it mutates global-ish state.

```js
class Cart {
  static items = [];
  add(item) {
    this.constructor.items.push(item); // same as Cart.items.push(item)
  }
}

const a = new Cart();
const b = new Cart();
a.add("apple");
b.add("banana");
console.log(Cart.items); // ["apple", "banana"]  // leaked across instances
```

**Fix:** make it an instance field unless you truly want a singleton/class-level cache.

```js
class Cart {
  items = [];
  add(item) { this.items.push(item); }
}
```

---

### 4. `this` in a static method is the class, not an instance

Static methods cannot reach instance properties through `this`.

```js
class Foo {
  value = 42;
  static report() {
    console.log(this.value); // undefined (this === Foo)
  }
}
Foo.report();
```

**Fix:** pass the instance explicitly, or make the method an instance method.

```js
class Foo {
  value = 42;
  static report(instance) { console.log(instance.value); }
  report() { console.log(this.value); }
}
```

---

### 5. Instance field initializers cannot see static fields via `this`

In an instance field initializer, `this` is the instance, so `this.staticProp` is `undefined`.

```js
class Config {
  static defaults = { retries: 3 };
  settings = this.defaults;            // undefined
  // settings = this.constructor.defaults; // correct
  // settings = Config.defaults;          // also correct
}
console.log(new Config().settings); // undefined
```

---

### 6. Arrow-function instance fields are not on the prototype

They are own properties created per instance: higher memory use, not shared, and not patchable via the prototype.

```js
class Greeter {
  greet = () => "hi";   // instance own property
}

const g = new Greeter();
console.log(Object.hasOwn(g, "greet")); // true
console.log(Object.hasOwn(Greeter.prototype, "greet")); // false

// Patching the prototype does NOT affect existing instances:
Greeter.prototype.greet = () => "patched";
console.log(g.greet()); // still "hi"
```

Also, because they are fields, subclass override semantics differ from prototype methods; `super.greet()` may not behave as expected if the parent field has not been initialized yet.

---

### 7. Private static and private instance fields can share a name

`#x` (instance) and `static #x` (static) are distinct slots. Mixing them up causes confusing errors.

```js
class C {
  #x = 1;          // instance-private
  static #x = 2;   // static-private

  get instanceX() { return this.#x; }   // 1
  static get staticX() { return this.#x; } // 2
}

const c = new C();
console.log(c.instanceX);   // 1
console.log(C.staticX);     // 2
// c.staticX; // undefined (not inherited)
// There is no way to reach static #x from an instance.
```

Subclasses cannot access either `#x`, which is another common surprise.

---

### 8. Class declarations are not hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone)

Unlike function declarations, you cannot use a class before its declaration.

```js
const x = new Foo(); // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'Foo' before initialization
class Foo {}
```

This bites factory code and circular module dependencies.

---

### 9. Static inheritance only works through `extends`

Static members are inherited only when the subclass truly extends the base class (the subclass function’s `[[Prototype]]` is the base class).

```js
function Base() {}
Base.staticMethod = () => "base";

class Child extends Base {}
console.log(Child.staticMethod()); // "base"  (inherited)

// Without extends, no inheritance:
class Orphan {}
Object.setPrototypeOf(Orphan, Base); // manual, works but unusual
```

Also, if you override a static method and forget `super`, you lose base behavior:

```js
class Base { static greet() { return "base"; } }
class Child extends Base {
  static greet() {
    return super.greet() + " + child"; // correct
    // return this.greet() + " + child"; // infinite recursion
  }
}
```

---

### 10. Calling a static method with `new`

Static methods are ordinary functions on the constructor; they are not constructors unless you explicitly return an object. Accidentally using `new` usually creates a useless object.

```js
class Foo {
  static make() { return new Foo(); }
}

const x = new Foo.make(); // x is an object whose prototype is Foo.make.prototype (not Foo)
console.log(x instanceof Foo); // false
```

**Fix:** call static factories without `new`: `Foo.make()`.

---

### 11. Static blocks and initialization order

Static initialization blocks (ES2022) run in source order with static fields. Referencing a field before it is initialized yields `undefined`.

```js
class A {
  static a = 1;
  static {
    console.log(this.b); // undefined (b not yet initialized)
  }
  static b = 2;
}
```

Also, static blocks cannot use `await` at top level unless the class is in an async context? Actually static blocks can contain `await` only if the surrounding module/function is async; top-level class static block in a module cannot `await` directly. This surprises people expecting async class init.

---

### 12. Enumerability differences break `for...in` / `Object.keys`

Prototype methods and static methods are **non-enumerable**, but instance class fields are **own enumerable** properties. Mixing them changes iteration results.

```js
class Example {
  instanceField = 1;
  method() {}
  static staticField = 2;
  static staticMethod() {}
}

const e = new Example();
console.log(Object.keys(e));              // ["instanceField"]
console.log(Object.keys(Example));        // ["staticField"]  (static fields enumerable; static methods not)
```

If you rely on enumerating an object’s “API,” class methods will be missing.

---

### 13. Instance methods cannot be “statically” patched for all instances after creation

Because instance fields (especially arrow functions) become own properties, monkey-patching the prototype does not update existing instances. Static members, by contrast, are patched globally on the constructor.

```js
class Box {
  size = 10;
  getSize() { return this.size; } // prototype method
}
const b = new Box();
Box.prototype.getSize = function () { return 999; };
console.log(b.getSize()); // 999 (prototype method patched)

// But if getSize were an arrow field, patching prototype would NOT affect b.
```

---

### 14. Serialization drops all methods (static and instance)

`JSON.stringify` only serializes own enumerable properties. Instance methods and static members are never serialized, and class identity is lost on `JSON.parse`.

```js
class Point {
  constructor(x, y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
  distance() { return Math.hypot(this.x, this.y); }
  static origin = new Point(0, 0);
}

const p = new Point(3, 4);
const json = JSON.stringify(p);          // '{"x":3,"y":4}'
const back = JSON.parse(json);           // plain object, no methods, not a Point
console.log(back instanceof Point);      // false
console.log(back.distance);              // undefined
```

**Fix:** add a `toJSON`/reviver or reconstruct via a factory/static method.

---

### 15. Confusing `this.constructor` in subclasses

Inside an instance method, `this.constructor` may be the subclass, not the class where the method was defined. That is usually what you want for static factory methods, but it can surprise you.

```js
class Base {
  clone() { return new this.constructor(this.value); } // polymorphic
  static make() { return new this(); }                 // this = subclass if called as Sub.make()
}
class Sub extends Base { value = 7; }

const s = Sub.make();
console.log(s instanceof Sub); // true
const c = s.clone();
console.log(c instanceof Sub); // true
```

If you instead hardcode `new Base()` or `Base.make()`, you break polymorphism. Decide intentionally which you want.

---

### Quick checklist

- Bind instance methods used as callbacks (arrow fields or `.bind`).
- Reach statics via `ClassName` or `this.constructor`, never `this`.
- Treat static mutable state as shared/global; prefer instance state.
- Remember static methods see the class, not an instance.
- Don’t `new` a static method unless it returns an instance.
- Account for TDZ: classes aren’t hoisted.
- Keep private static and instance fields conceptually separate.
- Don’t assume prototype patching affects arrow-field instances.
- Rehydrate class identity after `JSON.parse`.

## Exercise

**Task:** Implement a `Product` class in JavaScript with the following rules:

1. Each instance stores `name` (string) and `price` (number).
2. The class tracks how many products have ever been created using a **static** property `totalProducts`, incremented in the constructor.
3. Provide a **static** method `getTotal()` that returns `totalProducts`.
4. Provide an **instance** method `applyDiscount(percent)` that returns the discounted price **without mutating** `this.price`.
5. Provide a **static** factory method `fromObject(obj)` that creates a `Product` from `{ name, price }`.

Then demonstrate usage and print results.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1 — Understand static vs instance

- **Instance members** (`this.name`, `this.price`, `applyDiscount`) belong to each object created with `new`.
- **Static members** (`Product.totalProducts`, `Product.getTotal`, `Product.fromObject`) belong to the class itself, not to any instance. They are accessed as `Product.xxx`, not `instance.xxx`.

### Step 2 — Define the class skeleton

```js
class Product {
  // static field (class-level state)
  static totalProducts = 0;

  constructor(name, price) {
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
    Product.totalProducts++; // increment shared counter
  }
}
```

### Step 3 — Add instance method

```js
applyDiscount(percent) {
  if (percent < 0 || percent > 100) throw new Error("percent must be 0..100");
  return this.price * (1 - percent / 100);
}
```

This does **not** change `this.price`, satisfying immutability.

### Step 4 — Add static methods

```js
static getTotal() {
  return Product.totalProducts;
}

static fromObject(obj) {
  if (!obj || typeof obj.name !== "string" || typeof obj.price !== "number") {
    throw new Error("Invalid object");
  }
  return new Product(obj.name, obj.price);
}
```

### Step 5 — Assemble and test

```js
class Product {
  static totalProducts = 0;

  constructor(name, price) {
    if (typeof name !== "string" || name.trim() === "") throw new Error("name required");
    if (typeof price !== "number" || price < 0) throw new Error("price must be non-negative number");
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
    Product.totalProducts++;
  }

  applyDiscount(percent) {
    if (typeof percent !== "number" || percent < 0 || percent > 100) {
      throw new Error("percent must be a number in [0, 100]");
    }
    return this.price * (1 - percent / 100);
  }

  static getTotal() {
    return Product.totalProducts;
  }

  static fromObject(obj) {
    return new Product(obj?.name, obj?.price);
  }

  toString() {
    return `${this.name}: $${this.price.toFixed(2)}`;
  }
}

// --- Usage ---
const a = new Product("Keyboard", 80);
const b = new Product("Mouse", 40);
const c = Product.fromObject({ name: "Monitor", price: 300 });

console.log(a.toString());                 // Keyboard: $80.00
console.log("discounted:", a.applyDiscount(25)); // 60
console.log("price unchanged:", a.price);   // 80
console.log("total:", Product.getTotal());  // 3
console.log("via instance? ", a.getTotal);  // undefined (static not on instance)
```

### Key takeaways

- `Product.totalProducts` is shared across **all** instances; `this.price` is per-instance.
- Static methods are called on the class: `Product.getTotal()`. They do not have access to `this` as an instance (inside a static method, `this` refers to the class).
- `applyDiscount` returns a new value instead of mutating, which is a good practice for predictable state.

### Common pitfalls

- Forgetting to increment via `Product.totalProducts++` (using `this.totalProducts++` inside constructor would create an instance property shadowing the static one).
- Calling a static method on an instance: `instance.getTotal()` → `undefined`.
- Mutating `this.price` inside `applyDiscount` when the spec says “without mutating.”

---

