# Edge Cases in JavaScript Statistics & Math

Below is a thorough catalogue of the edge cases that bite real code, each with a runnable example. I'll group them by theme.

---

## 1. Floating-Point Representation

### 1.1 Classic precision loss
```js
console.log(0.1 + 0.2);        // 0.30000000000000004
console.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3); // false

// Edge case: equality comparisons on floats
function approxEqual(a, b, eps = Number.EPSILON) {
  return Math.abs(a - b) < eps * Math.max(1, Math.abs(a), Math.abs(b));
}
```

### 1.2 Subnormal / very small numbers
```js
console.log(5e-324);   // smallest positive subnormal
console.log(1e-325);   // 0  (underflows to zero)
console.log(Number.MIN_VALUE * 2); // 1e-323? nope, still subnormal
```

### 1.3 Catastrophic cancellation
```js
// (a+b)-a should equal b, but doesn't always
const a = 1e16, b = 1;
console.log((a + b) - a); // 0, not 1
```

---

## 2. Special Numeric Values: `NaN`, `Infinity`, `-0`

### 2.1 `NaN` propagation and detection
```js
console.log(NaN === NaN);           // false (!)
console.log(Object.is(NaN, NaN));   // true

// Edge: every comparison with NaN is false
console.log(NaN > 0, NaN < 0, NaN === 0); // false false false

// Edge: NaN infects aggregations
[1, 2, NaN, 4].reduce((a,b) => a+b); // NaN
```

### 2.2 Infinity arithmetic
```js
console.log(1 / 0);        // Infinity
console.log(-1 / 0);       // -Infinity
console.log(Infinity - Infinity); // NaN
console.log(Infinity * 0);        // NaN
console.log(1 / Infinity);        // 0
console.log(Math.max(Infinity, 100)); // Infinity
```

### 2.3 Negative zero
```js
console.log(0 === -0);            // true
console.log(Object.is(0, -0));    // false
console.log(1 / -0);              // -Infinity
console.log(Math.sign(-0));       // -0  (not -1!)

// Edge: JSON.stringify(-0) === "0"; JSON.parse("0") === 0 (loses sign)
console.log(JSON.stringify(-0));  // "0"
```

### 2.4 Detection helpers
```js
function isFiniteNumber(x) {
  return typeof x === 'number' && Number.isFinite(x);
}
```

---

## 3. Division by Zero & Empty Aggregations

### 3.1 Mean of empty array
```js
const mean = arr => arr.reduce((s,x)=>s+x,0) / arr.length;
console.log(mean([])); // NaN  (0 / 0)
```

### 3.2 Variance / std-dev with n=1 or n=0
```js
function variance(arr, sample = true) {
  if (arr.length === 0) return NaN;
  if (sample && arr.length < 2) return NaN; // undefined
  const m = arr.reduce((s,x)=>s+x,0)/arr.length;
  const ss = arr.reduce((s,x)=>s+(x-m)**2, 0);
  const denom = sample ? arr.length - 1 : arr.length;
  return ss / denom; // 0/0 when n=0 sample
}
console.log(variance([]));      // NaN
console.log(variance([5], true)); // NaN
```

### 3.3 Median of empty / single
```js
function median(arr) {
  if (arr.length === 0) return NaN;
  const s = [...arr].sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const mid = s.length >> 1;
  return s.length % 2 ? s[mid] : (s[mid-1]+s[mid])/2;
}
```

---

## 4. `reduce` Pitfalls

### 4.1 Empty array without initial value throws
```js
[].reduce((a,b)=>a+b); // TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value
```

### 4.2 Using `reduce` for max/min with empty array
```js
Math.max(...[]); // -Infinity
Math.min(...[]); // Infinity
```

### 4.3 Spread operator stack limits
```js
const big = Array(200000).fill(1);
// Math.max(...big) -> RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
// Use reduce instead:
const mx = big.reduce((a,b)=>a>b?a:b, -Infinity);
```

---

## 5. Safe Integers & Large Numbers

### 5.1 Precision loss beyond 2^53
```js
console.log(9007199254740992 + 1); // 9007199254740992 (no change)
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(9007199254740993)); // false
```

### 5.2 Bitwise ops truncate to 32-bit signed
```js
console.log(0xFFFFFFFF | 0);          // -1
console.log(Math.pow(2,32) | 0);      // 0
console.log((2**31) >>> 0);           // 2147483648 (unsigned)
```

### 5.3 Parsing huge strings
```js
console.log(parseInt('9007199254740993', 10)); // 9007199254740992
console.log(Number('9007199254740993'));        // 9007199254740992
```

### 5.4 BigInt mixing
```js
// 1n + 1; // TypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types
const x = 1n + BigInt(1); // ok
console.log(typeof (x + 1n)); // bigint
```

