## Regular Expressions for Text Processing

Regex is central to JavaScript text processing.

```js
const text = "Email: ada@example.com, bob@test.org";

const emailRegex = /[a-z]+@[a-z]+\.[a-z]{2,}/gi;
console.log(text.match(emailRegex));
// ["ada@example.com", "bob@test.org"]
```

### Testing

```js
const re = /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/;
console.log(re.test("2025-01-31")); // true
console.log(re.test("2025-1-31"));  // false
```

### Replacing with capture groups

```js
const source = "Last, First";
const result = source.replace(/(\w+), (\w+)/, "$2 $1");
console.log(result); // "First Last"
```

### `matchAll`

```js
const text = "x=10 y=20 z=30";
const re = /(\w+)=(\d+)/g;

for (const match of text.matchAll(re)) {
  console.log(match[1], match[2]);
}
// x 10
// y 20
// z 30
```

### Unicode-aware regex

```js
const text = "café 😀";

// u flag: treat pattern as Unicode code points
console.log(/\p{Letter}+/u.test("café")); // true

// v flag: newer, enables set notation and better Unicode handling
console.log(/\p{Emoji}/v.test("😀")); // true
```

---

## Unicode and Character-Level Processing

JavaScript strings are UTF-16. For user-facing character processing, prefer code-point-aware APIs.

```js
const s = "😀";

console.log(s.length);                   // 2
console.log(s.codePointAt(0));           // 128512
console.log(String.fromCodePoint(128512)); // "😀"

// Iterate by code point
for (const ch of s) {
  console.log(ch); // "😀"
}
```

### Normalization

Accented characters may be represented differently:

```js
const a = "café";        // e + combining accent
const b = "café";        // single composed character

console.log(a === b); // false
console.log(a.normalize("NFC") === b.normalize("NFC")); // true
```

### Grapheme clusters (user-visible characters)

Some things that look like one character, such as emoji with skin tones or flags, consist of multiple code points. Use `Intl.Segmenter`:

```js
const text = "👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 hi";
const segmenter = new Intl.Segmenter("en", { granularity: "grapheme" });

const chars = [...segmenter.segment(text)].map(s => s.segment);
console.log(chars); // ["👨‍👩‍👧‍👦", " ", "h", "i"]
```

