## Step-by-Step Solution

### Step 1 — Declare variables with explicit, varied types

```js
const userName = "Ava";                 // string
let age = 28;                           // number
const isMember = true;                  // boolean
let couponCode = "SAVE5";               // string | null
let shippingNote;                       // undefined (not yet set)
const invoiceId = 9007199254740993n;   // BigInt

const cartItems = [
  { name: "Keyboard", price: 49.99, qty: 1 },
  { name: "Mouse",    price: "25.50", qty: 2 }, // string price on purpose
  { name: "USB Hub",  price: 19.0,   qty: 1 }
];
```

**Why this matters:** Real data often arrives as strings (e.g., from forms/JSON), so we must coerce safely.

### Step 2 — Validate and coerce types

```js
function toNumber(value) {
  const n = Number(value);
  if (!Number.isFinite(n)) {
    throw new TypeError(`Expected a finite number, got: ${value}`);
  }
  return n;
}

function normalizeItem(item) {
  if (!item || typeof item !== "object") throw new TypeError("Item must be an object");
  const name = String(item.name ?? "Unnamed");
  const price = toNumber(item.price);
  const qty = toNumber(item.qty);

  if (qty <= 0) throw new RangeError(`Quantity must be > 0 for ${name}`);
  if (price < 0) throw new RangeError(`Price cannot be negative for ${name}`);

  return { name, price, qty };
}
```

### Step 3 — Compute subtotal safely

```js
const normalized = cartItems.map(normalizeItem);

const subtotal = normalized.reduce((sum, item) => {
  return sum + item.price * item.qty;
}, 0);

// Round to avoid floating-point noise (e.g., 0.1 + 0.2)
const subtotalRounded = Math.round(subtotal * 100) / 100;
```

### Step 4 — Apply discounts conditionally

```js
let discountRate = 0;

if (isMember === true) discountRate += 0.10; // member discount

const hasCoupon = typeof couponCode === "string" && couponCode.trim() !== "";
if (hasCoupon) discountRate += 0.05;        // coupon discount

const discountAmount = subtotalRounded * discountRate;
const total = subtotalRounded - discountAmount;
```

**Type notes:**
- `isMember === true` avoids truthy coercion (e.g., `1` would otherwise pass).
- `typeof couponCode === "string"` ensures we don’t treat `null` as a string.

### Step 5 — Format and print

```js
const currency = (n) => n.toLocaleString("en-US", {
  style: "currency",
  currency: "USD"
});

const lines = [];
lines.push(`Customer: ${userName} (age ${age})`);
lines.push(`Member: ${isMember ? "Yes" : "No"}`);
lines.push("");
lines.push("Items:");
for (const item of normalized) {
  lines.push(`  - ${item.name}: ${item.qty} x ${currency(item.price)} = ${currency(item.price * item.qty)}`);
}
lines.push("");
lines.push(`Subtotal: ${currency(subtotalRounded)}`);
lines.push(`Discount: ${currency(discountAmount)} (${(discountRate * 100).toFixed(0)}%)`);
lines.push(`Total: ${currency(total)}`);
lines.push(`Invoice ID: ${invoiceId.toString()}`); // BigInt -> string
lines.push(`Note: ${shippingNote ?? "(no note provided)"}`); // nullish coalescing

console.log(lines.join("\n"));
```

