## Minimal end-to-end example (Node)

```js
import { createReadStream } from "node:fs";
import { parse } from "csv-parse";
import { stringify } from "csv-stringify";
import { createWriteStream } from "node:fs";

const input = createReadStream("in.csv").pipe(parse({ columns: true }));
const output = createWriteStream("out.csv");
const stringifier = stringify({ header: true });
stringifier.pipe(output);

for await (const row of input) {
  // transform
  row.fullName = `${row.firstName} ${row.lastName}`;
  stringifier.write(row);
}
stringifier.end();
```

**Summary:** treat CSV as text that needs a parser/stringifier; use libraries (PapaParse in browser, `csv-parse`/`csv-stringify` in Node), stream large files, and handle quoting, encoding, and injection carefully.

## Typical mistake

```js
const fs = require('fs');

const csv = fs.readFileSync('users.csv', 'utf8');

// ❌ Naive parsing
const rows = csv
  .split('\n')
  .map(line => line.split(','));

console.log(rows);
```

This looks fine for a trivial file like:

```csv
name,age,city
Alice,30,Paris
Bob,25,London
```

But it breaks on very common CSV cases.

---

## Corrected version

A small, dependency-free parser that handles quoted fields, commas inside quotes, quoted newlines, CRLF, and a UTF-8 BOM:

```js
const fs = require('fs');

function parseCSV(text) {
  // Remove UTF-8 BOM if present
  if (text.charCodeAt(0) === 0xFEFF) {
    text = text.slice(1);
  }

  const rows = [];
  let row = [];
  let field = '';
  let inQuotes = false;

  for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
    const char = text[i];

    if (inQuotes) {
      if (char === '"') {
        // "" inside a quoted field is an escaped quote
        if (text[i + 1] === '"') {
          field += '"';
          i++;
        } else {
          inQuotes = false;
        }
      } else {
        field += char;
      }
    } else {
      if (char === '"') {
        inQuotes = true;
      } else if (char === ',') {
        row.push(field);
        field = '';
      } else if (char === '\n') {
        row.push(field);
        rows.push(row);
        row = [];
        field = '';
      } else if (char === '\r') {
        // Accept CRLF; ignore a lone CR only when followed by LF
        if (text[i + 1] !== '\n') {
          row.push(field);
          rows.push(row);
          row = [];
          field = '';
        }
      } else {
        field += char;
      }
    }
  }

  if (inQuotes) {
    throw new Error('CSV parse error: unclosed quoted field');
  }

  // Push the final field/row if the file does not end with a newline
  if (field !== '' || row.length > 0) {
    row.push(field);
    rows.push(row);
  }

  return rows;
}

const raw = fs.readFileSync('users.csv', 'utf8');
const rows = parseCSV(raw);

// Convert rows into objects using the header row
const [header, ...data] = rows;
const records = data.map((row) =>
  Object.fromEntries(header.map((name, index) => [name, row[index] ?? '']))
);

console.log(records);
```

---

## What was wrong and why

### 1. `split(',')` does not understand quoting
CSV fields may contain commas if they are quoted:

```csv
name,comment
Alice,"Hello, world"
```

`"Hello, world"` must become one field, but naive splitting produces:

```js
['Alice', '"Hello', ' world"']
```

The corrected parser tracks `inQuotes`, so commas inside quotes are treated as literal characters.

### 2. `split('\n')` breaks on quoted newlines
A quoted field may legally contain a newline:

```csv
name,note
Alice,"Line one
Line two"
```

Splitting the whole text by `\n` first breaks the record into pieces before CSV semantics are considered. The corrected parser only ends a row on an unquoted newline.

### 3. CRLF files leave `\r` in the last field
Many CSV files use `\r\n` line endings. With `split('\n')`, every last field keeps a trailing `\r`:

```js
"Paris\r"
```

The corrected parser recognizes `\r\n` as one line ending and ignores the `\r`.

### 4. UTF-8 BOM can corrupt the first header name
Files exported from Excel often start with a BOM (`U+FEFF`). Naive parsing leaves it attached to the first header:

```js
"\uFEFFname" !== "name"
```

Then `record["name"]` is `undefined`. The corrected parser strips the BOM before parsing.

### 5. Escaped quotes need special handling
In CSV, a quote inside a quoted field is represented by two quotes:

```csv
name,quote
Alice,"She said ""hi"""
```

Naive splitting keeps the doubled quotes. The corrected parser converts `""` into a single `"`.

### 6. Trailing newline handling
A file ending in `\n` should not create an extra empty row. The corrected parser only pushes a final row when there is actual trailing content.

