## Comparison in practice

### Arrays win when you need:

```ts
const users: { id: number; name: string }[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ada" },
  { id: 2, name: "Grace" },
];

// Random access
console.log(users[1].name); // "Grace"

// Built-in algorithms
const byId = users.find(u => u.id === 2);

// Serialization
const json = JSON.stringify(users);
```

### Linked lists win when you need:

- Frequent **prepend/remove at head** (e.g., maintaining recent items)
- **Splice** without shifting many elements
- Stable node references (advanced data structures: LRU cache internal buckets, graph adjacency via lists)
- Teaching/algorithm work (reverse list, cycle detection, merge sorted lists)

Example: simple recent-items list where newest goes to front:

```ts
const recent = new SinglyLinkedList<string>();

function visitPage(page: string) {
  recent.prepend(page);
}

visitPage("/home");
visitPage("/docs");
visitPage("/about");

console.log(recent.toArray()); // ["/about", "/docs", "/home"]
```

### Real-world TypeScript/JS guidance

- Use arrays for almost everything.
- If you need an efficient queue, arrays with `shift()` are `O(n)`. For heavy queue workloads, consider a **deque** implementation or a library, not a basic singly linked list unless you only need front operations.
- If you need a stack, arrays are ideal: `push`/`pop`.
- If you need insertion/deletion in the middle frequently, arrays still often beat linked lists in JavaScript due to cache locality and V8 optimizations, but measure your workload.
- Prefer immutable array updates in state management:
  ```ts
  const next = [...items, newItem];
  const removed = items.filter((_, i) => i !== index);
  ```

### Mental model

- **Array**: “Give me index 47” is instant, but “insert before index 0” shifts everything.
- **Linked list**: “Add to front” is instant, but “give me item 47” requires walking 47 nodes.

That tradeoff is the entire decision.

A common mistake is using a plain array as a queue and calling `shift()` to remove the front element. That operation is `O(n)` because every remaining element must be re-indexed, so repeated dequeues make the whole program `O(n²)`.

### Mistaken version

```ts
class Queue<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  enqueue(value: T): void {
    this.items.push(value);
  }

  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    // Typical mistake: O(n) removal from the front of an array
    return this.items.shift();
  }

  size(): number {
    return this.items.length;
  }
}
```

### Corrected version

```ts
class Node<T> {
  constructor(public value: T, public next: Node<T> | null = null) {}
}

class LinkedListQueue<T> {
  private head: Node<T> | null = null;
  private tail: Node<T> | null = null;
  private count = 0;

  enqueue(value: T): void {
    const node = new Node(value);

    if (!this.tail) {
      this.head = this.tail = node;
    } else {
      this.tail.next = node;
      this.tail = node;
    }

    this.count++;
  }

  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    if (!this.head) return undefined;

    const value = this.head.value;
    this.head = this.head.next;

    if (!this.head) {
      this.tail = null;
    }

    this.count--;
    return value;
  }

  size(): number {
    return this.count;
  }
}
```

### What was wrong and why

- `Array.prototype.shift()` removes the first element, then moves every later element one position to the left. For an array of length `n`, that is an `O(n)` operation.
- In a queue, dequeuing is expected to be cheap, ideally `O(1)`. Doing `shift()` repeatedly turns a simple queue into an `O(n²)` algorithm.
- A singly linked list with both `head` and `tail` pointers fixes this: `enqueue` appends after `tail`, and `dequeue` advances `head`. Both operations touch only a constant number of pointers, so they are `O(1)`.
- The linked-list version also keeps an explicit `count` instead of scanning nodes to compute size, preserving `O(1)` size queries.
- One trade-off worth noting: arrays have better cache locality and are usually faster for small collections or workloads dominated by random access. The linked list is the better choice when you frequently insert or remove at the front/back and need predictable `O(1)` mutation cost.

# Arrays vs. Linked Lists in TypeScript: Common Pitfalls

Below are the most common pitfalls, grouped by data structure, with minimal reproducible code. I assume strict-ish TS (`strict: true`); several pitfalls only bite under (or are hidden without) `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`.

---

## Part 1 — Arrays

### 1. Mutating methods return surprising values
`push`, `unshift`, `splice` return the new **length**, not the array. `sort`, `reverse`, `splice` mutate in place and return the same array (easy to chain, easy to miss).

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
const x = arr.push(4);        // x === 4 (length), not the array
const y = arr.sort();         // y === arr (same reference, mutated)
console.log(arr);             // [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

**Fix:** treat `push` as statement; use spread/`concat`/`toSorted` (ES2023) when you want purity.

---

### 2. Default `sort` is lexicographic
Numbers are converted to strings.

```ts
[10, 2, 1].sort();                    // [1, 10, 2]
[10, 2, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b);    // [1, 2, 10]
```

---

### 3. Sparse arrays and “holes”
`new Array(n)` creates holes; many methods skip holes, producing confusing lengths/results.

```ts
const a = new Array(3);            // [ <3 empty items> ]
a.map(x => x + 1);                // [ <3 empty items> ]  (map skips holes)
a.forEach(x => console.log(x));    // nothing printed
console.log(a.length);             // 3
console.log(1 in a);               // false
```

**Fix:** `Array.from({ length: 3 }, () => 0)` or `new Array(3).fill(0)`.

---

### 4. Iterating while mutating
Splicing inside a forward loop skips elements.

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  if (arr[i] % 2 === 0) arr.splice(i, 1);
}
console.log(arr); // [1, 3, 5]? Actually [1, 3, 4, 5] — 4 skipped
```

**Fix:** iterate backwards, or filter into a new array:
```ts
const odd = arr.filter(n => n % 2 !== 0);
```

---

### 5. Index access is typed as `T`, but is `undefined` at runtime
Without `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `arr[i]` is `T` even for out-of-bounds.

```ts
const arr: number[] = [1, 2, 3];
const x = arr[99];          // typed as number, actually undefined
x.toFixed();                // compiles, throws at runtime
```

**Fix:** enable `noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true` in `tsconfig.json`, or guard:
```ts
const x = arr[99];
if (x !== undefined) x.toFixed();
```

---

### 6. Unsounded array covariance
TS allows `Dog[]` → `Animal[]`. Mutating through the wider type corrupts the original.

```ts
class Animal { name = "a"; }
class Dog extends Animal { bark() {} }

const dogs: Dog[] = [new Dog()];
const animals: Animal[] = dogs;   // allowed (unsound)
animals.push(new Animal());        // compiles
// dogs now contains an Animal that isn't a Dog
dogs[1].bark();                   // runtime error
```

**Fix:** treat arrays as invariant in your own code; use `readonly` parameters and avoid pushing through widened references.

---

### 7. `readonly` arrays and mutation surprises
`readonly T[]` prevents mutation methods, but a mutable array is assignable to it — and the original can still be mutated elsewhere.

```ts
function print(a: readonly number[]) { /* can't push */ }
const m: number[] = [1, 2];
print(m);          // ok
m.push(3);         // mutates the very array print "saw"
```

Also note: `readonly number[]` ≠ `ReadonlyArray<number>` in some inference edge cases, and tuples `readonly [a, b]` are distinct from arrays.

---

### 8. Tuple vs. array confusion
`[string, number]` is a tuple (fixed length, positional types); `string[]` is an array. Spreading/concatenation widens tuples.

```ts
const t: [number, string] = [1, "a"];
const a = [...t, true];          // (number | string | boolean)[]
const u = t.concat([2, "b"]);   // (number | string)[]
```

`as const` freezes literals into readonly tuples:
```ts
const r = [1, 2] as const;      // readonly [1, 2]
r.push(3);                      // error
```

---

### 9. `find` / `findIndex` return possibly-`undefined`
```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
const x = arr.find(n => n > 10);  // number | undefined
x?.toFixed();                     // must narrow
```

---

### 10. `forEach` returns `undefined`; async callbacks are not awaited
```ts
const doubled = arr.forEach(x => x * 2);   // undefined, not an array

const results = arr.map(async x => fetch(`/api/${x}`));
// results: Promise<Response>[]  — not awaited!
await Promise.all(results);
```

---

### 11. Destructuring defaults only trigger on `undefined`, not `null`
```ts
const [a = 5] = [null];   // a: null, NOT 5
const [b = 5] = [undefined]; // b: 5
```

---

### 12. `Object.keys` / `for...in` on arrays
Keys are **strings**, and inherited enumerable props may appear.

```ts
const arr = ["a", "b"];
Object.keys(arr).forEach(k => {
  console.log(typeof k);      // "string"
  // arr[k] is string-indexed; with noUncheckedIndexedAccess -> string | undefined
});
```

Prefer `for (const x of arr)`, `arr.forEach`, or `arr.entries()`.

---

### 13. Reference sharing / accidental mutation
```ts
const a = [1, 2, 3];
const b = a;          // same reference
a.length = 0;         // b is now []
b.push(9);            // a is now [9]
```

Shallow copy: `[...a]`, `a.slice()`, `Array.from(a)`. Deep copy needs `structuredClone`.

---

### 14. `Array` constructor overloads
```ts
new Array(3);        // 3 holes
new Array("3");      // ["3"]
new Array(1, 2, 3);  // [1, 2, 3]
```

---

### 15. `includes` typing and `NaN`
`arr.includes(NaN)` works for `number[]` (uses SameValueZero), but for `any[]`/union arrays the needle type is restricted.

```ts
const nums = [1, 2, NaN];
nums.includes(NaN);            // true
const mixed: (number | string)[] = [1, "a"];
mixed.includes(2);             // compile error: 2 not in (number|string)
```

---

## Part 2 — Linked Lists (custom implementations)

TS has no built-in linked list, so pitfalls are mostly implementation + typing.

### 16. A correct node/link type with nullability
```ts
class Node<T> {
  constructor(public value: T, public next: Node<T> | null = null) {}
}

class LinkedList<T> {
  head: Node<T> | null = null;
  tail: Node<T> | null = null;
  size = 0;
}
```

The single most common bug: forgetting `null` in `next`/`prev` types, then getting runtime `Cannot read properties of null`.

---

### 17. Edge cases: empty list, single node, head/tail updates
```ts
append(value: T) {
  const node = new Node(value);
  if (!this.tail) {                 // empty list
    this.head = this.tail = node;
  } else {
    this.tail.next = node;
    this.tail = node;              // forgetting this → tail goes stale
  }
  this.size++;
}

removeLast() {
  if (!this.head) return;
  if (this.head === this.tail) {    // single node
    this.head = this.tail = null;
    return;
  }
  // walk to predecessor
  let curr = this.head;
  while (curr.next !== this.tail) curr = curr.next!;
  curr.next = null;
  this.tail = curr;                 // forgetting → dangling tail
  this.size--;
}
```

---

### 18. Reversing: losing the rest of the list
Classic bug: overwrite `next` before saving it.

```ts
// WRONG
let prev: Node<T> | null = null;
let curr = this.head;
while (curr) {
  curr.next = prev;          // rest of list lost
  prev = curr;
  curr = curr.next;          // now null → loop ends early, list truncated
}

// CORRECT
let prev: Node<T> | null = null;
let curr = this.head;
while (curr) {
  const next = curr.next;    // save first
  curr.next = prev;
  prev = curr;
  curr = next;
}
this.head = prev;
```

---

### 19. Mutating while traversing
Removing the current node and then doing `curr = curr.next` skips or crashes.

```ts
// WRONG: removing then advancing via stale next
let curr = this.head;
while (curr) {
  if (shouldRemove(curr)) this.removeNode(curr);
  curr = curr.next;          // may be null after removal
}

// BETTER: track previous
let prev: Node<T> | null = null;
let curr = this.head;
while (curr) {
  const next = curr.next;
  if (shouldRemove(curr)) {
    if (prev) prev.next = next;
    else this.head = next;
    if (curr === this.tail) this.tail = prev;
    this.size--;
  } else {
    prev = curr;
  }
  curr = next;
}
```

---

### 20. Deep recursion / stack overflow
Recursive traversal on a 10⁵-node list overflows the call stack.

```ts
// risky
function length(n: Node<T> | null): number {
  return n ? 1 + length(n.next) : 0;
}

// safe
function length(head: Node<T> | null): number {
  let count = 0;
  for (let n = head; n; n = n.next) count++;
  return count;
}
```

Also avoid recursive type aliases that force deep instantiation; keep node types shallow.

---

### 21. Reference vs. value equality
`===` compares node identity, not value. Searching by value needs an equality predicate.

```ts
find(value: T): Node<T> | null {
  for (let n = this.head; n; n = n.next) {
    if (Object.is(n.value, value)) return n;   // or a custom equals
  }
  return null;
}
```

For objects, `Object.is` is reference equality; pass an `equals` function for deep comparison.

---

### 22. Doubly linked lists: keeping `prev`/`next` consistent
Forgetting one direction leaves a half-linked structure.

```ts
insertAfter(node: Node<T>, value: T) {
  const n = new Node(value);
  n.next = node.next;
  n.prev = node;            // doubly linked
  if (node.next) node.next.prev = n;
  node.next = n;
  if (node === this.tail) this.tail = n;
}
```

---

### 23. Sentinel / dummy nodes trade-offs
A dummy head simplifies insertion/deletion but changes indexing and `size` accounting.

```ts
class LinkedList<T> {
  private dummy = new Node<T>(null as unknown as T); // sentinel
  head = this.dummy.next; // careful: must sync
}
```

Pitfall: sentinel value must be typed around (often `any`/`unknown`), weakening type safety; and `size` must not count the sentinel.

---

### 24. `this` binding when passing methods as callbacks
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
const f = list.append;   // `this` lost
f(5);                    // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
```

**Fix:** bind in constructor, use arrow fields, or call as `list.append(5)`.

---

### 25. Serialization / JSON
`JSON.stringify` on a linked list recurses through `next` (huge output) and throws on cycles.

```ts
toJSON() {
  const out: T[] = [];
  for (let n = this.head; n; n = n.next) out.push(n.value);
  return out;
}
```
Implement `toJSON()` (or a `Symbol.iterator`) to control output. Also implement `Symbol.iterator` for `for...of`.

---

### 26. Memory / dangling references
Removed nodes may linger if something still references them (e.g., an iterator, a cached node). In GC languages this is usually fine, but in long-lived structures, clear `node.next = null` on removal to avoid retaining large sublists.

```ts
removeNode(node: Node<T>) {
  // ... unlink ...
  node.next = null;   // help GC, avoid accidental traversal
}
```

---

### 27. Cycle detection (if your list can become circular)
Floyd’s algorithm; naive traversal never terminates.

```ts
hasCycle(): boolean {
  let slow = this.head, fast = this.head;
  while (fast?.next) {
    slow = slow!.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

---

## Part 3 — Choosing between them (pitfall-level)

- **Default to arrays.** JS arrays are dynamic, cache-friendly, and heavily optimized; linked lists are rarely faster in JS/TS due to object overhead and poor locality.
- Reaching for a linked list for “O(1) insert” ignores that you usually must traverse O(n) to find the position, and you lose index access.
- If you need queue/deque semantics, prefer a ring buffer or two-stack queue over a hand-rolled linked list.
- For immutable updates, arrays + spread are usually simpler and safer than rebuilding linked-list pointers.

---

### Quick checklist
- Arrays: watch mutation return values, `sort` comparator, holes, covariance, index typing, async `map`.
- Linked lists: watch nullability, head/tail on empty/single, save `next` before rewire, iterative over recursive, update both directions, clear references on removal, provide `toJSON`/iterator.

If you want, I can expand any single pitfall (e.g., a full type-safe `LinkedList<T>` with tests, or array covariance workarounds) into a deeper dive.

## Exercise

**Build a “Playlist” manager** that supports these operations:

1. `append(song)` – add a song to the end  
2. `insertAt(index, song)` – insert a song at a specific position  
3. `removeAt(index)` – remove the song at a position  
4. `getAt(index)` – retrieve a song by index  
5. iteration – loop over songs in order  

Implement it **twice**: once with a native TypeScript/JavaScript `Array`, and once with a **singly linked list**. Then answer:

- Which operations are `O(1)` vs `O(n)` in each implementation?
- In what real scenarios would you pick one over the other?

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1 — Define the shared type

Both implementations work with the same `Song` shape.

```ts
interface Song {
  id: string;
  title: string;
}
```

### Step 2 — Array-backed playlist

Arrays in JS/TS are dynamic arrays. Appending is amortized `O(1)`, random access is `O(1)`, but inserting/removing in the middle shifts elements, so it is `O(n)`.

```ts
class ArrayPlaylist {
  private items: Song[] = [];

  append(song: Song): void {
    this.items.push(song);
  }

  insertAt(index: number, song: Song): boolean {
    if (index < 0 || index > this.items.length) return false;
    this.items.splice(index, 0, song);
    return true;
  }

  removeAt(index: number): Song | undefined {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.items.length) return undefined;
    return this.items.splice(index, 1)[0];
  }

  getAt(index: number): Song | undefined {
    return this.items[index];
  }

  get length(): number {
    return this.items.length;
  }

  [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<Song> {
    return this.items[Symbol.iterator]();
  }
}
```

### Step 3 — Singly linked list playlist

A linked list stores nodes connected by `next` pointers. We keep both `head` and `tail` so `append` can be `O(1)`. Index-based access and middle mutations require traversal, so they are `O(n)`.

```ts
class Node {
  constructor(public value: Song, public next: Node | null = null) {}
}

class LinkedListPlaylist {
  private head: Node | null = null;
  private tail: Node | null = null;
  private size = 0;

  append(song: Song): void {
    const node = new Node(song);
    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = this.tail = node;
    } else {
      this.tail!.next = node;
      this.tail = node;
    }
    this.size++;
  }

  insertAt(index: number, song: Song): boolean {
    if (index < 0 || index > this.size) return false;

    const node = new Node(song);

    if (index === 0) {
      node.next = this.head;
      this.head = node;
      if (!this.tail) this.tail = node;
      this.size++;
      return true;
    }

    if (index === this.size) {
      this.append(song);
      return true;
    }

    // Traverse to the node just before the insertion point.
    let prev = this.head;
    for (let i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {
      prev = prev!.next;
    }

    node.next = prev!.next;
    prev!.next = node;
    this.size++;
    return true;
  }

  removeAt(index: number): Song | undefined {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.size) return undefined;

    let removed: Node;

    if (index === 0) {
      removed = this.head!;
      this.head = removed.next;
      if (!this.head) this.tail = null;
    } else {
      let prev = this.head;
      for (let i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {
        prev = prev!.next;
      }
      removed = prev!.next!;
      prev!.next = removed.next;

      if (index === this.size - 1) {
        this.tail = prev;
      }
    }

    this.size--;
    return removed.value;
  }

  getAt(index: number): Song | undefined {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.size) return undefined;

    let current = this.head;
    for (let i = 0; i < index; i++) {
      current = current!.next;
    }
    return current!.value;
  }

  get length(): number {
    return this.size;
  }

  [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<Song> {
    let current = this.head;
    return {
      next(): IteratorResult<Song> {
        if (!current) return { done: true, value: undefined };
        const value = current.value;
        current = current.next;
        return { done: false, value };
      },
    };
  }
}
```

### Step 4 — Use both implementations side by side

```ts
const songs: Song[] = [
  { id: "1", title: "Intro" },
  { id: "2", title: "Verse" },
  { id: "3", title: "Chorus" },
];

const arr = new ArrayPlaylist();
const list = new LinkedListPlaylist();

for (const song of songs) {
  arr.append(song);
  list.append(song);
}

arr.insertAt(1, { id: "x", title: "Bridge" });
list.insertAt(1, { id: "x", title: "Bridge" });

console.log("Array:", [...arr].map((s) => s.title));
console.log("List:", [...list].map((s) => s.title));

console.log("Array getAt(2):", arr.getAt(2)?.title);
console.log("List getAt(2):", list.getAt(2)?.title);

console.log("Removed from array:", arr.removeAt(0)?.title);
console.log("Removed from list:", list.removeAt(0)?.title);
```

### Step 5 — Complexity comparison

| Operation | ArrayPlaylist | LinkedListPlaylist |
|---|---:|---:|
| `append` | `O(1)` amortized | `O(1)` |
| `insertAt(0, ...)` | `O(n)` (shifts all) | `O(1)` |
| `insertAt(middle)` | `O(n)` | `O(n)` (traversal) |
| `removeAt(0)` | `O(n)` | `O(1)` |
| `removeAt(middle/end)` | `O(n)` | `O(n)` (traversal) |
| `getAt(index)` | `O(1)` | `O(n)` |
| iteration | `O(n)`, cache-friendly | `O(n)`, pointer chasing |

### Step 6 — When to choose which

- **Prefer arrays** for most application code: random access, iteration speed, low memory overhead, and excellent CPU cache locality.
- **Prefer linked lists** when you frequently insert/remove at the front, or when you already hold a node reference and can splice in `O(1)` (for example, LRU caches, undo stacks, or certain queue/deque patterns).
- In TypeScript/JavaScript, a real-world “linked list” is often better represented by an array plus an index, or by a `Map`/`Set`, unless pointer-based splicing is genuinely needed.

