This gives you both implementations, a clear complexity story, and guidance for choosing between them in real TypeScript projects.

## Arrays vs Linked Lists in TypeScript

### Big picture

| Feature | Array (`T[]`) | Linked List (`ListNode<T>`) |
|---|---|---|
| **Access by index** | `O(1)` | `O(n)` |
| **Insert at beginning** | `O(n)` (shifts elements) | `O(1)` |
| **Insert at end** | `O(1)` amortized with `push` | `O(1)` if tail is maintained |
| **Insert in middle** | `O(n)` | `O(n)` to find node, then `O(1)` |
| **Delete by index/value** | `O(n)` | `O(n)` to find node, then `O(1)` |
| **Memory layout** | Contiguous-ish, cache-friendly | Scattered nodes, extra pointer per node |
| **Growth** | Dynamic capacity, usually doubles | One node at a time |
| **Best use** | Random access, dense data, iteration | Frequent head insertions, unknown size, stable node references |

### TypeScript-specific notes

- Arrays are typed with `T[]`, tuples like `[string, number]`, or `ReadonlyArray<T>`.
- Linked lists are usually implemented manually with generics: `class LinkedList<T>`.
- TypeScript does **not** provide a built-in linked list, so you own pointer/null handling.
- For numeric data, `TypedArray` (`Int32Array`, `Float64Array`) can be even more memory-efficient than `number[]`.

---

## Core implementation: generic singly linked list

This implementation keeps both `head` and `tail`, so prepend and append are `O(1)`.

```ts
/**
 * A single node in a singly linked list.
 */
class ListNode<T> {
  constructor(
    public value: T,
    public next: ListNode<T> | null = null,
  ) {}
}

/**
 * A generic singly linked list with O(1) prepend/append.
 *
 * Trade-offs vs Array:
 * - No random access: get(index) is O(n).
 * - Insert/delete near the front is cheap.
 * - Every node carries an extra `next` pointer.
 */
export class SinglyLinkedList<T> implements Iterable<T> {
  private head: ListNode<T> | null = null;
  private tail: ListNode<T> | null = null;
  private _size = 0;

  /** Number of stored elements. */
  get size(): number {
    return this._size;
  }

  isEmpty(): boolean {
    return this._size === 0;
  }

  /** Add to the front in O(1). */
  prepend(value: T): void {
    const node = new ListNode(value, this.head);
    this.head = node;

    // First element: tail must point to it too.
    if (this.tail === null) {
      this.tail = node;
    }

    this._size++;
  }

  /** Add to the back in O(1). */
  append(value: T): void {
    const node = new ListNode(value);

    if (this.tail === null) {
      // Empty list: node is both head and tail.
      this.head = this.tail = node;
    } else {
      this.tail.next = node;
      this.tail = node;
    }

    this._size++;
  }

  /** Insert at a valid index in [0, size]. */
  insertAt(index: number, value: T): void {
    if (index < 0 || index > this._size) {
      throw new RangeError(
        `Index ${index} is out of bounds for size ${this._size}`,
      );
    }

    if (index === 0) {
      this.prepend(value);
      return;
    }

    if (index === this._size) {
      this.append(value);
      return;
    }

    // Find the node immediately before the insertion point.
    let previous = this.head!;
    for (let i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {
      previous = previous.next!;
    }

    const node = new ListNode(value, previous.next);
    previous.next = node;
    this._size++;
  }

  /** Remove and return the value at index. */
  removeAt(index: number): T {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this._size) {
      throw new RangeError(
        `Index ${index} is out of bounds for size ${this._size}`,
      );
    }

    // Special case: removing the head.
    if (index === 0) {
      const removed = this.head!;
      this.head = removed.next;

      // List became empty.
      if (this.head === null) {
        this.tail = null;
      }

      this._size--;
      return removed.value;
    }

    // Find previous node, then unlink target.
    let previous = this.head!;
    for (let i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {
      previous = previous.next!;
    }

    const removed = previous.next!;
    previous.next = removed.next;

    // If we removed the tail, update tail.
    if (removed === this.tail) {
      this.tail = previous;
    }

    this._size--;
    return removed.value;
  }

  /** Remove the first node whose value is strictly equal to `value`. */
  remove(value: T): boolean {
    if (this.head === null) return false;

    // Head removal.
    if (Object.is(this.head.value, value)) {
      this.head = this.head.next;
      if (this.head === null) {
        this.tail = null;
      }
      this._size--;
      return true;
    }

    let previous = this.head;
    let current = this.head.next;

    while (current !== null) {
      if (Object.is(current.value, value)) {
        previous.next = current.next;

        // Keep tail correct.
        if (current === this.tail) {
          this.tail = previous;
        }

        this._size--;
        return true;
      }

      previous = current;
      current = current.next;
    }

    return false;
  }

  /** O(n) random access, provided for completeness. */
  get(index: number): T | undefined {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this._size) return undefined;

    let current = this.head;
    for (let i = 0; i < index; i++) {
      current = current!.next;
    }

    return current?.value;
  }

  /** Find first value matching a predicate. */
  find(predicate: (value: T) => boolean): T | undefined {
    for (const value of this) {
      if (predicate(value)) return value;
    }
    return undefined;
  }

  /** Convert to a plain array. */
  toArray(): T[] {
    const result: T[] = [];
    for (const value of this) result.push(value);
    return result;
  }

  /** Remove all elements. */
  clear(): void {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
    this._size = 0;
  }

  /** Iteration support: for...of, spread, Array.from. */
  *[Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<T> {
    let current = this.head;
    while (current !== null) {
      yield current.value;
      current = current.next;
    }
  }
}
```

---

## Usage example

```ts
const list = new SinglyLinkedList<number>();

list.append(10);
list.append(20);
list.prepend(5);

console.log(list.toArray()); // [5, 10, 20]
console.log(list.get(1));    // 10
console.log(list.size);      // 3

list.insertAt(1, 7);
console.log(list.toArray()); // [5, 7, 10, 20]

list.remove(10);
console.log(list.toArray()); // [5, 7, 20]

for (const value of list) {
  console.log(value); // 5, 7, 20
}
```

