## Interview takeaway

Use an **array** when you need fast indexing, dense storage, iteration, or built-in methods like `map`, `filter`, and `reduce`. Use a **linked list** when insertions/deletions at the front or middle are frequent and node identity matters. In JavaScript/TypeScript, arrays are usually the better default because they are highly optimized and cache-friendly; linked lists shine mainly in algorithmic problems, queues, LRU caches, and cases where `O(1)` splice operations are important.

# Arrays vs. Linked Lists in TypeScript: Edge Cases

Below are the most common (and dangerous) edge cases for each structure, with runnable TypeScript examples. I assume `strict: true` (especially `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`) since that changes array element types to `T | undefined`.

---

## Part 1 — Arrays

### 1. Out-of-bounds reads return `undefined`, not an error
```ts
const nums: number[] = [10, 20, 30];

// With noUncheckedIndexedAccess: type is `number | undefined`
const x = nums[99]; // undefined at runtime, type `number | undefined`

// Without it, type is `number` but value is still undefined → silent bug
if (x !== undefined) {
  console.log(x.toFixed(2)); // safe only because of the guard
}
```
**Edge case:** code that assumes `arr[i]` is always defined will compile (without `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`) but crash at runtime.

---

### 2. Sparse arrays and “holes”
```ts
const sparse = new Array<number>(3); // [ <3 empty items> ]
sparse[1] = 42;

console.log(sparse.length);        // 3
console.log(0 in sparse);         // false  (hole)
console.log(sparse[0]);            // undefined (indistinguishable from a real undefined)

// Iteration skips holes
sparse.forEach((v, i) => console.log(i, v)); // only prints "1 42"
for (const v of sparse) console.log(v);      // prints undefined, 42, undefined
```
**Edge case:** `forEach`/`map`/`filter` skip holes, but `for...of` and spread do not. Mixing them causes inconsistent counts.

---

### 3. Mutating an array while iterating it
```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

// BUG: removing items shifts indices; "3" is skipped
arr.forEach((v, i) => {
  if (v % 2 === 0) arr.splice(i, 1);
});
console.log(arr); // [1, 3, 4, 5]  (4 survived!)

// Safer: iterate backwards or build a new array
for (let i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  if (arr[i]! % 2 === 0) arr.splice(i, 1);
}
```

---

### 4. `sort()` mutates in place and is not stable in older engines
```ts
const items = [{ n: 2 }, { n: 1 }, { n: 2 }];
const sorted = items.sort((a, b) => a.n - b.n); // mutates `items` too!
console.log(items === sorted); // true

// Edge case: default sort coerces to string
[10, 2, 1].sort(); // [1, 10, 2]  ← wrong for numbers
```

---

### 5. Length truncation / extension
```ts
const a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
a.length = 2;          // truncates → [1, 2]
a.length = 5;          // extends with holes → [1, 2, <3 empty>]
console.log(3 in a);   // false
```

---

### 6. Negative indices are not supported
```ts
const arr = [10, 20, 30];
console.log(arr[-1]); // undefined (not 30)
// Use arr.at(-1) (ES2022) instead:
console.log(arr.at(-1)); // 30
```

---

### 7. Reference vs. value semantics
```ts
const matrix: number[][] = [[1, 2], [3, 4]];
const row = matrix[0]!;   // reference, not copy
row.push(99);
console.log(matrix);      // [[1, 2, 99], [3, 4]]

// Shallow copy pitfalls
const copy = [...matrix];
copy[0]!.push(0);        // mutates original row too
```

---

### 8. `readonly` arrays protect the container, not the elements
```ts
type Item = { value: number };
const arr: readonly Item[] = [{ value: 1 }];
// arr.push(...)        // ❌ compile error
arr[0]!.value = 99;    // ✅ allowed — element is mutable
```

---

### 9. Tuple types vs. arrays
```ts
const t: [number, string] = [1, "a"];
// t[2] = 3;           // ❌ compile error (fixed length)
const arr: (number | string)[] = t; // widening loses tuple info

// Rest elements
type Head<T extends any[]> = T extends [infer H, ...any[]] ? H : never;
```

---

### 10. Very large arrays and memory
```ts
// Allocating huge arrays can throw or be slow
const big = new Array(1e8); // ~800MB of holes (still costly)
// Prefer typed arrays for numeric data:
const typed = new Float64Array(1e6); // contiguous, cache-friendly
```

---

## Part 2 — Linked Lists

A minimal, type-safe singly linked list in TypeScript:

```ts
class Node<T> {
  constructor(public value: T, public next: Node<T> | null = null) {}
}

class LinkedList<T> {
  private head: Node<T> | null = null;
  private tail: Node<T> | null = null;
  private size = 0;

  get length() { return this.size; }
  isEmpty() { return this.size === 0; }

  prepend(value: T) {
    const node = new Node(value, this.head);
    this.head = node;
    if (!this.tail) this.tail = node;
    this.size++;
  }

  append(value: T) {
    const node = new Node(value);
    if (!this.tail) { this.head = this.tail = node; }
    else { this.tail.next = node; this.tail = node; }
    this.size++;
  }

  // Remove first occurrence by value
  remove(value: T): boolean {
    if (!this.head) return false;

    if (this.head.value === value) {
      this.head = this.head.next;
      if (!this.head) this.tail = null; // emptied list
      this.size--;
      return true;
    }

    let prev = this.head;
    let cur = this.head.next;
    while (cur) {
      if (cur.value === value) {
        prev.next = cur.next;
        if (cur === this.tail) this.tail = prev; // update tail
        this.size--;
        return true;
      }
      prev = cur;
      cur = cur.next;
    }
    return false;
  }

  at(index: number): T | undefined {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.size) return undefined;
    let cur = this.head;
    for (let i = 0; i < index && cur; i++) cur = cur.next;
    return cur?.value;
  }

  *[Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<T> {
    let cur = this.head;
    while (cur) {
      yield cur.value;
      cur = cur.next;
    }
  }

  // Cycle detection (Floyd) — important for corrupted lists
  hasCycle(): boolean {
    let slow = this.head, fast = this.head;
    while (fast && fast.next) {
      slow = slow!.next;
      fast = fast.next.next;
      if (slow === fast) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

### 1. Empty list operations
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
console.log(list.at(0));      // undefined
console.log(list.remove(1));  // false
list.prepend(5);
console.log([...list]);       // [5]
```
**Edge case:** `tail` must be nulled when the last node is removed; otherwise dangling tail breaks `append`.

---

### 2. Single-node removal (head === tail)
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
list.append(42);
list.remove(42);
console.log(list.isEmpty()); // true
list.append(7);             // must still work → requires tail === null reset
console.log([...list]);      // [7]
```

---

### 3. Insertion order and pointer maintenance
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
list.append(1);
list.append(2);
list.prepend(0);
console.log([...list]); // [0, 1, 2]

// Edge case: prepend to empty list must set BOTH head and tail
```

---

### 4. Deletion of tail node must update `tail`
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
list.append(1); list.append(2); list.append(3);
list.remove(3);
list.append(4);
console.log([...list]); // [1, 2, 4]  (not [1, 2, 4] with broken tail)
```
If you forget `if (cur === this.tail) this.tail = prev`, the next `append` overwrites `tail.next` of a removed node, corrupting the list.

---

### 5. Index access is O(n) — and bounds matter
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
list.append(10); list.append(20);
console.log(list.at(-1));  // undefined
console.log(list.at(5));   // undefined
console.log(list.at(1));   // 20
```
**Edge case:** unlike arrays, `at` cannot be O(1); callers expecting array-like performance will have O(n²) loops.

---

### 6. Iteration safety during mutation
```ts
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
[1, 2, 3, 4].forEach(n => list.append(n));

// BUG: mutating while iterating with a manual loop can skip nodes
let cur = (list as any).head;
while (cur) {
  if (cur.value % 2 === 0) (list as any).remove(cur.value); // removes current; cur.next may be stale
  cur = cur.next; // can skip or NPE
}

// Safer: collect targets first
const evens = [...list].filter(v => v % 2 === 0);
evens.forEach(v => list.remove(v));
```

---

### 7. Cycles cause infinite loops
```ts
// Corrupt a list manually to simulate a cycle
const a = new Node(1), b = new Node(2);
a.next = b; b.next = a; // cycle

// Any naive traversal hangs:
// let c = a; while (c) { c = c.next; } // infinite loop

// Always guard with visited set or Floyd (see hasCycle above)
```
**Edge case:** cycles can be introduced by buggy pointer updates or by sharing nodes between lists.

---

### 8. Shared nodes between lists (aliasing)
```ts
const n = new Node(99);
const l1 = new LinkedList<number>(); (l1 as any).head = n; (l1 as any).tail = n; (l1 as any).size = 1;
const l2 = new LinkedList<number>(); (l2 as any).head = n; (l2 as any).tail = n; (l2 as any).size = 1;

// Mutating via one list corrupts the other
n.value = 0;
console.log([...l1], [...l2]); // both [0]
```

---

### 9. Generic type safety and `null` handling
```ts
// Strict null checks force explicit null handling
function find<T>(list: LinkedList<T>, pred: (v: T) => boolean): T | undefined {
  for (const v of list) if (pred(v)) return v;
  return undefined;
}

// Doubly linked lists need prev-pointer updates too:
class DNode<T> { constructor(public value: T, public next: DNode<T> | null = null, public prev: DNode<T> | null = null) {} }
```

---

### 10. Memory leaks in long-lived lists
```ts
// Removed nodes are GC-eligible ONLY if no other references exist.
// If you keep a reference to a removed node, the whole tail stays alive.
const list = new LinkedList<number>();
list.append(1); list.append(2); list.append(3);
const removed = (list as any).head; // keep reference
list.remove(1);
// `removed` still references node 1 → node 2, 3 stay reachable if still linked
```
**Fix:** null out `removed.next` (and `prev` in doubly linked lists) on removal.

---

## Part 3 — Choosing Between Them

| Concern | Array | Linked List |
|---|---|---|
| Random access | O(1) | O(n) |
| Insert/delete at ends | O(1) amortized (push/pop) | O(1) |
| Insert/delete middle | O(n) (splice) | O(1) if you have the node |
| Memory overhead | Low (contiguous) | High (per-node objects) |
| Cache locality | Excellent | Poor |
| Type safety in TS | Excellent (tuples, readonly) | Manual (generic classes) |

### Practical TypeScript guidance
- **Default to arrays.** V8 optimizes them heavily; even `splice` in the middle is often faster than a linked list for small-to-medium sizes due to cache locality.
- **Use linked lists** only when you need O(1) splicing at arbitrary known positions, or when implementing queues/deques where you hold node references.
- **Use `ReadonlyArray<T>` / `readonly T[]`** for function parameters to prevent accidental mutation.
- **Enable `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`** to force handling of `undefined` from array access.
- **For numeric workloads**, prefer `TypedArray` (`Uint8Array`, `Float64Array`) over `number[]`.

---

### Quick reference: safe patterns

```ts
// Safe array access
function get<T>(arr: readonly T[], i: number): T | undefined {
  return i >= 0 && i < arr.length ? arr[i] : undefined;
}

// Safe linked-list node traversal with cycle guard
function toArray<T>(head: Node<T> | null): T[] {
  const out: T[] = [];
  const seen = new Set<Node<T>>();
  let cur = head;
  while (cur && !seen.has(cur)) {
    seen.add(cur);
    out.push(cur.value);
    cur = cur.next;
  }
  return out;
}
```

If you want, I can extend any of these into a full production-ready collection (e.g., a doubly linked list with `Symbol.iterator`, `splice`, and unit-test-style assertions).

Below is a **complete, runnable** answer in two parts:

1. **Idiomatic TypeScript** using the language’s built‑in `async` / `await`.
2. **A from‑scratch implementation** (the same idea the TypeScript/ Babel compilers use under the hood): turning an `async` function into a **generator + a small promise “runner”**. This is the most useful thing to study if you want to *understand* how `async/await` actually works.

---

## Part 1 – Idiomatic, runnable TypeScript

### `main.ts`

```ts
// --- Fake async I/O ---------------------------------------------------------
function delay(ms: number): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((res) => setTimeout(res, ms));
}

interface User { id: number; name: string; }
interface Post { id: number; title: string; }

async function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  await delay(200);
  if (id <= 0) throw new Error("invalid id");
  return { id, name: `User ${id}` };
}

async function fetchPosts(userId: number): Promise<Post[]> {
  await delay(150);
  return [{ id: 1, title: `Post by ${userId}` }];
}

// --- Sequential vs. parallel -----------------------------------------------
async function sequential(id: number): Promise<{ user: User; posts: Post[] }> {
  const user = await fetchUser(id);          // waits 200ms
  const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id);   // then 150ms  -> ~350ms total
  return { user, posts };
}

async function parallel(id: number): Promise<{ user: User; posts: Post[] }> {
  const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([ // both start together
    fetchUser(id),
    fetchPosts(id),
  ]);
  // posts here uses id, not user.id, but demonstrates concurrency: ~200ms total
  return { user, posts };
}

// --- Error handling ---------------------------------------------------------
async function safe(id: number) {
  try {
    return await sequential(id);
  } catch (e) {
    // `e` is `unknown` in strict mode; narrow it
    const msg = e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e);
    console.error("failed:", msg);
    return null;
  } finally {
    console.log("cleanup for", id);
  }
}

// --- Top-level await (ESM / TS 4.5+ with module: "esnext") -----------------
// (Shown conceptually; in CommonJS you'd wrap in an async IIFE.)

(async () => {
  console.time("seq");
  console.log(await safe(1));
  console.timeEnd("seq");

  console.time("par");
  console.log(await parallel(2));
  console.timeEnd("par");

  console.log(await safe(0)); // triggers error path
})();
```

