## Quick checklist

- Every `async` call is `await`ed, `void`ed with `.catch`, or returned.
- No `async` in `forEach`/`filter`/`map` predicates.
- `return await` inside `try`/`catch`/`finally`.
- Parallelize independent awaits with `Promise.all`.
- Use `allSettled` for partial failures.
- Pass `AbortSignal` for cancellation.
- Never use async promise executors.
- Initialize async objects via factory methods.
- Bind `this` or use arrow functions.
- Limit concurrency for large batches.

If you want, I can drill into any one (e.g., cancellation patterns, or typing async utilities).

## Exercise

Write an async TypeScript function `loadDashboard(userId: number)` that:

1. Fetches a `User` by id. If the user does not exist, throw a `NotFoundError`.
2. After the user is known, fetch their `Post[]` and `Notification[]` **in parallel**.
3. Return a typed object `{ user, posts, notifications }`.
4. Handle errors at the call site with `try/catch`.

Mock the network calls with small delays. Type every boundary (inputs, outputs, errors).

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Define domain types

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

interface Post {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  userId: number;
}

interface Notification {
  id: number;
  message: string;
  read: boolean;
}
```

### 2. Define a typed error

```ts
class NotFoundError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "NotFoundError";
  }
}
```

### 3. Mock async data sources

These simulate network I/O. They return `Promise<T>` so callers must `await` them.

```ts
function delay(ms: number): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
}

async function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User | null> {
  await delay(80);
  if (id === 0) return null;
  return { id, name: `User ${id}`, email: `user${id}@example.com` };
}

async function fetchPosts(userId: number): Promise<Post[]> {
  await delay(120);
  return [
    { id: 1, title: "Hello async", userId },
    { id: 2, title: "TypeScript tips", userId },
  ];
}

async function fetchNotifications(userId: number): Promise<Notification[]> {
  await delay(100);
  return [{ id: 1, message: "Welcome back!", read: false }];
}
```

### 4. Implement `loadDashboard`

Key ideas:
- `await` pauses until `fetchUser` resolves.
- Guard against `null` before using `user`.
- Use `Promise.all` to run independent requests concurrently.
- The return type is inferred, but we annotate it explicitly for clarity.

```ts
async function loadDashboard(
  userId: number
): Promise<{ user: User; posts: Post[]; notifications: Notification[] }> {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);

  if (!user) {
    throw new NotFoundError(`User with id ${userId} was not found.`);
  }

  // Independent requests run in parallel
  const [posts, notifications] = await Promise.all([
    fetchPosts(user.id),
    fetchNotifications(user.id),
  ]);

  return { user, posts, notifications };
}
```

### 5. Consume it safely

```ts
async function main() {
  try {
    const dashboard = await loadDashboard(42);
    console.log("User:", dashboard.user.name);
    console.log("Posts:", dashboard.posts.map((p) => p.title));
    console.log("Notifications:", dashboard.notifications.length);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof NotFoundError) {
      console.error("Not found:", err.message);
    } else {
      console.error("Unexpected error:", err);
    }
  }

  // Demonstrate the not-found path
  try {
    await loadDashboard(0);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error((err as Error).message);
  }
}

main();
```

