## Basic syntax

### Create and edit bytes

```ts
// A Uint8Array is the most common "bytes" type
const bytes = new Uint8Array([0xde, 0xad, 0xbe, 0xef]);

bytes[0] = 0xca;              // write a byte
const first: number = bytes[0]; // read a byte

console.log(bytes.length);    // 4
console.log(bytes);           // Uint8Array(4) [ 202, 173, 190, 239 ]
```

### `ArrayBuffer` + typed array view

```ts
const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(8);          // 8 raw bytes
const u8 = new Uint8Array(buffer);          // view as bytes
const u32 = new Uint32Array(buffer);        // another view, same memory

u8[0] = 0x78;
u8[1] = 0x56;
u8[2] = 0x34;
u8[3] = 0x12;

console.log(u32[0]); // depends on platform endianness; often 305419896
```

Use a typed array when all elements have the same type and you want array-like performance.

### `DataView` for mixed types and endianness

```ts
const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(12);
const view = new DataView(buffer);

// little-endian write
view.setUint32(0, 0x12345678, true);
view.setUint16(4, 42, true);
view.setFloat64(6, 3.14159, true);

// read back
const magic = view.getUint32(0, true);
const count = view.getUint16(4, true);
const pi = view.getFloat64(6, true);
```

Use `DataView` for file formats, network protocols, and binary headers where fields have different sizes.

---

## Text and encoding

### Browser / standard Web APIs

```ts
const encoder = new TextEncoder();
const bytes: Uint8Array = encoder.encode("Hello, 世界");

const decoder = new TextDecoder("utf-8");
const text: string = decoder.decode(bytes);
```

### Node.js `Buffer`

Node’s `Buffer` extends `Uint8Array` and adds encoding helpers.

```ts
import { Buffer } from "node:buffer";

const b = Buffer.from("hello", "utf8");
const hex = b.toString("hex");        // "68656c6c6f"
const b64 = b.toString("base64");     // "aGVsbG8="

const parsed = Buffer.from(hex, "hex");
```

In Node, you can often use `Buffer` directly, but returning `Uint8Array` is safer for browser compatibility.

