## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Define the node type
We use a class so we can attach helper methods later.

```ts
class TreeNode {
  value: number;
  left: TreeNode | null;
  right: TreeNode | null;

  constructor(value: number) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}
```

### 2. Insert into a BST
Standard recursive insertion keeps the BST invariant: left < node < right.

```ts
function insert(root: TreeNode | null, value: number): TreeNode {
  if (root === null) return new TreeNode(value);
  if (value < root.value) {
    root.left = insert(root.left, value);
  } else if (value > root.value) {
    root.right = insert(root.right, value);
  }
  // duplicates ignored
  return root;
}
```

### 3. Maximum depth (recursive)
Base case: `null` → 0. Otherwise `1 + max(left, right)`.

```ts
function maxDepth(root: TreeNode | null): number {
  if (root === null) return 0;
  return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));
}
```

### 4. In-order traversal (verification)
For a valid BST, in-order yields sorted ascending values.

```ts
function inorder(root: TreeNode | null, out: number[] = []): number[] {
  if (root === null) return out;
  inorder(root.left, out);
  out.push(root.value);
  inorder(root.right, out);
  return out;
}
```

### 5. Build from array + demo
```ts
function buildBST(values: number[]): TreeNode | null {
  let root: TreeNode | null = null;
  for (const v of values) root = insert(root, v);
  return root;
}
```

