```

### Notes on production use

- **Expiration vs. eviction**: TTL handles staleness; LRU handles memory bounds. Combine them (e.g., an LRU of TTL entries) for a full cache.
- **Request coalescing** (`AsyncCache`) is the single most impactful pattern for backend services: it prevents N identical DB queries when N requests arrive simultaneously for a cold key.
- **Cache invalidation**: expose `invalidate(key)` / `clear()` and call them on writes. For distributed systems, add a pub/sub channel (Redis `PUBLISH`) to invalidate across instances.
- **Serialization**: if you need to persist to Redis/Memcached, store `JSON.stringify` values and rehydrate with a reviver; keep the in-memory layer as the hot path.
- **Metrics**: track hit/miss/eviction counts; alert when hit rate drops below ~80% for hot keys.

If you want, I can extend this with a **Redis-backed adapter** implementing the same interface, or add **stale-while-revalidate** semantics.

## Caching in TypeScript

Caching stores the result of an expensive operation and returns it on subsequent requests instead of recomputing/re-fetching. TypeScript adds **type safety** to the keys, values, expiration, and invalidation logic.

---

### 1. Core idea

A cache is usually a key/value store with three operations:

- `get(key)` → return cached value or `undefined`
- `set(key, value, ttl?)` → store value
- `delete(key)` / `clear()` → invalidate

Important design choices:

| Concern | Typical solution |
|---|---|
| Key equality | string/number keys, or serialize objects |
| Value type | generic `T` |
| Expiration | TTL (time-to-live) |
| Eviction | LRU, size limits |
| Concurrency | dedupe in-flight promises |
| Invalidation | event-driven or versioned keys |

---

### 2. Basic typed cache with `Map`

```ts
type CacheEntry<T> = {
  value: T;
  expiresAt: number;
};

class TTLCache<T> {
  private store = new Map<string, CacheEntry<T>>();

  get(key: string): T | undefined {
    const entry = this.store.get(key);
    if (!entry) return undefined;

    if (Date.now() >= entry.expiresAt) {
      this.store.delete(key);
      return undefined;
    }
    return entry.value;
  }

  set(key: string, value: T, ttlMs: number): void {
    this.store.set(key, {
      value,
      expiresAt: Date.now() + ttlMs,
    });
  }

  delete(key: string): void {
    this.store.delete(key);
  }
}

// Usage
const userCache = new TTLCache<{ id: string; name: string }>();

userCache.set("42", { id: "42", name: "Ada" }, 60_000);
const user = userCache.get("42"); // { id: "42", name: "Ada" } | undefined
```

Notes:

- `Map` is preferred over plain objects because it preserves insertion order and avoids prototype-key surprises.
- TTL is checked lazily on `get`, which avoids timers for every key.

---

### 3. Generic memoization of pure functions

For pure functions, you can cache by arguments.

```ts
function memoize<Args extends unknown[], R>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => R
): (...args: Args) => R {
  const cache = new Map<string, R>();

  return (...args: Args): R => {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    const cached = cache.get(key);
    if (cached !== undefined) return cached;

    const result = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}

const expensive = (n: number): number => {
  console.log("computing");
  return n * n;
};

const memoized = memoize(expensive);
memoized(5); // computing -> 25
memoized(5); // 25, no log
```

TypeScript points:

- `Args extends unknown[]` lets the returned function keep the original parameter types.
- `R` is inferred from `fn`.
- `JSON.stringify(args)` is simple but not perfect for objects/functions; use a custom key serializer in production.

---

### 4. Async caching and promise deduplication

A common bug: two callers request the same data before the first request finishes, causing duplicate network calls. Cache the **promise**, not just the resolved value.

```ts
type Fetcher<T> = () => Promise<T>;

class AsyncCache<T> {
  private cache = new Map<string, Promise<T>>();

  getOrCreate(key: string, fetcher: Fetcher<T>): Promise<T> {
    const existing = this.cache.get(key);
    if (existing) return existing;

    const promise = fetcher().finally(() => {
      // optional: remove rejected promises so next call retries
    });

    this.cache.set(key, promise);
    return promise;
  }

  invalidate(key: string): void {
    this.cache.delete(key);
  }
}

const cache = new AsyncCache<{ id: string }>();

async function getUser(id: string) {
  return cache.getOrCreate(`user:${id}`, async () => {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    return res.json();
  });
}
```

Production refinement: remove rejected promises so failures aren't cached forever.

```ts
const promise = fetcher().catch((err) => {
  this.cache.delete(key);
  throw err;
});
```

---

### 5. Strongly typed cache with branded keys

Avoid mixing different key domains by using branded types.

```ts
type UserId = string & { readonly brand: unique symbol };
type PostId = string & { readonly brand: unique symbol };

function userId(id: string): UserId {
  return id as UserId;
}

class TypedCache<K, V> {
  private store = new Map<K, V>();

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    return this.store.get(key);
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    this.store.set(key, value);
  }
}

const users = new TypedCache<UserId, { name: string }>();
const posts = new TypedCache<PostId, { title: string }>();

users.set(userId("1"), { name: "Ada" });
// users.set("1", { name: "Ada" }); // type error
```

This prevents accidentally using a post ID as a user ID.

---

### 6. `WeakMap` for object-keyed caches

When keys are objects and you want garbage collection, use `WeakMap`.

```ts
const computed = new WeakMap<object, number>();

function expensive(obj: { value: number }): number {
  const cached = computed.get(obj);
  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;

  const result = obj.value * 2;
  computed.set(obj, result);
  return result;
}
```

TypeScript note: `WeakMap` keys must be objects, not primitives. This is great for caching derived data tied to object lifetimes.

---

### 7. Decorator-style caching (experimental/legacy)

Some codebases use decorators for method caching. With TypeScript decorators (stage 3 / TS 5+), you can wrap methods.

```ts
function Cached(ttlMs: number) {
  return function (
    target: (...args: any[]) => any,
    context: ClassMethodDecoratorContext
  ) {
    const cache = new Map<string, { value: any; expires: number }>();

    return function (this: any, ...args: any[]) {
      const key = JSON.stringify(args);
      const hit = cache.get(key);
      if (hit && Date.now() < hit.expires) return hit.value;

      const result = target.apply(this, args);
      cache.set(key, { value: result, expires: Date.now() + ttlMs });
      return result;
    };
  };
}

class Service {
  @Cached(5000)
  compute(x: number): number {
    console.log("running");
    return x * 2;
  }
}
```

Use sparingly: decorators hide caching behavior and can complicate debugging/invalidation.

---

### 8. Typical use cases

1. **API client caching**
   - Cache GET responses by URL + params.
   - Use TTL + invalidation on mutations.

2. **Computed selectors**
   - Memoize derived state in Redux/Zustand selectors.

3. **Expensive transformations**
   - Large JSON normalization, parsing, formatting.

4. **Rate limiting / request coalescing**
   - Deduplicate identical in-flight requests.

5. **Feature flags / config**
   - Cache remote config with periodic refresh.

6. **Database query results**
   - Cache hot reads by ID; invalidate on writes.

---

### 9. Invalidation strategies

- **TTL only**: simplest, accepts stale data.
- **Event-based**: invalidate on create/update/delete.
- **Versioned keys**: `user:${id}:v${version}`.
- **Tag-based**: group keys and invalidate by tag.

Example tag-based cache:

```ts
class TagCache<V> {
  private store = new Map<string, V>();
  private tags = new Map<string, Set<string>>();

  set(key: string, value: V, tags: string[] = []): void {
    this.store.set(key, value);
    for (const tag of tags) {
      if (!this.tags.has(tag)) this.tags.set(tag, new Set());
      this.tags.get(tag)!.add(key);
    }
  }

  invalidateTag(tag: string): void {
    const keys = this.tags.get(tag);
    if (!keys) return;
    for (const key of keys) this.store.delete(key);
    this.tags.delete(tag);
  }
}
```

---

### 10. Production considerations

- **Key collisions**: object keys should be serialized deterministically.
- **Memory growth**: add max size + LRU eviction.
- **Stale data**: combine TTL + explicit invalidation.
- **Type safety**: keep cache generic; avoid `any`.
- **Testing**: mock time with fake timers when testing TTL.
- **SSR/hydration**: don't leak server caches into client bundles unintentionally.

---

### Minimal production-style example

```ts
class LRU<K, V> {
  private max: number;
  private map = new Map<K, V>();

  constructor(max: number) {
    this.max = max;
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    const v = this.map.get(key);
    if (v === undefined) return undefined;
    // Move to end (most recently used)
    this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, v);
    return v;
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this.map.has(key)) this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, value);
    if (this.map.size > this.max) {
      const oldest = this.map.keys().next().value;
      this.map.delete(oldest);
    }
  }
}
```

This gives you bounded memory and LRU eviction with full TypeScript type safety.

