## Typical caching mistake: returning a mutable cached reference

### The buggy snippet

```ts
type User = { id: string; name: string; roles: string[] };

const cache = new Map<string, User>();

function getUser(id: string): User {
  const cached = cache.get(id);
  if (cached) return cached;               // ❌ returns the live cached object

  const user: User = fetchUserSync(id);     // pretend this hits a DB/API
  cache.set(id, user);
  return user;
}

// --- somewhere else ---
const a = getUser("42");
a.name = "Eve";                            // mutates the cached instance
a.roles.push("admin");

const b = getUser("42");
console.log(b.name);                       // "Eve"  (unexpected!)
console.log(b.roles);                      // includes "admin" (unexpected!)
```

### Why this is wrong

1. **Shared mutable state.** The cache stores *one* object instance per key. Every caller gets the *same* reference, so any mutation by one consumer leaks to all others (and to the cache itself).
2. **No invalidation / TTL.** Even if you never mutate, the data can become stale. There is no expiry, version, or invalidation hook.
3. **Sync-only assumption.** Real lookups are async; caching the *result* (not the *in-flight promise*) causes duplicate concurrent requests (thundering herd).
4. **No size bound.** An unbounded `Map` can grow forever (memory leak).

---

### Corrected version

```ts
type User = { id: string; name: string; roles: string[] };

// 1. Store immutable-ish snapshots; return deep clones.
// 2. Cache the in-flight promise for async dedup.
// 3. Add TTL + LRU-ish eviction.

interface CacheEntry<T> {
  value: T;
  expiresAt: number;
}

class UserCache {
  private readonly store = new Map<string, CacheEntry<User>>();
  private readonly inflight = new Map<string, Promise<User>>();
  private readonly ttlMs: number;
  private readonly maxSize: number;

  constructor(ttlMs = 60_000, maxSize = 1000) {
    this.ttlMs = ttlMs;
    this.maxSize = maxSize;
  }

  async get(id: string): Promise<User> {
    // Serve fresh (non-expired) cached value as a clone.
    const entry = this.store.get(id);
    if (entry && entry.expiresAt > Date.now()) {
      return structuredClone(entry.value);   // isolation from cache internals
    }

    // Dedup concurrent requests for the same id.
    const pending = this.inflight.get(id);
    if (pending) return pending.then(structuredClone);

    const promise = this.fetchAndStore(id);
    this.inflight.set(id, promise);

    try {
      const value = await promise;
      return structuredClone(value);
    } finally {
      this.inflight.delete(id);
    }
  }

  private async fetchAndStore(id: string): Promise<User> {
    const user = await fetchUser(id); // async source of truth

    // Evict oldest if at capacity (simple FIFO; use LRU in prod).
    if (this.store.size >= this.maxSize) {
      const oldestKey = this.store.keys().next().value;
      if (oldestKey !== undefined) this.store.delete(oldestKey);
    }

    this.store.set(id, {
      value: structuredClone(user),        // store a clone, not the live object
      expiresAt: Date.now() + this.ttlMs,
    });

    return user;
  }

  invalidate(id: string): void {
    this.store.delete(id);
    this.inflight.delete(id);
  }
}

// --- usage ---
const cache = new UserCache();

const a = await cache.get("42");
a.name = "Eve";            // safe: only mutates the caller's clone
a.roles.push("admin");

const b = await cache.get("42");
console.log(b.name);       // original name (unchanged)
console.log(b.roles);      // original roles (unchanged)
```

### Key takeaways

| Problem in buggy version | Fix in corrected version |
|---|---|
| Shared mutable reference | `structuredClone` on read *and* store |
| No expiry | `expiresAt` + TTL check |
| Duplicate concurrent fetches | `inflight` promise dedup |
| Unbounded growth | `maxSize` with eviction |
| No invalidation path | `invalidate(id)` method |
| Sync-only | `async get` returning `Promise<User>` |

**Rule of thumb:** caches should store *immutable values* (or treat them as such), and every read should return either a copy or a frozen object. If the underlying data changes, invalidate explicitly rather than relying on callers to “be careful.”

# Common Pitfalls When Working with Caching in TypeScript

Below are the most common pitfalls, each with a concrete code example and a fix. I'll group them roughly from "data correctness" to "performance" to "type-safety" issues.

---

## 1. Mutating cached objects (shared mutable references)

The cache returns the *same* object reference; mutating it corrupts every future read.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
const cache = new Map<string, User>();
cache.set("u1", { name: "Alice", roles: ["admin"] });

const u = cache.get("u1")!;
u.roles.push("superadmin"); // mutates cached value

console.log(cache.get("u1")!.roles); // ["admin", "superadmin"] — corrupted!
```

Fix: store immutable data, or clone on read/write.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: deep clone on read (or use immutable updates)
import { structuredClone } from "node:util"; // Node 17+

function get(key: string): User | undefined {
  const v = cache.get(key);
  return v ? structuredClone(v) : undefined;
}

// Or store frozen values
cache.set("u1", Object.freeze({ name: "Alice", roles: Object.freeze(["admin"]) }));
```

---

## 2. Cache key collisions from object serialization

Objects as keys, or naive `JSON.stringify`, collide on key order and nested types.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall: key order matters
function keyOf(params: { a: number; b: number }) {
  return JSON.stringify(params);
}
keyOf({ a: 1, b: 2 }) === keyOf({ b: 2, a: 1 }); // false → two entries

// ❌ Pitfall: undefined / functions dropped silently
JSON.stringify({ x: undefined, f: () => 1 }); // '{"...}"' — data loss
```

Fix: canonicalize keys.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: sorted canonical key
function canonicalKey(obj: Record<string, unknown>): string {
  return JSON.stringify(obj, (k, v) =>
    v && typeof v === "object" && !Array.isArray(v)
      ? Object.keys(v).sort().reduce((o, key) => ({ ...o, [key]: (v as any)[key] }), {})
      : v
  );
}
```

Better yet, avoid complex keys; prefer primitive composite keys: `` `user:${id}:${version}` ``.

---

## 3. Stale data / invalidation forgotten

Caching "forever" without an invalidation strategy is the classic bug.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
async function getUser(id: string) {
  if (cache.has(id)) return cache.get(id); // never expires, never invalidates
  const u = await db.users.find(id);
  cache.set(id, u);
  return u;
}
```

Fix: TTL + explicit invalidation on writes.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: TTL + invalidate on mutation
import { LRUCache } from "lru-cache";

const cache = new LRUCache<string, User>({ max: 1000, ttl: 1000 * 60 * 5 });

async function getUser(id: string) {
  const cached = cache.get(id);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const u = await db.users.find(id);
  cache.set(id, u);
  return u;
}

async function updateUser(id: string, patch: Partial<User>) {
  await db.users.update(id, patch);
  cache.delete(id); // invalidate
}
```

---

## 4. Not caching "not found" / null (cache penetration)

Repeated lookups for missing keys hit the DB every time; attackers can exploit this.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall: only success cached
async function getProduct(id: string) {
  const c = cache.get(id);
  if (c) return c;
  const p = await db.products.find(id); // null not cached
  if (p) cache.set(id, p);
  return p;
}
```

Fix: cache a sentinel for misses (with shorter TTL).

```ts
// ✅ Fix
const MISS = Symbol("miss");

async function getProduct(id: string): Promise<Product | null> {
  const c = cache.get(id);
  if (c === MISS) return null;
  if (c) return c;

  const p = await db.products.find(id);
  cache.set(id, p ?? MISS, { ttl: p ? 60_000 : 5_000 });
  return p;
}
```

---

## 5. Thundering herd / cache stampede

When a hot key expires, many concurrent requests recompute it simultaneously.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
async function getExpensiveReport(date: string) {
  if (cache.has(date)) return cache.get(date);
  const r = await computeReport(date); // 30s — N concurrent callers all run it
  cache.set(date, r);
  return r;
}
```

Fix: promise coalescing / single-flight.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: deduplicate in-flight computation
const inflight = new Map<string, Promise<Report>>();

async function getExpensiveReport(date: string): Promise<Report> {
  const cached = cache.get(date);
  if (cached) return cached;

  let p = inflight.get(date);
  if (!p) {
    p = computeReport(date).then((r) => {
      cache.set(date, r);
      return r;
    }).finally(() => inflight.delete(date));
    inflight.set(date, p);
  }
  return p;
}
```

---

## 6. Unbounded memory (memory leak)

A `Map` that grows forever in a long-running process.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
const cache = new Map<string, any>(); // grows unbounded
```

Fix: LRU with `max` entries, or weak references for object keys.

```ts
// ✅ Fix
import { LRUCache } from "lru-cache";
const cache = new LRUCache<string, any>({ max: 5000, ttl: 1000 * 60 * 10 });

// Or WeakMap when keys are objects and you don't need enumeration
const metaCache = new WeakMap<object, Meta>(); // GC'd when object unreachable
```

---

## 7. Type unsafety: `any` / `undefined` handling

Caching libraries often return `any` or `T | undefined`; ignoring that loses type safety.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
const cache = new Map<string, any>();
const u = cache.get("x"); // any — no error on u.foo.bar
```

Fix: typed cache wrapper with explicit `undefined` handling.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: typed wrapper
class TypedCache<K, V> {
  private m = new Map<K, V>();
  get(key: K): V | undefined { return this.m.get(key); }
  set(key: K, value: V): void { this.m.set(key, value); }
  getOrThrow(key: K): V {
    const v = this.m.get(key);
    if (v === undefined) throw new Error(`missing cache key: ${String(key)}`);
    return v;
  }
}

const cache = new TypedCache<string, User>();
```

For "value can be undefined legitimately", use a sentinel or `Map<K, { value: V }>`.

---

## 8. Async caching anti-patterns

Caching promises vs results; caching rejected promises; not awaiting.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall: cache the promise, but a rejection stays cached forever
cache.set(key, fetchUser(id)); // if it rejects, cached rejection forever
```

Fix: cache only resolved values, or clear on rejection.

```ts
// ✅ Fix
async function getUser(id: string) {
  const cached = cache.get(id);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const p = fetchUser(id).then((u) => {
    cache.set(id, u);
    return u;
  }).catch((e) => {
    cache.delete(id); // don't poison cache
    throw e;
  });
  return p;
}

// Or cache promises but with cleanup:
const p = fetchUser(id);
cache.set(id, p);
p.catch(() => cache.delete(id));
```

---

## 9. Serialization pitfalls (Date, Map, Set, BigInt)

`JSON.stringify` silently mangles non-plain types; round-tripping breaks types.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
const v = { created: new Date(), tags: new Set(["a"]) };
const s = JSON.stringify(v); // {"created":"2024-...","tags":{}}
JSON.parse(s).created instanceof Date; // false
```

Fix: use a serializer that preserves types, or store plain DTOs.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: superjson / devalue / or manual DTO conversion
import superjson from "superjson";

const s = superjson.stringify(v);
const restored = superjson.parse(s); // Date and Set restored

// Or store plain, typed DTOs
type UserDTO = { id: string; createdAt: string; tags: string[] };
```

---

## 10. Distributed cache consistency (multi-instance)

In-memory cache is per-process; updates on instance A don't propagate to B.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall: two Node processes each have their own Map
// Instance A updates user, instance B serves stale cached user
```

Fix: shared store (Redis) + pub/sub invalidation, or accept eventual consistency with short TTL.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: Redis + invalidation channel
import { createClient } from "redis";
const redis = createClient();
await redis.connect();

async function getUser(id: string) {
  const cached = await redis.get(`user:${id}`);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached) as User;
  const u = await db.users.find(id);
  await redis.set(`user:${id}`, JSON.stringify(u), { EX: 300 });
  return u;
}

// Publish invalidation
async function updateUser(id: string, patch: Partial<User>) {
  await db.users.update(id, patch);
  await redis.del(`user:${id}`);
  await redis.publish("invalidate", JSON.stringify({ type: "user", id }));
}
```

---

## 11. Cache stampede on cache warmup / cold start

First request after deploy computes everything at once.

Fix: pre-warm critical keys; use stale-while-revalidate.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: SWR — serve stale, refresh in background
async function get(key: string) {
  const entry = cache.get(key); // { value, expiresAt, staleUntil }
  const now = Date.now();
  if (entry && now < entry.expiresAt) return entry.value;       // fresh
  if (entry && now < entry.staleUntil) {
    refreshInBackground(key); // don't await
    return entry.value;       // stale but acceptable
  }
  return await refresh(key); // blocking
}
```

---

## 12. Equality / cache hit detection with objects

Using object identity as key means logically equal objects miss.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
const cache = new Map<object, Result>();
cache.set({ a: 1 }, result);
cache.get({ a: 1 }); // undefined — different reference
```

Fix: derive a primitive key.

```ts
// ✅ Fix
function keyOf(input: { a: number }) { return `a=${input.a}`; }
cache.set(keyOf(input), result);
cache.get(keyOf(otherInput));
```

---

## 13. Ignoring cache errors / fail-open vs fail-closed

A Redis outage can take down the whole app if you `throw`.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall
async function get(key: string) {
  const v = await redis.get(key); // throws on connection loss → 500 for all
  ...
}
```

Fix: fail open (treat cache as unavailable, fall back to source) with logging.

```ts
// ✅ Fix
async function get(key: string) {
  try {
    const v = await redis.get(key);
    if (v) return JSON.parse(v);
  } catch (e) {
    logger.warn("cache read failed", e);
  }
  return fetchFromSource(key); // degrade gracefully
}
```

---

## 14. Caching at the wrong layer (over-caching)

Caching derived/personalized data globally, or caching things that are cheap to compute.

```ts
// ❌ Pitfall: cache per-user personalized dashboard globally
cache.set("dashboard", personalizedData); // leaks user A's data to user B!
```

Fix: include identity in key; only cache expensive/shared data.

```ts
// ✅ Fix
const key = `dashboard:${userId}:${locale}`;
```

---

## 15. TTL clock skew / using wall clock

`Date.now()` comparisons with server-stored expiry can be off across machines.

Fix: prefer relative TTLs computed locally; if storing expiry, store as epoch ms and compare with local clock; avoid `setTimeout` for eviction in distributed settings.

---

## 16. Not handling cache eviction callbacks / stale listeners

When an entry is evicted, dependent resources (file handles, streams) may leak.

```ts
// ✅ Fix: use disposition/eviction hooks
const cache = new LRUCache<string, Resource>({
  max: 100,
  dispose: (value, key, reason) => {
    value.close?.(); // release handles
  },
});
```

---

## Quick checklist

- [ ] Mutable cached values cloned or frozen
- [ ] Canonical, primitive cache keys
- [ ] TTL on every entry; invalidation on writes
- [ ] Misses cached (shorter TTL) to avoid penetration
- [ ] Single-flight for expensive computations
- [ ] Bounded size (LRU) or WeakMap
- [ ] Typed cache, no `any`
- [ ] Cache results, not rejected promises
- [ ] Serializer preserves types (superjson/devalue)
- [ ] Identity in keys for personalized data
- [ ] Fail-open with logging
- [ ] Eviction hooks release resources
- [ ] SWR for hot keys
- [ ] Shared store + invalidation for multi-instance

