### Key takeaways

- `private`/`protected` enforce encapsulation; expose only validated operations.
- `readonly` prevents reassignment of identity fields like account numbers.
- Getters provide read-only derived views without leaking mutable state.
- `static` is right for utilities that don't depend on instance data.
- Subclasses call `super(...)` first, then add their own state; `protected` lets them read inherited internals safely.

## Classes and Objects in TypeScript

**Classes** are blueprints for creating **objects** (instances). In TypeScript, classes add static typing, access modifiers, and compile-time checks on top of JavaScript’s prototype-based model.

Key ideas:
- **Constructor**: initializes instance state.
- **Properties/fields**: data stored on an instance (`this.x`).
- **Methods**: behavior shared via the prototype.
- **Access modifiers**: `public` (default), `private`, `protected`.
- **Inheritance**: `extends` + `super()`.
- **Static members**: belong to the class itself, not instances.
- **Getters/setters**: computed/validated property access.
- **Abstract classes/interfaces**: contracts and partial implementations.
- **Readonly**: immutable after construction.

Objects are just runtime instances created with `new ClassName(...)`. You can also create plain objects via object literals, but classes give you encapsulation, reuse, and type safety.

---

### Clean, commented implementation of the core concepts

```ts
// core-classes.ts
// Demonstrates the core building blocks of classes and objects in TypeScript.

// ---------- 1. Interface: a compile-time contract ----------
interface Identifiable {
  readonly id: string; // every identifiable thing exposes a read-only id
}

interface Serializable {
  toJSON(): Record<string, unknown>;
}

// ---------- 2. Abstract base class: shared state + partial behavior ----------
abstract class Entity implements Identifiable {
  readonly id: string;
  protected createdAt: Date;

  constructor(id: string) {
    if (!id) throw new Error("id is required");
    this.id = id;
    this.createdAt = new Date();
  }

  // Subclasses must implement this (polymorphism)
  abstract describe(): string;

  // Shared concrete method
  get ageMs(): number {
    return Date.now() - this.createdAt.getTime();
  }

  protected touch(): void {
    // protected: visible to subclasses, hidden from callers
    this.createdAt = new Date();
  }
}

// ---------- 3. Concrete class: full implementation ----------
class User extends Entity implements Serializable {
  // Public field (default access)
  public name: string;

  // Private backing field (true privacy at runtime via # in modern TS/JS,
  // but `private` is the conventional TS modifier)
  private _email: string;

  // Static: belongs to the class, not instances
  static readonly MAX_NAME_LENGTH = 50;
  private static nextId = 1;

  constructor(name: string, email: string) {
    // super() must be called before using `this`
    super(`user-${User.nextId++}`);
    this.name = User.normalizeName(name);
    this._email = User.validateEmail(email);
  }

  // Static factory: alternative construction path
  static create(name: string, email: string): User {
    return new User(name, email);
  }

  // Static helper (private to the class)
  private static normalizeName(name: string): string {
    const trimmed = name.trim();
    if (trimmed.length === 0) throw new Error("name cannot be empty");
    if (trimmed.length > User.MAX_NAME_LENGTH) {
      throw new Error(`name too long (max ${User.MAX_NAME_LENGTH})`);
    }
    return trimmed;
  }

  private static validateEmail(email: string): string {
    if (!email.includes("@")) throw new Error("invalid email");
    return email.toLowerCase();
  }

  // Getter: computed/read-only view of private state
  get email(): string {
    return this._email;
  }

  // Setter: validated mutation
  set email(value: string) {
    this._email = User.validateEmail(value);
  }

  // Polymorphic implementation of abstract method
  describe(): string {
    return `User(${this.id}) ${this.name} <${this._email}>`;
  }

  // Interface implementation
  toJSON(): Record<string, unknown> {
    return { id: this.id, name: this.name, email: this._email };
  }

  // Instance method using protected member from base
  resetTimestamp(): void {
    this.touch();
  }
}

// ---------- 4. Using classes to create objects ----------
const ada = User.create("  Ada Lovelace  ", "Ada@Example.com");
console.log(ada.describe());          // User(user-1) Ada Lovelace <ada@example.com>
console.log(ada.toJSON());            // { id, name, email }
console.log("ageMs:", ada.ageMs);    // instance getter from base class

// Validated mutation via setter
ada.email = "ada.lovelace@new.com";
console.log(ada.email);

// Static access (no instance needed)
console.log("max name length:", User.MAX_NAME_LENGTH);

// ---------- 5. Plain object literal (contrast) ----------
// No encapsulation/validation; useful for DTOs, not domain behavior.
const dto: { id: string; name: string } = { id: "x", name: "Bob" };

// ---------- 6. Structural typing note ----------
// TS uses structural typing: a plain object can satisfy an interface
// if it has the right shape, even without `new`.
function printIdentifiable(x: Identifiable): void {
  console.log("id =", x.id);
}
printIdentifiable(ada); // works: User structurally matches Identifiable
printIdentifiable(dto); // works: dto also has readonly-compatible id
```

### Interview talking points to mention
- **`private` is compile-time only** in TS; use `#field` for true runtime privacy.
- **Structural typing** means interfaces are matched by shape, not nominal class identity.
- **Abstract classes** can carry implementation; interfaces are pure contracts (though TS interfaces can have optional/readonly members).
- **Static members** are not on instances; useful for factories, constants, counters.
- **`super()`** must run before `this` in subclass constructors.
- Prefer **composition over inheritance** for flexible designs; inheritance is best for clear “is-a” hierarchies.

If you want, I can extend this into a small domain model (e.g., `Order`/`OrderItem` with validation and a repository interface) or show how decorators/mixins work in modern TypeScript.

Below are common TypeScript class/object edge cases, why they matter, and idiomatic fixes.

## 1. Structural typing can make unrelated classes assignable

TypeScript uses structural typing. Two classes with the same public shape are assignable to each other unless private/protected members differ by declaration origin.

```ts
class Foo {
  constructor(public value: number) {}
}

class Bar {
  constructor(public value: number) {}
}

const f: Foo = new Bar(); // Allowed, even though types differ

// Make the type more nominal by using a private field.
class NominalFoo {
  private readonly brand = "NominalFoo";
  constructor(public value: number) {}
}

class NominalBar {
  private readonly brand = "NominalBar";
  constructor(public value: number) {}
}

// const x: NominalFoo = new NominalBar(); // Error
```

## 2. `this` is lost when a method is detached

Passing a method as a callback changes `this`. Under `strict`, `this` becomes `undefined` at runtime.

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;

  increment() {
    this.count++; // runtime error if called detached
  }
}

const c = new Counter();
const fn = c.increment;
// fn(); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined

// Fix 1: arrow class field
class CounterArrow {
  count = 0;
  increment = () => {
    this.count++;
  };
}

// Fix 2: bind in constructor
class CounterBound {
  count = 0;
  increment() { this.count++; }
  constructor() {
    this.increment = this.increment.bind(this);
  }
}
```

Note: arrow fields are own instance properties, not prototype methods, so they are not shared and behave differently with inheritance.

## 3. Fields may be uninitialized when base constructors run

Do not call overridable methods from a base constructor. Derived fields are not initialized yet.

```ts
class Base {
  constructor() {
    this.setup(); // virtual dispatch
  }
  setup() {}
}

class Derived extends Base {
  value = 42;

  override setup() {
    console.log(this.value); // undefined
  }
}

new Derived();
```

Prefer explicit initialization methods called after construction, or avoid overridable calls in constructors.

## 4. `useDefineForClassFields` changes assignment semantics

With `useDefineForClassFields: true`, a derived class field is defined as an own data property. With the older assign behavior, assigning to a property may invoke a base setter.

```ts
class Base {
  _x = 0;
  get x() { return this._x; }
  set x(v: number) { this._x = v * 2; }
}

class Derived extends Base {
  x = 5;
}

const d = new Derived();
console.log(d.x);

// With define semantics: often 5, because own data property shadows accessor
// With assign semantics: often 10, because setter is invoked
```

Be explicit when subclass fields intentionally override accessors.

## 5. `readonly` is shallow

`readonly` prevents reassignment of the property, not mutation of nested objects.

```ts
class PointHolder {
  readonly point = { x: 0, y: 0 };
}

const h = new PointHolder();
h.point.x = 99; // allowed
// h.point = { x: 1, y: 1 }; // error

// Deep readonly requires a recursive utility type
type DeepReadonly<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] extends object ? DeepReadonly<T[K]> : T[K];
};
```

## 6. Optional properties vs. `undefined`

With `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, an optional property is not the same as a required property typed as `T | undefined`.

```ts
interface User {
  nickname?: string;
}

function printNickname(user: User) {
  console.log(user.nickname ?? "(none)");
}

// printNickname({ nickname: undefined }); // may error with exactOptionalPropertyTypes

// If undefined is a valid stored value, model it explicitly:
interface UserWithUndefined {
  nickname: string | undefined;
}
```

## 7. Excess property checks only apply to object literals

A variable can bypass excess property checking even when its shape is too wide for the target type.

```ts
interface Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

const p1: Point = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 }; // error: excess property

const extra = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };
const p2: Point = extra; // allowed, but z exists at runtime
```

Validate or pick known keys when consuming untrusted data.

## 8. Index signatures interact poorly with known properties

If an index signature exists, known properties must also satisfy it.

```ts
interface Dictionary {
  [key: string]: number;
  count?: number; // okay: number satisfies index signature
  // name?: string; // error: string is not assignable to number
}

// Prefer a union type or separate known/unknown maps:
type BetterDictionary = {
  count?: number;
  values: Record<string, number>;
};
```

## 9. Classes are not hoisted

Unlike functions, class declarations cannot be used before their declaration.

```ts
// const x = new Foo(); // TDZ / use before declaration
// class Foo {}

class Foo {}
const x = new Foo(); // okay
```

## 10. Constructor overloads need one implementation

TypeScript overloads are compile-time only. Provide one implementation with a compatible signature.

```ts
class Parser {
  constructor(input: string);
  constructor(input: string, radix: number);
  constructor(input: string, radix = 10) {
    // runtime implementation
  }
}
```

## 11. Abstract classes and abstract properties

Abstract classes cannot be instantiated directly. Subclasses must initialize abstract properties.

```ts
abstract class Shape {
  abstract area: number;
  abstract describe(): string;
}

class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(public radius: number) {
    super();
  }
  get area() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;
  }
  describe() {
    return `circle radius ${this.radius}`;
  }
}

// new Shape(); // error
```

## 12. Private and protected are erased or enforced differently

TypeScript `private`/`protected` are compile-time only. ECMAScript `#private` is truly private at runtime.

```ts
class ApiClient {
  private token = "compile-time only";
  #secret = "runtime private";

  getToken() {
    return this.token;
  }

  getSecret() {
    return this.#secret;
  }
}

const client = new ApiClient() as any;
console.log(client.token); // accessible at runtime
// console.log(client.#secret); // syntax error at runtime
```

Use `#fields` for real runtime privacy.

## 13. Getters/setters and accessor pitfalls

Getters can return fresh objects, causing identity/reference surprises. Setters may transform values.

```ts
class Temperature {
  #celsius = 0;

  get fahrenheit() {
    return this.#celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
  }

  set fahrenheit(value: number) {
    if (!Number.isFinite(value)) throw new RangeError("invalid temperature");
    this.#celsius = (value - 32) * 5 / 9;
  }
}

const t = new Temperature();
t.fahrenheit = 72;
console.log(t.fahrenheit);
```

Avoid expensive or side-effect-heavy getters; callers expect property access to be cheap.

## 14. Static fields initialize in textual order

Referencing a later static field during initialization yields `undefined`, not an error.

```ts
class Config {
  static mode = "prod";
  static label = `${Config.mode}:app`; // okay
}

class BadOrder {
  static first = BadOrder.second; // undefined at this moment
  static second = 42;
}

console.log(BadOrder.first); // undefined
```

## 15. `static` members cannot use class type parameters

A class-level generic parameter is not available on static members. Use a generic static method instead.

```ts
class Box<T> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}

  // static create<U>(value: U) { return new Box(value); } // okay
  static from(value: unknown) {
    return new Box(value);
  }
}

// static value: T = ...; // error: static cannot reference instance type param
```

## 16. Methods are not enumerable, fields usually are

`Object.keys`, spread, and JSON serialization include enumerable own properties but not prototype methods.

```ts
class User {
  name = "Ada";
  greet() {
    return `Hi, ${this.name}`;
  }
}

const u = new User();
console.log(Object.keys(u)); // ["name"]
console.log({ ...u }); // { name: "Ada" }

const json = JSON.stringify(u); // {"name":"Ada"}
```

## 17. JSON loses prototype and methods

`JSON.parse` returns a plain object. Rehydrate carefully if you need class behavior.

```ts
class User {
  name = "";
  greet() {
    return `Hi, ${this.name}`;
  }
}

const raw = JSON.stringify(new User());
const parsed = JSON.parse(raw) as User;

// parsed.greet(); // runtime error: not a function

const revived = Object.assign(new User(), parsed);
console.log(revived.greet()); // works
```

Also be careful: `Object.assign` does not run constructor defaults for missing fields.

## 18. `instanceof` is realm- and bundle-sensitive

Multiple copies of a class, iframes, or transpiled builds can break `instanceof`.

```ts
class User {
  readonly brand = "User" as const;
}

function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return (
    value !== null &&
    typeof value === "object" &&
    "brand" in value &&
    (value as { brand?: unknown }).brand === "User"
  );
}
```

For cross-realm or plugin architectures, use branding, `Symbol.toStringTag`, or explicit tags.

## 19. `Object.create(null)` objects have no prototype

`Object.create(null)` produces dictionary objects. They are not `instanceof Object`, and inherited helpers such as `hasOwnProperty` are unavailable.

```ts
const dict = Object.create(null);
dict.x = 1;

// dict.hasOwnProperty("x"); // TypeError
console.log(Object.hasOwn(dict, "x")); // true
console.log(dict instanceof Object); // false
```

Use `Object.hasOwn` instead of calling `hasOwnProperty` directly.

## 20. `hasOwnProperty` inherited checks can be shadowed

An own property named `hasOwnProperty` can shadow `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty`.

```ts
const obj = { hasOwnProperty: () => false };
console.log(Object.hasOwn(obj, "x")); // safe
```

Prefer `Object.hasOwn`.

## 21. Spread and `Object.assign` copy values, not accessors

Copying an instance copies enumerable own data properties. Getters are evaluated and their values copied.

```ts
class WithAccessor {
  get value() {
    return Math.random();
  }
}

const original = new WithAccessor();
const copy = { ...original };

console.log(typeof copy.value); // "number", not a getter
```

Do not expect copied objects to preserve accessor behavior.

## 22. Prototype mutation is performance- and correctness-sensitive

Changing an object's prototype after creation can deoptimize engines and break assumptions.

```ts
class A {}
class B extends A {}

const o = new A();
// Object.setPrototypeOf(o, B.prototype); // avoid when possible
```

Prefer creating the correct prototype from the start.

## 23. Mixins require constructor constraints

Mixin functions should constrain base constructors and return typed classes.

```ts
type Constructor<T = object> = new (...args: any[]) => T;

function Timestamped<TBase extends Constructor>(Base: TBase) {
  return class extends Base {
    timestamp = Date.now();
  };
}

function Named<TBase extends Constructor>(Base: TBase) {
  return class extends Base {
    name = "";
  };
}

class User {
  id = 1;
}

const TimestampedNamedUser = Timestamped(Named(User));
const u = new TimestampedNamedUser();
console.log(u.id, u.timestamp, u.name);
```

Compose mixins in a known order; later mixins override earlier members.

## 24. Singleton and private constructors

A private constructor controls instantiation but still allows internal static access.

```ts
class Singleton {
  private static instance: Singleton | undefined;
  private constructor(public value: number) {}

  static getInstance(value = 0) {
    if (!Singleton.instance) {
      Singleton.instance = new Singleton(value);
    }
    return Singleton.instance;
  }
}

const a = Singleton.getInstance(1);
const b = Singleton.getInstance(2);
console.log(a === b); // true
console.log(a.value); // 1
```

If you want to prevent subclassing, also make the class `final` conceptually by not exporting or by freezing prototypes.

## 25. `delete` and optional/required properties

`delete` is primarily meaningful for optional properties. Required property types cannot simply be deleted without a type error unless modeled as optional.

```ts
type RecordLike = {
  id: number;
  label?: string;
};

const obj: RecordLike = { id: 1, label: "x" };
delete obj.label; // okay
// delete obj.id; // type error
```

## 26. `for...in` and object identity checks

`for...in` enumerates inherited enumerable keys, while `Object.keys` enumerates own enumerable keys.

```ts
class Base {
  baseValue = 1;
}

class Derived extends Base {
  derivedValue = 2;
}

const d = new Derived();

console.log(Object.keys(d)); // ["baseValue", "derivedValue"] (own fields)

const names: string[] = [];
for (const key in d) names.push(key);
console.log(names); // may include inherited enumerable properties
```

Use `Object.hasOwn` and `Object.keys` for safe own-property enumeration.

## 27. Nullish handling and definite assignment assertions

The `!` assertion tells TypeScript to trust initialization that happens outside normal constructor flow, such as dependency injection.

```ts
class Service {
  client!: { request(): void }; // initialized by DI framework

  run() {
    this.client.request(); // no compile-time undefined check
  }
}
```

Prefer constructors or factories when possible; `!` hides real initialization bugs.

## 28. Class as both type and value

`MyClass` is a type for instances, while `typeof MyClass` is the constructor type.

```ts
class MyClass {
  static factory() {
    return new MyClass();
  }
}

function create(Ctor: typeof MyClass) {
  return new Ctor();
}

const instance: MyClass = create(MyClass);
```

This matters for dependency injection, factories, and generic constructor constraints.

## 29. Event listeners and `this`

Passing a method reference to an event emitter loses `this` unless bound.

```ts
class ButtonHandler {
  clicked = false;

  handleClick = () => {
    this.clicked = true;
  };
}

const h = new ButtonHandler();
const callback = h.handleClick;
callback(); // safe because arrow field
```

For DOM events, arrow fields are usually the simplest fix.

## 30. Decorators are version-sensitive

Legacy `experimentalDecorators` and standard Stage 3 decorators have different semantics and initialization timing. Do not mix their assumptions.

```ts
// Legacy-style example
function logged(target: any, key: string) {
  // legacy decorator receives prototype/member descriptor
}

// Stage 3 decorators have a different API and initialization model.
```

Check your `tsconfig` decorator settings and library compatibility before using decorators heavily.

## Practical checklist

- Enable `strict`, and consider `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `noImplicitOverride`, and `useDefineForClassFields`.
- Avoid calling overridable methods from constructors.
- Use `#private` for true runtime privacy.
- Bind or use arrow fields for callbacks.
- Treat `readonly` as shallow unless you implement deep immutability.
- Rehydrate class instances after JSON parsing.
- Prefer `Object.hasOwn` over `hasOwnProperty`.
- Brand values for nominal behavior and cross-realm safety.

