## Custom exceptions in TypeScript (interview answer)

### What they are
A **custom exception** is a class that extends `Error` (or a base custom error) so you can:
- distinguish failure kinds with `instanceof` (not just string matching),
- attach domain data (`code`, `statusCode`, `details`),
- preserve the original failure via **error chaining** (`cause`),
- keep a useful stack trace,
- serialize safely for logs/API responses.

### Key implementation concerns
1. **Prototype chain** — when transpiled to ES5, `instanceof` can break; fix with `Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype)`.
2. **Stack trace** — V8 supports `Error.captureStackTrace`; call it to remove constructor noise.
3. **Chaining** — store `cause` (ES2022) so root cause isn’t lost.
4. **Naming** — set `this.name` so logs show `NotFoundError` instead of `Error`.
5. **Normalization** — convert unknown thrown values into your error type at boundaries.
6. **Serialization** — `toJSON` must avoid circular structures and not leak internals.

---

### Core implementation

```ts
// errors/base.ts
export interface ErrorOptions {
  message: string;
  code: string;
  statusCode?: number;
  details?: unknown;
  cause?: unknown;
}

/**
 * Base application error.
 * All domain errors should extend this so callers can catch `AppError`
 * and inspect `code` / `statusCode`.
 */
export class AppError extends Error {
  readonly code: string;
  readonly statusCode: number;
  readonly details?: unknown;
  readonly cause?: unknown;

  constructor(opts: ErrorOptions) {
    super(opts.message);

    // Preserve prototype chain when targeting ES5/transpiled.
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);

    this.name = this.constructor.name;
    this.code = opts.code;
    this.statusCode = opts.statusCode ?? 500;
    this.details = opts.details;

    // Error chaining (ES2022). Assigned manually for broader target support.
    if (opts.cause !== undefined) {
      (this as { cause?: unknown }).cause = opts.cause;
    }

    // V8: capture a clean stack trace excluding this constructor.
    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, new.target);
    }
  }

  /** Safe serialization for HTTP responses / structured logs. */
  toJSON(): Record<string, unknown> {
    return {
      name: this.name,
      message: this.message,
      code: this.code,
      statusCode: this.statusCode,
      details: this.details,
      cause:
        this.cause instanceof Error
          ? { name: this.cause.name, message: this.cause.message }
          : this.cause,
      stack: this.stack,
    };
  }
}
```

```ts
// errors/domain.ts
import { AppError } from './base';

export class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource: string, id?: string | number, cause?: unknown) {
    super({
      message: id !== undefined ? `${resource} not found: ${id}` : `${resource} not found`,
      code: 'NOT_FOUND',
      statusCode: 404,
      cause,
    });
  }
}

export class ValidationError extends AppError {
  constructor(message: string, details?: unknown, cause?: unknown) {
    super({
      message,
      code: 'VALIDATION_ERROR',
      statusCode: 400,
      details,
      cause,
    });
  }
}

export class UnauthorizedError extends AppError {
  constructor(message = 'Unauthorized', cause?: unknown) {
    super({
      message,
      code: 'UNAUTHORIZED',
      statusCode: 401,
      cause,
    });
  }
}

export class ConflictError extends AppError {
  constructor(message: string, details?: unknown, cause?: unknown) {
    super({
      message,
      code: 'CONFLICT',
      statusCode: 409,
      details,
      cause,
    });
  }
}
```

```ts
// errors/normalize.ts
import { AppError } from './base';

/**
 * Convert any thrown value into an AppError at system boundaries
 * (HTTP handlers, queue consumers, RPC layers).
 */
export function toAppError(err: unknown): AppError {
  if (err instanceof AppError) return err;

  if (err instanceof Error) {
    return new AppError({
      message: err.message || 'Unknown error',
      code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR',
      statusCode: 500,
      cause: err,
    });
  }

  // Non-Error throws (strings, numbers, objects).
  return new AppError({
    message: typeof err === 'string' ? err : 'Unknown error',
    code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR',
    statusCode: 500,
    details: err,
  });
}

/** Type guard for catch blocks. */
export function isAppError(err: unknown): err is AppError {
  return err instanceof AppError;
}
```

### Usage example

```ts
import { NotFoundError, ValidationError, toAppError } from './errors';

function getUser(id: string) {
  const user = db.find(id);
  if (!user) throw new NotFoundError('User', id);
  return user;
}

function createUser(input: unknown) {
  if (!isValid(input)) {
    throw new ValidationError('Invalid user payload', { field: 'email' });
  }
  // ...
}

// Boundary handling
try {
  const user = getUser('123');
  res.json(user);
} catch (err) {
  const appErr = toAppError(err);
  res.status(appErr.statusCode).json(appErr.toJSON());
}
```

### Interview talking points (short)
- **Why not just `throw new Error('...')`?** Strings aren’t type-safe; `instanceof` + `code` enables precise handling.
- **Why `cause`?** Preserves root cause without string concatenation.
- **Why normalize?** Third-party/unknown throws break assumptions; normalize at boundaries.
- **Why `Object.setPrototypeOf`?** Transpilation safety for `instanceof`.
- **Why `captureStackTrace`?** Cleaner debugging output.

# Edge cases when using custom exceptions in TypeScript

Custom errors in TypeScript are usually `class MyError extends Error { ... }`. Most edge cases come from JavaScript’s `Error` semantics (non-enumerable fields, engine-specific stacks, cross-realm objects), TypeScript’s strict typing (`unknown` in `catch`), and transpilation targets.

Below are the important edge cases, each with idiomatic code.

---

## 1. Prototype chain breaks under ES5 transpilation

If you compile to ES5, `instanceof` and inherited methods can fail unless you repair the prototype.

```ts
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);

    // Repair prototype chain for ES5/transpiled output.
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);

    this.name = "AppError";
  }
}
```

`new.target` may not be available on very old targets. Prefer `target: "ES2020"` or newer in `tsconfig.json` when possible.

---

## 2. Stack trace points at the constructor instead of the throw site

In V8 (Node/Chrome), call `Error.captureStackTrace` and exclude the constructor frame.

```ts
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "AppError";

    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      // `this.constructor` excludes constructor frames in subclasses too.
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
    }
  }
}
```

`Error.captureStackTrace` does not exist in all engines, so always guard it.

---

## 3. `name` is not inherited correctly

`super(message)` sets `name = "Error"`. Override it explicitly.

```ts
class ValidationError extends Error {
  readonly field: string;

  constructor(field: string, message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ValidationError";
    this.field = field;
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }
}
```

Avoid relying on `constructor.name` in minified production code; use an explicit string.

---

## 4. Error `cause` chaining and older TypeScript libs

Modern TS supports `ErrorOptions`, but you may need fallbacks.

```ts
type ErrorOptionsLike = { cause?: unknown };

class AppError extends Error {
  readonly code: string;
  readonly details?: unknown;
  readonly cause?: unknown;

  constructor(
    message: string,
    options?: { code?: string; details?: unknown; cause?: unknown }
  ) {
    // Prefer native cause support; fall back manually.
    try {
      super(message, { cause: options?.cause } as ErrorOptions);
    } catch {
      super(message);
      (this as { cause?: unknown }).cause = options?.cause;
    }

    this.name = "AppError";
    this.code = options?.code ?? "APP_ERROR";
    this.details = options?.details;

    if (options?.cause !== undefined && this.cause === undefined) {
      this.cause = options.cause;
    }

    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }
}
```

When rethrowing, preserve the original error as `cause`:

```ts
try {
  await db.query(sql);
} catch (e) {
  throw new AppError("Database query failed", {
    code: "DB_QUERY_FAILED",
    cause: e,
  });
}
```

---

## 5. `catch (e)` is `unknown` in strict TypeScript

With `useUnknownInCatchVariables`, you cannot access properties without narrowing.

```ts
function handle(err: unknown): string {
  if (err instanceof AppError) {
    return `${err.code}: ${err.message}`;
  }

  if (err instanceof Error) {
    return `Error: ${err.message}`;
  }

  // Non-Error throws are legal in JavaScript.
  return `Unknown thrown value: ${String(err)}`;
}
```

Never do `const e = err as AppError;` without a guard.

---

## 6. Thrown values are not always `Error` instances

JavaScript allows `throw "boom"`, `throw null`, `throw 42`. Custom-error code must tolerate that.

```ts
try {
  risky();
} catch (e) {
  const wrapped =
    e instanceof Error
      ? new AppError("Operation failed", { cause: e })
      : new AppError("Operation failed with non-Error throw", {
          details: e,
        });

  throw wrapped;
}
```

---

## 7. JSON serialization loses Error data

`Error` fields such as `message`, `stack`, and `name` are non-enumerable, so `JSON.stringify(err)` often yields `{}`. Implement `toJSON`.

```ts
class AppError extends Error {
  readonly code: string;
  readonly details?: unknown;

  constructor(message: string, code = "APP_ERROR", details?: unknown) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "AppError";
    this.code = code;
    this.details = details;
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }

  toJSON(): Record<string, unknown> {
    const seen = new WeakSet<object>();

    const serializeCause = (value: unknown): unknown => {
      if (value instanceof Error) {
        return value instanceof AppError ? value.toJSON() : {
          name: value.name,
          message: value.message,
          stack: value.stack,
        };
      }
      return value;
    };

    // Guard against circular cause chains.
    if (this.cause && typeof this.cause === "object") {
      if (seen.has(this.cause as object)) {
        return { name: this.name, message: this.message, code: this.code, cause: "[Circular]" };
      }
      seen.add(this.cause as object);
    }

    return {
      name: this.name,
      message: this.message,
      code: this.code,
      details: this.details,
      stack: this.stack,
      cause: serializeCause(this.cause),
    };
  }
}
```

Also note that spreading an error does not copy non-enumerable fields:

```ts
const err = new AppError("boom", "X");
console.log(Object.keys(err));        // usually []
console.log({ ...err });              // usually {}
console.log(JSON.stringify(err));     // {} without toJSON
```

---

## 8. `instanceof` fails across realms (iframe, VM, worker)

Objects created in another realm have a different `Error` constructor, so `instanceof` is false. Use a brand or duck-typing check.

```ts
const APP_ERROR_BRAND = Symbol.for("mylib.AppError");

class AppError extends Error {
  [APP_ERROR_BRAND] = true;
  readonly code: string;

  constructor(message: string, code = "APP_ERROR") {
    super(message);
    this.name = "AppError";
    this.code = code;
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }

  static is(value: unknown): value is AppError {
    return (
      typeof value === "object" &&
      value !== null &&
      (value as Record<PropertyKey, unknown>)[APP_ERROR_BRAND] === true
    );
  }
}
```

`Symbol.for` is shared across realms, making the brand reliable. `instanceof` is still fine for same-realm code.

---

## 9. `finally` blocks can mask the original error

If cleanup throws, the original custom error is lost.

```ts
async function runJob() {
  let original: unknown;

  try {
    await doWork(); // may throw AppError
  } catch (e) {
    original = e;
    throw e;
  } finally {
    try {
      await cleanup();
    } catch (cleanupError) {
      // Log, but do not throw over the original error.
      console.error("Cleanup failed", cleanupError);

      if (original !== undefined) {
        // Optionally attach cleanup failure as cause/metadata.
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Same issue exists in synchronous `try/finally`.

---

## 10. Async propagation and unhandled rejections

Custom errors propagate through promises, but thrown inside async constructors/callbacks can become unhandled rejections. Wrap boundary code.

```ts
async function main() {
  try {
    await service.call();
  } catch (e) {
    if (AppError.is(e) && e.code === "NOT_FOUND") {
      return null;
    }
    throw e; // preserve stack and cause
  }
}

main().catch((e) => {
  console.error(e instanceof Error ? e.stack : e);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

In Node, use `process.on("unhandledRejection", ...)` only as a last resort; prefer fixing boundary handling.

---

## 11. `toString()` and logging expectations

Default `Error.prototype.toString()` returns `name: message`. If you add a code, override carefully.

```ts
class AppError extends Error {
  readonly code: string;

  constructor(message: string, code = "APP_ERROR") {
    super(message);
    this.name = "AppError";
    this.code = code;
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }

  toString(): string {
    return `${this.name} [${this.code}]: ${this.message}`;
  }
}
```

Note: loggers often use `stack`, not `toString()`. Keep `stack` intact.

---

## 12. `AggregateError` and multiple failures

If aggregating multiple custom errors, use the built-in `AggregateError` or subclass it correctly.

```ts
class BatchError extends AggregateError {
  readonly operation: string;

  constructor(errors: unknown[], operation: string, message?: string) {
    super(errors, message ?? `${errors.length} failures in ${operation}`);
    this.name = "BatchError";
    this.operation = operation;
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }
}
```

Edge: an empty errors array is allowed; decide whether that is valid in your domain.

---

## 13. Redacting sensitive data in stacks/messages

Custom errors may accidentally include secrets. Sanitize before constructing or in `toJSON`.

```ts
class HttpError extends Error {
  readonly status: number;
  readonly url: string;

  constructor(status: number, url: string, body?: unknown) {
    const safeUrl = url.replace(/token=[^&]+/, "token=[REDACTED]");
    super(`HTTP ${status} for ${safeUrl}`);
    this.name = "HttpError";
    this.status = status;
    this.url = safeUrl;

    // Do not store raw body if it may contain secrets.
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);
  }
}
```

---

## 14. `Error.prepareStackTrace` and custom formatting

V8 allows global stack formatting overrides. Your `captureStackTrace` still works, but stack may be formatted differently. Do not parse stack strings.

```ts
const original = Error.prepareStackTrace;

Error.prepareStackTrace = (err, frames) => {
  // Custom formatting; be careful not to break tooling.
  return `${err.name}: ${err.message}\n` + frames.map(f => `  at ${f}`).join("\n");
};

// Restore in tests/cleanup:
Error.prepareStackTrace = original;
```

---

## 15. Recommended base class

A solid reusable base:

```ts
const BRAND = Symbol.for("app.AppError");

export class AppError extends Error {
  [BRAND] = true;

  readonly code: string;
  readonly details?: unknown;
  readonly cause?: unknown;

  constructor(
    message: string,
    options?: { code?: string; details?: unknown; cause?: unknown }
  ) {
    try {
      super(message, { cause: options?.cause } as ErrorOptions);
    } catch {
      super(message);
      (this as { cause?: unknown }).cause = options?.cause;
    }

    this.name = "AppError";
    this.code = options?.code ?? "APP_ERROR";
    this.details = options?.details;

    if (options?.cause !== undefined && this.cause === undefined) {
      this.cause = options.cause;
    }

    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);

    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {
      Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
    }
  }

  static is(value: unknown): value is AppError {
    return (
      typeof value === "object" &&
      value !== null &&
      (value as Record<PropertyKey, unknown>)[BRAND] === true
    );
  }

  toJSON(): Record<string, unknown> {
    return {
      name: this.name,
      message: this.message,
      code: this.code,
      details: this.details,
      stack: this.stack,
      cause:
        this.cause instanceof Error
          ? { name: this.cause.name, message: this.cause.message, stack: this.cause.stack }
          : this.cause,
    };
  }
}
```

### Practical checklist

- Repair prototype for ES5 targets.
- Guard `Error.captureStackTrace`.
- Set `name` explicitly.
- Support and preserve `cause`.
- Narrow `unknown` in `catch` with type guards.
- Implement `toJSON` for serialization.
- Use branding for cross-realm checks.
- Avoid throwing from `finally`.
- Sanitize sensitive values.
- Do not parse stack strings.

# Database Indexes in TypeScript — Complete Runnable Example

Below is a self-contained, dependency-free TypeScript implementation of a mini database engine with **hash indexes** and **sorted (B-tree-style) indexes**, plus a **query planner** that automatically picks the best index. It demonstrates equality lookups, range scans, composite (multi-column) indexes, insert/delete maintenance, `EXPLAIN`-style plans, and performance comparisons.

## Key ideas implemented

- **`HashIndex`** — `Map<key, Set<rowId>>`. O(1) equality lookup; cannot do ranges.
- **`SortedIndex`** — sorted array + binary search (O(log n) lookup, O(log n) range bounds). Supports prefix equality and range on the leading column.
- **Composite indexes** — leading-column rule: `idx(city, age)` can serve `city = ?` and `city = ? AND age range`, but not `age` alone.
- **Query planner** — prefers hash for full-key equality, then sorted prefix equality, then sorted range, else full scan.
- **Maintenance** — every `insert`/`delete` updates all indexes.
- **Statistics** — scanned-row counts and index sizes, so you can see *why* an index helps.

## How to run

```bash
npm init -y
npm i -D typescript ts-node @types/node
npx ts-node database-indexes.ts
```

## Source: `database-indexes.ts`

```typescript
// database-indexes.ts
// Run with: npx ts-node database-indexes.ts

type Value = string | number | boolean | null;
type Row = Record<string, Value>;

interface RangeSpec {
  lower: Value | null;
  upper: Value | null;
  li: boolean; // lower inclusive
  ui: boolean; // upper inclusive
}

// ---------- comparison helpers ----------

function cmp(a: Value, b: Value): number {
  if (a === b) return 0;
  if (a === null) return -1;
  if (b === null) return 1;
  if (typeof a === 'number' && typeof b === 'number') return a < b ? -1 : 1;
  if (typeof a === 'boolean' && typeof b === 'boolean') return (a ? 1 : 0) - (b ? 1 : 0);
  return String(a) < String(b) ? -1 : 1;
}

function cmpKeys(a: Value[], b: Value[]): number {
  const n = Math.max(a.length, b.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const r = cmp(a[i] ?? null, b[i] ?? null);
    if (r !== 0) return r;
  }
  return 0;
}

// ---------- Index abstraction ----------

abstract class Index {
  abstract name: string;
  abstract columns: string[];
  abstract type: 'hash' | 'sorted';
  abstract insert(row: Row, rowId: number): void;
  abstract remove(rowId: number, row: Row): void;
  abstract findEqual(values: Value[]): number[] | null;
  abstract findRange(column: string, lower: Value | null, upper: Value | null, li: boolean, ui: boolean): number[] | null;
  abstract size(): number;
}

// ---------- Hash index (O(1) equality) ----------

class HashIndex extends Index {
  name: string;
  columns: string[];
  type: 'hash' = 'hash';
  private map = new Map<string, Set<number>>();

  constructor(name: string, columns: string[]) {
    super();
    this.name = name;
    this.columns = columns;
  }

  private keyOf(row: Row): string {
    return JSON.stringify(this.columns.map((c) => row[c] ?? null));
  }

  insert(row: Row, id: number): void {
    const k = this.keyOf(row);
    if (!this.map.has(k)) this.map.set(k, new Set());
    this.map.get(k)!.add(id);
  }

  remove(id: number, row: Row): void {
    const k = this.keyOf(row);
    const s = this.map.get(k);
    if (!s) return;
    s.delete(id);
    if (s.size === 0) this.map.delete(k);
  }

  findEqual(values: Value[]): number[] | null {
    if (values.length !== this.columns.length) return null;
    const k = JSON.stringify(values.map((v) => v ?? null));
    const s = this.map.get(k);
    return s ? [...s] : [];
  }

  findRange(): number[] | null {
    return null; // hash indexes cannot serve ranges
  }

  size(): number {
    let n = 0;
    for (const s of this.map.values()) n += s.size;
    return n;
  }
}

// ---------- Sorted index (binary search; B-tree-like semantics) ----------

class SortedIndex extends Index {
  name: string;
  columns: string[];
  type: 'sorted' = 'sorted';
  private entries: { key: Value[]; id: number }[] = [];

  constructor(name: string, columns: string[]) {
    super();
    this.name = name;
    this.columns = columns;
  }

  private keyOf(row: Row): Value[] {
    return this.columns.map((c) => row[c] ?? null);
  }

  insert(row: Row, id: number): void {
    const key = this.keyOf(row);
    let lo = 0;
    let hi = this.entries.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (cmpKeys(this.entries[mid].key, key) <= 0) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid;
    }
    this.entries.splice(lo, 0, { key, id });
  }

  remove(id: number, row: Row): void {
    const key = this.keyOf(row);
    let lo = 0;
    let hi = this.entries.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (cmpKeys(this.entries[mid].key, key) < 0) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid;
    }
    for (let i = lo; i < this.entries.length; i++) {
      if (cmpKeys(this.entries[i].key, key) !== 0) break;
      if (this.entries[i].id === id) {
        this.entries.splice(i, 1);
        return;
      }
    }
  }

  findEqual(values: Value[]): number[] | null {
    if (values.length === 0 || values.length > this.columns.length) return null;
    const padded = values.map((v) => v ?? null);
    let lo = 0;
    let hi = this.entries.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (cmpKeys(this.entries[mid].key, padded) < 0) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid;
    }
    const out: number[] = [];
    for (let i = lo; i < this.entries.length; i++) {
      let match = true;
      for (let j = 0; j < values.length; j++) {
        if (cmp(this.entries[i].key[j], values[j] ?? null) !== 0) {
          match = false;
          break;
        }
      }
      if (!match) break;
      out.push(this.entries[i].id);
    }
    return out;
  }

  findRange(column: string, lower: Value | null, upper: Value | null, li: boolean, ui: boolean): number[] | null {
    if (column !== this.columns[0]) return null;

    let lo = 0;
    if (lower !== null) {
      let hi = this.entries.length;
      while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
        const c = cmp(this.entries[mid].key[0], lower);
        const ge = li ? c >= 0 : c > 0;
        if (ge) hi = mid;
        else lo = mid + 1;
      }
    }

    let hi = this.entries.length;
    if (upper !== null) {
      let l = 0;
      let h = this.entries.length;
      while (l < h) {
        const mid = (l + h) >> 1;
        const c = cmp(this.entries[mid].key[0], upper);
        const le = ui ? c <= 0 : c < 0;
        if (le) l = mid + 1;
        else h = mid;
      }
      hi = l;
    }

    const out: number[] = [];
    for (let i = lo; i < hi; i++) out.push(this.entries[i].id);
    return out;
  }

  size(): number {
    return this.entries.length;
  }
}

// ---------- Table ----------

class Table {
  name: string;
  columns: string[];
  rows: (Row | null)[] = [];
  indexes: Index[] = [];

  constructor(name: string, columns: string[]) {
    this.name = name;
    this.columns = columns;
  }

  createIndex(name: string, columns: string[], type: 'hash' | 'sorted' = 'sorted'): Index {
    const idx = type === 'hash' ? new HashIndex(name, columns) : new SortedIndex(name, columns);
    this.rows.forEach((r, i) => {
      if (r) idx.insert(r, i);
    });
    this.indexes.push(idx);
    return idx;
  }

  dropIndex(name: string): boolean {
    const i = this.indexes.findIndex((x) => x.name === name);
    if (i < 0) return false;
    this.indexes.splice(i, 1);
    return true;
  }

  insert(row: Row): number {
    const r: Row = {};
    for (const c of this.columns) r[c] = row[c] ?? null;
    const id = this.rows.length;
    this.rows.push(r);
    for (const idx of this.indexes) idx.insert(r, id);
    return id;
  }

  deleteById(id: number): boolean {
    const row = this.rows[id];
    if (!row) return false;
    for (const idx of this.indexes) idx.remove(id, row);
    this.rows[id] = null;
    return true;
  }

  private normalizeWhere(where: Record<string, any>): {
    eq: Map<string, Value>;
    ranges: Map<string, RangeSpec>;
    all: Record<string, any>;
  } {
    const eq = new Map<string, Value>();
    const ranges = new Map<string, RangeSpec>();
    const all: Record<string, any> = {};
    for (const [col, cond] of Object.entries(where || {})) {
      all[col] = cond;
      if (cond && typeof cond === 'object' && !Array.isArray(cond)) {
        const r: RangeSpec = { lower: null, upper: null, li: false, ui: false };
        let isRange = false;
        if ('$gt' in cond) {
          r.lower = cond.$gt;
          r.li = false;
          isRange = true;
        }
        if ('$gte' in cond) {
          r.lower = cond.$gte;
          r.li = true;
          isRange = true;
        }
        if ('$lt' in cond) {
          r.upper = cond.$lt;
          r.ui = false;
          isRange = true;
        }
        if ('$lte' in cond) {
          r.upper = cond.$lte;
          r.ui = true;
          isRange = true;
        }
        if ('$between' in cond) {
          r.lower = cond.$between[0];
          r.upper = cond.$between[1];
          r.li = true;
          r.ui = true;
          isRange = true;
        }
        if (isRange) {
          ranges.set(col, r);
          continue;
        }
      }
      eq.set(col, cond as Value);
    }
    return { eq, ranges, all };
  }

  private matches(row: Row, where: Record<string, any>): boolean {
    for (const [col, cond] of Object.entries(where || {})) {
      const v = row[col];
      if (cond && typeof cond === 'object' && !Array.isArray(cond)) {
        if ('$gt' in cond && !(cmp(v, cond.$gt) > 0)) return false;
        if ('$gte' in cond && !(cmp(v, cond.$gte) >= 0)) return false;
        if ('$lt' in cond && !(cmp(v, cond.$lt) < 0)) return false;
        if ('$lte' in cond && !(cmp(v, cond.$lte) <= 0)) return false;
        if ('$between' in cond && !(cmp(v, cond.$between[0]) >= 0 && cmp(v, cond.$between[1]) <= 0)) return false;
        if ('$ne' in cond && cmp(v, cond.$ne) === 0) return false;
        if ('$in' in cond && !cond.$in.includes(v)) return false;
      } else {
        if (cmp(v, cond as Value) !== 0) return false;
      }
    }
    return true;
  }

  select(where: Record<string, any> = {}): { rows: Row[]; plan: string; scanned: number } {
    const { eq, ranges, all } = this.normalizeWhere(where);
    let candidateIds: number[] | null = null;
    let plan = '';

    // 1) Hash index: full equality on all of its columns
    for (const idx of this.indexes) {
      if (idx.type === 'hash' && idx.columns.length > 0 && idx.columns.every((c) => eq.has(c))) {
        const vals = idx.columns.map((c) => eq.get(c)!);
        const res = idx.findEqual(vals);
        if (res !== null) {
          candidateIds = res;
          plan = `HashIndex "${idx.name}" equality(${idx.columns.join(',')})`;
          break;
        }
      }
    }

    // 2) Sorted index: prefix equality on leading columns
    if (candidateIds === null) {
      for (const idx of this.indexes) {
        if (idx.type !== 'sorted') continue;
        const prefix: Value[] = [];
        for (const c of idx.columns) {
          if (eq.has(c)) prefix.push(eq.get(c)!);
          else break;
        }
        if (prefix.length > 0) {
          const res = idx.findEqual(prefix);
          if (res !== null) {
            candidateIds = res;
            plan = `SortedIndex "${idx.name}" prefix-eq(${prefix.map((_, i) => idx.columns[i]).join(',')})`;
            break;
          }
        }
      }
    }

    // 3) Sorted index: range on leading column
    if (candidateIds === null) {
      for (const idx of this.indexes) {
        if (idx.type !== 'sorted') continue;
        const first = idx.columns[0];
        if (ranges.has(first)) {
          const r = ranges.get(first)!;
          const res = idx.findRange(first, r.lower, r.upper, r.li, r.ui);
          if (res !== null) {
            candidateIds = res;
            plan = `SortedIndex "${idx.name}" range(${first})`;
            break;
          }
        }
      }
    }

    // 4) Full table scan
    if (candidateIds === null) {
      candidateIds = this.rows.map((_, i) => i).filter((i) => this.rows[i] !== null);
      plan = 'Full table scan';
    }

    const result: Row[] = [];
    for (const id of candidateIds) {
      const row = this.rows[id];
      if (!row) continue;
      if (this.matches(row, all)) result.push({ ...row, _id: id });
    }
    return { rows: result, plan, scanned: candidateIds.length };
  }

  count(): number {
    return this.rows.filter((r) => r !== null).length;
  }
}

// ---------- Demo ----------

function time(fn: () => void): number {
  const t = Date.now();
  fn();
  return Date.now() - t;
}

function main(): void {
  console.log('=== Database Indexes Demo (TypeScript) ===\n');

  const table = new Table('users', ['id', 'name', 'email', 'age', 'city']);

  const N = 50000;
  const cities = ['NYC', 'LA', 'SF', 'Chicago', 'Boston', 'Seattle'];
  console.log(`Inserting ${N} rows...`);
  const tInsert = time(() => {
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      table.insert({
        id: i,
        name: `user_${i}`,
        email: `user${i}@example.com`,
        age: 18 + (i % 60),
        city: cities[i % cities.length],
      });
    }
  });
  console.log(`Inserted in ${tInsert}ms\n`);

  table.createIndex('idx_email', ['email'], 'hash');
  table.createIndex('idx_age', ['age'], 'sorted');
  table.createIndex('idx_city_age', ['city', 'age'], 'sorted');

  console.log('Indexes:', table.indexes.map((i) => `${i.name}(${i.columns.join(',')}) [${i.type}]`).join(', '));
  console.log('');

  let r = table.select({ email: 'user42424@example.com' });
  console.log('Q1 email equality ->', r.rows.length, 'row | plan:', r.plan, '| scanned:', r.scanned);

  r = table.select({ age: { $gte: 25, $lte: 35 } });
  console.log('Q2 age range 25-35 ->', r.rows.length, 'rows | plan:', r.plan, '| scanned:', r.scanned);

  r = table.select({ city: 'SF' });
  console.log('Q3 city=SF ->', r.rows.length, 'rows | plan:', r.plan, '| scanned:', r.scanned);

  r = table.select({ city: 'SF', age: { $gte: 30, $lte: 40 } });
  console.log('Q4 city=SF & age 30-40 ->', r.rows.length, 'rows | plan:', r.plan, '| scanned:', r.scanned);

  r = table.select({ name: 'user_9999' });
  console.log('Q5 name equality (no idx) ->', r.rows.length, 'row | plan:', r.plan, '| scanned:', r.scanned);

  r = table.select({ age: { $between: [20, 25] }, city: { $in: ['SF', 'LA'] } });
  console.log('Q6 age 20-25 & city in (SF,LA) ->', r.rows.length, 'rows | plan:', r.plan, '| scanned:', r.scanned);

  console.log('\n--- Performance comparison ---');
  const t1 = time(() => {
    for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) table.select({ email: `user${i}@example.com` });
  });
  console.log(`1000 email lookups (indexed): ${t1}ms`);

  const t2 = time(() => {
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) table.select({ name: `user_${i}` });
  });
  console.log(`100 name lookups (full scan): ${t2}ms`);

  const t3 = time(() => {
    for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) table.select({ age: { $gte: 20, $lte: 40 } });
  });
  console.log(`50 age-range queries (indexed): ${t3}ms`);

  // Demonstrate maintenance on delete
  const before = table.count();
  table.deleteById(42424);
  console.log('\nAfter delete id=42424 -> count', table.count(), '(was', before, ')');
  r = table.select({ email: 'user42424@example.com' });
  console.log('Lookup deleted email ->', r.rows.length, 'rows (expect 0)');

  console.log('\nTotal rows:', table.count());
  console.log('Index sizes:', table.indexes.map((i) => `${i.name}=${i.size()}`).join(', '));
}

main();
```

