## Final solution (single file)

```ts
// db.ts
export interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  age: number;
  active: boolean;
  createdAt: Date;
}

export class Table<T extends Record<string, any>> {
  private rows: T[] = [];
  private autoId = 1;

  constructor(
    private opts: { primaryKey: keyof T & string; uniqueFields?: (keyof T & string)[] }
  ) {}

  insert(row: Omit<T, "id"> & Partial<Pick<T, "id">>): T {
    const id = row.id ?? this.autoId++;
    const full = { ...row, id } as T;

    for (const field of this.opts.uniqueFields ?? []) {
      if (this.rows.some(r => r[field] === full[field])) {
        throw new Error(`Unique constraint violated on ${field}`);
      }
    }
    this.rows.push(full);
    return full;
  }

  findById(id: T[keyof T]): T | undefined {
    return this.rows.find(r => r[this.opts.primaryKey] === id);
  }

  update(id: T[keyof T], patch: Partial<T>): T {
    const row = this.findById(id);
    if (!row) throw new Error("Row not found");

    for (const field of this.opts.uniqueFields ?? []) {
      if (patch[field] !== undefined && this.rows.some(r => r !== row && r[field] === patch[field])) {
        throw new Error(`Unique constraint violated on ${field}`);
      }
    }
    Object.assign(row, patch);
    return row;
  }

  delete(id: T[keyof T]): boolean {
    const idx = this.rows.findIndex(r => r[this.opts.primaryKey] === id);
    if (idx === -1) return false;
    this.rows.splice(idx, 1);
    return true;
  }

  find(
    predicate: (row: T) => boolean = () => true,
    options?: { sort?: { field: keyof T; dir?: "asc" | "desc" }; page?: number; pageSize?: number }
  ) {
    let result = this.rows.filter(predicate);
    if (options?.sort) {
      const { field, dir = "asc" } = options.sort;
      result = [...result].sort((a, b) => {
        const va = a[field], vb = b[field];
        if (va < vb) return dir === "asc" ? -1 : 1;
        if (va > vb) return dir === "asc" ? 1 : -1;
        return 0;
      });
    }
    const total = result.length;
    const page = options?.page ?? 1;
    const pageSize = options?.pageSize ?? (total || 1);
    const start = (page - 1) * pageSize;
    return { data: result.slice(start, start + pageSize), total, page, pageSize };
  }

  transaction<R>(fn: (table: this) => R): R {
    const snapshot = this.rows.map(r => ({ ...r }));
    const prevAuto = this.autoId;
    try {
      return fn(this);
    } catch (e) {
      this.rows = snapshot;
      this.autoId = prevAuto;
      throw e;
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const users = new Table<User>({ primaryKey: "id", uniqueFields: ["email"] });

users.insert({ name: "Zoe",  email: "zoe@example.com",  age: 25, active: true,  createdAt: new Date() });
users.insert({ name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com", age: 17, active: true,  createdAt: new Date() });
users.insert({ name: "Bob",   email: "bob@example.com",   age: 30, active: false, createdAt: new Date() });
users.insert({ name: "Cara",  email: "cara@example.com",  age: 22, active: true,  createdAt: new Date() });

const page = users.find(
  u => u.active && u.age > 18,
  { sort: { field: "name", dir: "asc" }, page: 1, pageSize: 10 }
);

console.log(page);
```

### Notes for real databases

- In production, replace this in-memory structure with a real driver (`pg`, `better-sqlite3`, `mysql2`) and use **parameterized queries** to avoid SQL injection.
- Unique constraints become `UNIQUE` columns; transactions become `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK`.
- The `find` predicate maps to a `WHERE` clause; sorting/pagination map to `ORDER BY` / `LIMIT` / `OFFSET`.
- Indexes (e.g. on `email`) are created in SQL, not in TypeScript.

## Database Basics (interview-style)

A **database** is organized storage for structured data. The core mental model:

- **Table / Collection** – a named set of rows (records).
- **Row / Record** – one entity instance.
- **Column / Field** – an attribute with a **type** (string, number, boolean, date).
- **Schema** – declares columns, types, and **constraints**.
- **Primary Key (PK)** – uniquely identifies a row; usually indexed for O(1) lookup.
- **Constraints** – `UNIQUE`, `NOT NULL`, `DEFAULT`, foreign keys (referential integrity).
- **CRUD** – Create (`INSERT`), Read (`SELECT`), Update (`UPDATE`), Delete (`DELETE`).
- **Query** – filter (`WHERE`), project (`SELECT cols`), sort (`ORDER BY`), paginate (`LIMIT/OFFSET`).
- **Index** – auxiliary data structure (hash map / B-tree) mapping a column value → row locations, trading write cost + memory for read speed.
- **ACID** – Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability (transactions). In-memory toy DBs skip durability; real DBs use WAL + fsync.
- **Normalization** – splitting data to avoid redundancy (1NF/2NF/3NF); joins reassemble.

Below is a small, clean, in-memory relational core in TypeScript: schema validation, constraints, CRUD, querying, and hash indexes.

```ts
// ============================================================
// MiniDB — a tiny in-memory relational database core
// Demonstrates: schema, types, constraints, CRUD, queries, indexes
// ============================================================

// ---------- Types ----------

export type ColumnType = "string" | "number" | "boolean" | "date";

export interface ColumnDef {
  name: string;
  type: ColumnType;
  primaryKey?: boolean;   // unique + identifies row
  unique?: boolean;       // no duplicate values (nulls allowed)
  nullable?: boolean;     // default: false (NOT NULL)
  default?: unknown;      // applied when value is omitted on insert
}

export interface TableSchema {
  name: string;
  columns: ColumnDef[];
}

export type Row = Record<string, unknown>;

export interface Query {
  where?: (row: Row) => boolean;                 // filter predicate
  columns?: string[];                            // projection
  orderBy?: { column: string; dir?: "asc" | "desc" };
  limit?: number;
  offset?: number;
}

export class DBError extends Error {}

// ---------- Helpers ----------

function typeOfValue(v: unknown): ColumnType | null {
  if (typeof v === "string") return "string";
  if (typeof v === "number") return "number";
  if (typeof v === "boolean") return "boolean";
  if (v instanceof Date) return "date";
  return null;
}

function compare(a: unknown, b: unknown): number {
  if (a === b) return 0;
  if (a == null) return -1;
  if (b == null) return 1;
  if (typeof a === "number" && typeof b === "number") return a - b;
  if (a instanceof Date && b instanceof Date) return a.getTime() - b.getTime();
  return String(a) < String(b) ? -1 : 1;
}

// ---------- Table ----------

export class Table {
  private schema: TableSchema;
  private columns: Map<string, ColumnDef>;
  private pkColumn: string | null = null;
  private rows: Row[] = [];

  // index: column -> Map<value, Set<rowIndex>>
  private indexes: Map<string, Map<unknown, Set<number>>> = new Map();

  constructor(schema: TableSchema) {
    this.schema = schema;
    this.columns = new Map(schema.columns.map((c) => [c.name, c]));

    const pks = schema.columns.filter((c) => c.primaryKey);
    if (pks.length > 1) throw new DBError("A table may have only one primary key");
    if (pks.length === 1) this.pkColumn = pks[0].name;

    // Auto-index PK and UNIQUE columns (hash index).
    for (const c of schema.columns) {
      if (c.primaryKey || c.unique) this.createIndex(c.name);
    }
  }

  get name(): string {
    return this.schema.name;
  }

  // ----- Index management -----

  createIndex(column: string): void {
    if (!this.columns.has(column)) throw new DBError(`Unknown column: ${column}`);
    if (this.indexes.has(column)) return; // idempotent
    const idx = new Map<unknown, Set<number>>();
    this.rows.forEach((r, i) => {
      const v = r[column];
      if (!idx.has(v)) idx.set(v, new Set());
      idx.get(v)!.add(i);
    });
    this.indexes.set(column, idx);
  }

  private indexAdd(column: string, value: unknown, rowIndex: number): void {
    const idx = this.indexes.get(column);
    if (!idx) return;
    if (!idx.has(value)) idx.set(value, new Set());
    idx.get(value)!.add(rowIndex);
  }

  private indexRemove(column: string, value: unknown, rowIndex: number): void {
    const idx = this.indexes.get(column);
    if (!idx) return;
    const set = idx.get(value);
    if (!set) return;
    set.delete(rowIndex);
    if (set.size === 0) idx.delete(value);
  }

  private rebuildIndexes(): void {
    for (const col of this.indexes.keys()) {
      const idx = new Map<unknown, Set<number>>();
      this.rows.forEach((r, i) => {
        const v = r[col];
        if (!idx.has(v)) idx.set(v, new Set());
        idx.get(v)!.add(i);
      });
      this.indexes.set(col, idx);
    }
  }

  // ----- Validation -----

  private validateAndNormalize(input: Partial<Row>): Row {
    const row: Row = {};

    for (const col of this.schema.columns) {
      let v = input[col.name];

      if (v === undefined) {
        if (col.default !== undefined) v = col.default;
        else if (col.nullable) v = null;
        else if (col.primaryKey) v = undefined; // may be auto-generated
        else throw new DBError(`Column "${col.name}" is required`);
      }

      if (v === null) {
        if (!col.nullable) throw new DBError(`Column "${col.name}" cannot be null`);
      } else if (v !== undefined) {
        const t = typeOfValue(v);
        if (t !== col.type) {
          throw new DBError(
            `Column "${col.name}" expects ${col.type}, got ${t ?? typeof v}`
          );
        }
      }

      row[col.name] = v;
    }

    // Reject unknown columns.
    for (const k of Object.keys(input)) {
      if (!this.columns.has(k)) throw new DBError(`Unknown column: ${k}`);
    }

    return row;
  }

  // ----- CRUD -----

  insert(input: Partial<Row>): Row {
    const row = this.validateAndNormalize(input);

    // Auto-increment numeric primary key when omitted.
    if (this.pkColumn && row[this.pkColumn] === undefined) {
      const col = this.columns.get(this.pkColumn)!;
      if (col.type === "number") {
        const max = this.rows.reduce(
          (m, r) => Math.max(m, Number(r[this.pkColumn!] ?? 0)),
          0
        );
        row[this.pkColumn] = max + 1;
      } else {
        throw new DBError(`Primary key "${this.pkColumn}" is required`);
      }
    }

    // Enforce PK uniqueness.
    if (this.pkColumn) {
      const v = row[this.pkColumn];
      const idx = this.indexes.get(this.pkColumn)!;
      if (idx.has(v)) throw new DBError(`Duplicate primary key: ${v}`);
    }

    // Enforce UNIQUE columns (nulls are allowed to repeat).
    for (const col of this.schema.columns) {
      if (col.unique && !col.primaryKey) {
        const v = row[col.name];
        const idx = this.indexes.get(col.name);
        if (idx && v !== null && v !== undefined && idx.has(v)) {
          throw new DBError(`Duplicate value for unique column "${col.name}": ${v}`);
        }
      }
    }

    const index = this.rows.length;
    this.rows.push(row);

    // Maintain indexes.
    for (const col of this.indexes.keys()) {
      this.indexAdd(col, row[col], index);
    }

    return { ...row };
  }

  select(q: Query = {}): Row[] {
    let result = this.rows.map((r, i) => ({ r, i }));

    if (q.where) result = result.filter(({ r }) => q.where!(r));

    if (q.orderBy) {
      const { column, dir = "asc" } = q.orderBy;
      if (!this.columns.has(column)) throw new DBError(`Unknown column: ${column}`);
      result.sort((a, b) => {
        const c = compare(a.r[column], b.r[column]);
        return dir === "asc" ? c : -c;
      });
    }

    if (q.offset) result = result.slice(q.offset);
    if (q.limit !== undefined) result = result.slice(0, q.limit);

    return result.map(({ r }) => {
      if (q.columns && q.columns.length) {
        const proj: Row = {};
        for (const c of q.columns) {
          if (!this.columns.has(c)) throw new DBError(`Unknown column: ${c}`);
          proj[c] = r[c];
        }
        return proj;
      }
      return { ...r };
    });
  }

  // O(1) primary-key lookup via hash index.
  findByPk(value: unknown): Row | undefined {
    if (!this.pkColumn) return undefined;
    const idx = this.indexes.get(this.pkColumn);
    const set = idx?.get(value);
    if (!set || set.size === 0) return undefined;
    const i = [...set][0];
    return { ...this.rows[i] };
  }

  update(q: { where?: (r: Row) => boolean; set: Partial<Row> }): number {
    const setCols = Object.keys(q.set);
    for (const c of setCols) {
      if (!this.columns.has(c)) throw new DBError(`Unknown column: ${c}`);
    }

    // Validate new values.
    const normalized: Partial<Row> = {};
    for (const c of setCols) {
      const col = this.columns.get(c)!;
      const v = q.set[c];
      if (v === null) {
        if (!col.nullable) throw new DBError(`Column "${c}" cannot be null`);
      } else if (v !== undefined) {
        const t = typeOfValue(v);
        if (t !== col.type) {
          throw new DBError(`Column "${c}" expects ${col.type}, got ${t ?? typeof v}`);
        }
      }
      normalized[c] = v;
    }

    const targets = this.rows
      .map((r, i) => ({ r, i }))
      .filter(({ r }) => (q.where ? q.where(r) : true));

    let count = 0;
    for (const { i } of targets) {
      const row = this.rows[i];

      // Constraint checks for changed values.
      for (const c of setCols) {
        const col = this.schema.columns.find((x) => x.name === c)!;
        if (col.unique || col.primaryKey) {
          const newVal = normalized[c];
          if (newVal !== row[c]) {
            const idx = this.indexes.get(c);
            const set = idx?.get(newVal);
            if (set && [...set].some((x) => x !== i)) {
              throw new DBError(`Duplicate value for "${c}": ${newVal}`);
            }
          }
        }
      }

      // Remove old index entries, apply, re-add.
      for (const col of this.indexes.keys()) this.indexRemove(col, row[col], i);
      Object.assign(row, normalized);
      for (const col of this.indexes.keys()) this.indexAdd(col, row[col], i);

      count++;
    }
    return count;
  }

  delete(q: { where?: (r: Row) => boolean } = {}): number {
    const toDelete = this.rows
      .map((r, i) => ({ r, i }))
      .filter(({ r }) => (q.where ? q.where(r) : true))
      .map((x) => x.i)
      .sort((a, b) => b - a); // delete high->low keeps earlier indices stable

    let count = 0;
    for (const i of toDelete) {
      const row = this.rows[i];
      for (const col of this.indexes.keys()) this.indexRemove(col, row[col], i);
      this.rows.splice(i, 1);
      count++;
      // Rebuild indexes to repair shifted indices.
      this.rebuildIndexes();
    }
    return count;
  }
}

// ---------- Database ----------

export class Database {
  private tables: Map<string, Table> = new Map();

  createTable(schema: TableSchema): Table {
    if (this.tables.has(schema.name)) {
      throw new DBError(`Table "${schema.name}" already exists`);
    }
    const t = new Table(schema);
    this.tables.set(schema.name, t);
    return t;
  }

  table(name: string): Table {
    const t = this.tables.get(name);
    if (!t) throw new DBError(`Table "${name}" not found`);
    return t;
  }

  dropTable(name: string): void {
    if (!this.tables.delete(name)) throw new DBError(`Table "${name}" not found`);
  }

  listTables(): string[] {
    return [...this.tables.keys()];
  }
}

// ============================================================
// Demo
// ============================================================

const db = new Database();

const users = db.createTable({
  name: "users",
  columns: [
    { name: "id", type: "number", primaryKey: true },
    { name: "name", type: "string" },
    { name: "email", type: "string", unique: true },
    { name: "age", type: "number", nullable: true },
    { name: "active", type: "boolean", default: true },
  ],
});

users.insert({ name: "Alice", email: "alice@x.com", age: 30 });
users.insert({ name: "Bob", email: "bob@x.com" }); // id auto, active default true

console.log("all:", users.select());
console.log("by PK 1:", users.findByPk(1));
console.log("adults:", users.select({ where: (r) => Number(r.age ?? 0) >= 18, columns: ["name", "email"] }));
console.log("sorted:", users.select({ orderBy: { column: "name", dir: "desc" } }));

users.update({ where: (r) => r.name === "Bob", set: { age: 25 } });
users.delete({ where: (r) => r.name === "Alice" });

console.log("after mutations:", users.select());
console.log("tables:", db.listTables());
```

### Key design notes (what an interviewer wants to hear)

- **Schema-first**: columns declared with types; inserts/updates are validated before touching storage.
- **Constraints enforced in code**: PK uniqueness, `UNIQUE`, `NOT NULL`, `DEFAULT`, auto-increment PK.
- **Indexes**: hash `Map<value, Set<rowIndex>>` for PK/unique columns → O(1) equality lookup; `createIndex` allows ad-hoc indexes. Range queries would use a sorted structure (B-tree) in a real DB.
- **Query pipeline**: `filter → sort → offset/limit → project`, applied in that order (projection last keeps sorting correct).
- **Mutation safety**: index entries removed/re-added on update; delete rebuilds indexes to repair shifted array indices.
- **Not covered (real DBs)**: durability (WAL + fsync), concurrency/isolation (MVCC, locks), query optimizer, joins, foreign keys, transactions (begin/commit/rollback). This core is the "storage + access" layer those build on.

Below are the most important edge cases when doing “database basics” in TypeScript, with concrete code. I’ll use **PostgreSQL via `pg`** + **Zod** for runtime validation because that combination exposes the real pitfalls; the same ideas apply to MySQL, SQLite, Prisma, Drizzle, etc.

---

## 1. SQL injection & “stringy” query building
**Edge case:** concatenating user input (even numbers) creates injection & type-coercion bugs.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const id = req.params.id; // "1 OR 1=1"
await pool.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${id}`);

// ✅ GOOD: parameterized + typed parser
import { Pool } from "pg";
import { z } from "zod";

const pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });

const IdSchema = z.coerce.number().int().positive();

async function getUser(idRaw: unknown) {
  const id = IdSchema.parse(idRaw); // throws on bad input
  const { rows } = await pool.query("SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE id = $1", [id]);
  // rows is any[]; validate shape too
  return z.object({ id: z.number(), email: z.string() }).array().parse(rows);
}
```
**Also handle:** `$1` placeholders for arrays (`ANY($1)`), and never trust `JSON.stringify` for SQL.

---

## 2. `null` vs `undefined` vs “not found”
**Edge case:** a row missing, a column NULL, and TS `undefined` are three different things. ORMs often collapse them.

```ts
type User = { id: number; bio: string | null };

async function findUser(id: number): Promise<User | null> {
  const { rows } = await pool.query<User>(
    "SELECT id, bio FROM users WHERE id = $1", [id]
  );
  if (rows.length === 0) return null; // not found
  const u = rows[0];
  // bio can be null in DB; keep it nullable, don't default to ""
  return { id: u.id, bio: u.bio }; // bio: string | null
}

// Caller must distinguish
const u = await findUser(42);
if (u === null) { /* 404 */ }
else if (u.bio === null) { /* exists but no bio */ }
```

---

## 3. Transactions & partial failure
**Edge case:** if step 2 fails, step 1 must roll back; connections must be released even on throw.

```ts
import { PoolClient } from "pg";

async function transfer(from: number, to: number, amount: number) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const { rowCount } = await client.query(
      "UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - $1 WHERE id = $2 AND balance >= $1",
      [amount, from]
    );
    if (rowCount === 0) throw new Error("INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS");
    await client.query(
      "UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + $1 WHERE id = $2",
      [amount, to]
    );
    await client.query("COMMIT");
  } catch (e) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw e;
  } finally {
    client.release(); // CRITICAL: prevents pool exhaustion
  }
}
```
**Edge:** nested transaction attempts (SAVEPOINT) if you compose transactional helpers.

---

## 4. Concurrency: lost updates & deadlocks
**Edge case:** two requests update same row; one overwrites the other. Also lock ordering deadlocks.

```ts
// Optimistic concurrency with version column
async function updateProfile(id: number, bio: string, expectedVersion: number) {
  const { rowCount } = await pool.query(
    "UPDATE users SET bio = $1, version = version + 1 WHERE id = $2 AND version = $3",
    [bio, id, expectedVersion]
  );
  if (rowCount === 0) throw new Error("CONFLICT"); // 409
}

// Pessimistic: lock row; always lock in same global order to avoid deadlocks
async function lockTwo(a: number, b: number) {
  const [x, y] = [a, b].sort((n,m)=>n-m); // canonical order
  await pool.query("SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE", [x]);
  await pool.query("SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE", [y]);
}
```

---

## 5. Decimal / money precision
**Edge case:** JS `number` is float; Postgres `NUMERIC`/`DECIMAL` come back as strings by default in `pg`.

```ts
// pg returns numeric as string. Validate/convert explicitly.
const { rows } = await pool.query<{ price: string }>(
  "SELECT price::text AS price FROM products WHERE id = $1", [1]
);
import Decimal from "decimal.js";
const price = new Decimal(rows[0].price); // exact

// Or configure pg type parser (global, be careful)
import { types } from "pg";
types.setTypeParser(types.builtins.NUMERIC, (v) => new Decimal(v));
```
**Never** do `Number(row.price)` for money.

---

## 6. Dates, timezones, and `timestamptz`
**Edge case:** JS `Date` is UTC instant; DB may store local naive timestamps; drivers parse `timestamp` as local time of process (often wrong).

```ts
// Store and read as timestamptz. Parse explicitly.
const { rows } = await pool.query<{ created_at: Date }>(
  "SELECT created_at FROM events WHERE id = $1", [1]
);
// pg returns Date in UTC. Format at boundary.
console.log(rows[0].created_at.toISOString());

// For date-only columns, use string not Date
const { rows: r2 } = await pool.query<{ d: string }>(
  "SELECT birth_date::text AS d FROM users WHERE id = $1", [1]
);
```

---

## 7. JSON / JSONB columns
**Edge case:** TS type says shape, DB may contain anything; also `json` vs `jsonb` indexing.

```ts
import { z } from "zod";
const Meta = z.object({ tags: z.array(z.string()), count: z.number() });

const { rows } = await pool.query<{ meta: unknown }>(
  "SELECT meta FROM docs WHERE id = $1", [1]
);
const meta = Meta.parse(rows[0].meta); // runtime validate, don't cast
```

---

## 8. BigInt (`int8`) serialization
**Edge case:** Postgres `bigint` → JS `BigInt`, which `JSON.stringify` cannot serialize.

```ts
const { rows } = await pool.query<{ big: bigint }>(
  "SELECT 9223372036854775807::bigint AS big"
);
// rows[0].big is bigint
// Convert at API boundary
const safe = rows[0].big.toString(); // or Number if safe range
```

---

## 9. Empty results & `LIMIT` performance
**Edge case:** `SELECT ... WHERE x = $1` with no index → seq scan; also `.rows[0]` undefined.

```ts
const { rows } = await pool.query("SELECT ... WHERE email = $1", [email]);
const user = rows[0]; // possibly undefined
if (!user) throw new NotFound(); // handle explicitly
```
Add indexes; use `EXPLAIN` in tests.

---

## 10. Connection pool exhaustion / leaks
**Edge case:** forgetting `release()`, long queries, too few connections.

```ts
// Use query() for one-off (auto release). connect() only for transactions.
await pool.query("SELECT 1"); // safe

// Monitor
pool.on("error", (err) => console.error("idle client error", err));
// Set timeouts
const pool = new Pool({ max: 20, idleTimeoutMillis: 30000, connectionTimeoutMillis: 5000 });
```

---

## 11. Constraint violations (typed errors)
**Edge case:** unique violation, FK violation must be mapped to domain errors, not 500.

```ts
import { DatabaseError } from "pg";

function mapPgError(e: unknown): Error {
  if (e instanceof DatabaseError) {
    if (e.code === "23505") return new Error("UNIQUE_VIOLATION"); // duplicate
    if (e.code === "23503") return new Error("FOREIGN_KEY_VIOLATION");
    if (e.code === "23502") return new Error("NOT_NULL_VIOLATION");
  }
  return e as Error;
}

try {
  await pool.query("INSERT INTO users(email) VALUES($1)", [email]);
} catch (e) {
  throw mapPgError(e);
}
```

---

## 12. N+1 & batching
**Edge case:** looped queries; use `ANY`, joins, or `DataLoader`.

```ts
// ❌ N+1
for (const id of ids) await pool.query("SELECT ... WHERE id=$1", [id]);

// ✅ batch
const { rows } = await pool.query(
  "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ANY($1::int[])", [ids]
);
```

---

## 13. Cursor / streaming large results
**Edge case:** `SELECT *` on huge table → OOM. Use cursor/stream.

```ts
import { Cursor } from "pg-cursor";
const client = await pool.connect();
const cursor = client.query(new Cursor("SELECT id FROM big_table"));
cursor.read(100, (err, rows) => {
  // process chunk; call again; close cursor + release client
});
```

---

## 14. Retry on transient failures (idempotency)
**Edge case:** network blip during read can be retried; write retries need idempotency keys.

```ts
async function withRetry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, attempts = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (e) {
      if (i === attempts - 1) throw e;
      if (!isTransient(e)) throw e;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 50 * 2 ** i));
    }
  }
  throw new Error("unreachable");
}
function isTransient(e: any) {
  return e?.code === "ECONNRESET" || e?.code === "57P01"; // admin shutdown
}
```

---

## 15. Schema drift & migrations in TS
**Edge case:** DB schema changes but TS types stale → runtime crashes.

```ts
// Generate types from DB (e.g. kysely-codegen, prisma generate, drizzle-kit)
// Or validate every query result with Zod at boundary:
const UserRow = z.object({ id: z.number(), email: z.string() });
const { rows } = await pool.query("SELECT id, email FROM users");
const users = UserRow.array().parse(rows); // fails early if migration drifted
```

---

## 16. `RETURNING` and insert shape
**Edge case:** insert then `SELECT` again is racy; use `RETURNING`.

```ts
const { rows } = await pool.query<{ id: number; email: string }>(
  "INSERT INTO users(email) VALUES($1) RETURNING id, email",
  [email]
);
```

---

## 17. Empty arrays in `IN` / `ANY`
**Edge case:** `WHERE id = ANY($1)` with empty array returns no rows (fine), but `IN ()` is syntax error.

```ts
if (ids.length === 0) return []; // short-circuit instead of building "IN ()"
const { rows } = await pool.query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ANY($1::int[])", [ids]);
```

---

## 18. Unicode / collation surprises
**Edge case:** case-insensitive email uniqueness depends on DB collation; TS `.toLowerCase()` ≠ SQL `ILIKE` semantics.

```sql
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_ci ON users (LOWER(email));
```
```ts
await pool.query("INSERT INTO users(email) VALUES(LOWER($1))", [email.trim()]);
```

---

### Quick checklist
- Parameterize every value; validate with Zod.
- Distinguish `null` (column) vs “not found” vs `undefined`.
- Wrap multi-statement writes in transactions; always `release()`.
- Treat `numeric`/`decimal` and `bigint` specially.
- Map Postgres error codes to domain errors.
- Validate rows at boundary to catch schema drift.
- Batch with `ANY`; stream large reads.
- Use optimistic locking or `FOR UPDATE` with canonical lock order.

