# Deadlocks and Race Conditions in TypeScript (Node.js)

TypeScript/Node.js is single-threaded, but concurrency still happens through the event loop (`async/await`, timers, I/O). Shared mutable state accessed across `await` points is where races occur, and **async locks (mutexes)** are where deadlocks occur.

Below is a complete, self-contained example you can run with:

```bash
npx ts-node demo.ts
```

## `demo.ts`

```ts
// demo.ts
// Run: npx ts-node demo.ts

function sleep(ms: number): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((res) => setTimeout(res, ms));
}

// ============================================================
// A small async Mutex (reentrant-safe enough for these demos)
// ============================================================
class Mutex {
  private locked = false;
  private waiters: Array<() => void> = [];

  async acquire(): Promise<() => void> {
    if (!this.locked) {
      this.locked = true;
    } else {
      await new Promise<void>((res) => this.waiters.push(res));
      this.locked = true;
    }

    let released = false;
    return () => {
      if (released) return;
      released = true;
      const next = this.waiters.shift();
      if (next) next(); // wake next waiter (it will set locked=true)
      else this.locked = false;
    };
  }
}

// ============================================================
// 1) RACE CONDITION (read-modify-write across an await)
// ============================================================
let balance = 1000;

async function withdrawUnsafe(amount: number, label: string) {
  const current = balance;          // read
  await sleep(10);                  // context switch can happen here
  balance = current - amount;       // write (stale value!)
  console.log(`  ${label}: read ${current}, wrote ${balance}`);
}

async function raceDemo() {
  console.log("\n=== Race condition (unsafe) ===");
  balance = 1000;
  await Promise.all([withdrawUnsafe(100, "A"), withdrawUnsafe(100, "B")]);
  console.log("  Final balance (expected 800):", balance);
}

// ---- Fix: serialize with a mutex ----
const balanceMutex = new Mutex();

async function withdrawSafe(amount: number, label: string) {
  const release = await balanceMutex.acquire();
  try {
    const current = balance;
    await sleep(10);
    balance = current - amount;
    console.log(`  ${label}: read ${current}, wrote ${balance}`);
  } finally {
    release(); // always release, even on throw
  }
}

async function raceFixDemo() {
  console.log("\n=== Race condition (fixed with mutex) ===");
  balance = 1000;
  await Promise.all([withdrawSafe(100, "A"), withdrawSafe(100, "B")]);
  console.log("  Final balance (expected 800):", balance);
}

// ============================================================
// 2) DEADLOCK (inconsistent lock ordering)
// ============================================================
async function deadlockDemo() {
  console.log("\n=== Deadlock (unsafe lock ordering) ===");
  const lockA = new Mutex();
  const lockB = new Mutex();

  const task1 = async () => {
    const ra = await lockA.acquire();
    console.log("  task1 acquired A");
    await sleep(50);
    console.log("  task1 waiting for B");
    const rb = await lockB.acquire(); // blocks forever: task2 holds B, waits A
    console.log("  task1 acquired B (never reached)");
    rb();
    ra();
  };

  const task2 = async () => {
    const rb = await lockB.acquire();
    console.log("  task2 acquired B");
    await sleep(50);
    console.log("  task2 waiting for A");
    const ra = await lockA.acquire(); // blocks forever
    console.log("  task2 acquired A (never reached)");
    ra();
    rb();
  };

  const work = Promise.all([task1(), task2()]);
  const timeout = sleep(500).then(() =>
    console.log("  DEADLOCK DETECTED: tasks did not finish within 500ms")
  );
  await Promise.race([work, timeout]);
}

// ---- Fix: always acquire locks in the same global order (A then B) ----
async function deadlockFixDemo() {
  console.log("\n=== Deadlock (fixed by consistent ordering) ===");
  const lockA = new Mutex();
  const lockB = new Mutex();

  const task1 = async () => {
    const ra = await lockA.acquire();
    try {
      console.log("  task1 acquired A");
      await sleep(50);
      const rb = await lockB.acquire();
      try {
        console.log("  task1 acquired B");
      } finally {
        rb();
      }
    } finally {
      ra();
    }
  };

  const task2 = async () => {
    // Same order as task1: A first, then B
    const ra = await lockA.acquire();
    try {
      console.log("  task2 acquired A");
      await sleep(50);
      const rb = await lockB.acquire();
      try {
        console.log("  task2 acquired B");
      } finally {
        rb();
      }
    } finally {
      ra();
    }
  };

  await Promise.all([task1(), task2()]);
  console.log("  Both tasks completed (no deadlock).");
}

// ============================================================
// Runner
// ============================================================
async function main() {
  await raceDemo();
  await raceFixDemo();
  await deadlockDemo();
  await deadlockFixDemo();
}

main().catch(console.error);
```

## Expected output

```
=== Race condition (unsafe) ===
  A: read 1000, wrote 900
  B: read 1000, wrote 900
  Final balance (expected 800): 900

=== Race condition (fixed with mutex) ===
  A: read 1000, wrote 900
  B: read 900, wrote 800
  Final balance (expected 800): 800

=== Deadlock (unsafe lock ordering) ===
  task1 acquired A
  task2 acquired B
  task1 waiting for B
  task2 waiting for A
  DEADLOCK DETECTED: tasks did not finish within 500ms

=== Deadlock (fixed by consistent ordering) ===
  task1 acquired A
  task2 acquired A
  task1 acquired B
  task2 acquired B
  Both tasks completed (no deadlock).
```

## Key takeaways

- **Race condition**: any `read → await → write` sequence on shared state can be interleaved. Fix by holding a mutex across the whole critical section, or better, avoid mutable shared state (message passing, immutable updates).
- **Deadlock**: with two or more locks, tasks acquiring them in different orders can wait on each other forever. Fix with a **global lock ordering** (always A then B), lock timeouts, or by reducing lock scope.
- **Always release in `finally`** so exceptions don't leave locks held.
- For **true OS-level parallelism** (`worker_threads`), use `SharedArrayBuffer` + `Atomics` for counters and Atomics-based mutexes; the same ordering rules apply.

### True parallelism note (worker_threads)

If you use `worker_threads`, the race is even more direct because threads run simultaneously:

```ts
// racy (lost updates)
sharedArray[0] = sharedArray[0] + 1;

// safe
Atomics.add(sharedArray, 0, 1);
```

And deadlocks there require real blocking (e.g., `Atomics.wait` on a futex) with the same ordering discipline shown above.

# Deadlocks & Race Conditions in TypeScript

TypeScript runs on a single-threaded event loop (Node.js / browser).  
So “deadlocks” and “race conditions” are usually **logical**, not OS-level: they come from interleaving of `await` points, shared mutable state, or promises that never settle. (True thread deadlocks only appear with `SharedArrayBuffer` + `Atomics` in Workers.)

---

## 1. Race conditions

### Idea
A race condition happens when the final result depends on the **relative timing** of async operations. Because `await` yields control, two functions can interleave between a “read” and a “write.”

### Syntax / typical pattern
- Shared variable outside an `async` function
- `await` between reading and writing
- `Promise.all`, `setTimeout`, I/O, fetch

### Example 1 — lost update (read-modify-write)

```ts
let counter = 0;

const sleep = (ms: number) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));

async function increment() {
  const current = counter;          // read
  await sleep(10);                  // yield; other task may run here
  counter = current + 1;            // write (stale value)
}

await Promise.all([increment(), increment()]);
console.log(counter); // often 1, expected 2
```

### Example 2 — check-then-act (inventory / booking)

```ts
let seats = 1;

async function book() {
  if (seats > 0) {                 // check
    await chargeCard();             // yield
    seats -= 1;                    // act (may over-sell)
  }
}
```

### Typical use cases
- Updating counters, caches, inventory
- UI state (double-submit buttons)
- Database rows without transactions/atomic ops
- File writes from multiple handlers

### Fixes
- Atomic/transactional operations (`UPDATE ... WHERE stock > 0`)
- Serialize with a queue or mutex
- Use immutable updates + compare-and-swap
- Debounce/disable UI, idempotency keys

---

## 2. Deadlocks

### Idea
A deadlock is a set of operations that **wait forever** because each waits for a resource held by another (or a promise that never resolves). In TS this usually means:
- Circular `await` (A waits for B, B waits for A)
- Locks acquired in inconsistent order
- A promise that never settles (missing `resolve`/`reject`)
- Reentrant lock usage (awaiting while holding a lock that you need again)

### Example 1 — circular promise deadlock

```ts
function taskA(): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    taskB().then(() => resolve()); // waits for B
  });
}
function taskB(): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    taskA().then(() => resolve()); // waits for A
  });
}
taskA(); // never completes
```

### Example 2 — lock-order deadlock (with `async-mutex`)

```ts
import { Mutex } from "async-mutex";

const lockX = new Mutex();
const lockY = new Mutex();

async function worker1() {
  const rX = await lockX.acquire();
  try {
    await sleep(10);
    const rY = await lockY.acquire(); // waits for Y
    try { /* work */ } finally { rY(); }
  } finally { rX(); }
}

async function worker2() {
  const rY = await lockY.acquire();
  try {
    await sleep(10);
    const rX = await lockX.acquire(); // waits for X -> deadlock
    try { /* work */ } finally { rX(); }
  } finally { rY(); }
}
```

### Example 3 — self-deadlock (reentrancy)

```ts
const mutex = new Mutex();

async function outer() {
  const release = await mutex.acquire();
  try {
    await inner(); // inner tries to acquire same mutex -> deadlock
  } finally { release(); }
}
async function inner() {
  const release = await mutex.acquire(); // never acquired
  release();
}
```

### Typical use cases
- Coordinating multiple shared resources (DB connections, caches)
- Producer/consumer queues with backpressure
- Browser extension / worker message passing
- Distributed locks (Redis) with bad TTL/order

### Fixes
- Always acquire locks in a **global order**
- Use timeouts (`Promise.race` with a timer)
- Avoid nested locking; use higher-level transactions
- Prefer message passing / queues over shared locks
- Never `await` inside a critical section unless the awaited work cannot re-enter
- For Workers: avoid `Atomics.wait` on the main thread; use timeouts

---

## 3. True threads (Workers)

If you use `SharedArrayBuffer` + `Atomics`, classic deadlocks return:

```ts
// worker.ts
const i32 = new Int32Array(sharedBuf);
Atomics.wait(i32, 0, 0); // blocks thread until notified
```

If two workers each hold a lock and wait for the other’s notification, you get a real deadlock. Mitigate with `Atomics.wait` timeouts and careful lock ordering.

---

## Quick checklist

| Problem | Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Race condition | Inconsistent counts, duplicate actions | Atomic ops, serialization, idempotency |
| Deadlock | Promise never resolves, app hangs | Consistent lock order, timeouts, avoid nested awaits |
| Livelock | Tasks retry forever | Backoff, circuit breakers |

**Bottom line:** In TypeScript, treat `await` as a potential context switch. Keep critical sections small, avoid shared mutable state, and prefer transactions/queues over manual locks.

## Buggy version

```ts
import { Mutex } from "async-mutex";

class Account {
  id: number;
  balance = 0;
  lock = new Mutex();
  constructor(id: number) { this.id = id; }
}

// BUG 1: locks acquired in caller-dependent order -> classic deadlock
// BUG 2: balance update is read-modify-write across an await -> lost update race
async function transfer(from: Account, to: Account, amount: number): Promise<void> {
  await from.lock.acquire();
  await to.lock.acquire();

  // If two transfers run in opposite directions at the same time,
  // T1 holds from=A/to=B while T2 holds from=B/to=A, and both wait forever.
  const fromBalance = from.balance;
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 10)); // simulate async I/O / DB call
  from.balance = fromBalance - amount;

  const toBalance = to.balance;
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 10));
  to.balance = toBalance + amount;

  from.lock.release();
  to.lock.release();
}

// BUG 3: unprotected global counter updated across awaits
let requestCount = 0;
async function handleRequest(): Promise<void> {
  const n = requestCount;
  await fetch("/api");
  requestCount = n + 1; // lost update: two concurrent requests can both read 5
}
```

### What is wrong

1. **Deadlock from inconsistent lock ordering.**  
   `transfer(A, B)` locks `A` then `B`. `transfer(B, A)` locks `B` then `A`. If both run concurrently, each can acquire its first lock and wait forever for the other’s, so both stalls block the event loop’s pending callbacks and the program hangs.

2. **Race condition from non-atomic read-modify-write.**  
   Even with locks held, caching `from.balance` in a local, awaiting, and writing back is fragile. If any code path updates the account without the same discipline, or if the lock boundary is wrong, updates are lost. More importantly, the global `requestCount` example has no lock at all: two coroutines can read the same value, await, and overwrite with the same incremented result, losing one increment.

3. **Release is not guaranteed.**  
   If an exception happens between `acquire()` and `release()`, the mutex stays locked forever. That turns a temporary error into a permanent hang.

---

## Corrected version

```ts
import { Mutex } from "async-mutex";

class Account {
  id: number;
  balance = 0;
  lock = new Mutex();
  constructor(id: number) { this.id = id; }
}

// Acquire multiple locks in a globally consistent order (by id).
async function acquireLocks(accounts: Account[]): Promise<() => void> {
  const sorted = [...accounts].sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id);
  const releases: Array<() => void> = [];
  for (const acc of sorted) {
    const release = await acc.lock.acquire();
    releases.push(release);
  }
  // Return a single release function that releases in reverse order.
  return () => {
    for (const r of releases.reverse()) r();
  };
}

async function transfer(from: Account, to: Account, amount: number): Promise<void> {
  if (from.id === to.id) return;
  const releaseAll = await acquireLocks([from, to]);
  try {
    // Hold locks across the whole update so no other transfer can interleave.
    // Do the bookkeeping synchronously; keep awaits out of the critical state update.
    if (from.balance < amount) throw new Error("insufficient funds");
    from.balance -= amount;
    to.balance += amount;
  } finally {
    releaseAll();
  }
}

// Protected global counter with a single mutex.
const requestMutex = new Mutex();
let requestCount = 0;

async function handleRequest(): Promise<void> {
  await requestMutex.runExclusive(async () => {
    const n = requestCount;
    await fetch("/api");
    requestCount = n + 1;
  });
}
```

### Why this fixes it

- **Consistent lock ordering prevents deadlock.** Every transfer acquires account locks sorted by `id`. No two transfers can each hold one lock while waiting for the other, because there is only one possible acquisition sequence.
- **`try/finally` guarantees release.** Even if validation throws, every acquired lock is released, so one failed transfer cannot permanently freeze an account.
- **Critical section is short and coherent.** The balance update happens while both locks are held and without interleaving `await`s in the middle of the read-modify-write. If you need real I/O (DB write), prefer performing the in-memory update synchronously, then persisting, or better use a database transaction with row-level locking/optimistic concurrency rather than in-process mutexes.
- **Shared mutable state is serialized.** `runExclusive` ensures only one `handleRequest` coroutine executes the read-await-write sequence at a time, eliminating the lost update.
- **Edge case handled.** Transferring an account to itself does nothing instead of trying to lock the same mutex twice, which could self-deadlock depending on the mutex implementation.

### Practical note

In-process mutexes only protect one Node.js process. In production, correctness for money or counters usually belongs in the database: use a transaction with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, a conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= amount`, or optimistic versioning. Lock ordering still matters there, but the database engine enforces it.

Below are the most common pitfalls, grouped into **race conditions** and **deadlocks**. TypeScript itself doesn’t change JavaScript’s single-threaded event-loop concurrency model, but the type system can hide async boundaries and make these bugs harder to spot.

## Race conditions

### 1. Read-modify-write across an `await`
The classic lost update: you read state, await something, then write based on the stale read.

```ts
let count = 0;

const delay = (ms: number) => new Promise<void>(r => setTimeout(r, ms));

async function increment(): Promise<void> {
  const current = count;      // read
  await delay(10);            // async gap → another increment can run
  count = current + 1;        // write stale value
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  await Promise.all([increment(), increment()]);
  console.log(count); // often 1, expected 2
}

main();
```

**Fix:** use a mutex/queue, database atomic update, or restructure so the read and write happen without an async gap.

---

### 2. Stale closures in async callbacks
Callbacks capture variables by reference, but values read at execution time may differ from what you expected when scheduling.

```ts
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(i); // 0,1,2 with let — but with var it would be 3,3,3
  }, 0);
}
```

More subtle: capturing an object property that changes later:

```ts
const state = { value: 0 };

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log(state.value); // reads latest value, not snapshot
}, 100);

state.value = 42;
```

**Fix:** capture a snapshot (`const snapshot = state.value`) or use functional state updates.

---

### 3. Pushing to a shared array from concurrent async tasks
`Array.prototype.push` is synchronous, so there’s no memory corruption, but completion order is nondeterministic. If downstream code assumes order, you get a logical race.

```ts
const items = [1, 2, 3];
const results: number[] = [];

await Promise.all(
  items.map(async (item) => {
    const data = await fetch(`/api/${item}`);
    results.push(await data.json()); // order depends on network timing
  })
);

console.log(results); // order may not match items
```

**Fix:** assign by index.

```ts
const results = new Array(items.length);
await Promise.all(
  items.map(async (item, index) => {
    const data = await fetch(`/api/${item}`);
    results[index] = await data.json();
  })
);
```

---

### 4. Event emitter reentrancy
Synchronous emission can re-enter your code while state is half-updated.

```ts
import { EventEmitter } from "events";

const bus = new EventEmitter();
let count = 0;

bus.on("tick", () => {
  count++;
  if (count === 1) {
    bus.emit("tick"); // reentrant call while stack is still in first handler
  }
});

bus.emit("tick");
console.log(count); // 2, but intermediate invariants may be observed
```

**Fix:** defer with `process.nextTick`, `setImmediate`, or queue events instead of emitting synchronously.

---

### 5. Assuming `setTimeout` runs before microtasks
Microtasks (Promise resolutions) run before the next macrotask. Code that assumes timer order can race.

```ts
console.log("start");

setTimeout(() => console.log("timeout"), 0);

Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("microtask"));

console.log("end");

// Output:
// start
// end
// microtask
// timeout
```

**Fix:** don’t rely on implicit ordering; make dependencies explicit with `await` or events.

---

### 6. Async iteration with external mutation
Mutating a collection while iterating it asynchronously can skip or duplicate elements.

```ts
const queue: string[] = ["a", "b", "c"];

async function drain(): Promise<void> {
  for (const item of queue) {
    await delay(10);
    console.log(item);
    queue.push("late"); // mutation during async iteration
  }
}
```

**Fix:** snapshot with `[...queue]` before iterating, or use a proper async queue.

---

## Deadlocks

### 7. Circular Promise dependencies
Two Promises each wait for the other to settle. Neither ever resolves.

```ts
let resolveA!: () => void;
let resolveB!: () => void;

const pA = new Promise<void>(resolve => {
  resolveA = resolve;
}).then(() => {
  // waits for pB before resolving A
  return pB;
});

const pB = new Promise<void>(resolve => {
  resolveB = resolve;
}).then(() => {
  return pA;
});

// Neither pA nor pB can ever settle.
// await pA; // hangs forever
```

**Fix:** break the cycle—resolve based on a shared condition, not on each other.

---

### 8. Acquiring the same non-reentrant lock twice
A naive mutex that doesn’t support reentrancy will deadlock when the holder tries to lock again.

```ts
class Mutex {
  private locked = false;
  private queue: (() => void)[] = [];

  lock(): Promise<void> {
    return new Promise(resolve => {
      if (!this.locked) {
        this.locked = true;
        resolve();
      } else {
        this.queue.push(resolve);
      }
    });
  }

  unlock(): void {
    const next = this.queue.shift();
    if (next) next();
    else this.locked = false;
  }
}

const m = new Mutex();

async function outer(): Promise<void> {
  await m.lock();
  await inner();   // tries to lock again → deadlock
  m.unlock();
}

async function inner(): Promise<void> {
  await m.lock();  // waits forever because outer holds lock
  m.unlock();
}
```

**Fix:** use a reentrant lock, avoid nested locking, or restructure to acquire all locks up front in a consistent order.

---

### 9. Holding a lock across an `await` that needs the same lock
Even with a non-circular dependency, awaiting work that itself needs the lock deadlocks.

```ts
const m = new Mutex();
const data: { loaded: boolean } = { loaded: false };

async function load(): Promise<void> {
  await m.lock();
  try {
    data.loaded = true;
  } finally {
    m.unlock();
  }
}

async function ensureLoaded(): Promise<void> {
  await m.lock();
  try {
    if (!data.loaded) {
      await load(); // load() needs the lock → deadlock
    }
  } finally {
    m.unlock();
  }
}
```

**Fix:** release the lock before awaiting, use a double-checked pattern with an event/condition, or restructure so only one function owns the lock.

---

### 10. A Promise that never settles
`await` on a Promise that never resolves hangs the async function forever. This is a logical deadlock even with no locks.

```ts
function neverResolve(): Promise<never> {
  return new Promise(() => {}); // no resolve/reject ever called
}

async function work(): Promise<void> {
  console.log("before");
  await neverResolve();          // hangs here forever
  console.log("after");          // never runs
}

work();
```

**Fix:** always pair async operations with timeouts or cancellation tokens.

```ts
function withTimeout<T>(p: Promise<T>, ms: number): Promise<T> {
  return Promise.race([
    p,
    new Promise<T>((_, reject) =>
      setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("timeout")), ms)
    ),
  ]);
}
```

---

### 11. Worker thread / `SharedArrayBuffer` deadlocks with `Atomics.wait`
In worker threads, `Atomics.wait` blocks the thread. If the main thread is waiting for a worker response while the worker is blocked waiting for the main thread, both hang.

```ts
// main.ts
const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(4);
const view = new Int32Array(sab);
const worker = new Worker("./worker.js");

// Main thread signals then waits for worker
Atomics.store(view, 0, 1);
Atomics.notify(view, 0, 1);
Atomics.wait(view, 0, 1); // main thread blocks

// worker.ts
const view = new Int32Array(self.sharedBuffer);
Atomics.wait(view, 0, 0); // worker blocks waiting for main to store 1
// but main is blocked above → deadlock
```

**Fix:** never block the main thread; use message passing, condition variables with timeouts, or avoid `Atomics.wait` on the main thread.

---

### 12. Producer/consumer deadlock in async generators
A consumer awaiting `next()` while the producer awaits a consumer-side condition creates a cycle.

```ts
async function* producer(): AsyncGenerator<number> {
  let resolveReady!: () => void;
  const ready = new Promise<void>(r => (resolveReady = r));

  // producer waits for consumer to signal readiness
  await ready;

  yield 42;
}

async function consumer(): Promise<void> {
  const gen = producer();
  // consumer waits for first value
  const { value } = await gen.next();
  console.log(value);
}

// producer waits for consumer's signal; consumer waits for producer's yield → deadlock
```

**Fix:** make the readiness handshake unidirectional, or drive the generator with a push queue/channel instead of bilateral awaits.

---

## Practical guidelines

| Smell | Better practice |
|---|---|
| `count++` between awaits | Atomic update, mutex, or single owner |
| Shared array pushed from concurrent tasks | Write by index or use a transactional store |
| Synchronous event emission during mutation | Defer with `nextTick`/`setImmediate` |
| `await` inside a lock | Release first, or use condition variables |
| Promise with no timeout | Always race against a timeout/cancellation |
| Nested lock acquisition | Lock ordering or avoid reentrancy |
| Worker `Atomics.wait` on main thread | Message passing, never block main |
| Assuming microtask/macrotask order | Make ordering explicit |

For most TypeScript applications, the root fix for both races and deadlocks is **reducing shared mutable state**: prefer immutable data, message passing, and transactional stores over hand-rolled locking.

## Exercise

You are building a tiny in-memory bank in TypeScript.

### Scenario
There are two accounts, `A` and `B`, each starting with `100`.

You must implement:

```ts
transfer(from: AccountId, to: AccountId, amount: number): Promise<void>
```

### Requirements
1. **No deadlock**: Two concurrent transfers in opposite directions, e.g. `A -> B` and `B -> A`, must finish.
2. **No race condition**: Balance checks and updates must be atomic enough that:
   - an account balance never becomes negative,
   - total money is conserved.
3. **Concurrent transfers** should be supported.

### Buggy starter idea
A naive implementation might do this:

```ts
async function transfer(from, to, amount) {
  await lock(from);
  await delay(10); // simulate work
  await lock(to);   // can deadlock with opposite transfer
  // read-modify-write without holding both locks consistently
}
```

and an unprotected implementation may read `balance`, check it, then update later, allowing two transfers to overdraw the same account.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Identify the deadlock
If transfer `A -> B` locks `A` then `B`, while transfer `B -> A` locks `B` then `A`, both can acquire their first lock and wait forever for the second lock.

### 2. Fix deadlock with consistent lock ordering
Always acquire locks in the same global order, e.g. sorted account IDs.  
For accounts `A` and `B`, both transfers lock `A` first, then `B`.

### 3. Identify the race condition
Without holding locks during the whole read-check-update sequence, two concurrent transfers from the same account can both see enough balance and both subtract, causing overdraft or lost updates.

### 4. Make updates atomic under locks
Hold both account locks for the entire operation:
- read balances,
- check source balance,
- update both balances,
- release locks.

### 5. Use a small mutex and lock manager
Implement a FIFO mutex and a helper that locks multiple resources in sorted order.

---

## Final TypeScript solution

```ts
type AccountId = "A" | "B";

class Mutex {
  private locked = false;
  private queue: Array<() => void> = [];

  async acquire(): Promise<void> {
    if (!this.locked) {
      this.locked = true;
      return;
    }

    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
      this.queue.push(resolve);
    });
  }

  release(): void {
    const next = this.queue.shift();
    if (next) {
      next();
    } else {
      this.locked = false;
    }
  }
}

class LockManager {
  private mutexes = new Map<string, Mutex>();

  private getMutex(id: string): Mutex {
    if (!this.mutexes.has(id)) {
      this.mutexes.set(id, new Mutex());
    }
    return this.mutexes.get(id)!;
  }

  async withLocks<T>(ids: string[], fn: () => Promise<T> | T): Promise<T> {
    const uniqueSorted = [...new Set(ids)].sort();

    // Acquire in consistent global order to avoid deadlock
    for (const id of uniqueSorted) {
      await this.getMutex(id).acquire();
    }

    try {
      return await fn();
    } finally {
      // Release in reverse order
      for (const id of [...uniqueSorted].reverse()) {
        this.getMutex(id).release();
      }
    }
  }
}

class Bank {
  private balances = new Map<AccountId, number>([
    ["A", 100],
    ["B", 100],
  ]);

  private locks = new LockManager();

  getBalance(id: AccountId): number {
    return this.balances.get(id)!;
  }

  getTotal(): number {
    return [...this.balances.values()].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  }

  async transfer(from: AccountId, to: AccountId, amount: number): Promise<void> {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      throw new Error("Amount must be positive");
    }
    if (from === to) {
      return;
    }

    await this.locks.withLocks([from, to], async () => {
      const fromBalance = this.balances.get(from)!;

      if (fromBalance < amount) {
        throw new Error(`Insufficient funds in ${from}`);
      }

      // Simulate async work while both locks are held
      await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 10));

      this.balances.set(from, fromBalance - amount);
      this.balances.set(to, this.balances.get(to)! + amount);
    });
  }
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  const bank = new Bank();

  console.log("Before:", bank.getBalance("A"), bank.getBalance("B"), "total =", bank.getTotal());

  // Concurrent opposite transfers
  await Promise.all([
    bank.transfer("A", "B", 60),
    bank.transfer("B", "A", 40),
  ]);

  console.log("After:", bank.getBalance("A"), bank.getBalance("B"), "total =", bank.getTotal());

  // Demonstrate race protection
  try {
    await bank.transfer("A", "B", 10_000);
    console.log("Unexpected success");
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("Expected failure:", (err as Error).message);
  }

  console.log("Final:", bank.getBalance("A"), bank.getBalance("B"), "total =", bank.getTotal());
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

### Why this works
- **Deadlock-free**: Every transfer locks accounts in sorted ID order, so no circular wait.
- **Race-free**: The full check-and-update sequence happens while both relevant locks are held.
- **Conservation**: Money is moved in one atomic logical step, so the total stays constant.
- **Failure-safe**: If validation fails, the `finally` block still releases all locks.

### Note on real systems
In production, prefer database transactions, optimistic concurrency control, or atomic compare-and-update operations. In-memory mutexes are useful for teaching and local coordination, but they do not coordinate across processes or machines.

## Deadlocks and Race Conditions in TypeScript

### 1. Mental model: TS/JS concurrency

TypeScript runs on a **single event loop per thread**. There is no preemptive threading inside one JS context, so:

- **Race conditions** usually come from *interleaving of async work* (microtasks/macrotasks), shared mutable state, or multiple workers/threads sharing memory.
- **Deadlocks** usually come from **async synchronization primitives** (mutexes/semaphores) or **worker threads waiting on each other**, not from language-level locks (JS has none built in).

### 2. Race condition

A race happens when the result depends on timing/ordering. Classic example: two `await`-based increments that read-modify-write a shared counter without isolation.

### 3. Deadlock

A deadlock requires **circular wait** on resources. In async JS this looks like:

- Task 1 holds Lock A and waits for Lock B.
- Task 2 holds Lock B and waits for Lock A.
- Neither can progress; if both are on the event loop, the app hangs.

Common prevention strategies:
- **Consistent lock ordering** (always acquire A before B).
- **Timeouts / try-lock**.
- **Avoid nested locks**; prefer message passing / structured concurrency.

---

## Runnable core (Node / TypeScript)

```ts
// concurrency.ts
// Demonstrates race conditions + deadlock + fixes with a minimal async mutex.

type Release = () => void;

/** Minimal async mutex built on a waiter queue. */
class AsyncMutex {
  private locked = false;
  private waiters: Array<() => void> = [];

  /** Acquire the lock; resolves with a release function. */
  async acquire(): Promise<Release> {
    if (!this.locked) {
      this.locked = true;
      return this.makeRelease();
    }

    // Queue this waiter; it will be resumed when release() runs.
    return new Promise<Release>((resolve) => {
      this.waiters.push(() => {
        this.locked = true;
        resolve(this.makeRelease());
      });
    });
  }

  /** Try to acquire without waiting; returns null if already locked. */
  tryAcquire(): Release | null {
    if (this.locked) return null;
    this.locked = true;
    return this.makeRelease();
  }

  private makeRelease(): Release {
    let released = false;
    return () => {
      if (released) return;
      released = true;
      this.locked = false;
      // Wake next waiter (if any).
      const next = this.waiters.shift();
      if (next) next();
    };
  }
}

function delay(ms: number): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
}

// ---------------- Race condition demo ----------------

let unsafeCounter = 0;

/** BUGGY: read-modify-write with an await in between. */
async function unsafeIncrement(): Promise<void> {
  const snapshot = unsafeCounter;       // read
  await delay(0);                     // yield; another task can run
  unsafeCounter = snapshot + 1;        // write (may clobber)
}

/** Fixed: serialize the critical section with a mutex. */
const counterMutex = new AsyncMutex();
let safeCounter = 0;

async function safeIncrement(): Promise<void> {
  const release = await counterMutex.acquire();
  try {
    const snapshot = safeCounter;
    await delay(0);
    safeCounter = snapshot + 1;
  } finally {
    release();
  }
}

// ---------------- Deadlock demo ----------------

const lockA = new AsyncMutex();
const lockB = new AsyncMutex();

/** DEADLOCK: opposite acquisition order. */
async function deadlockTask1(): Promise<string> {
  const rA = await lockA.acquire();
  await delay(10); // ensure overlap
  const rB = await lockB.acquire(); // waits for task2 to release B
  rB();
  rA();
  return "task1 done";
}

async function deadlockTask2(): Promise<string> {
  const rB = await lockB.acquire();
  await delay(10);
  const rA = await lockA.acquire(); // waits for task1 to release A
  rA();
  rB();
  return "task2 done";
}

/** Fixed: consistent global ordering (A then B). */
async function orderedTask1(): Promise<string> {
  const rA = await lockA.acquire();
  try {
    await delay(10);
    const rB = await lockB.acquire();
    try {
      return "task1 done";
    } finally {
      rB();
    }
  } finally {
    rA();
  }
}

async function orderedTask2(): Promise<string> {
  // Same order as task1: A first, then B.
  const rA = await lockA.acquire();
  try {
    await delay(10);
    const rB = await lockB.acquire();
    try {
      return "task2 done";
    } finally {
      rB();
    }
  } finally {
    rA();
  }
}

// ---------------- Harness ----------------

async function runRaceDemo() {
  unsafeCounter = 0;
  safeCounter = 0;

  const N = 200;
  const unsafeJobs = Array.from({ length: N }, () => unsafeIncrement());
  const safeJobs = Array.from({ length: N }, () => safeIncrement());

  await Promise.all(unsafeJobs);
  await Promise.all(safeJobs);

  console.log("Race demo:");
  console.log("  expected:", N);
  console.log("  unsafe  :", unsafeCounter, unsafeCounter === N ? "OK" : "RACE LOST");
  console.log("  safe    :", safeCounter, safeCounter === N ? "OK" : "UNEXPECTED");
}

async function withTimeout<T>(p: Promise<T>, ms: number): Promise<T> {
  return Promise.race([
    p,
    new Promise<T>((_, reject) =>
      setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`Timeout after ${ms}ms (possible deadlock)`)), ms)
    ),
  ]);
}

async function runDeadlockDemo() {
  // Reset locks by recreating (mutexes are stateful; fresh instances avoid contamination).
  const a = new AsyncMutex();
  const b = new AsyncMutex();

  // Inline deadlock tasks bound to fresh locks.
  async function t1() {
    const rA = await a.acquire();
    await delay(10);
    const rB = await b.acquire();
    rB(); rA();
    return "t1";
  }
  async function t2() {
    const rB = await b.acquire();
    await delay(10);
    const rA = await a.acquire();
    rA(); rB();
    return "t2";
  }

  try {
    await withTimeout(Promise.all([t1(), t2()]), 200);
    console.log("Deadlock demo: completed (unexpected)");
  } catch (e) {
    console.log("Deadlock demo:", (e as Error).message);
  }

  // Fixed version
  const a2 = new AsyncMutex();
  const b2 = new AsyncMutex();
  async function o1() {
    const rA = await a2.acquire();
    try {
      await delay(10);
      const rB = await b2.acquire();
      try { return "o1"; } finally { rB(); }
    } finally { rA(); }
  }
  async function o2() {
    const rA = await a2.acquire();
    try {
      await delay(10);
      const rB = await b2.acquire();
      try { return "o2"; } finally { rB(); }
    } finally { rA(); }
  }
  const res = await Promise.all([o1(), o2()]);
  console.log("Ordered demo:", res.join(", "));
}

(async () => {
  await runRaceDemo();
  await runDeadlockDemo();
})();
```

### Key takeaways for an interview

- **JS is single-threaded, but not “race-free.”** Any `await` is a suspension point; other code can run and mutate shared state.
- **Mutexes serialize critical sections**, but introducing locks reintroduces **deadlock risk** if acquisition order is inconsistent.
- **Prefer simpler designs:** immutable data, message passing, actor-style queues, or `Atomics` in workers; reserve locks for hot, truly shared state.
- **Always pair `acquire` with `try/finally` release**, and add **timeouts** in production to detect stalls early.

# Concurrency edge cases in TypeScript/JavaScript

JavaScript is single-threaded (one event loop per realm), but **concurrency still exists** through:
- async/await and Promises (interleaving at `await`)
- timers, I/O, message channels
- Web Workers / `worker_threads` (true threads, shared memory via `SharedArrayBuffer`)

So “deadlocks” are usually *logical* (circular waits on Promises/events), and “races” are usually *interleavings* of microtasks/macrotasks or cross-thread memory access.

Below are the most important edge cases with runnable TypeScript examples and fixes.

---

## 1) Lost update (check-then-act race)

**Edge case:** reading state, yielding, then writing based on stale value.

```ts
let counter = 0;

async function increment() {
  const current = counter;          // read
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); // yield → another increment can run
  counter = current + 1;             // write stale value
}

await Promise.all([increment(), increment()]);
console.log(counter); // 1 (expected 2)
```

**Fix:** make the read+write atomic with respect to the event loop (do it synchronously), or use a mutex.

```ts
let counter = 0;
async function incrementSafe() {
  // synchronous critical section before any await
  const next = ++counter; // atomic on main thread
  await doWork(next);
}
```

---

## 2) Duplicate in-flight work (thundering herd)

**Edge case:** multiple callers request the same resource before the first resolves.

```ts
const cache = new Map<string, Promise<string>>();

async function load(key: string): Promise<string> {
  if (!cache.has(key)) {
    // race: two callers both miss cache and both fetch
    const p = fetch(`/api/${key}`).then(r => r.text());
    cache.set(key, p);
  }
  return cache.get(key)!;
}
```

Even the above has a subtle race if two calls arrive in the same microtask before `cache.set`.  
**Fix:** store the promise *before* awaiting, and use a “get-or-create” helper.

```ts
const inflight = new Map<string, Promise<string>>();

function load(key: string): Promise<string> {
  let p = inflight.get(key);
  if (!p) {
    p = fetch(`/api/${key}`).then(r => r.text()).finally(() => inflight.delete(key));
    inflight.set(key, p);
  }
  return p;
}
```

---

## 3) Circular await deadlock

**Edge case:** two async functions each wait for the other to resolve a deferred.

```ts
class Deferred<T> {
  promise = new Promise<T>((res, rej) => { this.resolve = res; this.reject = rej; });
  resolve!: (v: T) => void;
  reject!: (e: any) => void;
}

const a = new Deferred<void>();
const b = new Deferred<void>();

async function task1() {
  await a.promise;   // waits for task2
  b.resolve();        // never reached
}

async function task2() {
  await b.promise;   // waits for task1
  a.resolve();        // never reached
}

Promise.all([task1(), task2()]); // hangs forever
```

**Fix:** never create cyclic dependencies in promise graphs; use timeouts, or restructure to a single coordinator.

```ts
// Coordinator pattern
async function runBoth() {
  const [r1, r2] = await Promise.all([doA(), doB()]);
  return combine(r1, r2);
}
```

---

## 4) Lock-ordering deadlock (async mutex)

Even in JS, if you serialize with an async mutex and acquire two locks in inconsistent order, you can deadlock.

```ts
import { Mutex } from "async-mutex";

const lockA = new Mutex();
const lockB = new Mutex();

async function transferXtoY() {
  await lockA.acquire();
  try {
    await lockB.acquire();
    try { /* move */ } finally { lockB.release(); }
  } finally { lockA.release(); }
}

async function transferYtoX() {
  await lockB.acquire(); // opposite order
  try {
    await lockA.acquire(); // waits forever if XtoY holds A and wants B
    ...
  }
}
```

**Fix:** always acquire multiple locks in a global, fixed order; prefer a single coarse lock; or avoid locks by using message passing/queues.

```ts
async function transfer(from: "A" | "B", to: "A" | "B") {
  const [first, second] = from === "A" ? [lockA, lockB] : [lockB, lockA];
  await first.acquire();
  try {
    await second.acquire();
    try { /* move */ } finally { second.release(); }
  } finally { first.release(); }
}
```

---

## 5) Promise that never resolves (missing branch)

**Edge case:** constructor callback forgets to resolve/reject on some path.

```ts
function fetchWithTimeout(url: string, ms: number) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("timeout")), ms);
    fetch(url).then(r => {
      clearTimeout(t);
      if (r.ok) resolve(r.json());
      // BUG: non-ok path never resolves/rejects
    });
  });
}
```

**Fix:** handle every branch; add a final safety timer or use `Promise.race`.

```ts
function fetchWithTimeout(url: string, ms: number) {
  return Promise.race([
    fetch(url).then(r => {
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
      return r.json();
    }),
    new Promise((_, reject) =>
      setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("timeout")), ms)
    ),
  ]);
}
```

---

## 6) Microtask starvation

**Edge case:** recursive `Promise.resolve().then(...)` never lets timers/I/O run.

```ts
function starve() {
  Promise.resolve().then(starve); // infinite microtask chain
}
starve();
setTimeout(() => console.log("never prints"), 0);
```

**Fix:** yield to the macrotask queue periodically.

```ts
async function heavyLoop(items: number[]) {
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    process(items[i]);
    if (i % 1000 === 0) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); // yield
  }
}
```

---

## 7) Partial failure leaving inconsistent state

**Edge case:** multi-step async operation fails midway; earlier steps are not rolled back.

```ts
async function createUserAndProfile(data: any) {
  const user = await db.insertUser(data);      // step 1 ok
  await db.insertProfile(user.id, data);       // step 2 throws
  // user row now orphaned
}
```

**Fix:** transaction, or compensating actions in `finally`.

```ts
async function createUserAndProfile(data: any) {
  const user = await db.insertUser(data);
  try {
    await db.insertProfile(user.id, data);
  } catch (e) {
    await db.deleteUser(user.id); // compensate
    throw e;
  }
}
```

---

## 8) Cancellation race (AbortSignal)

**Edge case:** operation completes after abort; callback touches freed state.

```ts
const controller = new AbortController();
const signal = controller.signal;

doAsyncWork(signal).then(result => {
  // race: abort may have already torn down resources
  updateUI(result);
});

controller.abort();
```

**Fix:** check `signal.aborted` before using results; propagate signal to all downstream calls; make handlers idempotent.

```ts
async function doAsyncWork(signal: AbortSignal) {
  const res = await fetch("/data", { signal });
  if (signal.aborted) throw new DOMException("Aborted", "AbortError");
  const json = await res.json();
  if (signal.aborted) throw new DOMException("Aborted", "AbortError");
  return json;
}
```

---

## 9) `Promise.all` vs `allSettled` ordering & short-circuit

**Edge case:** one rejection rejects whole `Promise.all` immediately, but other operations keep running (not cancelled) and may mutate state.

```ts
await Promise.all([
  mutateA(), // slow
  mutateB(), // fast, throws
]);
// mutateA still running in background; unhandled if it later rejects? (it's attached, but you lost the handle)
```

**Fix:** use `Promise.allSettled` when you need all outcomes, or attach per-promise catch; cancel via AbortSignal.

```ts
const results = await Promise.allSettled([mutateA(sig), mutateB(sig)]);
for (const r of results) {
  if (r.status === "rejected") console.error(r.reason);
}
```

---

## 10) Reentrancy / reentrant async callbacks

**Edge case:** an async function is called again before its first invocation finishes, and both touch the same state.

```ts
let busy = false;
async function handleRequest() {
  if (busy) return; // guard
  busy = true;
  await doWork();
  busy = false;      // if doWork throws, busy stays true forever
}
```

**Fix:** use `try/finally`, and prefer a queue or state machine over boolean flags.

```ts
let busy = false;
async function handleRequest() {
  if (busy) return;
  busy = true;
  try {
    await doWork();
  } finally {
    busy = false;
  }
}
```

Better: serialize with a queue.

```ts
class SerialQueue {
  private tail = Promise.resolve();
  run<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
    const next = this.tail.then(fn, fn);
    this.tail = next.then(() => {}, () => {});
    return next;
  }
}
```

---

## 11) Cross-thread races with Workers / `SharedArrayBuffer`

**Edge case:** two threads read-modify-write a shared counter without atomics → lost updates.

```ts
// worker.ts
const buf = new SharedArrayBuffer(4);
const view = new Int32Array(buf);
// main and worker both do:
view[0] = view[0] + 1; // data race
```

**Fix:** use `Atomics`.

```ts
Atomics.add(view, 0, 1); // atomic
// read consistently:
const v = Atomics.load(view, 0);
```

**Edge case:** `Atomics.wait` on main thread throws (`TypeError`). Only call it in workers.

```ts
// worker only
Atomics.wait(view, 0, 0); // blocks worker thread, OK
```

**Edge case:** false sharing / torn reads on non-atomic multi-word structures. Keep shared state to single atomic words or use a lock.

---

## 12) Event-loop blocking in workers

**Edge case:** synchronous heavy computation in a worker blocks message handling, causing apparent deadlock of request/response correlation.

```ts
// worker
onmessage = (e) => {
  const result = heavySync(e.data); // blocks
  postMessage(result);
};
```

If main sends a second message expecting the first’s response context, ordering assumptions break.  
**Fix:** chunk work with `setTimeout`/`scheduler.yield()`, or split into smaller tasks.

---

## 13) Message ordering assumptions

**Edge case:** assuming messages arrive in send order across different channels or after awaits.

```ts
worker.postMessage({ type: "A" });
await delay(10);
worker.postMessage({ type: "B" });
// B may be processed before A if A triggers async work internally
```

**Fix:** include sequence numbers and correlate responses by id.

```ts
let seq = 0;
function request(payload: any) {
  const id = ++seq;
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    const handler = (e: MessageEvent) => {
      if (e.data.id === id) { worker.removeEventListener("message", handler); res(e.data); }
    };
    worker.addEventListener("message", handler);
    worker.postMessage({ id, ...payload });
  });
}
```

---

## 14) Unhandled rejection as silent failure

**Edge case:** a rejected promise with no `.catch`/`await` can crash Node or leave state half-updated.

```ts
async function run() {
  doWork(); // forgot await; rejection becomes unhandled
}
```

**Fix:** always `await`, or attach `.catch`. Add global handlers for safety, but fix root cause.

```ts
process.on("unhandledRejection", (reason) => {
  console.error("unhandledRejection", reason);
  // optionally exit
});
```

---

## 15) Time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) with external systems

**Edge case:** checking DB state, then acting later; another process changes it.

```ts
const exists = await db.exists(id);
if (!exists) await db.insert(id); // race with another instance
```

**Fix:** use unique constraints + upsert, or atomic conditional inserts.

```ts
await db.insert(id).onConflict("id").ignore(); // atomic at DB level
```

---

## 16) Idempotency & exactly-once illusions

Network retries can cause duplicate execution. Make handlers idempotent (idempotency keys).

```ts
async function charge(orderId: string, idempotencyKey: string) {
  const existing = await payments.find(idempotencyKey);
  if (existing) return existing;
  return payments.create({ orderId, idempotencyKey });
}
```

---

## A small “safe concurrency” toolkit

### Mutex with timeout (avoid indefinite deadlock)
```ts
class TimeoutMutex {
  private queue: Array<() => void> = [];
  private locked = false;

  async acquire(timeoutMs = 5000): Promise<() => void> {
    if (!this.locked) { this.locked = true; return () => this.release(); }
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("lock timeout")), timeoutMs);
      const grant = () => { clearTimeout(timer); resolve(() => this.release()); };
      this.queue.push(grant);
    });
  }
  private release() {
    const next = this.queue.shift();
    if (next) next(); else this.locked = false;
  }
}
```

### Dedup + timeout wrapper
```ts
function withTimeout<T>(p: Promise<T>, ms: number): Promise<T> {
  return Promise.race([
    p,
    new Promise<T>((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("timeout")), ms)),
  ]);
}
```

### Deterministic tests (avoid flaky timing)
- Don’t rely on `setTimeout(0)` ordering; use explicit deferreds/queues in tests.
- Use fake timers (`jest.useFakeTimers`) carefully—microtask ordering still matters.

---

## Quick checklist

- Never hold a lock across an `await` unless the lock is async-aware and you have a timeout.
- Acquire multiple locks in a consistent global order.
- Store in-flight promises before awaiting to dedup.
- Handle every branch in `new Promise` constructors.
- Propagate `AbortSignal` and check `aborted` before touching state.
- Use `Atomics` for all shared memory access across threads.
- Make side-effecting operations idempotent and resumable.
- Yield periodically in long microtask chains.
- Add compensating rollback for multi-step async workflows.

If you want, I can adapt these patterns to a specific runtime (Node `worker_threads`, browser Web Workers, or a frontend framework like React).

