# Design Patterns in TypeScript

Design patterns are **reusable solutions to recurring design problems**. They are not copy-paste code snippets; they are *structures and relationships* (interfaces, composition, inheritance) that make systems easier to change, test, and extend. TypeScript makes many classic patterns safer and more expressive through **interfaces, abstract classes, generics, access modifiers, and structural typing**.

---

## 1. Why patterns matter in TypeScript

- **Contracts via interfaces** → decouple callers from implementations.
- **Generics** → type-safe reusable components (factories, repositories).
- **Abstract classes** → shared partial implementation + enforced overrides.
- **Access modifiers (`private`, `protected`)** → encapsulate state.
- **Structural typing** → adapters and proxies are easy to implement without explicit inheritance.

---

## 2. Creational Patterns (object creation)

### Singleton — one instance, global access point
```ts
class Logger {
  private static instance: Logger;
  private constructor() {} // prevent new Logger()

  static getInstance(): Logger {
    if (!Logger.instance) Logger.instance = new Logger();
    return Logger.instance;
  }

  log(msg: string) { console.log(`[LOG] ${msg}`); }
}

const log = Logger.getInstance();
```
**Use case:** configuration, logging, DB connection pool.  
**Caution:** can hide dependencies; prefer DI in large apps.

### Factory Method — subclass decides which class to instantiate
```ts
interface Product { operation(): string; }

class ConcreteA implements Product { operation() { return "Result A"; } }
class ConcreteB implements Product { operation() { return "Result B"; } }

abstract class Creator {
  abstract factoryMethod(): Product;
  someOperation(): string {
    return `Creator: ${this.factoryMethod().operation()}`;
  }
}

class CreatorA extends Creator { factoryMethod() { return new ConcreteA(); } }
class CreatorB extends Creator { factoryMethod() { return new ConcreteB(); } }
```
**Use case:** framework hooks where the framework calls your factory.

### Builder — step-by-step construction of complex objects
```ts
class Pizza {
  constructor(public size: string, public cheese: boolean, public toppings: string[]) {}
}

class PizzaBuilder {
  private size = "medium";
  private cheese = false;
  private toppings: string[] = [];

  setSize(s: string) { this.size = s; return this; }
  addCheese() { this.cheese = true; return this; }
  addTopping(t: string) { this.toppings.push(t); return this; }
  build() { return new Pizza(this.size, this.cheese, this.toppings); }
}

const pizza = new PizzaBuilder().setSize("large").addCheese().addTopping("pepperoni").build();
```
**Use case:** objects with many optional parameters (avoids telescoping constructors).

---

## 3. Structural Patterns (composition of objects)

### Adapter — make incompatible interfaces work together
```ts
interface Target { request(): string; }

class Adaptee { specificRequest(): string { return "specific data"; } }

class Adapter implements Target {
  constructor(private adaptee: Adaptee) {}
  request(): string { return this.adaptee.specificRequest(); }
}
```
**Use case:** integrating third-party SDKs, legacy APIs.

### Decorator (structural) — add behavior dynamically
```ts
interface Coffee { cost(): number; description(): string; }

class SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {
  cost() { return 5; }
  description() { return "Simple coffee"; }
}

class MilkDecorator implements Coffee {
  constructor(private coffee: Coffee) {}
  cost() { return this.coffee.cost() + 2; }
  description() { return this.coffee.description() + ", milk"; }
}

const coffee = new MilkDecorator(new SimpleCoffee());
```
**Use case:** middleware, I/O streams, UI components.  
*Note:* TS decorators (`@decorator`) are a language feature, distinct from this GoF pattern.

### Facade — simplified entry point to a subsystem
```ts
class SubsystemA { operationA() { return "A"; } }
class SubsystemB { operationB() { return "B"; } }

class Facade {
  private a = new SubsystemA();
  private b = new SubsystemB();
  doWork() { return `${this.a.operationA()} + ${this.b.operationB()}`; }
}
```
**Use case:** hiding complex module internals from controllers.

### Proxy — control access to an object
```ts
interface Image { display(): void; }

class RealImage implements Image {
  constructor(private filename: string) { this.loadFromDisk(); }
  private loadFromDisk() { console.log(`Loading ${this.filename}`); }
  display() { console.log(`Displaying ${this.filename}`); }
}

class ProxyImage implements Image {
  private real?: RealImage;
  constructor(private filename: string) {}
  display() {
    if (!this.real) this.real = new RealImage(this.filename);
    this.real.display();
  }
}
```
**Use case:** lazy loading, caching, access control, logging.

### Composite — tree structures treated uniformly
```ts
interface Component { operation(): string; }

class Leaf implements Component { operation() { return "Leaf"; } }

class Composite implements Component {
  private children: Component[] = [];
  add(c: Component) { this.children.push(c); }
  operation() { return this.children.map(c => c.operation()).join(" + "); }
}
```
**Use case:** file systems, UI trees, ASTs.

---

## 4. Behavioral Patterns (communication & responsibility)

### Strategy — interchangeable algorithms
```ts
interface SortStrategy { sort(data: number[]): number[]; }

class QuickSort implements SortStrategy { sort(d) { return [...d].sort((a,b)=>a-b); } }
class ReverseSort implements SortStrategy { sort(d) { return [...d].sort((a,b)=>b-a); } }

class Sorter {
  constructor(private strategy: SortStrategy) {}
  setStrategy(s: SortStrategy) { this.strategy = s; }
  sort(data: number[]) { return this.strategy.sort(data); }
}
```
**Use case:** payment methods, sorting, validation rules.

### Observer — one-to-many notifications
```ts
interface Observer { update(data: any): void; }

class Subject {
  private observers: Observer[] = [];
  subscribe(o: Observer) { this.observers.push(o); }
  unsubscribe(o: Observer) { this.observers = this.observers.filter(x => x !== o); }
  notify(data: any) { this.observers.forEach(o => o.update(data)); }
}
```
**Use case:** event systems, reactive state, pub/sub.

### Command — encapsulate a request as an object
```ts
interface Command { execute(): void; undo(): void; }

class Light { on() {} off() {} }

class TurnOnCommand implements Command {
  constructor(private light: Light) {}
  execute() { this.light.on(); }
  undo() { this.light.off(); }
}
```
**Use case:** undo/redo, queues, macro recording.

### State — behavior changes with internal state
```ts
interface State { handle(ctx: Context): void; }

class Context {
  state: State = new IdleState();
  request() { this.state.handle(this); }
}

class IdleState implements State {
  handle(ctx: Context) { console.log("idle -> running"); ctx.state = new RunningState(); }
}
class RunningState implements State {
  handle(ctx: Context) { console.log("running -> idle"); ctx.state = new IdleState(); }
}
```
**Use case:** finite state machines, order lifecycle, TCP connections.

### Template Method — skeleton in base class, steps overridden
```ts
abstract class DataProcessor {
  process() {
    this.read();
    this.parse();
    this.save();
  }
  protected abstract read(): void;
  protected abstract parse(): void;
  protected save() { console.log("saving"); }
}

class CsvProcessor extends DataProcessor {
  protected read() { console.log("read csv"); }
  protected parse() { console.log("parse csv"); }
}
```
**Use case:** ETL pipelines, report generation.

### Chain of Responsibility — pass request along a chain
```ts
abstract class Handler {
  private next?: Handler;
  setNext(h: Handler) { this.next = h; return h; }
  handle(req: string): string | null {
    if (this.next) return this.next.handle(req);
    return null;
  }
}

class AuthHandler extends Handler {
  handle(req: string) {
    if (!req.includes("token")) return "401 Unauthorized";
    return super.handle(req);
  }
}
```
**Use case:** middleware pipelines (Express/Nest), validation chains.

---

## 5. TypeScript-specific “patterns” you’ll see in real code

- **Repository + Service** (layered architecture): `UserRepository` hides DB; `UserService` holds business logic.
- **Dependency Injection**: pass dependencies via constructor; frameworks (NestJS, Angular) use decorators/reflect-metadata.
- **Module pattern**: ES modules + `private` fields; no need for IIFE.
- **Type-safe Event Emitter** with generics:
```ts
class Emitter<Events extends Record<string, any>> {
  private listeners: { [K in keyof Events]?: ((p: Events[K]) => void)[] } = {};
  on<K extends keyof Events>(e: K, cb: (p: Events[K]) => void) {
    (this.listeners[e] ??= []).push(cb);
  }
  emit<K extends keyof Events>(e: K, payload: Events[K]) {
    this.listeners[e]?.forEach(cb => cb(payload));
  }
}
```

---

## 6. Practical guidance

| Goal | Pattern |
|------|---------|
| Hide complexity | Facade |
| Add behavior without subclassing | Decorator |
| Swap algorithms at runtime | Strategy |
| React to changes | Observer |
| Decouple creation from usage | Factory / DI |
| Undo/redo or queue actions | Command |
| Lazy/expensive resources | Proxy |
| Tree structures | Composite |

**Rules of thumb**
1. Prefer **composition over inheritance** (Strategy, Decorator, Composite).
2. Program to **interfaces**, not concrete classes.
3. Don’t force a pattern where a plain function works—TS functions are first-class.
4. Keep patterns **small**; over-abstraction hurts more than duplication.
5. Use **generics** to keep patterns type-safe instead of `any`.

If you want, I can expand any single pattern into a full mini-project example (e.g., a NestJS service using Strategy + Factory + DI).

A common mistake is implementing a **Strategy** (or any variant behavior) with a growing `if/else` or `switch` instead of polymorphism. It works at first, but every new variant forces editing the central class, violating the Open/Closed Principle and making the code hard to test and extend.

### Mistaken version

```ts
// payment-processor.bad.ts
class PaymentProcessor {
  process(type: string, amount: number): void {
    if (type === "credit") {
      console.log(`Charging credit card: ${amount}`);
      // credit-specific validation, fees, retries...
    } else if (type === "paypal") {
      console.log(`Charging PayPal: ${amount}`);
      // paypal-specific flow...
    } else if (type === "crypto") {
      console.log(`Charging crypto wallet: ${amount}`);
      // crypto-specific flow...
    } else {
      throw new Error(`Unknown payment type: ${type}`);
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const processor = new PaymentProcessor();
processor.process("credit", 100);
processor.process("crypto", 50);
```

**Why this is wrong**

- **Open/Closed Principle violation**: adding `apple_pay` means editing `PaymentProcessor`, re-testing all branches, and risking regressions in unrelated paths.
- **Hidden coupling**: callers pass a magic string; the compiler cannot catch typos like `"creditt"`.
- **Low cohesion**: one class knows the internals of every payment method (validation, fees, retries).
- **Hard to test**: you must exercise every branch inside one method; mocking a single strategy is awkward.
- **No reuse/composition**: you cannot inject a different implementation (e.g., a sandbox provider) without changing the class.

---

### Corrected version (Strategy pattern)

```ts
// payment-strategy.ts
export interface PaymentStrategy {
  readonly name: string;
  pay(amount: number): Promise<void>;
}

export class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
  readonly name = "credit";

  async pay(amount: number): Promise<void> {
    console.log(`Charging credit card: ${amount}`);
    // credit-specific logic lives here only
  }
}

export class PayPalPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
  readonly name = "paypal";

  async pay(amount: number): Promise<void> {
    console.log(`Charging PayPal: ${amount}`);
  }
}

export class CryptoPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
  readonly name = "crypto";

  async pay(amount: number): Promise<void> {
    console.log(`Charging crypto wallet: ${amount}`);
  }
}

// payment-processor.ts
export class PaymentProcessor {
  constructor(private readonly strategy: PaymentStrategy) {}

  async process(amount: number): Promise<void> {
    if (amount <= 0) throw new Error("Amount must be positive");
    await this.strategy.pay(amount);
  }
}

// Optional: a small registry keeps the "string -> strategy" mapping in one place
// instead of scattering it across the processor.
export class PaymentStrategyRegistry {
  private readonly strategies = new Map<string, PaymentStrategy>();

  register(strategy: PaymentStrategy): this {
    this.strategies.set(strategy.name, strategy);
    return this;
  }

  resolve(name: string): PaymentStrategy {
    const strategy = this.strategies.get(name);
    if (!strategy) throw new Error(`Unknown payment type: ${name}`);
    return strategy;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```ts
const registry = new PaymentStrategyRegistry()
  .register(new CreditCardPayment())
  .register(new PayPalPayment())
  .register(new CryptoPayment());

const processor = new PaymentProcessor(registry.resolve("credit"));
await processor.process(100);

// Adding Apple Pay later: create ApplePayPayment, register it. No edits to PaymentProcessor.
```

---

### What changed and why

1. **Behavior is now a polymorphic object, not a string branch.**  
   Each payment method implements `PaymentStrategy`. The processor depends on the interface, not on concrete types, so you can add new methods without touching existing code.

2. **The processor becomes trivial and stable.**  
   It only validates input and delegates. Its tests no longer need to cover every payment type—each strategy is tested independently.

3. **Construction is separated from execution.**  
   `PaymentProcessor` receives its strategy via the constructor (Dependency Injection). This makes it easy to inject a fake strategy in unit tests or a sandbox implementation in staging.

4. **The registry centralizes the string-to-object mapping.**  
   If you truly need string-based selection (e.g., from user input or a database), put that mapping in one small, dedicated place. The processor itself stays closed to modification.

5. **Type safety improves.**  
   Callers can pass a `PaymentStrategy` directly, eliminating magic strings at the API boundary. When strings are unavoidable, the registry throws a clear error at resolution time rather than deep inside business logic.

### When not to over-engineer

If you have only two or three stable variants that never change, a simple `switch` may be acceptable and even clearer. The Strategy pattern pays off when variants are expected to grow, need independent testing, or must be selected at runtime. The key lesson: **don't let variant-specific behavior accumulate inside a single class's control flow**—extract it behind an interface.

# Common Pitfalls When Working with Design Patterns in TypeScript

Below are the most frequent traps, grouped by pattern family, each with a "bad" and "better" snippet. The recurring themes: **type erasure at runtime**, **structural typing surprises**, **`this` binding**, **memory leaks**, and **over-engineering**.

---

## 1. Singleton — Async initialization races & hidden global state

```ts
// BAD: lazy init with no synchronization → race condition under concurrent calls
class DbConnection {
  private static instance: DbConnection | null = null;
  private constructor() {}

  static async getInstance(): Promise<DbConnection> {
    if (!this.instance) {
      this.instance = await connectToDb(); // two callers can both enter here
    }
    return this.instance;
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: eager init, or a promise cache, or dependency injection
class DbConnection {
  private static instancePromise: Promise<DbConnection> | null = null;

  static getInstance(): Promise<DbConnection> {
    if (!this.instancePromise) {
      this.instancePromise = connectToDb().then(c => new DbConnection(c));
    }
    return this.instancePromise;
  }
}
// Even better: don't use a singleton at all — inject the connection.
```

**Pitfall summary:** Singletons make unit testing painful (hard to swap fakes), hide dependencies, and in TS the `private constructor` is only a compile-time guard — reflection/JS can still bypass it.

---

## 2. Observer — Memory leaks from forgotten unsubscriptions

```ts
// BAD: no unsubscribe handle; component never cleaned up
class EventBus {
  private listeners = new Set<(e: Event) => void>();
  on(cb: (e: Event) => void) { this.listeners.add(cb); }
  emit(e: Event) { this.listeners.forEach(cb => cb(e)); }
}

// Usage in a React/Vue component → listener leaks after unmount
bus.on(e => setState(e));
```

```ts
// BETTER: return an unsubscribe function; prefer WeakRef for auto-cleanup
class EventBus {
  private listeners = new Set<(e: Event) => void>();
  on(cb: (e: Event) => void): () => void {
    this.listeners.add(cb);
    return () => this.listeners.delete(cb);
  }
  emit(e: Event) { this.listeners.forEach(cb => cb(e)); }
}

// Usage
const off = bus.on(e => setState(e));
onUnmount(() => off());
```

**Pitfall summary:** TS won't warn you about leaked closures; combine with `AbortSignal` or `WeakRef` for safety.

---

## 3. Observer — Infinite notification loops & re-entrancy

```ts
// BAD: emitting inside a listener re-triggers the same listener
bus.on(e => {
  if (e.type === 'update') bus.emit({ type: 'audit', payload: e.payload });
});
```

```ts
// BETTER: defer with queueMicrotask / separate phase, or use a "dirty" flag
bus.on(e => {
  if (e.type === 'update') queueMicrotask(() => bus.emit({ type: 'audit', payload: e.payload }));
});
```

---

## 4. Factory — Reaching for `any`/`as` instead of discriminated unions

```ts
// BAD: stringly-typed factory, no exhaustiveness checking
function createShape(kind: string): any {
  if (kind === 'circle') return new Circle();
  if (kind === 'square') return new Square();
  // forgot triangle → returns undefined at runtime, TS happy
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: discriminated union + exhaustive switch
type ShapeKind = 'circle' | 'square' | 'triangle';

function createShape(kind: ShapeKind): Shape {
  switch (kind) {
    case 'circle': return new Circle();
    case 'square': return new Square();
    case 'triangle': return new Triangle();
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = kind;
      throw new Error(`Unknown shape: ${_exhaustive}`);
    }
  }
}
```

**Pitfall summary:** TS erases types at runtime, so a factory that *looks* type-safe can still ship `undefined`. Always add a `never` exhaustiveness check.

---

## 5. Decorator — `this` binding lost & experimental vs. stage-3 syntax

```ts
// BAD: method decorator loses `this` when passed as callback
class Service {
  @Log
  handle(x: number) { return this.value + x; }
  value = 10;
}
const s = new Service();
const fn = s.handle; // `this` is undefined at call time
fn(5); // runtime error
```

```ts
// BETTER: bind in constructor, or use an arrow field
class Service {
  value = 10;
  handle = (x: number) => this.value + x; // arrow preserves `this`
}
```

```ts
// Also: enable the RIGHT decorator flag. TS 5+ uses stage-3 by default.
// tsconfig.json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "experimentalDecorators": false,   // use standard decorators
    "emitDecoratorMetadata": false      // rarely needed; breaks with bundlers
  }
}
```

**Pitfall summary:** Old tutorials use `experimentalDecorators: true` + `reflect-metadata`. Mixing the two systems silently breaks. Pick one and stay consistent.

---

## 6. Strategy — Over-abstraction & runtime type checks

```ts
// BAD: strategy interface with one implementation, plus `instanceof` dispatch
interface SortStrategy { sort(a: number[]): number[]; }
class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { sort(a) { /*...*/ return a; } }

function sort(data: number[], strategy: SortStrategy | 'bubble') {
  if (typeof strategy === 'string') strategy = new BubbleSort();
  return strategy.sort(data);
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: keep it a plain function type; let TS infer
type Sorter = (data: number[]) => number[];
const bubble: Sorter = (data) => [...data].sort((a, b) => a - b);
const sort = (data: number[], sorter: Sorter = bubble) => sorter(data);
```

**Pitfall summary:** Strategy is just "pass a function." In TS, a function type is usually cheaper than a class hierarchy.

---

## 7. Builder — Fluent API loses type information across steps

```ts
// BAD: every method returns `this`, so partial state isn't tracked
class RequestBuilder {
  private url = '';
  private method = 'GET';
  setUrl(u: string) { this.url = u; return this; }
  setMethod(m: string) { this.method = m; return this; }
  build() { return { url: this.url, method: this.method }; }
}
// `build()` always allowed, even with empty url
```

```ts
// BETTER: phantom types track required fields at compile time
type Empty = { url?: never; method?: never };
type WithUrl<U extends string> = { url: U };
type WithMethod<M extends string> = { method: M };

class RequestBuilder<U = never, M = never> {
  private constructor(private state: { url?: string; method?: string }) {}
  static create() { return new RequestBuilder<never, never>({}); }

  setUrl<V extends string>(u: V): RequestBuilder<V, M> {
    return new RequestBuilder({ ...this.state, url: u });
  }
  setMethod<V extends string>(m: V): RequestBuilder<U, V> {
    return new RequestBuilder({ ...this.state, method: m });
  }

  // only callable when both are set
  build(this: RequestBuilder<string, string>) {
    return this.state as { url: string; method: string };
  }
}
```

**Pitfall summary:** Fluent builders look elegant but `this`-returning methods erase "which fields are set." Phantom type parameters fix it, at the cost of verbosity — only use when the safety is worth it.

---

## 8. Prototype / cloning — Shallow copy leaks & lost class identity

```ts
// BAD: spread only shallow-copies; Date/Map/Set/typed arrays break
const clone = { ...original };
clone.nested.items.push(x); // mutates original.nested
clone.createdAt.setFullYear(2020); // mutates original Date
```

```ts
// BETTER: structuredClone (modern) or a typed deep-clone
const clone = structuredClone(original);

// For class instances, add a typed clone method
class User {
  constructor(public name: string, public roles: string[]) {}
  clone(): User { return new User(this.name, [...this.roles]); }
}
```

**Pitfall summary:** `Object.assign`/spread preserve prototypes but only one level deep; `structuredClone` drops methods and class identity. Match the clone strategy to the data.

---

## 9. Adapter — Leaky abstractions & type coercion

```ts
// BAD: adapter exposes underlying type, callers depend on it
class LegacyApiAdapter {
  fetchUser(id: string): LegacyUser { /*...*/ return {} as LegacyUser; }
}
// callers now import LegacyUser → adapter failed
```

```ts
// BETTER: adapter owns the translation; expose only domain types
interface User { id: string; name: string; }

class LegacyApiAdapter {
  async fetchUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    const raw = await legacyFetch(id);
    return { id: raw.user_id, name: raw.full_name };
  }
}
```

**Pitfall summary:** If the adapted type leaks into your public API, the adapter is just a rename. TS makes this easy to miss because `as` casts compile fine.

---

## 10. General TS-specific — Structural typing & nominal intent

```ts
// BAD: two "distinct" types that are structurally identical → assignable
type UserId = string;
type OrderId = string;
function getUser(id: UserId) {}
getUser('order-123' as OrderId); // compiles! no error
```

```ts
// BETTER: branded types for nominal safety
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };
type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: 'OrderId' };

const toUserId = (s: string) => s as UserId;
const toOrderId = (s: string) => s as OrderId;

function getUser(id: UserId) {}
getUser(toOrderId('x')); // compile error ✅
```

**Pitfall summary:** Design patterns (especially Factory/Repository) often introduce "ID" or "Handle" types. Without branding, TS treats them as interchangeable primitives.

---

## 11. General — Circular dependencies between pattern participants

```ts
// BAD: Subject imports Observer, Observer imports Subject → bundle cycle
// subject.ts
import { Observer } from './observer';
// observer.ts
import { Subject } from './subject';
```

```ts
// BETTER: depend on an interface defined in a third file, or invert
// events.ts
export interface DomainEvent { type: string; }
// subject.ts
import { DomainEvent } from './events';
// observer.ts
import { DomainEvent } from './events';
```

**Pitfall summary:** TS `import type` helps (`import type { X }` is erased), but runtime cycles still break module evaluation. Use `import type` for type-only references.

---

## 12. General — Type erasure breaks patterns needing runtime metadata

```ts
// BAD: reflection-based DI container reads constructor param types at runtime
@Injectable()
class Foo { constructor(private bar: Bar) {} }
// TS erases `Bar` → container can't resolve unless emitDecoratorMetadata is on
```

```ts
// BETTER: explicit registration, or codegen (e.g., tsyringe with reflect-metadata
// only if you accept the metadata cost), or just manual wiring
const container = new Container();
container.register(Bar, () => new Bar());
container.register(Foo, c => new Foo(c.resolve(Bar)));
```

**Pitfall summary:** Patterns like DI, serialization, and validation that *need* runtime type info conflict with TS's erasure. Prefer explicit registration or build-time codegen (e.g., `typia`, `io-ts`).

---

## Quick checklist

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Singleton races / testability | Prefer DI; if singleton, cache the *promise* |
| Observer leaks | Always return an unsubscribe handle |
| Factory `any` returns | Discriminated unions + `never` exhaustiveness |
| Decorator `this` loss | Arrow fields or explicit bind |
| Decorator flag mismatch | Standard decorators (TS 5+) or consistently experimental |
| Strategy over-abstraction | Function types first |
| Builder loses field tracking | Phantom types or accept runtime checks |
| Shallow clone | `structuredClone` or typed `.clone()` |
| Adapter leakage | Translate fully to domain types |
| Structural ID confusion | Branded types |
| Circular imports | `import type`, shared event interfaces |
| Runtime type needs | Explicit wiring or codegen |

The overarching rule: **TypeScript's type system is compile-time only.** Any pattern that assumes runtime type information, identity, or ordering must be verified explicitly (exhaustiveness checks, `instanceof` guards, tests) rather than trusted to the compiler.

## Exercise

Build a small **order pricing engine** in TypeScript that:

1. Calculates a final price from an order subtotal and line items.
2. Supports at least three discount kinds: **percentage off**, **fixed amount off**, and **buy X get Y free**.
3. Lets you add new discount kinds later **without editing** the pricing engine or existing discounts.
4. Can create discounts from plain config objects such as `{ type: "percentage", value: 10 }`.
5. Never returns a negative total.

Use this to practice **Strategy**, **Factory Method**, and the **Open/Closed Principle**.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Identify the patterns

- **Strategy**: each discount is an interchangeable algorithm implementing one interface.
- **Factory Method**: `createDiscount(config)` maps configuration data to concrete strategy instances.
- **Open/Closed Principle**: `PricingEngine` depends only on `DiscountStrategy`, so new strategies can be added without changing the engine.

### 2. Model the domain

We need order items for BOGO calculations and a subtotal for percentage/fixed discounts.

```ts
export interface OrderItem {
  readonly sku: string;
  readonly price: number;
  readonly quantity: number;
}

export interface OrderContext {
  readonly items: readonly OrderItem[];
  readonly subtotal: number;
}
```

### 3. Define the Strategy interface

```ts
export interface DiscountStrategy {
  readonly name: string;
  apply(context: OrderContext, currentTotal: number): number;
}
```

Each strategy receives the current running total and may use the original order context.

### 4. Implement concrete strategies

```ts
export class PercentageDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
  readonly name = "percentage";

  constructor(private readonly percent: number) {
    if (percent < 0 || percent > 100) {
      throw new Error("Percentage must be between 0 and 100");
    }
  }

  apply(_context: OrderContext, currentTotal: number): number {
    return currentTotal * (1 - this.percent / 100);
  }
}

export class FixedDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
  readonly name = "fixed";

  constructor(private readonly amount: number) {
    if (amount < 0) {
      throw new Error("Discount amount must be non-negative");
    }
  }

  apply(_context: OrderContext, currentTotal: number): number {
    return currentTotal - this.amount;
  }
}

export class BogoDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
  readonly name = "bogo";

  constructor(
    private readonly buy: number,
    private readonly get: number,
  ) {
    if (buy <= 0 || get <= 0) {
      throw new Error("Buy and get quantities must be positive");
    }
  }

  apply(context: OrderContext, currentTotal: number): number {
    let discount = 0;

    for (const item of context.items) {
      const groupSize = this.buy + this.get;
      const completeGroups = Math.floor(item.quantity / groupSize);
      const freeQuantity = completeGroups * this.get;

      discount += freeQuantity * item.price;
    }

    return currentTotal - discount;
  }
}
```

### 5. Add the Factory Method

Configuration objects are a common real-world input, for example from JSON or a database.

```ts
export type DiscountConfig =
  | { type: "percentage"; value: number }
  | { type: "fixed"; value: number }
  | { type: "bogo"; buy: number; get: number };

export function createDiscount(config: DiscountConfig): DiscountStrategy {
  switch (config.type) {
    case "percentage":
      return new PercentageDiscount(config.value);

    case "fixed":
      return new FixedDiscount(config.value);

    case "bogo":
      return new BogoDiscount(config.buy, config.get);

    default: {
      // Exhaustiveness check: TypeScript will error if a case is missing.
      const exhaustive: never = config;
      throw new Error(`Unknown discount type: ${JSON.stringify(exhaustive)}`);
    }
  }
}
```

### 6. Implement the pricing engine

The engine only knows about `DiscountStrategy`, so it is closed for modification but open for extension.

```ts
export class PricingEngine {
  constructor(
    private readonly strategies: readonly DiscountStrategy[],
    private readonly minimumTotal = 0,
  ) {}

  calculate(context: OrderContext): number {
    let total = context.subtotal;

    for (const strategy of this.strategies) {
      total = strategy.apply(context, total);
    }

    const rounded = Math.round(total * 100) / 100;
    return Math.max(this.minimumTotal, rounded);
  }
}
```

### 7. Use it

```ts
const items = [
  { sku: "BOOK", price: 20, quantity: 3 },
];

const context: OrderContext = {
  items,
  subtotal: 60,
};

const engine = new PricingEngine([
  createDiscount({ type: "percentage", value: 10 }), // 60 -> 54
  createDiscount({ type: "bogo", buy: 1, get: 1 }),  // one free book: 54 - 20 = 34
  createDiscount({ type: "fixed", value: 4 }),        // 34 - 4 = 30
]);

console.log(engine.calculate(context)); // 30
```

### 8. Add tests

Example Jest-style tests:

```ts
import {
  BogoDiscount,
  FixedDiscount,
  PercentageDiscount,
  PricingEngine,
  createDiscount,
} from "./pricing";

describe("PricingEngine", () => {
  const items = [{ sku: "PEN", price: 10, quantity: 3 }];
  const context = { items, subtotal: 30 };

  it("applies discounts in sequence", () => {
    const engine = new PricingEngine([
      new PercentageDiscount(10),
      new FixedDiscount(5),
    ]);

    expect(engine.calculate(context)).toBe(22); // 30 * 0.9 = 27; 27 - 5 = 22
  });

  it("applies BOGO discount", () => {
    const engine = new PricingEngine([
      new BogoDiscount(1, 1),
    ]);

    // Buy 1 get 1 free: one free pen among 3 pens.
    expect(engine.calculate(context)).toBe(20);
  });

  it("never returns a negative total", () => {
    const engine = new PricingEngine([
      new FixedDiscount(1000),
    ]);

    expect(engine.calculate(context)).toBe(0);
  });

  it("creates discounts from config", () => {
    const engine = new PricingEngine([
      createDiscount({ type: "percentage", value: 50 }),
    ]);

    expect(engine.calculate(context)).toBe(15);
  });
});
```

