### Bonus: Strategy pattern in 15 lines (the “replace if/else” pattern)

```ts
interface PricingStrategy {
  price(base: number): number;
}

class RegularPricing implements PricingStrategy {
  price(base: number) { return base; }
}

class VipPricing implements PricingStrategy {
  price(base: number) { return base * 0.8; }
}

class Checkout {
  constructor(private strategy: PricingStrategy) {}
  total(base: number) { return this.strategy.price(base); }
  setStrategy(s: PricingStrategy) { this.strategy = s; } // runtime swap
}

// Usage: const co = new Checkout(new VipPricing()); co.total(100); // 80
```

**Takeaway for the interview:** Name the pattern, state the problem it solves, show the contract (interface), show the concrete pieces, and mention trade-offs (indirection cost, testability, extensibility). That checklist alone puts you ahead of most candidates.

## Edge cases when using design patterns in TypeScript

Below are the most common design patterns, the TypeScript-specific edge cases they introduce, and how to handle them.

---

### 1. Singleton

**Edge cases**
- `private constructor` is only a compile-time guard; it can be bypassed with `any` or reflection.
- Subclassing can accidentally create multiple instances if you use `new this()`.
- Module caching makes the instance global to the module graph, which breaks in SSR / tests / HMR.
- `this` inside static methods refers to the class, not the instance.

```ts
// singleton.ts
export class Singleton {
  // Use a module-level variable instead of a static field for clarity
  private static instance: Singleton | undefined;

  private constructor(private readonly value: number) {}

  static getInstance(value = 0): Singleton {
    if (!this.instance) {
      // `this` is the class; subclasses could override getInstance
      this.instance = new this(value);
    }
    return this.instance;
  }

  // Test-only reset (do not expose in production builds)
  static _resetForTests(): void {
    this.instance = undefined;
  }

  getValue(): number {
    return this.value;
  }
}

// Edge case: bypassing the constructor at runtime
// const s = new (Singleton as any)(42); // compiles only with `any`
```

**Safer variant (no subclassing, no `this` ambiguity):**

```ts
class Singleton {
  private constructor() {}
  static readonly instance = new Singleton();
}
```

---

### 2. Factory Method / Abstract Factory

**Edge cases**
- Return-type inference can widen to a base type and lose discriminants.
- Missing `default` case in `switch` is not caught unless you use `never`.
- Async factories need explicit `Promise<T>` and error boundaries.
- `this` inside factory methods may refer to a subclass.

```ts
type Kind = "email" | "sms";

interface Notifier {
  send(msg: string): Promise<void>;
}

class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
  async send(msg: string) { /* ... */ }
}
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
  async send(msg: string) { /* ... */ }
}

function createNotifier(kind: Kind): Notifier {
  switch (kind) {
    case "email": return new EmailNotifier();
    case "sms":   return new SmsNotifier();
    default: {
      // Exhaustiveness check
      const _exhaustive: never = kind;
      throw new Error(`Unknown kind: ${_exhaustive}`);
    }
  }
}
```

**Async factory with typed errors:**

```ts
class FactoryError extends Error {}

async function createFromRemote(id: string): Promise<Notifier> {
  const res = await fetch(`/notifiers/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new FactoryError(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  const data = (await res.json()) as { kind: Kind };
  return createNotifier(data.kind);
}
```

---

### 3. Builder

**Edge cases**
- Fluent APIs must return `this`, but `this` can be widened to the base class in subclasses.
- `Partial<T>` + `as T` hides missing required fields.
- Mutating the same builder instance is not thread/async-safe.
- Required-field enforcement at `build()` time vs compile time.

```ts
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email?: string;
}

class UserBuilder {
  private data: Partial<User> = {};

  setId(id: string): this {
    this.data.id = id;
    return this;
  }

  setName(name: string): this {
    this.data.name = name;
    return this;
  }

  setEmail(email: string): this {
    this.data.email = email;
    return this;
  }

  build(): User {
    // Validate instead of casting
    if (!this.data.id)   throw new Error("id is required");
    if (!this.data.name) throw new Error("name is required");
    return { id: this.data.id, name: this.data.name, email: this.data.email };
  }
}
```

**Compile-time required fields (phantom types):**

```ts
type BuilderState = { id?: string; name?: string };

class TypedBuilder<S extends BuilderState = {}> {
  private data: S = {} as S;

  setId(id: string): TypedBuilder<S & { id: string }> {
    return Object.assign(new TypedBuilder(), { ...this.data, id });
  }

  setName(name: string): TypedBuilder<S & { name: string }> {
    return Object.assign(new TypedBuilder(), { ...this.data, name });
  }

  // Only callable when both id and name are present
  build(this: TypedBuilder<{ id: string; name: string }>): User {
    return this.data as User;
  }
}
```

---

### 4. Observer / Pub-Sub

**Edge cases**
- Memory leaks if listeners are not removed.
- Mutating the listener set while iterating causes skipped/duplicate calls.
- An exception in one listener can stop the rest.
- `this` binding when passing methods as callbacks.
- Sync vs async emission ordering.

```ts
type Listener<T> = (payload: T) => void;

class Emitter<T> {
  private listeners = new Set<Listener<T>>();

  on(cb: Listener<T>): () => void {
    this.listeners.add(cb);
    // Return an unsubscribe function (idiomatic cleanup)
    return () => this.listeners.delete(cb);
  }

  emit(payload: T): void {
    // Snapshot to avoid mutation-during-iteration bugs
    for (const cb of [...this.listeners]) {
      try {
        cb(payload);
      } catch (err) {
        // Isolate listener failures
        console.error("Listener threw:", err);
      }
    }
  }
}

// Edge case: `this` binding
class Handler {
  count = 0;
  handle = (p: number) => { this.count += p; }; // arrow preserves `this`
}
```

**Weak-reference variant (auto-cleanup):**

```ts
class WeakEmitter<T> {
  private listeners = new Set<WeakRef<Listener<T>>>();

  on(cb: Listener<T>): () => void {
    const ref = new WeakRef(cb);
    this.listeners.add(ref);
    return () => this.listeners.delete(ref);
  }

  emit(payload: T): void {
    for (const ref of [...this.listeners]) {
      const cb = ref.deref();
      if (!cb) this.listeners.delete(ref);
      else cb(payload);
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 5. Strategy

**Edge cases**
- Strategies as classes vs plain functions; `this` differs.
- Context must not leak strategy internals.
- Exhaustive strategy selection.
- Generic strategies and variance.

```ts
interface Strategy<TInput, TOutput> {
  execute(input: TInput): TOutput;
}

class Context<TInput, TOutput> {
  constructor(private strategy: Strategy<TInput, TOutput>) {}

  setStrategy(s: Strategy<TInput, TOutput>): void {
    this.strategy = s;
  }

  run(input: TInput): TOutput {
    return this.strategy.execute(input);
  }
}

// Function-as-strategy (no `this` issues)
type SortStrategy = (a: number, b: number) => number;

const ascending: SortStrategy = (a, b) => a - b;
const descending: SortStrategy = (a, b) => b - a;
```

**Edge case: strategy needs context state — pass it explicitly, don't bind `this`:**

```ts
interface PricingStrategy {
  price(base: number, ctx: { quantity: number }): number;
}
```

---

### 6. Decorator (wrapper pattern, not TS decorators)

**Edge cases**
- `instanceof` checks fail on wrapped objects.
- Forwarding all methods is error-prone; use `Proxy` or explicit delegation.
- Interface drift when the wrapped type changes.
- Method binding and `this`.

```ts
interface Repository {
  save(user: { id: string }): void;
  find(id: string): { id: string } | undefined;
}

class LoggingRepository implements Repository {
  constructor(private inner: Repository) {}

  save(user: { id: string }): void {
    console.log("save", user.id);
    this.inner.save(user);
  }

  find(id: string): { id: string } | undefined {
    const r = this.inner.find(id);
    console.log("find", id, r);
    return r;
  }
}

// Edge case: `instanceof Repository` is false for the wrapper.
// Use a brand or interface check instead.
```

---

### 7. Proxy (structural)

**Edge cases**
- `this` inside proxied methods refers to the proxy only if you use `Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver)`.
- Revocable proxies must handle post-revoke calls.
- Trapping `get` for functions requires `apply` trap.
- Typing `ProxyHandler` with index signatures.

```ts
const target = {
  x: 1,
  getX() { return this.x; },
};

const proxy = new Proxy(target, {
  get(t, prop, receiver) {
    // Preserve `this` by passing receiver
    return Reflect.get(t, prop, receiver);
  },
  set(t, prop, value, receiver) {
    if (prop === "x" && typeof value !== "number") {
      throw new TypeError("x must be a number");
    }
    return Reflect.set(t, prop, value, receiver);
  },
});

// Revocable
const { proxy: rp, revoke } = Proxy.revocable(target, {});
revoke();
// rp.x; // throws TypeError
```

---

### 8. Command

**Edge cases**
- Undo requires capturing prior state; mutations must be immutable or snapshotted.
- Async commands and partial failure.
- Command history unbounded memory growth.
- Serialization of commands for replay.

```ts
interface Command {
  execute(): void | Promise<void>;
  undo(): void | Promise<void>;
}

class SetValueCommand implements Command {
  constructor(
    private target: { value: number },
    private next: number,
    private prev = target.value,
  ) {}

  execute(): void { this.target.value = this.next; }
  undo(): void { this.target.value = this.prev; }
}

class History {
  private stack: Command[] = [];
  private cursor = -1;

  async run(cmd: Command): Promise<void> {
    // Drop redo branch
    this.stack = this.stack.slice(0, this.cursor + 1);
    await cmd.execute();
    this.stack.push(cmd);
    this.cursor++;
  }

  async undo(): Promise<void> {
    const cmd = this.stack[this.cursor];
    if (!cmd) return;
    await cmd.undo();
    this.cursor--;
  }
}
```

---

### 9. Cross-cutting TypeScript edge cases

#### A. `this` typing and `noImplicitThis`

```ts
class A {
  value = 10;
  // Bad: `this` is implicitly `any` under strict mode if detached
  double = function () { return this.value * 2; };

  // Good: arrow or explicit `this` param
  doubleOk = () => this.value * 2;

  method(this: A): number {
    return this.value * 2;
  }
}
```

#### B. `private` vs `#private`

```ts
class C {
  private x = 1;   // compile-time only; accessible via `any`
  #y = 2;         // runtime private; not enumerable; survives `as any`? still inaccessible
}
```

#### C. `useDefineForClassFields` (TS 3.7+ / ES2022)

```ts
class Base {
  value = 1;
}
class Derived extends Base {
  value = 2; // with useDefineForClassFields, this overwrites without calling super's setter
}
```

#### D. Strict null in pattern code

```ts
function find<T>(arr: T[], pred: (x: T) => boolean): T | undefined {
  return arr.find(pred);
}

const r = find([1, 2, 3], x => x === 2);
// r is number | undefined — handle before using
if (r === undefined) throw new Error("not found");
```

#### E. Generics and variance

```ts
interface Producer<out T> { produce(): T; }   // covariant
interface Consumer<in T> { consume(x: T): void; } // contravariant

// Edge: readonly arrays are covariant; mutable arrays are invariant
function takeStrings(xs: readonly string[]) { /* ... */ }
const mixed: (string | number)[] = ["a", 1];
// takeStrings(mixed); // error: mutable array is invariant
```

#### F. Decorators (experimental / stage-3)

```ts
// tsconfig: "experimentalDecorators": true, "emitDecoratorMetadata": true
function Log(target: any, key: string, desc: PropertyDescriptor) {
  const orig = desc.value;
  desc.value = function (...args: any[]) {
    console.log(key, args);
    return orig.apply(this, args);
  };
}

class Svc {
  @Log
  run(x: number) { return x * 2; }
}
```

**Edge cases:** metadata requires `reflect-metadata`; decorator factory ordering; `this` inside decorated method.

