## 3. Syntax you use every day

### Callbacks (old style)

```ts
import fs from "node:fs";

fs.readFile("data.txt", "utf8", (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error(err);
    return;
  }
  console.log(data);
});
```

### Promises

```ts
import fs from "node:fs/promises";

async function loadConfig(path: string): Promise<Record<string, unknown>> {
  const raw = await fs.readFile(path, "utf8");
  return JSON.parse(raw) as Record<string, unknown>;
}
```

### async/await with typed errors

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

async function getUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  return (await res.json()) as User;
}
```

### Typed event handlers

```ts
type EventMap = {
  connect: [host: string, port: number];
  message: [payload: string];
  close: [];
};

class Client {
  private listeners = new Map<keyof EventMap, Set<Function>>();

  on<K extends keyof EventMap>(
    event: K,
    cb: (...args: EventMap[K]) => void
  ): this {
    if (!this.listeners.has(event)) this.listeners.set(event, new Set());
    this.listeners.get(event)!.add(cb);
    return this;
  }

  emit<K extends keyof EventMap>(event: K, ...args: EventMap[K]): void {
    this.listeners.get(event)?.forEach((cb) => cb(...args));
  }
}

const client = new Client();
client.on("connect", (host, port) => console.log(host, port));
client.emit("connect", "localhost", 3000);
```

---

## 4. Node.js scheduling details

```ts
console.log("sync");

process.nextTick(() => console.log("nextTick"));
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("microtask"));
setTimeout(() => console.log("timeout"), 0);
setImmediate(() => console.log("immediate"));

console.log("sync end");
```

Typical output:

```
sync
sync end
nextTick
microtask
timeout   // or immediate depending on phase/context
immediate
```

Key points:

- `process.nextTick` runs before Promise microtasks in Node.
- `setTimeout(fn, 0)` and `setImmediate` are both macrotasks; their relative order can depend on whether you are in the main module or inside an I/O callback.
- Never block the loop with long synchronous work.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls

### Blocking the event loop

```ts
// Bad: blocks all other requests/timers
app.get("/heavy", (_req, res) => {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) sum += i;
  res.send(String(sum));
});
```

### Chunking work

```ts
function processInChunks(items: number[], onDone: () => void) {
  let i = 0;
  const chunkSize = 1000;

  function step() {
    const end = Math.min(i + chunkSize, items.length);
    for (; i < end; i++) {
      // process items[i]
    }
    if (i < items.length) {
      setImmediate(step); // yield to event loop
    } else {
      onDone();
    }
  }

  step();
}
```

### Unhandled rejections

```ts
process.on("unhandledRejection", (reason) => {
  console.error("Unhandled rejection:", reason);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

---

## 6. Typical use cases

### HTTP server (Node)

```ts
import http from "node:http";

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  // Each request callback is scheduled by the event loop
  res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
  res.end("ok");
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log("listening"));
```

### Debouncing UI/input (browser)

```ts
let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;

function onInput(value: string) {
  if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(() => {
    // runs after user stops typing
    search(value);
  }, 300);
}
```

### Streaming data

```ts
import { createReadStream } from "node:fs";

const stream = createReadStream("big.log");
stream.on("data", (chunk) => {
  // event loop schedules each 'data' callback
  process.stdout.write(chunk);
});
stream.on("end", () => console.log("done"));
```

### Pub/sub with typed events

```ts
import { EventEmitter } from "events";

interface AppEvents {
  "user:login": [userId: string];
  "user:logout": [userId: string, reason?: string];
}

class Bus extends EventEmitter {
  emit<E extends keyof AppEvents>(e: E, ...args: AppEvents[E]): boolean {
    return super.emit(e, ...args);
  }
  on<E extends keyof AppEvents>(e: E, cb: (...args: AppEvents[E]) => void): this {
    return super.on(e, cb);
  }
}
```

---

## 7. One-line summary

The event loop is TypeScript/JavaScript’s concurrency strategy: **run synchronous code to completion, drain microtasks, then handle the next macrotask**, all on one thread, while background APIs do the waiting.

A common event-loop mistake in TypeScript/JavaScript is using `Array.prototype.forEach` with an `async` callback and expecting the loop to “await” each iteration.

### Mistake

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

async function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve({ id, name: `User ${id}` }), 10);
  });
}

async function loadUsers(ids: number[]): Promise<User[]> {
  const users: User[] = [];

  ids.forEach(async (id) => {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    users.push(user);
  });

  console.log("Loaded", users.length); // Often prints 0
  return users; // Often returns []
}
```

### Corrected version

Option 1: sequential with `for...of`

```ts
async function loadUsers(ids: number[]): Promise<User[]> {
  const users: User[] = [];

  for (const id of ids) {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    users.push(user);
  }

  console.log("Loaded", users.length);
  return users;
}
```

Option 2: concurrent with `Promise.all`

```ts
async function loadUsers(ids: number[]): Promise<User[]> {
  const users = await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchUser(id)));

  console.log("Loaded", users.length);
  return users;
}
```

### What was wrong and why

`forEach` does not understand promises. Its callback is called synchronously for each array element, and any promise returned by an `async` callback is ignored. So this line:

```ts
ids.forEach(async (id) => {
  const user = await fetchUser(id);
  users.push(user);
});
```

does the following at runtime:

1. `forEach` synchronously schedules all callback invocations.
2. Each callback starts `fetchUser(id)`, hits `await`, and returns a promise.
3. `forEach` returns immediately after scheduling all callbacks.
4. Execution continues to:
   ```ts
   console.log("Loaded", users.length);
   return users;
   ```
   before any `fetchUser` promise has resolved.
5. Later, when the event loop processes microtasks/timers, the awaited fetches resolve and push into `users`, but too late for the already-returned array.

In other words, the async work is still happening, but it is detached from the control flow of `loadUsers`. The function does not wait for it.

Using `for...of` fixes this because `await` inside a normal loop pauses the async function itself until the promise resolves, keeping the operations in the intended order.

Using `Promise.all(ids.map(...))` fixes this by explicitly collecting all returned promises and waiting for all of them, while still allowing the fetches to run concurrently.

A good rule of thumb: in async code, use `for...of` when you need sequential awaits, and `Promise.all` when you want controlled concurrency. Avoid `forEach`, `map` without `Promise.all`, or `filter` when the callback contains `await`, unless you intentionally want fire-and-forget behavior.

# Common Event-Loop Pitfalls in TypeScript/Node.js

Below are frequent mistakes, why they matter, and idiomatic fixes. (Browser event loops share the same core ideas, but examples target Node.js.)

---

## 1. Blocking the event loop with synchronous work

CPU-heavy code prevents timers, I/O callbacks, and requests from running.

```ts
import http from "node:http";

const server = http.createServer((_req, res) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  while (Date.now() - start < 3000) {} // blocks everything
  res.end("done");
});

server.listen(3000);
```

**Fix:** move CPU work to `worker_threads`, chunk it with `setImmediate`, or use a queue/background service.

---

## 2. Assuming `await` in a loop is concurrent

Sequential awaits can make independent work much slower than necessary.

```ts
async function fetchAll(urls: string[]): Promise<Response[]> {
  const results: Response[] = [];
  for (const url of urls) {
    results.push(await fetch(url)); // one at a time
  }
  return results;
}
```

**Fix:** use `Promise.all` for independent work, or a concurrency limiter for large batches.

```ts
const results = await Promise.all(urls.map((url) => fetch(url)));
```

---

## 3. Floating promises and unhandled rejections

An async function returns a promise; if nobody awaits or catches it, failures disappear or crash the process.

```ts
async function riskyOperation(): Promise<void> {
  throw new Error("boom");
}

riskyOperation(); // floating promise
```

**Fix:** always handle returned promises.

```ts
riskyOperation().catch((err) => {
  console.error("riskyOperation failed:", err);
});
```

Also install global handlers during startup:

```ts
process.on("unhandledRejection", (reason) => {
  console.error("Unhandled rejection:", reason);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

---

## 4. Starving the event loop with `process.nextTick` or microtasks

Recursive microtask scheduling can prevent macrotasks (timers, I/O) from ever running.

```ts
function starve(): void {
  process.nextTick(starve);
}

starve();

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("This timer may never run");
}, 0);
```

**Fix:** use `setImmediate` for deferral that yields to I/O, or batch work and explicitly schedule chunks.

---

## 5. `setInterval` overlap and drift

If the callback is slower than the interval, calls stack up or overlap.

```ts
setInterval(async () => {
  await slowDatabaseQuery(); // may take longer than 100ms
}, 100);
```

**Fix:** use recursive `setTimeout` and guard against overlap.

```ts
let running = false;

async function tick(): Promise<void> {
  if (running) return;
  running = true;
  try {
    await slowDatabaseQuery();
  } finally {
    running = false;
    setTimeout(tick, 100);
  }
}

setTimeout(tick, 100);
```

---

## 6. Event-emitter listener leaks

Adding anonymous listeners repeatedly makes them impossible to remove and can trigger `MaxListenersExceededWarning`.

```ts
function subscribe(emitter: EventEmitter) {
  emitter.on("data", (value: unknown) => {
    console.log(value); // new function every call
  });
}
```

**Fix:** define a named handler and remove it when done.

```ts
function onData(value: unknown): void {
  console.log(value);
}

emitter.on("data", onData);
// later
emitter.off("data", onData);
```

---

## 7. Retaining large objects in long-lived closures

Timers, listeners, and closures keep their entire lexical scope alive.

```ts
const hugeBuffer = Buffer.alloc(100 * 1024 * 1024);

setInterval(() => {
  console.log(hugeBuffer.length); // retains hugeBuffer forever
}, 1000);
```

**Fix:** store only what is needed, clear timers, and avoid capturing large scopes.

```ts
const size = hugeBuffer.length;
const timer = setInterval(() => console.log(size), 1000);

// when finished
clearInterval(timer);
```

---

## 8. Mixing callbacks and promises incorrectly

Manual promise wrapping often resolves/rejects twice or resolves after rejecting.

```ts
function legacyRead(path: string): Promise<Buffer> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fs.readFile(path, (err, data) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      resolve(data); // missing return; may resolve after reject
    });
  });
}
```

**Fix:** return after settling, or use `util.promisify`.

```ts
import { promisify } from "node:util";
import fs from "node:fs";

const readFile = promisify(fs.readFile);
const data = await readFile("file.txt");
```

Or manually:

```ts
if (err) return reject(err);
resolve(data);
```

---

## 9. Wrong assumptions about execution order

`nextTick`, microtasks, timers, and immediates have different priorities.

```ts
setTimeout(() => console.log("timeout"), 0);
setImmediate(() => console.log("immediate"));
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("microtask"));
process.nextTick(() => console.log("nextTick"));
```

Typical order is:

```text
nextTick
microtask
timeout / immediate  // relative order can vary by context
```

**Fix:** do not rely on subtle ordering. Make dependencies explicit with `await`, queues, or events.

---

## 10. Ignoring stream backpressure

Writing faster than a stream can consume causes memory growth.

```ts
readable.on("data", (chunk) => {
  writable.write(chunk); // ignores backpressure signal
});
```

**Fix:** respect `write()` return value or use `pipe`/`pipeline`.

```ts
import { pipeline } from "node:stream/promises";

await pipeline(readable, transform, writable);
```

---

## 11. Using synchronous filesystem APIs in async paths

Synchronous calls block the entire process.

```ts
import fs from "node:fs";

app.get("/data", (_req, res) => {
  const data = fs.readFileSync("./large.json", "utf8"); // blocks
  res.send(data);
});
```

**Fix:** use async APIs.

```ts
import fs from "node:fs/promises";

app.get("/data", async (_req, res) => {
  const data = await fs.readFile("./large.json", "utf8");
  res.send(data);
});
```

---

## 12. Exiting before async work completes

`process.exit()` terminates pending timers, promises, and I/O.

```ts
setTimeout(() => console.log("too late"), 100);
process.exit(0);
```

**Fix:** let the event loop drain, or await explicit completion.

```ts
await delay(100);
// process exits naturally when nothing remains
```

---

## 13. Event emitters throwing on `"error"`

If an `"error"` event is emitted with no listener, Node throws and may crash.

```ts
const emitter = new EventEmitter();
emitter.emit("error", new Error("something failed")); // throws
```

**Fix:** always attach an error handler, or wrap emission in try/catch at boundaries.

```ts
emitter.on("error", (err) => {
  console.error("Emitter error:", err);
});
```

---

## 14. `this` binding surprises in callbacks

Passing methods as callbacks can lose `this`.

```ts
class Worker {
  name = "worker";

  start() {
    setTimeout(this.run, 0); // `this` is undefined/lost
  }

  run() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
```

**Fix:** bind explicitly or use arrow functions.

```ts
setTimeout(() => this.run(), 0);
// or
setTimeout(this.run.bind(this), 0);
```

---

## 15. No cancellation for long-running async work

Without `AbortSignal`, slow operations continue after the caller no longer cares.

```ts
async function fetchWithTimeout(url: string): Promise<Response> {
  const res = await fetch(url); // no cancellation
  return res;
}
```

**Fix:** propagate `AbortSignal`.

```ts
async function fetchWithTimeout(url: string, ms: number): Promise<Response> {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), ms);

  try {
    return await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timeout);
  }
}
```

---

## Practical checklist

- Never block the main thread with sync CPU or sync I/O.
- Always `await` or `.catch()` async results.
- Avoid recursive `process.nextTick`.
- Use recursive `setTimeout` instead of overlapping `setInterval`.
- Remove event listeners when done.
- Use `pipeline` for streams.
- Keep closures small and timers cleared.
- Handle `"error"` events.
- Use `AbortSignal` for cancellation.
- Let the process exit naturally after async work finishes.

