### Practical tips

- Prefer **constraints** (`T extends ...`) when a generic must support certain operations.
- Use **default type parameters** for ergonomic APIs (`createArray(3)` defaults to `string[]`).
- Keep generic type parameters **meaningful**; too many (`Foo<A, B, C, D>`) usually signals an over-generalized API.
- Remember generics are erased at runtime—use runtime checks (`Array.isArray`, `value != null`, etc.) when behavior depends on actual values.

# Generics in TypeScript

## The idea

Generics let you write code that works with **any type** while **preserving type information**. Instead of losing type safety by using `any`, you declare a *type parameter* — a placeholder like `T` — and the compiler tracks it through your code.

```ts
// Without generics: type info lost
function first(arr: any[]): any { return arr[0]; }

// With generics: input type flows to output type
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T { return arr[0]; }

const n = first([1, 2, 3]); // n: number
const s = first(["a", "b"]); // s: string
```

The benefits: reusability, type safety, and better IDE autocomplete.

## The syntax

A type parameter is declared in angle brackets after the function/class/interface name.

```ts
// Function
function identity<T>(value: T): T { return value; }

// Arrow function
const identity = <T>(value: T): T => value;

// Class
class Box<T> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}
  get(): T { return this.value; }
}

// Interface
interface Repository<T> {
  find(id: string): T;
  save(item: T): void;
}
```

> Tip: In `.tsx` files, write `<T,>` (trailing comma) or `<T extends unknown>` so JSX parsing doesn't get confused.

## Typical use cases

### 1. Reusable data structures
```ts
class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  push(item: T) { this.items.push(item); }
  pop(): T | undefined { return this.items.pop(); }
}

const numbers = new Stack<number>();
numbers.push(1);
```

### 2. Generic functions with multiple type params
```ts
function pair<A, B>(a: A, b: B): [A, B] {
  return [a, b];
}
const p = pair("hello", 42); // [string, number]
```

### 3. Constraints (`extends`)
Restrict what types are allowed. Useful when you need to access properties.

```ts
function longest<T extends { length: number }>(a: T, b: T): T {
  return a.length >= b.length ? a : b;
}

longest("abc", "de");     // string
longest([1, 2], [3]);      // number[]
// longest(10, 20);        // error: number has no .length
```

### 4. Default type parameters
```ts
interface ApiResponse<T = unknown> {
  data: T;
  status: number;
}

const r1: ApiResponse = { data: "anything", status: 200 };        // T = unknown
const r2: ApiResponse<User> = { data: user, status: 200 };       // T = User
```

### 5. keyof and indexed access
Common pattern: a generic function that picks a property by key.

```ts
function getProp<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { name: "Ada", age: 36 };
const name = getProp(user, "name"); // string
const age  = getProp(user, "age");  // number
// getProp(user, "email");           // error: not a key
```

### 6. Conditional types
Choose a type based on a condition.

```ts
type IsArray<T> = T extends any[] ? true : false;

type A = IsArray<string[]>; // true
type B = IsArray<number>;   // false
```

### 7. Utility types (built on generics)
TypeScript ships generic helpers you use daily:

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string; email?: string };

type PartialUser = Partial<User>;        // all optional
type RequiredUser = Required<User>;       // all required
type ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;      // readonly
type NameOnly    = Pick<User, "name">;   // { name: string }
type NoEmail     = Omit<User, "email">;  // { id, name }
type UserRecord  = Record<string, User>; // index signature
```

### 8. Generic React components (tsx)
```tsx
function List<T>({ items, render }: { items: T[]; render: (x: T) => React.ReactNode }) {
  return <ul>{items.map((x, i) => <li key={i}>{render(x)}</li>)}</ul>;
}

<List items={users} render={u => u.name} />
```

## Inference

TypeScript usually infers `T` from arguments, so you rarely annotate explicitly:

```ts
// Inferred: T = number
const doubled = [1, 2, 3].map(x => x * 2); // number[]

// Sometimes you must hint when inference fails (e.g., empty arrays)
const empty = new Array<number>(); // without <number>, T = never/any depending on settings
```

## Best practices

- **Name type params meaningfully** for public APIs: `TItem`, `TKey`, `TValue` instead of `T`, `U`, `V` when it aids readability.
- **Prefer constraints** (`extends`) over `any` — they document requirements.
- **Don't over-genericize**: if a function only ever handles `string`, don't make it `<T>`.
- **Avoid `any` escape hatches** like `<T = any>` unless truly needed; prefer `unknown` with narrowing.

## Summary

Generics are TypeScript's mechanism for **parametric polymorphism**: they let types be variables, so one implementation can serve many concrete types without sacrificing the compiler's ability to check correctness. Mastering `extends`, `keyof`, conditional types, and the built-in utility types unlocks most of TypeScript's expressive power.

## Typical mistake: accessing a property on an unconstrained generic type

### Mistaken version

```ts
function getName<T>(item: T): string {
  // TS error: Property 'name' does not exist on type 'T'.
  return item.name;
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };
console.log(getName(user));
```

### Corrected version

```ts
function getName<T extends { name: string }>(item: T): string {
  return item.name;
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };
console.log(getName(user)); // "Ada"

// Also works with objects that merely have a string `name`
console.log(getName({ name: "Book", pages: 300 }));
```

### What was wrong

In TypeScript, a generic type parameter such as `T` does **not** automatically mean “any object.” Unless you constrain it, `T` is essentially opaque: TypeScript only knows what you explicitly tell it about `T`. Therefore, `item.name` is not allowed because TypeScript cannot prove that every possible `T` has a `name` property.

### Why the fix works

Adding a constraint:

```ts
T extends { name: string }
```

tells the compiler:

> “Whatever concrete type is passed for `T`, it must be assignable to `{ name: string }`.”

So inside the function, TypeScript can safely access `item.name`, and it knows the returned value is `string`.

### A closely related pattern

For dynamic property access, use `keyof` instead of hard-coding property names:

```ts
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };

const name = getProperty(user, "name"); // string
const id = getProperty(user, "id");     // number
// getProperty(user, "missing");        // compile-time error
```

Here, `K extends keyof T` guarantees that `key` is a valid property of `obj`, and `T[K]` preserves the exact property type.

### Important note about runtime behavior

Generics are erased at runtime. TypeScript does not generate checks such as “does this object really have a string `name`?” at runtime. The constraint only protects you during type checking. If data comes from untrusted sources, such as JSON, you still need runtime validation:

```ts
function getNameSafely(value: unknown): string {
  if (
    typeof value === "object" &&
    value !== null &&
    "name" in value &&
    typeof (value as { name: unknown }).name === "string"
  ) {
    return (value as { name: string }).name;
  }
  throw new Error("Invalid object: missing string name");
}
```

In short: generics provide compile-time parametric polymorphism, but property access requires an explicit constraint so TypeScript knows which members are guaranteed to exist.

## Common pitfalls when working with generics in TypeScript

Below are the issues I see most often in real codebases, with small examples and fixes.

---

### 1. Generics disappear at runtime

TypeScript generics are erased during compilation. You cannot branch, compare, or inspect them at runtime.

```ts
function isT<T>(x: unknown): x is T {
  // ❌ Error: 'T' only refers to a type, but is being used as a value
  return typeof x === T;
}
```

**Fix:** use runtime checks, type guards, or overloads.

```ts
function isString(x: unknown): x is string {
  return typeof x === "string";
}
```

---

### 2. Inference falls back to `unknown` (or fails entirely)

When a type parameter is not used in a position TypeScript can infer from, callers get surprising types.

```ts
function wrap<T>(value?: T) {
  return { value };
}

const a = wrap();        // T = unknown
const b = wrap(123);     // T = number
```

**Fix:** provide defaults and constraints, or require the argument.

```ts
function wrap<T = string>(value: T) {
  return { value };
}
```

---

### 3. Literal types get widened

A generic inferred from a variable often becomes the base type, not the literal.

```ts
function identity<T>(x: T): T {
  return x;
}

const s = "hello";          // type: string
const r = identity(s);      // T = string, not "hello"

const r2 = identity("hello"); // T = "hello" here, because the literal is passed directly
```

**Fix:** use `as const`, or TS 5.0+ `const` type parameters.

```ts
function identity<const T>(x: T): T {
  return x;
}

const s = "hello";
const r = identity(s); // T = "hello"
```

---

### 4. Distributive conditional types

A naked type variable in a conditional type distributes over unions.

```ts
type IsString<T> = T extends string ? "yes" : "no";

type R = IsString<string | number>;
// R = "yes" | "no"  (distributed)
```

**Fix:** wrap both sides in tuples to disable distribution.

```ts
type IsString<T> = [T] extends [string] ? "yes" : "no";

type R = IsString<string | number>;
// R = "no"
```

---

### 5. Mutable arrays are unsoundly covariant

TypeScript allows assigning `string[]` to `(string | number)[]`, which can corrupt data at runtime.

```ts
const strs: string[] = ["a", "b"];
const mixed: (string | number)[] = strs; // allowed

mixed.push(42); // runtime: strs now contains a number
```

**Fix:** avoid mutable shared arrays across different element types, or use readonly arrays.

```ts
const strs: readonly string[] = ["a", "b"];
const mixed: readonly (string | number)[] = strs; // safe-ish, but still be careful
```

Also enable `strictFunctionTypes` to catch contravariance issues in callbacks.

---

### 6. Overly loose constraints (`extends any` / `extends unknown`)

`any` as a constraint effectively disables checking.

```ts
function getLength<T extends any>(x: T) {
  return x.length; // no error, but T may be anything
}
```

**Fix:** constrain to the shape you actually need.

```ts
function getLength<T extends { length: number }>(x: T) {
  return x.length;
}
```

---

### 7. Over-genericizing simple APIs

Not everything needs a type parameter. Excessive generics reduce readability and create inference puzzles.

```ts
// ❌ Over-engineered
function first<TArray extends readonly unknown[]>(arr: TArray): TArray[number] | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}

// ✅ Simpler and equally safe
function first<T>(arr: readonly T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}
```

---

### 8. Inference conflicts from multiple arguments

When the same type parameter is inferred from multiple arguments, TypeScript may widen unexpectedly.

```ts
function choose<T>(a: T, b: T): T {
  return Math.random() > 0.5 ? a : b;
}

const r = choose("hello", 42); // T = string | number
```

**Fix:** use overloads or separate type parameters when arguments should not be unified.

```ts
function choose<T, U>(a: T, b: U): T | U {
  return Math.random() > 0.5 ? a : b;
}
```

---

### 9. Recursive / deeply nested generics hit compiler limits

Complex recursive types can fail with “Type instantiation is excessively deep and possibly infinite.”

```ts
type DeepPartial<T> = T extends object
  ? { [K in keyof T]?: DeepPartial<T[K]> }
  : T;

// Deeply nested input can trigger depth errors
type Huge = DeepPartial<SomeVeryDeepType>;
```

**Fix:** limit recursion depth, avoid unnecessary recursion, or restructure types.

```ts
type DeepPartial<T, Depth extends number = 5> =
  Depth extends 0
    ? T
    : T extends object
      ? { [K in keyof T]?: DeepPartial<T[K], Prev<Depth>> }
      : T;
```

---

### 10. `keyof` + indexing and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`

Generic indexing often becomes `T[K]`, but with `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` even known keys can include `undefined` in some shapes.

```ts
function get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key]; // okay for exact key, but
}

function fromRecord(obj: Record<string, number>, key: string) {
  const v = obj[key]; // number | undefined under noUncheckedIndexedAccess
  return v.toFixed(2); // ❌ possibly undefined
}
```

**Fix:** narrow or assert after a check.

```ts
function fromRecord(obj: Record<string, number>, key: string) {
  const v = obj[key];
  if (v === undefined) throw new Error("missing");
  return v.toFixed(2);
}
```

---

### 11. Default type parameters do not override inference

Defaults are only used when TypeScript cannot infer the parameter.

```ts
function make<T = string>(value?: T) {
  return value;
}

make();       // T = string
make(123);    // T = number (default ignored)
```

Also, defaults must come after non-default parameters.

**Fix:** if you need a default regardless of input, use overloads or a separate factory.

---

### 12. Generic classes require explicit type arguments when inference is weak

```ts
class Box<T> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}
}

const b = new Box(); // ❌ T inferred as unknown
const c = new Box<number>(42); // ✅
```

**Fix:** provide a factory function or default.

```ts
class Box<T = unknown> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}
}

function box<T>(value: T) {
  return new Box(value);
}
```

---

### 13. Higher-order functions lose overloads and contextual typing

Wrapping generic functions often erases overload sets.

```ts
function withLogging<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: T) {
  return (...args: Parameters<T>) => {
    console.log("calling");
    return fn(...args);
  };
}

function parse(x: string): number;
function parse(x: number): string;
function parse(x: any) { return x; }

const wrapped = withLogging(parse);
// wrapped may lose overloads and be typed as (...args: any[]) => any
```

**Fix:** preserve return/param types carefully, or avoid generic wrappers around overloaded APIs.

---

### 14. Using `as` instead of generics

Type assertions bypass the type system and can hide generic mistakes.

```ts
const data = fetchSomething() as User[]; // lies if runtime differs

// Better: validate or use a generic parser
function parseArray<T>(raw: unknown, guard: (x: unknown) => x is T): T[] {
  if (!Array.isArray(raw)) throw new Error("not array");
  return raw.filter(guard);
}
```

---

### 15. Mapping over non-object types

Mapped types over primitives iterate their properties/methods, producing huge or useless types.

```ts
type Wrap<T> = { [K in keyof T]: { value: T[K] } };

type W = Wrap<string>;
// Wraps String's methods/properties, not a single value
```

**Fix:** guard with a conditional.

```ts
type Wrap<T> = T extends object
  ? { [K in keyof T]: { value: T[K] } }
  : T;
```

---

### 16. Confusing constraint `extends` with identity

`T extends U` means “T is assignable to U,” not “T equals U.”

```ts
function f<T extends string>(x: T) {
  // T could be "hello", "world", or string
}
```

If you need exact literal equality, use conditional types or branded types.

```ts
type Exact<T, U> = [T] extends [U]
  ? [U] extends [T]
    ? true
    : false
  : false;
```

---

### Quick checklist

- Don’t use generics at runtime.
- Prefer concrete constraints over `any`/`unknown`.
- Watch literal widening; use `as const` or `const T`.
- Remember conditional types distribute unless wrapped in tuples.
- Be careful with mutable arrays and variance.
- Avoid unnecessary recursion and over-generic APIs.
- Enable `strictFunctionTypes`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, and `strict`.

If you want, I can turn any one of these into a deeper, project-specific guideline (for example, for React props, API clients, or state machines).

## Exercise

Build a generic in-memory repository for entities that have an `id`.

Requirements:

- `T` must extend `{ id: ID }`.
- `ID` defaults to `number`.
- Support `all()`, `find(id)`, `create(itemWithoutId)`, `save(item)`, `remove(id)`, and `where(key, value)`.
- `where` must be type-safe: `key` must be a key of `T`, and `value` must match that key’s value type.
- Creating an item should auto-generate an `id`.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Constrain `T` to entities

We need every stored item to have an `id`, so constrain `T`:

```ts
T extends { id: ID }
```

This prevents using the repository with types that do not have an `id`.

### 2. Give `ID` a default and a constraint

Most entities use numeric ids, but still allow the caller to choose:

```ts
ID extends number = number
```

This keeps auto-generation simple while remaining generic.

### 3. Use `keyof T` and indexed access in `where`

```ts
where<K extends keyof T>(key: K, value: T[K]): T[]
```

- `K extends keyof T` ensures only real property names are accepted.
- `T[K]` ensures the value type matches the selected property.

### 4. Use `Omit<T, "id">` for creation

When creating an item, the caller should not provide `id`:

```ts
create(item: Omit<T, "id">): T
```

### 5. Auto-generate ids internally

Keep `nextId` as `ID` and cast carefully because TypeScript cannot prove that `number` is exactly `ID`.

---

## Final solution

```ts
class InMemoryRepository<T extends { id: ID }, ID extends number = number> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  private nextId: ID = 1 as ID;

  constructor(initial: readonly T[] = []) {
    this.items = [...initial];
  }

  all(): readonly T[] {
    return this.items;
  }

  find(id: ID): T | undefined {
    return this.items.find((item) => item.id === id);
  }

  create(item: Omit<T, "id">): T {
    const id = this.nextId;
    this.nextId = (this.nextId + 1) as ID;

    const created = { ...item, id } as T;
    this.items.push(created);

    return created;
  }

  save(item: T): T {
    const index = this.items.findIndex((existing) => existing.id === item.id);

    if (index === -1) {
      this.items.push(item);
    } else {
      this.items[index] = item;
    }

    return item;
  }

  remove(id: ID): boolean {
    const index = this.items.findIndex((item) => item.id === id);

    if (index === -1) {
      return false;
    }

    this.items.splice(index, 1);
    return true;
  }

  where<K extends keyof T>(key: K, value: T[K]): T[] {
    return this.items.filter((item) => item[key] === value);
  }
}
```

### Usage example

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  role: "admin" | "user";
  active: boolean;
}

const users = new InMemoryRepository<User>();

const alice = users.create({
  name: "Alice",
  role: "admin",
  active: true,
});

users.save({ ...alice, active: false });

const admins = users.where("role", "admin");
const aliceAgain = users.find(alice.id);

// Type errors:
// users.where("role", "guest");     // "guest" is not assignable to "admin" | "user"
// users.find("1");                 // string is not assignable to number
// users.create({ name: "Bob" });    // missing "role" and "active"
```

### Bonus: a small generic helper

```ts
function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  key: K
): T[K][] {
  return items.map((item) => item[key]);
}

const names = pluck(users.all(), "name"); // string[]
```

This exercise demonstrates the core TypeScript generic tools you will use most often: **constraints**, **defaults**, **`keyof`**, **indexed access types**, and **`Omit`**.

## Generics in TypeScript (interview-style)

**What they are.** Generics let you write code that is *parameterized by a type* instead of hard-coding a single type. Think of a type parameter like a function parameter, but for types: `function identity<T>(x: T): T` says “for any type `T`, this function takes a `T` and returns a `T`.” The compiler checks the call site and substitutes `T` with the concrete type.

**Why they matter.**
- **Reusability without `any`:** one implementation works for `number`, `string`, `User`, etc., while keeping full type safety.
- **Inference:** TS usually infers `T` from arguments, so callers rarely annotate.
- **Constraints:** `T extends Foo` restricts acceptable types.
- **Defaults:** `T = string` makes parameters optional.
- **Variance:** TS checks assignability at use sites (covariance/contravariance) for function params/returns.

**Common shapes.**
- Generic functions: `function map<T, U>(arr: T[], fn: (x: T) => U): U[]`
- Generic classes: `class Stack<T> { ... }`
- Generic interfaces: `interface Repository<T> { find(id: string): T | null }`
- Utility types built on generics: `Partial<T>`, `Pick<T, K>`, `ReturnType<F>`.

**Pitfalls to mention.**
- Over-generic APIs become hard to read; prefer concrete types when only one is needed.
- `T extends object` vs `T extends {}` vs `unknown` have subtle differences.
- Inference can fail with complex overloads; sometimes explicit type args are needed.

---

## Implementing the “core” of generics

Below is a compact but complete showcase: a generic `Result` (error handling), a generic `Stack`, constrained generics, defaults, inference, and a mapped utility. All code is valid TypeScript.

```ts
// ============================================================
// 1. Generic function with inference
// ============================================================
function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}

// T is inferred as number; result type is number
const n = identity(42);

// ============================================================
// 2. Generic class: a type-safe stack
// ============================================================
class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  push(item: T): void {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  pop(): T | undefined {
    return this.items.pop();
  }

  peek(): T | undefined {
    return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
  }

  get size(): number {
    return this.items.length;
  }

  toArray(): T[] {
    return [...this.items];
  }
}

const numStack = new Stack<number>();
numStack.push(1);
// numStack.push("x"); // ❌ compile error: string not assignable to number

// ============================================================
// 3. Constraints: T must have an id
// ============================================================
interface HasId {
  id: string;
}

function getById<T extends HasId>(items: T[], id: string): T | undefined {
  return items.find((item) => item.id === id);
}

interface User { id: string; name: string; }
const users: User[] = [{ id: "1", name: "Ada" }];
const found = getById(users, "1"); // T inferred as User; found: User | undefined

// ============================================================
// 4. Default type parameter
// ============================================================
type ApiResult<T = unknown> = {
  data: T;
  status: number;
};

const r1: ApiResult = { data: "anything", status: 200 };        // T = unknown
const r2: ApiResult<number> = { data: 42, status: 200 };

// ============================================================
// 5. Generic Result type (Either-like) with narrowing
// ============================================================
type Result<T, E = Error> =
  | { ok: true; value: T }
  | { ok: false; error: E };

function ok<T>(value: T): Result<T, never> {
  return { ok: true, value };
}

function err<E>(error: E): Result<never, E> {
  return { ok: false, error };
}

function unwrap<T, E>(r: Result<T, E>): T {
  if (!r.ok) throw r.error;
  return r.value; // TS narrows: r is { ok: true; value: T }
}

// Usage
const parsed = ok(123);
console.log(unwrap(parsed)); // 123

// ============================================================
// 6. Generic utility: map with type transformation
// ============================================================
function mapArray<T, U>(arr: readonly T[], fn: (item: T, index: number) => U): U[] {
  const out: U[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) out.push(fn(arr[i], i));
  return out;
}

const lengths = mapArray(["a", "bb"], (s) => s.length); // number[]

// ============================================================
// 7. Conditional + mapped types (advanced core)
// ============================================================
type ReadonlyDeep<T> =
  T extends (infer U)[]
    ? ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyDeep<U>>
    : T extends object
      ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: ReadonlyDeep<T[K]> }
      : T;

type UserDTO = { name: string; tags: string[] };
type FrozenUser = ReadonlyDeep<UserDTO>;
// FrozenUser: { readonly name: string; readonly tags: readonly string[] }

// ============================================================
// 8. Generic interface + dependency injection style
// ============================================================
interface Repository<T extends HasId> {
  findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  save(entity: T): Promise<void>;
}

class InMemoryRepo<T extends HasId> implements Repository<T> {
  private store = new Map<string, T>();

  async findById(id: string): Promise<T | null> {
    return this.store.get(id) ?? null;
  }

  async save(entity: T): Promise<void> {
    this.store.set(entity.id, entity);
  }
}

// ============================================================
// 9. Variance note (function params are contravariant in TS)
// ============================================================
type Handler<T> = (x: T) => void;

// A handler for `Animal` can be used where `Dog` handler expected? (contravariance)
interface Animal { name: string; }
interface Dog extends Animal { bark(): void; }

declare const hAnimal: Handler<Animal>;
// const hDog: Handler<Dog> = hAnimal; // ✅ allowed (contravariant params)

// ============================================================
// 10. Exhaustiveness with generics + discriminated unions
// ============================================================
type Shape =
  | { kind: "circle"; radius: number }
  | { kind: "rect"; w: number; h: number };

function area<T extends Shape>(s: T): number {
  switch (s.kind) {
    case "circle": return Math.PI * s.radius ** 2;
    case "rect":   return s.w * s.h;
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = s;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

### Key takeaways to say out loud in an interview
1. **Generics = type parameters**, resolved at compile time (erased at runtime).
2. **Inference first**: let TS infer `T`; annotate only when inference fails or for public APIs.
3. **Constraints (`extends`)** express “T must support these members” without losing the concrete type.
4. **Defaults** reduce boilerplate for common cases.
5. **Conditional/mapped types** are where generics become a small type-level programming language—powerful but should stay readable.

If you want, I can zoom in on any one piece (e.g., implement a type-safe `pipe`/`compose`, or a mini type checker for a DSL).

# Edge Cases When Using Generics in TypeScript

Below is a categorized, thorough treatment. Each snippet is meant to compile under `strict: true`.

---

## 1. Inference fails → you must pass type arguments explicitly

TypeScript infers `T` from arguments. When there are no arguments, or the inference site is ambiguous, `T` becomes `unknown` (or the constraint).

```ts
function createBox<T>(): { value: T } {
  return { value: undefined as unknown as T }; // unsafe cast needed
}

// T inferred as unknown → value is unknown
const box = createBox();
// box.value.foo(); // Error

// Fix: explicit type argument
const box2 = createBox<number>();
box2.value.toFixed(2);
```

**Edge case:** multiple type params where only one is inferable.

```ts
function pair<A, B>(a: A): [A, B] {
  return [a, undefined as unknown as B];
}
// B cannot be inferred
const p = pair<number, string>(1); // must specify both
```

---

## 2. Constraints (`extends`) and `keyof` interactions

```ts
function getProp<T extends object, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const u = { id: 1, name: "a" };
getProp(u, "id");        // number
getProp(u, "missing");   // compile error ✓
```

**Edge case:** constraint too loose → `keyof T` becomes `string | number | symbol`.

```ts
// Bad: T extends {} allows primitives' wrappers; keyof is wide
function bad<T extends {}>(x: T, k: keyof T) { return x[k]; }
bad("hi", "length"); // works but T is string, weird

// Better: constrain to Record
function good<T extends Record<string, unknown>>(x: T, k: keyof T) { return x[k]; }
```

**Edge case:** `keyof` on optional/readonly properties.

```ts
type O = { a?: number; readonly b: string };
type K = keyof O; // "a" | "b"  (optional/readonly erased from key set)
```

---

## 3. Default type parameters

```ts
type Response<T = unknown> = { data: T; status: number };
type R1 = Response;        // data: unknown
type R2 = Response<string>; // data: string
```

**Edge case:** defaults can reference earlier params.

```ts
type Pair<A, B = A> = [A, B];
type P = Pair<number>; // [number, number]
```

**Edge case:** a default that depends on a constraint.

```ts
function f<T extends string = "default">(x?: T): T { return (x ?? "default") as T; }
```

---

## 4. Variance: generics are invariant by default in many positions

```ts
type Box<T> = { value: T };

// Invariance: Box<Dog> is NOT assignable to Box<Animal> nor vice versa
// when T appears in both read and write positions.

interface Animal { name: string }
interface Dog extends Animal { bark(): void }

const dogBox: Box<Dog> = { value: { name: "r", bark() {} } };
// const animalBox: Box<Animal> = dogBox; // Error (invariant)
```

**Covariance** (read-only) vs **contravariance** (write-only):

```ts
type ReadonlyBox<T> = { readonly value: T };        // covariant
type Writer<T> = { write(v: T): void };             // contravariant

const w: Writer<Animal> = { write(a) {} };
const wDog: Writer<Dog> = w; // OK: contravariant allows wider input
```

**Edge case:** variance of generic functions.

```ts
type Fn<T> = (x: T) => T; // invariant
type Producer<T> = () => T; // covariant
type Consumer<T> = (x: T) => void; // contravariant
```

---

## 5. Distribution over unions (conditional types)

```ts
type IsString<T> = T extends string ? "yes" : "no";
type R = IsString<string | number>; // "yes" | "no"  (distributed!)
```

**Edge case:** prevent distribution with `[T]`.

```ts
type IsString2<T> = [T] extends [string] ? "yes" : "no";
type R2 = IsString2<string | number>; // "no"
```

**Edge case:** `never` distributes to `never`.

```ts
type F<T> = T extends string ? T : never;
type R3 = F<never>; // never (not "never extends string ? ...")
```

---

## 6. `infer` and its pitfalls

```ts
type ReturnTypeOf<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;

type R = ReturnTypeOf<() => number>; // number
```

**Edge case:** multiple `infer` capture the last/union depending on position.

```ts
type First<T> = T extends (x: infer A, ...rest: any[]) => any ? A : never;
type Last<T> = T extends (...args: [...any[], infer L]) => any ? L : never;
```

**Edge case:** `infer` in template literals.

```ts
type ExtractId<T> = T extends `user-${infer Id}` ? Id : never;
type I = ExtractId<"user-42">; // "42"
```

**Edge case:** `infer` with constraints (TS 4.7+).

```ts
type GetNum<T> = T extends (x: any) => infer R extends number ? R : never;
```

---

## 7. Mapped types + generics

```ts
type ReadonlyDeep<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] extends object ? ReadonlyDeep<T[K]> : T[K];
};

type M = ReadonlyDeep<{ a: { b: number } }>;
```

**Edge case:** mapped type over `never`/`unknown`.

```ts
type Boxed<T> = { [K in keyof T]: { value: T[K] } };
type B = Boxed<never>; // never (mapped over never → never)
type B2 = Boxed<any>;  // { [x: string]: { value: any } }? actually any
```

**Edge case:** `as` key remapping (TS 4.1+).

```ts
type Getters<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};
type G = Getters<{ name: string; age: number }>;
// { getName: () => string; getAge: () => number }
```

**Edge case:** filtering keys with `never`.

```ts
type PickByType<T, U> = {
  [K in keyof T as T[K] extends U ? K : never]: T[K];
};
```

---

## 8. Generic classes: `this` typing and static members

```ts
class Container<T> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}
  map<U>(fn: (v: T) => U): Container<U> {
    return new Container(fn(this.value));
  }
  // `this` polymorphism
  clone(): this {
    return new (this.constructor as any)(this.value);
  }
}

const c = new Container(5).map(String); // Container<string>
```

**Edge case:** static members cannot reference class type params.

```ts
class Foo<T> {
  static default: Foo<any> = new Foo<any>(null as any); // must use any/unknown
}
```

**Edge case:** generic base classes and `super`.

```ts
class Base<T> { constructor(public x: T) {} }
class Derived<U> extends Base<U[]> {
  constructor(x: U[]) { super(x); }
}
```

---

## 9. Type narrowing doesn't flow through generics well

```ts
function isString<T>(x: T): x is T & string {
  return typeof x === "string";
}

function f<T>(x: T) {
  if (isString(x)) {
    x.toUpperCase(); // OK due to type predicate
  }
}
```

**Edge case:** `typeof` checks on generic `T` don't narrow `T`.

```ts
function g<T>(x: T) {
  if (typeof x === "string") {
    // x is T & string here, but T itself is NOT narrowed
    const y: T = x; // OK
    // x = "a"; // still error if T is not assignable from string
  }
}
```

**Edge case:** `Array.isArray` on generic.

```ts
function h<T>(x: T | T[]) {
  if (Array.isArray(x)) {
    x.push(1 as any); // x is T[] but T unknown
  }
}
```

---

## 10. `Object.keys` / index access typing

```ts
function keys<T extends object>(o: T): (keyof T)[] {
  return Object.keys(o) as (keyof T)[];
}

function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(o: T, k: K): T[K] {
  return o[k];
}
```

**Edge case:** indexing with arbitrary string.

```ts
function get<T>(o: T, k: string): unknown {
  return (o as any)[k]; // cast needed; T[k] invalid for arbitrary k
}
```

---

## 11. Runtime erasure: no generic info at runtime

```ts
function identity<T>(x: T): T { return x; }
// At runtime identity is just (x) => x. No T.

// Cannot do:
// if (T === number) ...  // compile error: T is type, not value
```

**Edge case:** `instanceof` with generic type params.

```ts
class Box<T> {}
function isBox(x: unknown): x is Box<unknown> {
  return x instanceof Box; // T erased; use Box<unknown>
}
```

---

## 12. Overloads + generics

```ts
function parse<T extends "json" | "text">(format: T): T extends "json" ? object : string;
function parse(format: string): unknown;
function parse(format: string): unknown {
  return format === "json" ? {} : "";
}
```

**Edge case:** overload resolution with generic inference can pick wrong overload. Order matters: most specific first.

---

## 13. Recursive generics & depth limits

```ts
type Json = string | number | boolean | null | Json[] | { [k: string]: Json };

type DeepReadonly<T> = T extends (infer U)[]
  ? DeepReadonly<U>[]
  : T extends object
  ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
  : T;
```

**Edge case:** deeply nested types hit "Type instantiation is excessively deep" error. Mitigate with conditional base cases / `NoInfer`.

---

## 14. `NoInfer<T>` (TS 5.4+) to control inference

```ts
function create<T>(initial: T, fallback: NoInfer<T>): T {
  return initial ?? fallback;
}
create(5, "x"); // Error: "x" not assignable to number
```

Previously you'd need overloads or explicit params.

---

## 15. Generic tuples and rest elements

```ts
function zip<A, B>(a: A[], b: B[]): [A, B][] {
  return a.map((x, i) => [x, b[i]]);
}

// Variadic tuple inference
function concat<T extends any[]>(...args: T): T {
  return args; // simplified
}
```

**Edge case:** readonly tuples.

```ts
type R = readonly [number, string];
function f<T extends readonly any[]>(x: T): T { return x; }
f([1, "a"] as const); // readonly [1, "a"]
```

---

## 16. `as const` + generics

```ts
function makeRoute<T extends string>(path: T) {
  return { path } as const;
}
const r = makeRoute("/home"); // { readonly path: "/home" } (literal preserved due to T extends string)
```

Without constraint, `T` widens to `string`.

---

## 17. Excess property checks & generics

```ts
function wrap<T>(x: T): { value: T } { return { value: x }; }
wrap({ a: 1, b: 2 }); // T inferred as { a: number; b: number }, no excess check issue

// But:
function accept<T extends { id: number }>(x: T) {}
accept({ id: 1, extra: 2 }); // OK (excess check only on fresh object literals to non-generic targets)
```

Excess property checking is skipped when target is generic → typos can slip through.

---

## 18. `satisfies` with generics

```ts
type Config<T> = { value: T };
const c = { value: 5 } satisfies Config<number>; // validates without widening
```

---

## 19. Generic async / Promise unwrapping

```ts
type Awaited<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? Awaited<U> : T;
type A = Awaited<Promise<Promise<number>>>; // number
```

**Edge case:** `Promise<any>` / `Promise<unknown>`.

```ts
async function f<T>(p: Promise<T>): Promise<T> { return p; }
```

---

## 20. Empty object type `{}` as constraint

```ts
// {} means "any non-nullish value" — dangerous
function foo<T extends {}>(x: T) {}
foo(0); foo(""); foo(true); // all OK! (primitives allowed)
foo(null); // error
```

Prefer `object` or `Record<string, unknown>`.

---

## 21. Generic type guards

```ts
function isArrayOf<T>(x: unknown, guard: (v: unknown) => v is T): x is T[] {
  return Array.isArray(x) && x.every(guard);
}
```

---

## 22. `keyof` + index signatures

```ts
type Dict<V> = { [k: string]: V };
type K = keyof Dict<number>; // string | number (numeric index also)
```

**Edge case:** `keyof` on `unknown`/`any`.

```ts
type K1 = keyof unknown; // never
type K2 = keyof any;     // string | number | symbol
```

---

## 23. Contravariant generics in event handlers

```ts
type Handler<E> = (e: E) => void;
// Handler<MouseEvent> assignable to Handler<Event> (contravariant input)
```

---

## 24. Generic enums / const assertions

```ts
const Status = { Ok: 0, Err: 1 } as const;
type Status = (typeof Status)[keyof typeof Status]; // 0 | 1
```

---

## 25. `this` parameters in generic methods

```ts
class Foo {
  method<T>(this: Foo, x: T): T { return x; }
}
```

---

## Summary checklist

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Inference fails | Pass explicit `<T>` |
| Loose constraint (`{}`) | Use `object` / `Record` |
| Invariance breaks assignment | Use `readonly`/function types for covariance |
| Union distribution surprises | Wrap in `[T]` |
| `infer` ambiguity | Use constraints / multiple infers carefully |
| Runtime erasure | Don't branch on `T`; use overloads/guards |
| Excess props skipped | Add explicit constraints or validate |
| Deep recursion limits | Base cases, `NoInfer` |
| Mapped over `never`/`any` | Handle specially |
| `Object.keys` typing | Cast via `keyof` |

These cover the most common and subtle generic edge cases in production TypeScript.

Below is a complete, runnable TypeScript implementation of a graph with common traversals and graph algorithms. It uses an adjacency-list representation, supports directed/undirected and weighted edges, and includes BFS, DFS (recursive and iterative), connected components, topological sorting, and Dijkstra’s shortest path.

### `graph.ts`

```ts
type Edge<V> = {
  to: V;
  weight?: number;
};

class Graph<V = string> {
  private adjacency = new Map<V, Edge<V>[]>();
  private directed: boolean;

  constructor(directed = false) {
    this.directed = directed;
  }

  // ---------- basic operations ----------

  addVertex(vertex: V): this {
    if (!this.adjacency.has(vertex)) {
      this.adjacency.set(vertex, []);
    }
    return this;
  }

  addEdge(from: V, to: V, weight?: number): this {
    this.addVertex(from);
    this.addVertex(to);

    this.adjacency.get(from)!.push({ to, weight });

    if (!this.directed) {
      this.adjacency.get(to)!.push({ to: from, weight });
    }

    return this;
  }

  removeVertex(vertex: V): this {
    this.adjacency.delete(vertex);

    for (const [from, edges] of this.adjacency) {
      this.adjacency.set(
        from,
        edges.filter((edge) => edge.to !== vertex)
      );
    }

    return this;
  }

  removeEdge(from: V, to: V): this {
    const fromEdges = this.adjacency.get(from);
    if (fromEdges) {
      this.adjacency.set(
        from,
        fromEdges.filter((edge) => edge.to !== to)
      );
    }

    if (!this.directed) {
      const toEdges = this.adjacency.get(to);
      if (toEdges) {
        this.adjacency.set(
          to,
          toEdges.filter((edge) => edge.to !== from)
        );
      }
    }

    return this;
  }

  hasVertex(vertex: V): boolean {
    return this.adjacency.has(vertex);
  }

  hasEdge(from: V, to: V): boolean {
    const edges = this.adjacency.get(from);
    return !!edges && edges.some((edge) => edge.to === to);
  }

  neighbors(vertex: V): V[] {
    return (this.adjacency.get(vertex) ?? []).map((edge) => edge.to);
  }

  vertices(): V[] {
    return [...this.adjacency.keys()];
  }

  edges(): Array<{ from: V; to: V; weight?: number }> {
    const seen = new Set<string>();
    const result: Array<{ from: V; to: V; weight?: number }> = [];

    for (const [from, edgeList] of this.adjacency) {
      for (const { to, weight } of edgeList) {
        if (!this.directed) {
          const key = [String(from), String(to)].sort().join("|");
          if (seen.has(key)) continue;
          seen.add(key);
        }
        result.push({ from, to, weight });
      }
    }

    return result;
  }

  // ---------- traversals ----------

  bfs(start: V, visit?: (vertex: V) => void): V[] {
    this.ensureVertex(start);

    const visited = new Set<V>();
    const order: V[] = [];
    const queue: V[] = [start];
    let head = 0;

    visited.add(start);

    while (head < queue.length) {
      const vertex = queue[head++];
      order.push(vertex);
      visit?.(vertex);

      for (const { to } of this.adjacency.get(vertex) ?? []) {
        if (!visited.has(to)) {
          visited.add(to);
          queue.push(to);
        }
      }
    }

    return order;
  }

  dfs(start: V, visit?: (vertex: V) => void): V[] {
    this.ensureVertex(start);

    const visited = new Set<V>();
    const order: V[] = [];

    const walk = (vertex: V): void => {
      if (visited.has(vertex)) return;

      visited.add(vertex);
      order.push(vertex);
      visit?.(vertex);

      for (const { to } of this.adjacency.get(vertex) ?? []) {
        walk(to);
      }
    };

    walk(start);
    return order;
  }

  dfsIterative(start: V, visit?: (vertex: V) => void): V[] {
    this.ensureVertex(start);

    const visited = new Set<V>();
    const order: V[] = [];
    const stack: V[] = [start];

    visited.add(start);

    while (stack.length > 0) {
      const vertex = stack.pop()!;
      order.push(vertex);
      visit?.(vertex);

      const neighbors = this.adjacency.get(vertex) ?? [];

      // Push in reverse order so the first neighbor is processed first.
      for (let i = neighbors.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        const to = neighbors[i].to;
        if (!visited.has(to)) {
          visited.add(to);
          stack.push(to);
        }
      }
    }

    return order;
  }

  connectedComponents(): V[][] {
    const globalVisited = new Set<V>();
    const components: V[][] = [];

    for (const vertex of this.vertices()) {
      if (globalVisited.has(vertex)) continue;

      const component = this.dfs(vertex);
      component.forEach((v) => globalVisited.add(v));
      components.push(component);
    }

    return components;
  }

  // ---------- DAG / weighted graph algorithms ----------

  topologicalSort(): V[] {
    if (!this.directed) {
      throw new Error("Topological sort requires a directed graph");
    }

    const indegree = new Map<V, number>();
    for (const vertex of this.vertices()) {
      indegree.set(vertex, 0);
    }

    for (const { to } of this.edges()) {
      indegree.set(to, (indegree.get(to) ?? 0) + 1);
    }

    const queue: V[] = [];
    for (const [vertex, degree] of indegree) {
      if (degree === 0) queue.push(vertex);
    }

    const order: V[] = [];

    while (queue.length > 0) {
      const vertex = queue.shift()!;
      order.push(vertex);

      for (const { to } of this.adjacency.get(vertex) ?? []) {
        const nextDegree = (indegree.get(to) ?? 0) - 1;
        indegree.set(to, nextDegree);

        if (nextDegree === 0) {
          queue.push(to);
        }
      }
    }

    if (order.length !== this.adjacency.size) {
      throw new Error("Graph contains a cycle; topological sort is impossible");
    }

    return order;
  }

  dijkstra(
    start: V,
    target: V
  ): { distance: number; path: V[] } | null {
    this.ensureVertex(start);
    this.ensureVertex(target);

    const distances = new Map<V, number>();
    const previous = new Map<V, V | null>();
    const unvisited = new Set<V>(this.vertices());

    for (const vertex of this.vertices()) {
      distances.set(vertex, Infinity);
      previous.set(vertex, null);
    }
    distances.set(start, 0);

    while (unvisited.size > 0) {
      // Simple O(V^2) selection; use a heap for larger graphs.
      let current: V | null = null;
      let minDistance = Infinity;

      for (const vertex of unvisited) {
        const d = distances.get(vertex)!;
        if (d < minDistance) {
          minDistance = d;
          current = vertex;
        }
      }

      if (current === null || minDistance === Infinity) {
        break;
      }

      unvisited.delete(current);

      if (current === target) {
        break;
      }

      for (const { to, weight } of this.adjacency.get(current) ?? []) {
        if (!unvisited.has(to)) continue;

        const alternative = minDistance + (weight ?? 1);
        if (alternative < distances.get(to)!) {
          distances.set(to, alternative);
          previous.set(to, current);
        }
      }
    }

    const targetDistance = distances.get(target)!;
    if (targetDistance === Infinity) {
      return null;
    }

    const path: V[] = [];
    let cursor: V | null = target;

    while (cursor !== null) {
      path.unshift(cursor);
      cursor = previous.get(cursor) ?? null;
    }

    return {
      distance: targetDistance,
      path,
    };
  }

  // ---------- helpers ----------

  private ensureVertex(vertex: V): void {
    if (!this.adjacency.has(vertex)) {
      throw new Error(`Vertex not found: ${String(vertex)}`);
    }
  }
}

// =========================
// Runnable example / demo
// =========================

function main(): void {
  // Undirected, unweighted graph
  const undirected = new Graph<string>(false);
  undirected
    .addEdge("A", "B")
    .addEdge("A", "C")
    .addEdge("B", "D")
    .addEdge("C", "D")
    .addEdge("D", "E");

  console.log("Undirected graph edges:", undirected.edges());
  console.log("BFS from A:", undirected.bfs("A"));
  console.log("DFS recursive from A:", undirected.dfs("A"));
  console.log("DFS iterative from A:", undirected.dfsIterative("A"));
  console.log("Connected components:", undirected.connectedComponents());

  // Directed acyclic graph for topological sort
  const dag = new Graph<string>(true);
  dag
    .addEdge("compile", "link")
    .addEdge("source", "compile")
    .addEdge("source", "docs")
    .addEdge("tests", "deploy")
    .addEdge("link", "deploy");

  console.log("Topological order:", dag.topologicalSort());

  // Weighted directed graph for Dijkstra
  const weighted = new Graph<string>(true);
  weighted
    .addEdge("S", "A", 4)
    .addEdge("S", "B", 2)
    .addEdge("A", "C", 3)
    .addEdge("B", "A", 1)
    .addEdge("B", "C", 5)
    .addEdge("C", "T", 2);

  const result = weighted.dijkstra("S", "T");
  console.log("Shortest path S -> T:", result);

  // Remove operations
  undirected.removeEdge("A", "C");
  undirected.removeVertex("E");
  console.log("After removals, BFS from A:", undirected.bfs("A"));
}

main();
```

