### Design notes

- **Adjacency list**: Each vertex maps to an array of `{ to, weight }` edges. This gives good average performance for sparse graphs.
- **Directed vs undirected**: The constructor flag controls whether `addEdge` inserts one or two directed edges.
- **BFS**: Uses a queue implemented as an array with a `head` pointer to avoid costly `Array.prototype.shift()` calls.
- **DFS**: Both recursive and iterative versions are included. The iterative version pushes neighbors in reverse order so traversal order matches the recursive version.
- **Connected components**: Runs DFS from every unvisited vertex, which correctly handles disconnected graphs.
- **Topological sort**: Uses Kahn’s algorithm and detects cycles by checking whether all vertices were emitted.
- **Dijkstra**: Uses a simple array-based priority selection. This is fine for small and medium graphs; for very large graphs, replace it with a binary or Fibonacci heap.

### Time complexity overview

| Operation | Complexity |
|---|---:|
| Add vertex | `O(1)` |
| Add edge | `O(1)` |
| BFS / DFS | `O(V + E)` |
| Connected components | `O(V + E)` |
| Topological sort | `O(V + E)` |
| Dijkstra, array priority queue | `O(V^2 + E)` |
| Dijkstra, binary heap | `O((V + E) log V)` |

# Graphs and traversals in TypeScript

## 1. What a graph is

A **graph** is a set of **vertices (nodes)** connected by **edges**. Edges can be:

- **Directed** or **undirected**
- **Weighted** (cost/length) or **unweighted**
- **Cyclic** or **acyclic** (DAG = directed acyclic graph)

Common representations:

| Representation | Best for | Memory |
|---|---|---|
| Adjacency list | Sparse graphs | O(V+E) |
| Adjacency matrix | Dense graphs, fast edge lookup | O(V²) |
| Edge list | Simple storage, algorithms like Kruskal | O(E) |

In TypeScript, an **adjacency list** using `Map` is usually the most practical and type-safe.

---

## 2. TypeScript representation

### Generic adjacency-list graph

```ts
class Graph<T> {
  private adj = new Map<T, T[]>();

  addNode(v: T): void {
    if (!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v, []);
  }

  addEdge(u: T, v: T, undirected = false): void {
    this.addNode(u);
    this.addNode(v);
    this.adj.get(u)!.push(v);
    if (undirected) this.adj.get(v)!.push(u);
  }

  removeEdge(u: T, v: T): void {
    const list = this.adj.get(u);
    if (list) this.adj.set(u, list.filter(x => x !== v));
  }

  neighbors(v: T): T[] {
    return this.adj.get(v) ?? [];
  }

  nodes(): T[] {
    return [...this.adj.keys()];
  }

  hasNode(v: T): boolean {
    return this.adj.has(v);
  }
}
```

Usage:

```ts
const g = new Graph<string>();
g.addEdge("A", "B");
g.addEdge("A", "C");
g.addEdge("B", "D");
console.log(g.neighbors("A")); // ["B", "C"]
```

### Weighted graph

```ts
type Edge<T> = { to: T; weight: number };

class WeightedGraph<T> {
  private adj = new Map<T, Edge<T>[]>();

  addEdge(u: T, v: T, w: number, undirected = false): void {
    if (!this.adj.has(u)) this.adj.set(u, []);
    if (!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v, []);
    this.adj.get(u)!.push({ to: v, weight: w });
    if (undirected) this.adj.get(v)!.push({ to: u, weight: w });
  }

  neighbors(v: T): Edge<T>[] {
    return this.adj.get(v) ?? [];
  }

  nodes(): T[] {
    return [...this.adj.keys()];
  }
}
```

---

## 3. Traversals

### BFS — Breadth-First Search

Uses a **queue**. Good for:
- shortest path in unweighted graphs
- level-order exploration
- connected components

```ts
function bfs<T>(g: Graph<T>, start: T): T[] {
  const visited = new Set<T>([start]);
  const queue: T[] = [start];
  const result: T[] = [];

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const u = queue.shift()!;        // dequeue
    result.push(u);

    for (const v of g.neighbors(u)) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) {
        visited.add(v);
        queue.push(v);              // enqueue
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

For large graphs, replace `queue.shift()` (O(n)) with a proper deque or index pointer:

```ts
let head = 0;
while (head < queue.length) {
  const u = queue[head++];
  // ...
}
```

### DFS — Depth-First Search

Two common forms.

Recursive (simple, but beware stack overflow on deep graphs):

```ts
function dfsRecursive<T>(g: Graph<T>, start: T, visited = new Set<T>()): T[] {
  visited.add(start);
  const result = [start];

  for (const v of g.neighbors(start)) {
    if (!visited.has(v)) {
      result.push(...dfsRecursive(g, v, visited));
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

Iterative (safer for large/deep graphs):

```ts
function dfsIterative<T>(g: Graph<T>, start: T): T[] {
  const visited = new Set<T>();
  const stack: T[] = [start];
  const result: T[] = [];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const u = stack.pop()!;

    if (visited.has(u)) continue;
    visited.add(u);
    result.push(u);

    // Push neighbors; reverse to preserve natural order
    for (const v of [...g.neighbors(u)].reverse()) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) stack.push(v);
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

---

## 4. Common graph algorithms in TypeScript

### Cycle detection (directed graph)

Uses three colors: `0 = unvisited`, `1 = visiting`, `2 = done`.

```ts
function hasCycle<T>(g: Graph<T>): boolean {
  const color = new Map<T, 0 | 1 | 2>();
  for (const n of g.nodes()) color.set(n, 0);

  const dfs = (u: T): boolean => {
    color.set(u, 1);
    for (const v of g.neighbors(u)) {
      const c = color.get(v)!;
      if (c === 1) return true;          // back edge
      if (c === 0 && dfs(v)) return true;
    }
    color.set(u, 2);
    return false;
  };

  for (const n of g.nodes()) {
    if (color.get(n) === 0 && dfs(n)) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

### Topological sort (Kahn's algorithm, DAG)

```ts
function topoSort<T>(g: Graph<T>): T[] {
  const indeg = new Map<T, number>();
  for (const u of g.nodes()) indeg.set(u, 0);

  for (const u of g.nodes()) {
    for (const v of g.neighbors(u)) {
      indeg.set(v, (indeg.get(v) ?? 0) + 1);
    }
  }

  const q = [...indeg.entries()]
    .filter(([, d]) => d === 0)
    .map(([n]) => n);

  const out: T[] = [];

  while (q.length > 0) {
    const u = q.shift()!;
    out.push(u);
    for (const v of g.neighbors(u)) {
      const d = indeg.get(v)! - 1;
      indeg.set(v, d);
      if (d === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (out.length !== g.nodes().length) {
    throw new Error("Graph has a cycle; topological sort impossible");
  }
  return out;
}
```

### Dijkstra (shortest path, weighted, non-negative weights)

```ts
function dijkstra<T>(g: WeightedGraph<T>, start: T): Map<T, number> {
  const dist = new Map<T, number>();
  for (const n of g.nodes()) dist.set(n, Infinity);
  dist.set(start, 0);

  // Simple array-based priority queue (use a heap for large graphs)
  const pq: [number, T][] = [[0, start]];

  while (pq.length > 0) {
    pq.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
    const [d, u] = pq.shift()!;

    if (d > dist.get(u)!) continue; // stale entry

    for (const { to: v, weight: w } of g.neighbors(u)) {
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist.get(v)!) {
        dist.set(v, nd);
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

### Connected components (undirected graph)

```ts
function components<T>(g: Graph<T>): T[][] {
  const seen = new Set<T>();
  const result: T[][] = [];

  for (const start of g.nodes()) {
    if (seen.has(start)) continue;
    const comp = dfsIterative(g, start);
    comp.forEach(n => seen.add(n));
    result.push(comp);
  }
  return result;
}
```

---

## 5. Adjacency matrix variant (dense graphs)

```ts
class MatrixGraph {
  private n: number;
  private m: number[][];

  constructor(n: number) {
    this.n = n;
    this.m = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  }

  addEdge(u: number, v: number, w = 1): void {
    this.m[u][v] = w;
  }

  neighbors(u: number): number[] {
    const res: number[] = [];
    for (let v = 0; v < this.n; v++) {
      if (this.m[u][v] !== 0) res.push(v);
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

