## Mistake: marking a node “visited” too late (classic cycle → infinite recursion)

```ts
// graph: adjacency list
const graph = new Map<string, string[]>([
  ["A", ["B"]],
  ["B", ["C"]],
  ["C", ["A"]], // cycle: C -> A
]);

function dfs(graph: Map<string, string[]>, start: string): string[] {
  const visited = new Set<string>();
  const order: string[] = [];

  function visit(node: string) {
    // Only skip if already fully processed
    if (visited.has(node)) return;

    order.push(node);

    for (const neighbor of graph.get(node) ?? []) {
      visit(neighbor); // recurse first
    }

    // ❌ Mistake: mark visited AFTER exploring neighbors
    visited.add(node);
  }

  visit(start);
  return order;
}

console.log(dfs(graph, "A")); // RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
```

### What goes wrong

- The node is added to `order` and then all neighbors are explored **before** the node is marked visited.
- Because `visited` is only checked at the *start* of `visit`, a cycle causes the recursion to re-enter the same node before it has ever been marked.
- `A -> B -> C -> A`: when we reach `C`, we call `visit("A")` again; `A` is not in `visited` yet, so we push `"A"` again and recurse forever.
- Even on acyclic graphs this can cause exponential blowup when the same node is reachable via multiple paths, because each path re-explores the subtree.

### Corrected version

Mark the node as visited **as soon as you decide to process it**, before touching its neighbors.

```ts
function dfsCorrect(graph: Map<string, string[]>, start: string): string[] {
  const visited = new Set<string>();
  const order: string[] = [];

  function visit(node: string) {
    if (visited.has(node)) return;

    // ✅ Mark visited immediately, before exploring neighbors
    visited.add(node);
    order.push(node);

    for (const neighbor of graph.get(node) ?? []) {
      visit(neighbor);
    }
  }

  visit(start);
  return order;
}

console.log(dfsCorrect(graph, "A")); // ["A", "B", "C"]
```

### Why this fixes it

- The first time a node is entered, it is committed to `visited` before any recursive call can revisit it.
- Later arrivals via another path or via a cycle hit `if (visited.has(node)) return;` and stop.
- Each node is pushed to `order` at most once, so the traversal is **O(V + E)** for an adjacency-list graph.

### Practical notes

- **Disconnected graphs:** this only traverses the component containing `start`. To cover everything, loop over all vertices and call `visit` on unvisited ones:

  ```ts
  for (const node of graph.keys()) {
    if (!visited.has(node)) visit(node);
  }
  ```

- **Deep graphs:** recursion can still overflow the call stack on very deep or chain-like graphs. For production code, prefer an iterative DFS with an explicit stack, or BFS with a queue:

  ```ts
  function bfs(graph: Map<string, string[]>, start: string): string[] {
    const visited = new Set<string>([start]);
    const queue: string[] = [start];
    const order: string[] = [];

    while (queue.length) {
      const node = queue.shift()!;
      order.push(node);
      for (const neighbor of graph.get(node) ?? []) {
        if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
          visited.add(neighbor);
          queue.push(neighbor);
        }
      }
    }
    return order;
  }
  ```

- **Visited check placement in iterative versions:** mark neighbors visited when you *enqueue* them, not when you *dequeue* them. Otherwise the same neighbor can be enqueued many times, blowing up memory/time.

# Common Pitfalls with Graphs & Traversals in TypeScript

Below are the pitfalls I see most often, grouped by category. Each includes the buggy pattern and an idiomatic fix.

---

## 1. Forgetting `visited` (or using a `Set` incorrectly)

**Pitfall:** In a cyclic graph, naïve DFS/BFS never terminates.

```ts
// BUG: infinite loop on cycles
function dfsBad(adj: Map<string, string[]>, start: string) {
  const stack = [start];
  while (stack.length) {
    const u = stack.pop()!;
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) stack.push(v); // never marks visited
  }
}
```

**Fix:** Mark visited when you **enqueue/push**, not when you pop (important for BFS to avoid duplicate enqueues).

```ts
function dfs(adj: Map<string, string[]>, start: string): string[] {
  const visited = new Set<string>();
  const out: string[] = [];
  const stack = [start];
  visited.add(start);
  while (stack.length) {
    const u = stack.pop()!;
    out.push(u);
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) {
        visited.add(v);
        stack.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

> **Subtle bug:** Marking visited only on *pop* in BFS causes the same node to be enqueued many times → exponential blowup on dense graphs.

---

## 2. Recursion depth → stack overflow

**Pitfall:** Recursive DFS on a 10⁵-node path crashes with `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`.

```ts
// BUG on deep graphs
function dfsRec(adj: Map<number, number[]>, u: number, visited = new Set<number>()) {
  visited.add(u);
  for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) if (!visited.has(v)) dfsRec(adj, v, visited);
}
```

**Fix:** Use an explicit stack. If you must recurse, raise the limit is not portable — prefer iterative.

```ts
function dfsIterative(adj: Map<number, number[]>, start: number): number[] {
  const visited = new Set<number>();
  const out: number[] = [];
  const stack: number[] = [start];
  visited.add(start);
  while (stack.length) {
    const u = stack.pop()!;
    out.push(u);
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) { visited.add(v); stack.push(v); }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

---

## 3. O(V) membership check with arrays

**Pitfall:** `visited.includes(v)` inside a loop makes traversal O(V·E).

```ts
const visited: string[] = [];
// O(n) per check
if (!visited.includes(v)) visited.push(v);
```

**Fix:** `Set` (or `Map`/boolean array indexed by node id).

```ts
const visited = new Set<string>();
if (!visited.has(v)) visited.add(v);
```

For integer node ids `0..N-1`, a `Uint8Array(N)` is even faster and GC-friendly.

---

## 4. Treating an undirected graph as directed (or vice versa)

**Pitfall:** Building adjacency only one way for an undirected graph → unreachable components / wrong distances.

```ts
// BUG: only adds u->v
edges.forEach(([u, v]) => (adj.get(u) ?? []).push(v));
```

**Fix:** Add both directions, and dedupe parallel edges if needed.

```ts
function buildUndirected(edges: [number, number][]): Map<number, number[]> {
  const adj = new Map<number, number[]>();
  const add = (a: number, b: number) => {
    (adj.get(a) ?? adj.set(a, []).get(a)!).push(b);
  };
  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    if (u === v) continue; // skip self-loop unless intended
    add(u, v);
    add(v, u);
  }
  return adj;
}
```

---

## 5. Missing nodes with no edges (isolated vertices)

**Pitfall:** Adjacency built only from edges drops isolated nodes → traversal misses them; connected-components count wrong.

```ts
// BUG: node 5 with no edges never appears in adj
```

**Fix:** Seed adjacency with all nodes first.

```ts
const nodes = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const adj = new Map(nodes.map(n => [n, [] as number[]]));
for (const [u, v] of edges) {
  adj.get(u)!.push(v);
  adj.get(v)!.push(u);
}
```

---

## 6. Mutating the graph during traversal

**Pitfall:** Deleting edges/nodes while iterating adjacency → `ConcurrentModification`-style skips or crashes.

```ts
// BUG: mutating a Map while iterating
for (const [u, nbrs] of adj) {
  nbrs.splice(0); // corrupts iteration
}
```

**Fix:** Collect mutations, apply after; or iterate over a snapshot (`[...adj.entries()]`).

```ts
const toRemove: number[] = [];
for (const [u, nbrs] of [...adj.entries()]) {
  if (shouldRemove(u)) toRemove.push(u);
}
for (const u of toRemove) adj.delete(u);
```

---

## 7. BFS with `Array.prototype.unshift` / `shift` (O(n²))

**Pitfall:**

```ts
const q: number[] = [start];
while (q.length) {
  const u = q.shift()!; // O(n) each → O(V²)
  ...
  q.unshift(v); // also O(n), and wrong order
}
```

**Fix:** Use a head index (or a real queue). Never `unshift` for BFS.

```ts
function bfs(adj: Map<number, number[]>, start: number): number[] {
  const visited = new Set<number>([start]);
  const q: number[] = [start];
  const out: number[] = [];
  let head = 0;
  while (head < q.length) {
    const u = q[head++];
    out.push(u);
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) { visited.add(v); q.push(v); }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

For huge graphs, use a ring buffer or a library queue (e.g. `denque`).

---

## 8. Dijkstra: not skipping stale priority-queue entries

**Pitfall:** Relaxing a node multiple times because you don't check `dist[u] < poppedDist`.

```ts
// BUG: processes same node many times
while (pq.size) {
  const { d, u } = pq.pop()!;
  for (const [v, w] of adj.get(u)!) {
    if (d + w < dist[v]) { dist[v] = d + w; pq.push({ d: dist[v], v }); }
  }
}
```

**Fix:** Skip stale entries.

```ts
while (pq.size) {
  const { d, u } = pq.pop()!;
  if (d !== dist[u]) continue; // stale
  for (const [v, w] of adj.get(u)!) {
    const nd = d + w;
    if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; pq.push({ d: nd, v }); }
  }
}
```

Also: initialize `dist` with `Infinity`, and use a proper min-heap (binary heap), not `Array.sort` per pop.

---

## 9. Topological sort: not detecting cycles

**Pitfall:** Kahn's algorithm silently outputs a partial order if there's a cycle.

```ts
// BUG: returns shorter array on cyclic graph; caller assumes full order
const order = kahn(adj);
use(order); // may be wrong
```

**Fix:** Compare output length to node count.

```ts
function topoSort(adj: Map<number, number[]>, indeg: Map<number, number>): number[] | null {
  const q: number[] = [];
  for (const [n, d] of indeg) if (d === 0) q.push(n);
  const order: number[] = [];
  let head = 0;
  while (head < q.length) {
    const u = q[head++];
    order.push(u);
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (--indeg.get(v)! === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  return order.length === indeg.size ? order : null; // null => cycle
}
```

---

## 10. Weak typing of adjacency (losing type safety)

**Pitfall:** `Map<any, any>` or `Record<string, any>` defeats TypeScript and hides nulls.

```ts
const adj: any = {};
adj[u].push(v); // compiles, crashes at runtime if adj[u] undefined
```

**Fix:** Strong types + non-null assertions only after seeding, or use a typed adjacency class.

```ts
type NodeId = string | number;
type WeightedEdge = { to: NodeId; w: number };

class Graph<N extends NodeId> {
  private adj = new Map<N, WeightedEdge[]>();
  addNode(n: N) { if (!this.adj.has(n)) this.adj.set(n, []); }
  addEdge(u: N, v: N, w = 1) {
    this.addNode(u); this.addNode(v);
    this.adj.get(u)!.push({ to: v, w });
  }
  neighbors(u: N): ReadonlyArray<WeightedEdge> {
    return this.adj.get(u) ?? [];
  }
  nodes(): Iterable<N> { return this.adj.keys(); }
}
```

---

## 11. `for...of` over a `Map`/`Set` while mutating

**Pitfall:** Deleting/inserting during iteration can skip elements or throw in strict modes (and is confusing).

```ts
for (const u of adj.keys()) {
  if (dead.has(u)) adj.delete(u); // risky
}
```

**Fix:** Iterate over a snapshot.

```ts
for (const u of [...adj.keys()]) {
  if (dead.has(u)) adj.delete(u);
}
```

---

## 12. Self-loops & parallel edges

**Pitfall:** Self-loop `u→u` makes DFS push `u` again; parallel edges inflate degrees and Dijkstra relaxes duplicates.

```ts
// In degree counting:
indeg[u]++; indeg[v]++; // self-loop adds 2 → never reaches 0
```

**Fix:** Decide semantics explicitly: skip self-loops, or count them; dedupe parallel edges with a `Set` of edge keys when building.

```ts
const edgeKey = (u: number, v: number) => `${u}->${v}`;
const seen = new Set<string>();
for (const [u, v] of rawEdges) {
  const k = edgeKey(u, v);
  if (seen.has(k)) continue;
  seen.add(k);
  addEdge(u, v);
}
```

---

## 13. Not resetting state between runs (reusing a visited set)

**Pitfall:** Reusing a module-level `visited` Set across calls yields stale results.

```ts
const visited = new Set<number>(); // module scope
export function hasPath(...) { visited.clear(); ... } // easy to forget
```

**Fix:** Create visited locally per call; or use a `runId` + `number[]` array for high performance.

```ts
function dfs(adj: Map<number, number[]>, start: number, target: number): boolean {
  const visited = new Set<number>();
  // ...
}
```

For performance-critical code, use a versioned array:

```ts
const seen = new Int32Array(N);
let runId = 0;
function visit(u: number) { seen[u] = runId; }
function wasVisited(u: number) { return seen[u] === runId; }
// call: runId++; before each traversal
```

---

## 14. Assuming connectivity / single component

**Pitfall:** Running one traversal from node 0 and assuming all nodes reached → wrong SCC/shortest-path results on disconnected graphs.

**Fix:** Outer loop over all nodes.

```ts
function components(adj: Map<number, number[]>): number[][] {
  const visited = new Set<number>();
  const comps: number[][] = [];
  for (const start of adj.keys()) {
    if (visited.has(start)) continue;
    const comp = bfsComponent(adj, start, visited);
    comps.push(comp);
  }
  return comps;
}
```

---

## 15. Off-by-one / wrong `head` pointer in queue (or mutating array length)

**Pitfall:** Mixing `q.shift()` with index-based access, or reassigning `q.length = 0`, corrupts traversal mid-flight.

**Fix:** Choose one strategy: either pure `shift()` (acceptable only for small graphs) or index-based with append-only array. Never both.

---

## 16. Memory: storing full path copies in BFS/DFS

**Pitfall:** `path.concat(v)` per node → O(V²) memory/time; or storing `parent` maps incorrectly.

**Fix:** Store `parent: Map<N, N>` and reconstruct at the end.

```ts
function shortestPath(adj: Map<number, number[]>, s: number, t: number): number[] | null {
  const parent = new Map<number, number>();
  const visited = new Set([s]);
  const q = [s];
  let head = 0;
  while (head < q.length) {
    const u = q[head++];
    if (u === t) {
      const path: number[] = [];
      let cur: number | undefined = t;
      while (cur !== undefined) { path.push(cur); cur = parent.get(cur); }
      return path.reverse();
    }
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) { visited.add(v); parent.set(v, u); q.push(v); }
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

---

## 17. Comparing nodes by reference instead of identity

**Pitfall:** Using object nodes and `===`/`Set` with objects uses reference equality; same logical node from two literals won't match.

```ts
const n1 = { id: 1 };
const n2 = { id: 1 };
visited.has(n2); // false even if n1 added
```

**Fix:** Key the graph by a primitive id (`number`/`string`), and store node data separately.

```ts
type Node = { id: number; label: string };
const adj = new Map<number, number[]>(); // id-based
const data = new Map<number, Node>();
```

---

## 18. Async traversal / generator pitfalls

**Pitfall:** `async function*` DFS without bounding concurrency; or using `await` inside tight loops causing ordering surprises; also forgetting to handle cancellation.

**Fix:** Keep traversal synchronous over an adjacency snapshot; push async I/O to the edges (lazy neighbors). If you need cancellation, check an `AbortSignal` between levels.

```ts
async function* bfsLevels(adj: Map<number, number[]>, start: number, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  let level = [start];
  while (level.length) {
    if (signal?.aborted) return;
    yield level;
    const next: number[] = [];
    for (const u of level) for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) { visited.add(v); next.push(v); }
    }
    level = next;
  }
}
```

---

## Quick checklist before shipping graph code

- [ ] Visited is a `Set`/array, marked at **enqueue** time for BFS.
- [ ] No recursion on potentially deep inputs.
- [ ] Handles disconnected graphs (outer loop over all nodes).
- [ ] Cycle behavior defined (topo sort returns `null`, DFS handles back-edges).
- [ ] Self-loops & parallel edges addressed.
- [ ] Undirected edges added both ways.
- [ ] Isolated nodes present in adjacency.
- [ ] No mutation during iteration.
- [ ] Dijkstra skips stale PQ entries.
- [ ] Nodes keyed by primitive id, not object reference.
- [ ] BFS queue is index-based, not `shift()`.

